window.__WB_BOOK_DATA__["C__20_b03"] = {"id": "C__20_b03", "title": "Scott Meyers《Effective Modern C++》", "workbench": "C++20与量化工程工作台.html", "chapters": [{"title": "原书逐页正文 p.1–p.25", "pages": "p.1–p.25", "raw_text": "#### 第 1 页原文\n\nPage 1 \n \n1 \nIntroduction 4 2 \nChapter 1 Deducing Types 11 3 \n Understand template type deduction. 11 Item 1:4 \n Understand auto type deduction. 21 Item 2:5 \n Understand decltype. 26 Item 3:6 \n Know how to view deduced types. 34 Item 4:7 \nChapter 2 auto 42 8 \n Prefer auto to explicit type declarations. 42 Item 5:9 \n Be aware of the typed initializer idiom. 48 Item 6:10 \nChapter 3 From C++98 to C++11 and C++14 56 11 \n Distinguish () and {} when creating objects. 57 Item 7:12 \n Prefer nullptr to 0 and NULL. 67 Item 8:13 \n Prefer alias declarations to typedefs. 72 Item 9:14 \n Prefer scoped enums to unscoped enums. 77 Item 10:15 \n Prefer deleted functions to private undefined ones. 84 Item 11:16 \n Declare overriding functions override. 89 Item 12:17 \n Prefer const_iterators to iterators. 96 Item 13:18 \n Use constexpr whenever possible. 101 Item 14:19 \n Make const member functions thread-safe. 107 Item 15:20 \n Declare functions noexcept whenever possible. 113 Item 16:21\n\n#### 第 2 页原文\n\nPage 2 \n \n Consider pass by value for cheap-to-move parameters that are Item 17:1 \nalways copied. 120 2 \n Consider emplacement instead of insertion. 128 Item 18:3 \n Understand special member function generation. 136 Item 19:4 \nChapter 4 Smart Pointers 145 5 \n Use std::unique_ptr for exclusive-ownership Item 20:6 \n resource management. 147 7 \n Use std::shared_ptr for shared-ownership resource Item 21:8 \nmanagement. 154 9 \n Use std::weak_ptr for std::shared_ptr-like pointers that can Item 22:10 \ndangle.  164 11 \n Prefer std::make_unique and std::make_shared to direct use of Item 23:12 \nnew.  170 13 \n When using the Pimpl Idiom, define special member functions in the Item 24:14 \nimplementation file. 179 15 \nChapter 5 Rvalue References, Move Semantics, and Perfect Forwarding 189 16 \n Understand std::move and std::forward. 190 Item 25:17 \n Distinguish universal references from rvalue references. 197 Item 26:18 \n Use std::move on rvalue references, std::forward on universal Item 27:19 \nreferences.  202 20 \n Avoid overloading on universal references. 210 Item 28:21 \n Familiarize yourself with alternatives to overloading on universal Item 29:22 \nreferences.  217 23 \n Understand reference collapsing. 228 Item 30:24\n\n#### 第 3 页原文\n\nPage 3 \n \n Assume that move operations are not present, not cheap, and not Item 31:1 \nused.  234 2 \n Familiarize yourself with perfect forwarding failure cases. 238 Item 32:3 \nChapter 6 Lambda Expressions 249 4 \n Avoid default capture modes. 251 Item 33:5 \n Use init capture to move objects into closures. 258 Item 34:6 \n Use decltype on auto&& parameters to std::forward them. 264 Item 35:7 \n Prefer lambdas to std::bind. 267 Item 36:8 \nChapter 7 The Concurrency API 275 9 \n Prefer task-based programming to thread-based. 275 Item 37:10 \n Specify std::launch::async if asynchronicity is essential. 280 Item 38:11 \n Make std::threads unjoinable on all paths. 286 Item 39:12 \n Be aware of varying thread handle destructor  behavior. 293 Item 40:13 \n Consider void futures for one-shot event communication. 299 Item 41:14 \n Use std::atomic for concurrency, volatile for Item 42:15 \nspecial memory. 308 16 \n 17\n\n#### 第 4 页原文\n\nPage 4 \n \n1 \nIf you’re an experienced C++ programmer and are anything like me, you initially 2 \napproached C++11 thinking, “Yes, yes,  I get it. I t’s C++, only more so.” But as you r 3 \nknowledge of the revised language increased, you were surprised by the scale of 4 \nthe changes. auto objects, range-based for loops, lambda expressions, and rvalue 5 \nreferences change the very face of C++, to say nothing of the new concurrency fea-6 \ntures. And then there are the idiomatic changes! 0 and typedefs are out, nullptr 7 \nand alias declarations are in. Enums should now be scoped. Smart pointers are 8 \nnow preferable to built -in ones. Moving objects is normally better than copying 9 \nthem. There’s a lot to learn about C++11.  10 \nThe adoption of C++14 hardly made things easier.  11 \nSo...a lot to learn. More importantly, a lot to learn about making effective use of the 12 \nnew capabilities. If you need information on the basic syntax or semantics of fe a-13 \ntures in “modern” C++, resources abound, but if you’re looking for guidance on 14 \nhow to employ the features to create software that’s correct, efficient, maintain a-15 \nble, and portable, the search is more challenging. That’s where this book comes in.  16 \nIt’s devoted not to describing the features of C++11 and C++14, but rather to their 17 \neffective application. 18 \nThe information in the book is broken into guidelines called Items. Want to under-19 \nstand the various forms of type deduction ? Or to know when  (and when not)  to 20 \ndeclare objects using auto? Are you i nterested in why const member functions 21 \nshould be thread -safe, how to implement the Pimpl Idiom using 22 \nstd::unique_ptr, why you should avoid default capture modes in lambda e x-23 \npressions, or the difference s between (and prop er uses of)  std::atomic and 24 \nvolatile? The answers are all here. Furthermore, they’re platform-independent, 25 \nStandards-conformant answers. This is a book about portable C++. 26 \nItems comprise guidelines, not rules, because guidelines have exceptions. The 27 \nmost important part of each Item is not the advice it offers, but the rationale b e-28 \nhind the advice. Once you’ve read that, you’ll be in a position to determine whether 29 \nthe circumstances of your project justify disregarding the Item’s guidance. The 30\n\n#### 第 5 页原文\n\nPage 5 \n \ntrue goal of th is book is n’t to tell you what to do or what to avoid doing, but  to 1 \nconvey a deeper understanding of how things work in C++11 and C++14.  2 \nTerminology and Conventions 3 \nTo make sure we understand one another, it’s important to agree on some term i-4 \nnology, beginning, ironically, with “C++.” There have been four standardized ver-5 \nsions of C++, each named after the year in which the corresponding ISO Standard 6 \nwas adopted: C++98, C++03, C++11, and C++14. C++98 and C++03 differ only in 7 \nsubtle technical details, so in this book, I refer to both as C++98. 8 \nWhen I mention C++98, I mean only that version of the language. Where I refer to 9 \nC++11, I mean both C++11 and C++14, be cause C++14 is effectively a superset of 10 \nC++11. When I write C++14, I mean specifically C++14. And i f I simply mention 11 \nC++, I’m making a broad statement that  pertains to all language versions. As a r e-12 \nsult, I might say that C++ places a premium on efficiency (true for all versions), 13 \nthat C++98 lacks support for concurrency (true for C++98 only ), that C++11 su p-14 \nports lambda expressions (true for C++11 and C++14), and that C++14 offers ge n-15 \neralized function return type deduction (true for C++14 only). 16 \nC++11’s most pervasive feature is probably move semantics, and the foundation of 17 \nmove semantics is  distinguishing expressions that are rvalues from those that are 18 \nlvalues. That’s because rvalues indicate objects eligible for move operations, while 19 \nlvalues generally don’t. In concept (though not always in practice) , rvalues corr e-20 \nspond to anonymous temporary objects returned from functions, while lvalues 21 \ncorrespond to objects you can refer to, either by name or by following a pointer or 22 \nreference.  23 \nA useful heuristic to determine whether an expression is an lvalue is to ask if you 24 \ncan take its address. If  you can, it typically is. If you can’t, it’s  usually an rvalue. A 25 \nnice feature of this heuristic is that it helps you remember that the type of an e x-26 \npression is independent of whether the expression is  an lvalue or an rvalue. That 27 \nis, given a type T, you can have both lvalues of type T and rvalues of type T. It’s es-28 \npecially important to remember this when dealing with a parameter of rvalue ref-29 \nerence type, because the parameter itself is an lvalue: 30\n\n#### 第 6 页原文\n\nPage 6 \n \nclass Widget { 1 \npublic: 2 \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs);    // rhs is an lvalue, though it has 3 \n  …                        // an rvalue reference type 4 \n};                          5 \nHere, it’d be perfectly valid to take rhs’s address inside Widget’s move construc-6 \ntor, so rhs is an lvalue, even though its type is an rvalue reference. (By similar rea-7 \nsoning, all parameters are lvalues.) 8 \nThis code example demonstrates several conventions I typically follow: 9 \n The class name is Widget. I use Widget whenever I want to refer to a n arbi-10 \ntrary user-defined type. Unless I need to show specific details of the class, I use 11 \nWidget without declaring it.  12 \n I use the parameter name rhs, which stands for “right -hand side.” It’s my pr e-13 \nferred parameter name for the move operations  (i.e., move constr uctor and 14 \nmove assignment operator) and the copy operations (i.e., copy constructor and 15 \ncopy assignment operator), though I also employ it for the right-hand parame-16 \nter of binary operators: 17 \nMatrix operator+(const Matrix& lhs, const Matrix& rhs);  18 \nIt’s no surprise, I hope, that lhs stands for “left-hand side.”  19 \n I highlight parts of code or parts of comments to draw your attention to them. 20 \nIn the code above, I’ve highlighted the declaration of rhs and the part of the 21 \ncomment noting that rhs is an lvalue.  22 \n I use “…” to indicate “other code could go here.”  This narrow ellipses is diffe r-23 \nent from the wide ellipsis (“ ...”) that’s used in the source code for C++11’s 24 \nvariadic templates. That sounds confusing, but it’s not. For example: 25 \ntemplate<typename... Ts>                // these are C++  26 \nvoid processVals(const Ts&... params)   // source code 27 \n{                                       // ellipses 28 \n  …                                     // this means \"some 29 \n                                        // code goes here\" 30 \n} 31\n\n#### 第 7 页原文\n\nPage 7 \n \nThe declaration of processVals shows that I use typename when declaring 1 \ntype parameters  in templates , but that’s merely a personal preference;  the 2 \nkeyword class would work just as well. On those few occasions where I show 3 \ncode excerpts from a C++ Standard, I declare type parameters using class, be-4 \ncause that’s what the Standards do. 5 \nWhen an object is initialized with another object of the same type, the new object 6 \nis said to be a copy of the initializ ing object, even if the copy was created via the 7 \nmove constructor. Regrettably, there’s no term inology in C++ that distinguishes 8 \nbetween an object that’s a copy -constructed copy and one that’s a move -9 \nconstructed copy: 10 \nvoid someFunc(Widget w);        // someFunc's parameter w 11 \n                                // is passed by value 12 \n 13 \nWidget wid;                     // wid is some Widget 14 \nsomeFunc(wid);                  // in this call to someFunc,  15 \n                                // w is a copy of wid that's 16 \n                                // created via copy construction 17 \nsomeFunc(std::move(wid));       // in this call to SomeFunc, 18 \n                                // w is a copy of wid that's 19 \n                                // created via move construction 20 \nCopies of rvalues are generally move-constructed, while copies of lvalues are typi-21 \ncally copy-constructed. An implication is that if you know only that an object is a 22 \ncopy of another object, it's not possible  to say how expensive it was to construct 23 \nthe copy. In the code above, for examp le, there’s no way to say how expensive it is 24 \nto create the parameter w without knowing whether rvalues or lvalues are passed 25 \nto someFunc. (You’d also have to know the cost of moving and copying Widgets.) 26 \nIn a function call , the expressions passed at the c all site are the function’s argu-27 \nments. The arguments are used to initialize  the function’s parameters. In the first 28 \ncall to someFunc above, the argument is wid. In the second call, the argument is 29 \nstd::move(wid). In both calls, the parameter is w. The distinction between argu-30 \nments and parameters is important, because parameters are lvalues, but the a r-31 \nguments with which they are initialized may be rvalues or lvalues. This is especial-32 \nly relevant during the process of perfect forwarding, whereby an argument passed 33\n\n#### 第 8 页原文\n\nPage 8 \n \nto a function is passed to a second function such that the original argument’s rval-1 \nueness or lvalueness is preserved. ( Perfect forwarding is discussed in more detail 2 \nin Item 32.) 3 \nWell-designed functions are exception-safe, meaning they offer at least the basic 4 \nexception safely guarantee (i.e., the basic guarantee). Such functions assure callers 5 \nthat even if an exception is emitted, program inv ariants remain intact (i.e., no data 6 \nstructures are corrupted) and no resources are leaked. Functions offering the 7 \nstrong exception safety guarantee ( i.e., the strong guarantee) assure callers that if 8 \nan exception is emitted, the state of the program remains as it was prior to the call. 9 \nAs Item 16 explains, C++98 Standard Library functions offering the strong guara n-10 \ntee constrain the applicability of move semantics in the C++11 Standard Library. 11 \nI use the term callable entity to refer to anything that can be called using the syntax 12 \nof a call to a non -member function, i.e., the syntax “functionName(arguments)”. 13 \nFunctions, function pointers, and function objects are all callable entities. Function 14 \nobjects created through lambda expressions  are known as closures. It’s seldom 15 \nnecessary to distinguish between lambda expressions and the closures they create, 16 \nso I often refer to both as lambdas.  17 \nIn the same vein, I rarely distinguish between function templates  (i.e., templates 18 \nthat generate functions) and template functions (i.e., the functions generated from 19 \nfunction templates). Ditto for class templates and template classes. 20 \nMany things  in C++  can be bot h declared and defined. Declarations introduce 21 \nnames without giving details, such as where storage is located or how the entity is 22 \nimplemented: 23 \nextern int x;                       // object declaration 24 \nclass Widget;                       // class declaration 25 \nint func(const Widget& w);          // function declaration 26 \nenum class Color;                   // scoped enum declaration 27 \n                                    // (see Item 10) 28 \nDefinitions provide the storage location or implementation details: 29 \nint x;                              // object definition 30\n\n#### 第 9 页原文\n\nPage 9 \n \nclass Widget {                      // class definition 1 \n  … 2 \n}; 3 \nint func(const Widget& w)  4 \n{ return w.size(); }                // function definition 5 \nenum class Color  6 \n{ Yellow, Red, Blue };              // scoped enum definition 7 \nA definition also qualifies as a declaration, so unless it’s really important that  8 \nsomething is a definition, I tend to refer to declarations. 9 \nNew C++ Standards generally preserve the validity of code written under older 10 \nones, but occasionally the Standardization Committee deprecates features. That’s a 11 \nwarning that the features may be r emoved from future Standards . Y ou should 12 \navoid deprecated features . (The reason for deprecation is usually that newer fe a-13 \ntures offer the same functionality, but with fewer restrictions or drawbacks. 14 \nstd::auto_ptr is deprecated in C++11, for example, because std::unique_ptr 15 \ndoes the same job, only better.) 16 \nSometimes the Standard says that the result of an operation is undefined behavior. 17 \nThat means that runtime  behavior is unpredictable, and it should go without sa y-18 \ning that you want to steer clear of such uncertainty. Examples of actions with un-19 \ndefined behavior include using square brackets (“[]”) to index beyond the bounds 20 \nof a std::vector, dereferencing an uninitialized iterator, or engaging in a data 21 \nrace (i.e., having two or more threads, at le ast one of which is a writer, simultan e-22 \nously access the same memory location). 23 \nIn source code comments, I sometimes abbreviate “constructor” as ctor and “de-24 \nstructor” as dtor. 25 \nReporting Bugs and Suggesting Improvements 26 \nI’ve done my best to fill this book with clear, accurate, useful information, but sure-27 \nly there are ways to make it better. If you find errors of any kind (technical, exposi-28 \ntory, grammatical, typographical, etc.), or if you have suggestions for how the book 29 \ncould otherwise be improved, please email me at emc++@aristeia.com. New 30\n\n#### 第 10 页原文\n\nPage 10 \n \nprintings give me the opportunity to revise Effective Modern C ++, and I can ’t ad-1 \ndress issues I don’t know about! 2 \nTo view the list of the issues I do know about, consult the book’s errata page, 3 \nhttp://www.aristeia.com/BookErrata/emc++-errata.html.  4\n\n#### 第 11 页原文\n\nPage 11 \n \n \n1 \nC++98 had a single set of rules for type deduction: the one  for function templates. 2 \nC++11 modifies those rules a bit  and adds two more, one for auto and one for 3 \ndecltype. C++14 then extends the usage contexts in which auto and decltype 4 \nmay be employed. The increasingly widespread application of type deduction frees 5 \nprogrammers from the tyranny of spelling out  types that are obvious or redu n-6 \ndant. It makes C++ software more adaptable, because changing a type at one point 7 \nin the source code automatically propagate s through type deduction to other loca-8 \ntions. However, it can render code more difficult to reason about, because the 9 \ntypes deduced by compilers may not be as apparent as we’d like. 10 \nEffective programming in modern C++ isn’t possible without a solid understanding 11 \nof how type deduction operates. There are just too many  contexts where it takes 12 \nplace: in calls to function templates, in most situations where  auto appears, in 13 \ndecltype expressions, and, as of C++14, where the enigmatic decltype(auto) 14 \nconstruct is employed.  15 \nThis chapter provides the foundational information about type deduction that eve-16 \nry C++ developer requires.  It explains how template type deduction  works, how 17 \nauto builds on that, and how decltype goes its own way . It even explains how 18 \nyou can force compilers to make the results of their type deduction s visible, thus 19 \nenabling you to ensure that compilers are deducing the types you want them to. 20 \n Item 1: Understand template type deduction. 21 \nIt’s said that imitation is the sincerest form of flattery, but blissful ignorance can be 22 \nan equally heartfelt accolade. When users of a complex system are ignorant of how 23 \nit works, yet happy with what it does, that says a lot about the design of that sy s-24 \ntem. By this measure, template type deduction in C++ is a tremendous success. Mil-25 \nlions of programmers have passed arguments to template functions with co m-26 \npletely satisfactory results, even though many of those programmers would be 27 \nhard-pressed to give more than the haziest description of how the types used by 28 \nthose functions were deduced. 29\n\n#### 第 12 页原文\n\nPage 12 \n \nIf that group includes you, I have good news and bad news. The good news is that 1 \nthe type deduction process for auto-declared variables is essentially the same as 2 \nfor templates (see Item 2), so when it comes to auto, you’re on familiar ground. 3 \nThe bad news is that when the template type deduction rules are employed by au-4 \nto, you’re more likely to be surprised by what happens. If you want to use auto 5 \n(and you certainly  should—see Item 5), you’ll need a reasonable understanding of 6 \nthe rules that drive template type deduction. They’re generally straightforward, so 7 \nthis poses little challenge. It’s just that they worked so naturally in C++98, you 8 \nprobably never had to think much about them. 9 \nIf you’re willing to overlook a pinch o f pseudocode , we can think of a function 10 \ntemplate as looking like this: 11 \ntemplate<typename T> 12 \nvoid f(ParamType param); 13 \nA call can look like this: 14 \nf(expr);                    // call f with some expression 15 \nDuring compilation, compilers use expr to deduce two types: one for T and one for 16 \nParamType. These types are frequently different, because ParamType often con-17 \ntains adornments, e.g., const- or reference qualifiers. For example, if the template 18 \nis declared like this, 19 \ntemplate<typename T> 20 \nvoid f(const T& param);     // ParamType is const T& 21 \nand we have this call, 22 \nint x = 0; 23 \nf(x);                       // call f with an int 24 \nT is deduced to be int, but ParamType is deduced to be const int&.  25 \nIt’s natural to expect that the type deduced for T is the same as the type of the a r-26 \ngument passed to the function, i.e., that T is the type of expr. In the above example, 27 \nthat’s the case: x is an int, and T is deduced to be int. But it doesn’t always work 28 \nthat way. The type deduced for T is dependent not just of the type of expr, but also 29 \non the form of ParamType. There are three cases: 30\n\n#### 第 13 页原文\n\nPage 13 \n \n ParamType is a pointer or reference type, but not a universal reference.  (Uni-1 \nversal references are described in Item 26. At this point, all you need to know 2 \nis that they exist.) 3 \n ParamType is a universal reference. 4 \n ParamType is neither a pointer nor a reference. 5 \nWe therefore have three type deduction scenarios to examine. Each will be based 6 \non our general form for templates and calls to it: 7 \ntemplate<typename T> 8 \nvoid f(ParamType param); 9 \nf(expr);                // deduce T and ParamType from expr 10 \nCase 1: ParamType is a Pointer or Reference, but not a Universal Reference 11 \nThe simplest situation is when ParamType is a pointer type or a reference type, 12 \nbut not a universal reference. In that case, type deduction works like this: 13 \n If expr’s type is a reference, ignore the reference part. 14 \n Pattern-match expr’s type against ParamType to determine T. 15 \nFor example, if this is our template, 16 \ntemplate<typename T> 17 \nvoid f(T& param);       // param is a reference 18 \nand we have these variable declarations, 19 \nint x = 27;             // x is an int 20 \nconst int cx = x;       // cx is a const int 21 \nconst int& rx = x;      // rx is a read-only view of x 22 \nthe deduced types for param and T in various calls are as follows: 23 \nf(x);                   // T is int, param's type is int& 24 \nf(cx);                  // T is const int, 25 \n                        // param's type is const int& 26 \nf(rx);                  // T is const int, 27 \n                        // param's type is const int&  28\n\n#### 第 14 页原文\n\nPage 14 \n \nIn the second and third calls, notice that because cx and rx designate const val-1 \nues, T is deduced to be const int, thus yielding a parameter type of const int&. 2 \nThat’s important to callers. When they pass a const object to a reference parame-3 \nter, they expect that object to remain unmodifiable, i.e., for the parameter to be a 4 \nreference-to-const. That’s why passing  a const object to a template taking a T& 5 \nparameter is safe: the constness of the object becomes part of the type deduced 6 \nfor T. 7 \nIn the third example, note that even though rx’s type is a reference, T is deduced to 8 \nbe a non -reference. That’s because rx’s reference-ness is ignored during type d e-9 \nduction. If this were not the case (i.e., if T were deduced to be const int&), 10 \nparam’s type would be const int& &, i.e., a reference to a reference. References to 11 \nreferences aren’t permitted in C++, and one way they’re avoided is by ignoring the 12 \nreference-ness of expressions during type deduction. 13 \nThese example all show lvalue reference parameters, but type deduction works 14 \nexactly the same way for rvalue reference parameters. Of course, only rvalu e ar-15 \nguments may be passed to rvalue reference  parameters, but that restriction has 16 \nnothing to do with type deduction. 17 \nIf we change the type of f’s parameter from T& to const T&, things change a little, 18 \nbut not in any really surprising ways. The constness of cx and rx continues to be 19 \nrespected, but because we’re now assuming that  param is a reference -to-const, 20 \nthere’s no longer a need for const to be deduced as part of T: 21 \ntemplate<typename T> 22 \nvoid f(const T& param);  // param is now a ref-to-const 23 \nint x = 27;              // as before 24 \nconst int cx = x;        // as before 25 \nconst int& rx = x;       // as before 26 \nf(x);                    // T is int, param's type is const int&  27 \nf(cx);                   // T is int, param's type is const int&  28 \nf(rx);                   // T is int, param's type is const int& 29 \nAs before, rx’s reference-ness is ignored during type deduction.  30\n\n#### 第 15 页原文\n\nPage 15 \n \nIf param were a pointer (or a pointer to const) instead of a reference, things 1 \nwould work essentially the same way: 2 \ntemplate<typename T> 3 \nvoid f(T* param);        // param is now a pointer 4 \nint x = 27;              // as before 5 \nconst int *px = &x;      // px is a ptr to a read-only view of x 6 \n 7 \nf(&x);                   // T is int, param's type is int*  8 \nf(px);                   // T is const int,  9 \n                         // param's type is const int*, 10 \nAt this point, you may find yourself yawning and nodding off, because C++’s type 11 \ndeduction rules work so naturally  for reference and pointer parameters , seeing 12 \nthem in written form is  really dull . Everything’s just obvious! Which is exactly 13 \nwhat you want in a type deduction system. 14 \nCase 2: ParamType is a Universal Reference 15 \nThings are less obvious for templates taking  universal reference parameters (i.e., 16 \n“T&&” parameters), because lvalue arguments get special treatment.  The complete 17 \nstory is told in Item 26, but here’s the headline version:  18 \n If expr is an lvalue , both T and ParamType are deduced to be lvalue refe r-19 \nences. 20 \n If expr is an rvalue, the usual type deduction rules apply.  21 \nFor example: 22 \ntemplate<typename T> 23 \nvoid f(T&& param);       // param is now a universal reference  24 \nint x = 27;              // as before 25 \nconst int cx = x;        // as before 26 \nconst int& rx = x;       // as before 27 \nf(x);                    // x is lvalue, so T is int&, 28 \n                         // param's type is also int& 29 \nf(cx);                   // cx is lvalue, so T is const int&, 30 \n                         // param's type is also const int& 31\n\n#### 第 16 页原文\n\nPage 16 \n \nf(rx);                   // rx is lvalue, so T is const int&, 1 \n                         // param's type is also const int& 2 \nf(27);                   // 27 is rvalue, so T is int, 3 \n                         // param's type is therefore int&& 4 \nItem 26 explains exactly why these examples play out the way they do, but t he key 5 \npoint is that the type deduction rules for parameters that are universal references 6 \nare different from those  for parameters that are lvalue references or rvalue refer-7 \nences. In particular, when universal references are in use, type deduction distin-8 \nguishes between lvalue arguments and rvalue arguments. That never happens for 9 \nnon-universal (i.e., “normal”) references.  10 \nCase 3: ParamType is Neither a Pointer nor a Reference 11 \nWhen ParamType is neither a pointer nor a reference, we’re dealing with pass-by-12 \nvalue: 13 \ntemplate<typename T> 14 \nvoid f(T param);         // param is now passed by value 15 \nThat means that param will be a copy of whatever is passed in —a completely new 16 \nobject. The fact that param will be a new object motivates the rules that govern 17 \nhow T is deduced from expr: 18 \n As before, if expr’s type is a reference, ignore the reference part. 19 \n If, after ignoring expr’s reference-ness, expr is const, ignore that , too. If it’s 20 \nvolatile, also ignore that. (volatile objects are uncommon. They’re gene r-21 \nally used only for implementing device drivers. For details, see Item 42.) 22 \nHence: 23 \nint x = 27;              // as before 24 \nconst int cx = x;        // as before 25 \nconst int& rx = x;       // as before 26 \nf(x);                    // T and param are both int 27 \nf(cx);                   // T and param are again both int 28 \nf(rx);                   // T and param are still both int 29\n\n#### 第 17 页原文\n\nPage 17 \n \nNote that even though cx and rx represent const values, param isn’t const. That 1 \nmakes sense. param is an object that’s completely independent of cx and rx—a 2 \ncopy of cx or rx. The fact that cx and rx can’t be modified says nothing about 3 \nwhether param can be. That’s why expr’s constness (if any) is ignored when d e-4 \nducing a type for param: just because expr can’t be modified doesn’t mean that a 5 \ncopy of it can’t be. 6 \nIt’s important to recognize that const is ignored only for by -value parameters. As 7 \nwe’ve seen, for parameters that are references -to- or pointers-to-const, the con-8 \nstness of expr is preserved during type deduction. But consider the case where 9 \nexpr is a const pointer to a const object, and expr is passed to a by-value param: 10 \ntemplate<typename T> 11 \nvoid f(T param);         // param is still passed by value 12 \nconst char* const ptr =  // ptr is const pointer to const object 13 \n  \"Fun with pointers\"; 14 \nf(ptr);                  // pass arg of type const char * const 15 \nHere, the const to the right of the asterisk declares ptr to be const: ptr can't be 16 \nmade to point to a different location, nor can it be set to null. (The const to the left 17 \nof the asterisk says that what ptr points to—the character string—is const, hence 18 \ncan't be modifie d.) When ptr is passed to f, the bits making up the pointer are 19 \ncopied into param. As such, the pointer itself (ptr) will be passed by value. In accord 20 \nwith the type deduction rule for by-value parameters, the constness of ptr will be 21 \nignored, and the type  deduced for param will be const char*, i.e., a modifiable 22 \npointer to a const character string. The constness of what ptr points to is pr e-23 \nserved during type deduction, but the constness of ptr itself is ignored when 24 \ncopying it to create the new pointer, param. 25 \nArray Arguments 26 \nThat pretty much covers it for mainstream template type deduction, but there’s a 27 \nsidestream case that is worth knowing about. It’s that array types are different 28 \nfrom pointer types, even though they sometimes seem to be interchangeable . A 29 \nprimary contributor to this illusion is that, in many contexts, an array decays into a 30 \npointer to its first element.  This decay is what permits code like this to compile: 31\n\n#### 第 18 页原文\n\nPage 18 \n \nconst char name[] = \"J. P. Briggs\";  // name's type is 1 \n                                     // const char[13] 2 \nconst char * ptrToName = name;       // array decays to pointer 3 \nHere, the const char* pointer ptrToName is being initialized with name, which is 4 \na const char[13], i .e., a 13 -element array of const char. These types ( const 5 \nchar* and const char[13]) are not the same, but because of the array-to-pointer 6 \ndecay rule, the code compiles.   7 \nBut what if  an array is passed to a template taking a by -value parameter? What 8 \nhappens then? 9 \ntemplate<typename T> 10 \nvoid f(T param);      // template with by-value parameter 11 \nf(name);              // what types are deduced for T and param? 12 \nWe begin with the observation that there is no such thing as a function parameter 13 \nthat's an array. Yes, yes, the syntax is legal, 14 \nvoid myFunc(int param[]); 15 \nbut the array declaration is treated as a pointer declaration, meaning that myFunc 16 \ncould be equivalently declared like this: 17 \nvoid myFunc(int* param);         // same function as above 18 \nThis equivalence of array and pointer parameters  is a bit of foliage springing from 19 \nthe C roots at the base of C++, and it f osters the illusion that array and pointer 20 \ntypes are the same. 21 \nBecause array parameter declarations are treated as if they were pointer param e-22 \nter declarations, the type of an array that’s passed to a template function by value 23 \nis deduced to be a pointer type . That means that in the call to the template f, its 24 \ntype parameter T is deduced to be const char*: 25 \nf(name);          // name is array, but T deduced as const char* 26 \nBut now comes a curve  ball. Although functions can’t declare parameters that are 27 \ntruly arrays, they can declare parameters that are  truly references to arrays! So if 28 \nwe modify the template f to take its argument by reference, 29\n\n#### 第 19 页原文\n\nPage 19 \n \ntemplate<typename T> 1 \nvoid f(T& param);      // template with by-reference parameter 2 \nand we pass an array to it, 3 \nf(name);               // pass array to f 4 \nthe type deduced for T is the actual type of the array! That type includes the size of 5 \nthe array, so in this example, T is deduced to be const char [13], and the type of 6 \nf's parameter (a reference to this array) is const char (&)[13]. Yes, the syntax 7 \nlooks toxic, but on the plus side, knowing it will score you mondo points with those 8 \nrare souls who care.  9 \nInterestingly, the ability to declare references to  arrays enables creation of a te m-10 \nplate to deduce the number of elements that an array contains:  11 \ntemplate<typename T, std::size_t N>        // return size of 12 \nconstexpr std::size_t arraySize(T (&)[N])  // an array as a  13 \n{                                          // compile-time 14 \n  return N;                                // constant 15 \n}        16 \nNote the use of constexpr (see Item 14) to make the result of th is function avail-17 \nable during compilation. That makes it possible to declare, say, an array with the 18 \nsame number of elements as a second array whose size is computed from list of 19 \ninitializers: 20 \nint keyVals[] = { 1, 3, 7, 9, 11, 22, 35 };      // keyVals has 21 \n                                                 // 7 elements 22 \nint mappedVals[arraySize(keyVals)];              // so does 23 \n                                                 // mappedVals 24 \nOf course, as a modern C++  developer, you’d naturally prefer a std::array to a 25 \nbuilt-in array: 26 \nstd::array<int, arraySize(keyVals)> mappedVals;  // mappedVals' 27 \n                                                 // size is 7 28 \nFunction Arguments 29 \nArrays aren’t the only entities in C++ that can decay into pointers.  Function types  30 \ncan decay into function pointers, too, and everything we’ve discussed regarding 31\n\n#### 第 20 页原文\n\nPage 20 \n \ntype deduction and arrays applies to type deduction for functions and their decay 1 \ninto function pointers. As a result: 2 \nvoid someFunc(int, double);   // someFunc is a function; 3 \n                              // type is void(int, double) 4 \ntemplate<typename T> 5 \nvoid f1(T param);             // in f1, param passed by value 6 \ntemplate<typename T> 7 \nvoid f2(T& param);            // in f2, param passed by ref 8 \nf1(someFunc);                 // param deduced as ptr-to-func; 9 \n                              // type is void (*)(int, double) 10 \nf2(someFunc);                 // param deduced as ref-to-func; 11 \n                              // type is void (&)(int, double) 12 \nThis rarely makes any differen ce in practice, but if you’re going to know about a r-13 \nray-to-pointer decay, you might as well know about function-to-pointer decay, too. 14 \nSo there you have it: the rules for template type deduction.  I remarked at the ou t-15 \nset that they’re pretty straightforward, and for the most part, they are. The special 16 \ntreatment accorded lvalues when deducing types for universal references muddies 17 \nthe water a bit, however, and the decay-to-pointer rules for arrays and functions 18 \nstirs up even greater turbidity. Sometimes you simply want to grab your compilers 19 \nand demand, “ Tell me what type you’re deducing!” When that happens, turn to  20 \nItem 4, because it’s devoted to coaxing compilers into doing just that.  21 \nThings to Remember 22 \n When deducing types for parameters that are pointers and non -universal ref-23 \nerences, whether the initializing expression is a reference is ignored. 24 \n When deducing types for parameters that are universal references, lvalue a r-25 \nguments yield lvalue references, and rvalue arguments yield rvalue references.  26 \n When deducing types for by-value parameters, whether the initializing expres-27 \nsion is a reference or is const is ignored. 28 \n During type deduction , arguments that are array or function names decay to 29 \npointers, unless they’re used to initialize references. 30\n\n#### 第 21 页原文\n\nPage 21 \n \n Item 2: Understand auto type deduction. 1 \nIf you’ve read Item 1 on template type deduction, you already know almost ever y-2 \nthing you need to know about auto type deduction, because, with only one curious 3 \nexception, auto type deduction is template type deduction. But how can that be? 4 \nTemplate type deduction involves templates and functions and parameters, but 5 \nauto deals with none of those things.  6 \nThat’s true, but it doesn’t matter. There’s a direct mapping between template type 7 \ndeduction and auto type deduction. There is literally an algorithmic transfo r-8 \nmation from one to the other. 9 \nIn Item 1, template type deduction is explained using this general function te m-10 \nplate 11 \ntemplate<typename T> 12 \nvoid f(ParamType param);  13 \nand this general call: 14 \nf(expr);                    // call f with some expression 15 \nIn the call to f, compilers use expr to deduce types for T and ParamType.  16 \nWhen a variable is declared using auto, auto plays the role of T in the template , 17 \nand the type specifier for the variable acts as ParamType. This is easier to show 18 \nthan to describe, so consider this example: 19 \nauto x = 27; 20 \nHere, the type specifier for x is simply auto by itself. On the other hand, in this 21 \ndeclaration, 22 \nconst auto cx = x; 23 \nthe type specifier is const auto. And here, 24 \nconst auto& rx = x; 25 \nthe type specifier  is const auto&. To deduce types for x, cx, and rx in these e x-26 \namples, compilers act as if there were a template for each declaration a s well as a 27 \ncall to that template with the corresponding initializing expression: 28\n\n#### 第 22 页原文\n\nPage 22 \n \ntemplate<typename T>               // conceptual template for  1 \nvoid func_for_x(T param);          // deducing x's type 2 \nfunc_for_x(27);                    // conceptual call: param's  3 \n                                   // deduced type is x's type 4 \ntemplate<typename T>               // conceptual template for  5 \nvoid func_for_cx(const T param);   // deducing cx's type 6 \nfunc_for_cx(x);                    // conceptual call: param's 7 \n                                   // deduced type is cx's type 8 \ntemplate<typename T>               // conceptual template for  9 \nvoid func_for_rx(const T& param);  // deducing rx's type 10 \nfunc_for_rx(x);                    // conceptual call: param's  11 \n                                   // deduced type is rx's type 12 \nAs I said, deducing types for auto is the same as deducing types for templates. 13 \nItem 1 divides template type deduction into three cases, based on the characteri s-14 \ntics of ParamType, the type specifier for param in the general function template. In 15 \na variable declaration using auto, the type specifier takes the place of ParamType, 16 \nso there are three cases for that, too: 17 \n Case 1: The type specifier is a pointer or reference, but not a universal refe r-18 \nence. 19 \n Case 2: The type specifier is a universal reference. 20 \n Case 3: The type specifier is neither a pointer nor a reference. 21 \nWe’ve already seen examples of cases 1 and 3: 22 \nauto x = 27;          // case 3 (x is neither ptr nor reference) 23 \nconst auto cx = x;    // case 3 (cx isn't either) 24 \nconst auto& rx = x;   // case 1 (rx is a non-universal ref.) 25 \nCase 2 works as you’d expect:  26 \nauto&& uref1 = x;     // x is int and lvalue, 27 \n                      // so uref1's type is int& 28 \nauto&& uref2 = cx;    // cx is const int and lvalue, 29 \n                      // so uref2's type is const int& 30\n\n#### 第 23 页原文\n\nPage 23 \n \nauto&& uref3 = 27;    // 27 is int and rvalue, 1 \n                      // so uref3's type is int&& 2 \nItem 1 concludes with a discussion of how array and function names decay into 3 \npointers for non -reference type specifiers. That happens in auto type deduction, 4 \ntoo, of course: 5 \nconst char name[] =            // name's type is const char[13] 6 \n  \"R. N. Briggs\"; 7 \nauto arr1 = name;              // arr1's type is const char* 8 \nauto& arr2 = name;             // arr2's type is 9 \n                               // const char (&)[13] 10 \n 11 \nvoid someFunc(int, double);    // someFunc is a function; 12 \n                               // type is void(int, double) 13 \nauto func1 = someFunc;         // func1's type is  14 \n                               // void (*)(int, double) 15 \nauto& func2 = someFunc;        // func2's type is  16 \n                               // void (&)(int, double) 17 \nAs you can see, auto type deduction really is template type deduction. They’re just 18 \ntwo sides of the same coin. 19 \nExcept for the one way they differ. We’ll start with the observation that if you want 20 \nto declare an int with an initial value of 27, C++98 gives you two syntactic choic-21 \nes: 22 \nint x1 = 27; 23 \nint x2(27); 24 \nC++11, through its support for uniform initialization, adds these: 25 \nint x3 = {27}; 26 \nint x4{27}; 27 \nAll in all, four syntaxes, but only one result: a variable with value 27. 28 \nBut as Item 5 explains, there are advantages to declaring variables using auto in-29 \nstead of fixed types, so it’d be nice to replace int with auto in the above variable 30 \ndeclarations. Straightforward textual substitution yields this code: 31\n\n#### 第 24 页原文\n\nPage 24 \n \nauto x1 = 27;  1 \nauto x2(27);  2 \nauto x3 = {27}; 3 \nauto x4{27}; 4 \nThese declarations all compile, b ut they don’t have the same meaning  as the ones 5 \nthey replace . The first two statements do, indeed, declare a variable of type int 6 \nwith value 27. The second two, however, declare a variable of type 7 \nstd::initializer_list<int> containing a single element with value 27! 8 \nauto x1 = 27;            // type is int, value is 27  9 \nauto x2(27);             // ditto 10 \nauto x3 = {27};          // type is std::initializer_list<int>, 11 \n                         // value is {27} 12 \nauto x4{27};             // ditto 13 \nThis is due to a special type deduction rule for auto. When the initializer for an 14 \nauto-declared variable is enclosed in braces, the deduced type is a 15 \nstd::initializer_list. If such a type can’t be deduced (e.g., because the va l-16 \nues in the braced initializer are of different types), the code will be rejected: 17 \nauto x5 = {1, 2, 3.0};   // error! can't deduce T for 18 \n                         // std::initializer_list<T> 19 \nAs the comment indicates, type deduction will fail in this case, but it’s important to 20 \nrecognize that there are actually two kinds of type deduction taking place . One 21 \nkind stems from the use of auto: x5’s type has to be deduced. Because x5’s initial-22 \nizer is in braces, x5 must be deduced to be a std::initializer_list. But 23 \nstd::initializer_list is a template. Instantiations are 24 \nstd::initizalizer_list<T> for some type T, and that means that T’s type 25 \nmust be deduced. Such deduction falls under the  purview of the second kind of 26 \ntype deduction occurring here: template type deduction. In this example, that d e-27 \nduction fails, because the values in the braced initializer don’t have a single type. 28 \nThe treatment of braced initializers is the only way in whi ch auto type deduction 29 \nand template type deduction differ.  When an auto variable is initialized with a 30 \nbraced initializer, the deduced type is some instantiation of 31 \nstd::initializer_list. If a template is faced with deducing the type for a 32\n\n#### 第 25 页原文\n\nPage 25 \n \nbraced initializer, however, the code is rejected . (This has consequences for pe r-1 \nfect forwarding, as Item 32 explains.) 2 \nYou might wonder why auto type deduction has a special rule for braced initiali z-3 \ners, but template type deduction does not. I wonder this, myself. Unfortunately, I 4 \nhave not been able to find a compelling explanation. But the rule is the rule, and 5 \nthis means that you must bear in mind that if you de clare a variable using auto 6 \nand you initialize it with a braced initializer, the deduced type will always be 7 \nstd::initializer_list. It’s especially important to bear this in mind if you 8 \nembrace the philosophy of uniform initialization —of enclosing initiali zing values 9 \nin braces as a matter of course. One of the most classic mistakes in C++11 pr o-10 \ngramming is accidently declaring a std::initializer_list variable when you 11 \nmean to declare something else. To reiterate: 12 \nauto x1 = 27;            // x1 and x2 are ints  13 \nauto x2(27); 14 \nauto x3 = {27};          // x3 and x4 are 15 \nauto x4{27};             // std::initializer_list<int>s 16 \nThis pitfall is one of the reasons some developers put braces around their initiali z-17 \ners only when they have to. (When you have to is discussed in Item 7. ) 18 \nFor C++11, this is the full story , but for C++14, the tale continues. C++14 permits 19 \nauto to indicate that a function’s return type should be deduced (see Item 3), and 20 \nC++14 lambda expressions may use auto in parameter declarations. However, 21 \nthese uses of auto employ template type deduction, not auto type deduction. That 22 \nmeans that braced initializers  in these contexts  cause type deduction to fail. So a 23 \nfunction with an auto return type that returns a braced initializer won’t compile: 24 \nauto createInitList() 25 \n{ 26 \n  return { 1, 2, 3 };         // error: can't deduce type 27 \n}                             // for { 1, 2, 3 }  28 \nThe same is true when auto is used in a parameter type specification in a C++14 29 \nlambda (thus yielding a generic lambda): 30 \nstd::vector v; 31 \n… 32", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.26–p.50", "pages": "p.26–p.50", "raw_text": "#### 第 26 页原文\n\nPage 26 \n \nauto resetV = 1 \n  [&v](const auto& newValue) { v = newValue; };  // C++14 only 2 \n… 3 \nresetV( { 1, 2, 3 } );        // error! can't deduce type 4 \n                              // for { 1, 2, 3 } 5 \nThe net result is that auto type deduction is identical to template type deduction 6 \nunless (1) a variable is being declared and (2) its initializer is inside braces. In that 7 \ncase only, auto deduces a std::initializer_list, but template type deduction 8 \nfails. 9 \nThings to Remember 10 \n auto type deduction is normally identical to template type deduction. 11 \n The sole exception is that i n variable declarations using auto and braced ini-12 \ntializers, auto deduces std::initializer_lists. 13 \n Template type deduction fails for braced initializers. 14 \n Item 3: Understand decltype. 15 \ndecltype is a funny beast. Given a name or an expression, decltype tells you the 16 \nname’s or the expression’s type. Typically, what it tells you is exactly what you’d 17 \npredict. Occasionally however, it provides results that leave you scratching your 18 \nhead and turning to reference works or online Q&A sites for revelation. 19 \nWe’ll begin with the typical cases —the ones harboring no surprises. In contrast to 20 \nwhat happens during type deduction for templates and auto (see Items  1 and 2), 21 \ndecltype almost always parrots back the type of the name or expression you give 22 \nit without any modification: 23 \nconst int i = 0;           // decltype(i) is const int 24 \nbool f(const Widget& w);   // decltype(w) is const Widget& 25 \n                           // decltype(f) is bool(const Widget&) 26 \nstruct Point { 27 \n  int x, y;                // decltype(Point::x) is int 28 \n};                         // decltype(Point::y) is int 29 \nWidget w;                  // decltype(w) is Widget 30\n\n#### 第 27 页原文\n\nPage 27 \n \nif (f(w)) …                // decltype(f(w)) is bool 1 \ntemplate<typename T>       // simplified version of std::vector 2 \nclass vector {  3 \npublic: 4 \n  … 5 \n  T& operator[](std::size_t index); 6 \n  … 7 \n}; 8 \nvector<int> v;             // decltype(v) is vector<int> 9 \n… 10 \nif (v[0] == 0) …           // decltype(v[i]) is int& 11 \nSee? No surprises.  12 \nIn C++11, the primary use for decltype is declaring function templates where the 13 \nfunction’s return type depends on its parameter types. For example, suppose we’d 14 \nlike to write a function that takes a container that supports indexing via square 15 \nbrackets (i.e., the use of “[]”) plus an index, then authenticates the user before r e-16 \nturning the result of the indexing operation. The return type of the function should 17 \nbe the same as the type returned by the indexing operation. 18 \noperator[] on a container  of objects of type T typically returns a T&. This is the 19 \ncase for std::deque, for example, and it’s almost always the case for 20 \nstd::vector. For std::vector<bool>, however, operator[] does not return a 21 \nbool&. Instead, it returns a brand new object. The whys and hows of this situation 22 \nare explored in Item 6, but what’s important here is that the type returned by  a 23 \ncontainer’s operator[] depends on the container. 24 \ndecltype makes it easy to express that. Here’s a first cut at the template we’d like 25 \nto write, showing the use of decltype to compute the return type. The template 26 \nneeds a bit of refinement, but we’ll defer that for now: 27 \ntemplate<typename Container, typename Index>    // works, but 28 \nauto authAndAccess(Container& c, Index i)       // requires 29 \n  -> decltype(c[i])                             // refinement 30 \n{ 31 \n  authenticateUser(); 32 \n  return c[i]; 33 \n} 34\n\n#### 第 28 页原文\n\nPage 28 \n \nThe use of auto before the function name has nothing to do with type deduction. 1 \nRather, it in dicates that C++11’s trailing return type  syntax is being used, i.e., that 2 \nthe function’s retur n type will be declared following the parameter list ( after the 3 \n“->”). A trailing return type has the advantage that the function’s parameters can 4 \nbe used in the specification of its return type. In authAndAccess, for example, we 5 \nspecify the return type us ing c and i. If we were to have the return type precede 6 \nthe function name in the conventional fashion, c and i would be unavailable, b e-7 \ncause they would not have been declared yet. 8 \nWith this declaration, authAndAccess returns whatever type operator[] re-9 \nturns when applied to the passed-in container, exactly as we desire. 10 \nC++11 permits return types for single -statement lambdas to be deduced, and 11 \nC++14 extends this to both all lambdas and all functions (including those with mul-12 \ntiple statements). In the case of authAndAccess, that means that in C++14 we can 13 \nomit the trailing return type, leaving just the leading auto. With that form of de c-14 \nlaration, auto does mean that type deduction will take place. In particular, it 15 \nmeans that compilers will deduce the function’s return type from the function ’s 16 \nimplementation: 17 \ntemplate<typename Container, typename Index>  // C++14 only, and 18 \nauto authAndAccess(Container& c, Index i)     // not quite  19 \n{                                             // correct 20 \n  authenticateUser(); 21 \n  return c[i];                  // return type deduced from c[i] 22 \n} 23 \nBut which of C++’s type deduction rules will be used to infer authAndAccess’s 24 \nreturn type: those for templates, those for auto, or those for decltype?  25 \nPerhaps surprisingly, functions with an auto return type perform type deduction 26 \nusing the template type deduction rules. It might seem that use of the auto type 27 \ndeduction rules would better correspond to the declar ation syntax, but remember 28 \nthat template type deduction and auto type deduction are nearly identical. The 29 \nonly difference is that template type deduction deduces no type for a braced in i-30 \ntializer. 31\n\n#### 第 29 页原文\n\nPage 29 \n \nIn this case, deducing authAndAccess’s return type using template type dedu c-1 \ntion is problematic, but auto’s type deduction rules would fare no better. The di f-2 \nficulty stems from something these forms of type deduction have in common: their 3 \ntreatment of expressions that are references. 4 \nAs we’ve discussed, operator[] for most containers-of-T returns a T&, but Item 1 5 \nexplains that during template type deduction, the reference -ness of an initia lizing 6 \nexpression is ignored.  Consider what that would mean for this client code  using 7 \nthe declaration for authAndAccess with an auto return type (i.e., using template 8 \ntype deduction for its return type): 9 \nstd::deque<int> d; 10 \n… 11 \nauthAndAccess(d, 5) = 10;  // authenticate user, return d[5], 12 \n                           // then assign 10 to it; 13 \n                           // this won't compile! 14 \nHere, d[5] returns an int&, but auto return type deduction for authAndAccess 15 \nwill strip off the reference, thus yielding a return type of int. That int, being the 16 \nreturn value of a function, is an rvalue, and the code above thus attempts to assign 17 \n10 to an rvalue int. That's forbidden in C++, so the code won't compile. 18 \nThe problem is that we're using template type deduction, which discards reference 19 \nqualifiers from its initializing expression . What we want in this case is decltype 20 \ntype deduction. Such type deduction would permit us to say that authAndAccess 21 \nshould return exactly the same type that the expression c[i] returns.  22 \nThe guardians of C++, anticipating the need to use decltype type deduction rules 23 \nin some cases where types are inferred, make this possible in C++14 through the 24 \ndecltype(auto) specifier. What may initially seem contradictory (decltype and 25 \nauto?) actually makes perfect sense: auto specifies that the type is to be deduced, 26 \nand decltype says that decltype rules should be used during the deduction. We 27 \ncan thus write authAndAccess like this: 28 \ntemplate<typename Container, typename Index>   // C++14 only; 29 \ndecltype(auto)                                 // works, but  30 \nauthAndAccess(Container& c, Index i)           // still requires 31 \n{                                              // refinement 32 \n  authenticateUser(); 33\n\n#### 第 30 页原文\n\nPage 30 \n \n  return c[i]; 1 \n} 2 \nNow authAndAccess will truly return whatever c[i] returns. In particular, for 3 \nthe common case where c[i] returns a T&, authAndAccess will also return a T&, 4 \nand in the uncommon case where c[i] returns a n object, authAndAccess will 5 \nreturn an object, too. 6 \nThe use of decltype(auto) is not limited to function return types. It can also be 7 \nconvenient for declaring variables when you want to apply the decltype type de-8 \nduction rules to the initializing expression: 9 \nWidget w; 10 \nconst Widget& cw = w; 11 \nauto myWidget1 = cw;             // auto type deduction: 12 \n                                 // myWidget1's type is Widget 13 \ndecltype(auto) myWidget2 = cw;   // decltype type deduction: 14 \n                                 // myWidget2's type is 15 \n                                 //   const Widget& 16 \nBut two things are bothering you, I know. One is the refinement to authAndAc-17 \ncess I mentioned, but have not yet described. Let’s address that now. 18 \nLook again at the declaration for the C++14 version of authAndAccess: 19 \ntemplate<typename Container, typename Index> 20 \ndecltype(auto) authAndAccess(Container& c, Index i); 21 \nThe container is passed by lvalue -reference-to-non-const, because returning a 22 \nreference to an element of the container permits clients to modify that container. 23 \nBut this means it’s not possible to pass rvalue containers to this function. Rvalues 24 \ncan't bind to lvalue references (unless they’re lvalue-references-to-const, which is 25 \nnot the case here).  26 \nAdmittedly, passing an rvalue container to authAndAccess is an edge case.  An 27 \nrvalue container, being a temporary object, would typically be destroyed at the 28 \nend of the statement containing the call to authAndAccess, and that means that a 29 \nreference to an element in that container (which is typically what authAndAccess 30 \nwould return) would dangle at the end of the statement that created it. Still, it 31\n\n#### 第 31 页原文\n\nPage 31 \n \ncould make sense to pass a temporary object to authAndAccess. A client might 1 \nsimply want to make a copy of an element in the temporary container, for exa m-2 \nple: 3 \nstd::deque<std::string> makeStringDeque();   // factory function 4 \n// make copy of 5th element of deque returned 5 \n// from makeStringDeque 6 \nauto s = authAndAccess(makeStringDeque(), 5); 7 \nSupporting such use means we need to revise the declaration for c to accept both 8 \nlvalues and rvalues, and that means that c needs to be a universal reference  (see 9 \nItem 26): 10 \ntemplate<typename Container, typename Index> 11 \ndecltype(auto) authAndAccess(Container&& c, Index i); 12 \nIn this template, we don’t know what type of container we’re operating on, and 13 \nthat means we’re equally ignorant of the type of index objects it uses. Employing 14 \npass-by-value for objects of an unknown type generally risks the performance hit 15 \nof unnecessary copying, the behavioral problems of object slicing  (see Item 17), 16 \nand the sting of our coworkers’ derision, but in the case of container indices, fo l-17 \nlowing the example of the Standard Library (e.g., std::string, std::vector, 18 \nand std::deque) seems reasonable, so we’ll stick with pass-by-value for them. 19 \nAll that remains is to update the template’s implementation to bring it into accord 20 \nwith Item 27’s admonition to apply std::forward to universal references: 21 \ntemplate<typename Container, typename Index>       // final 22 \ndecltype(auto)                                     // C++14 23 \nauthAndAccess(Container&& c, Index i)              // version 24 \n{ 25 \n  authenticateUser(); 26 \n  return std::forward<Container>(c)[i]; 27 \n} 28 \nThis should do everything we want, but it requires a C++14 compiler. If you don’t 29 \nhave one, you’ll need to use the C++11 version of the template. It’s the same as its 30 \nC++14 counterpart, except that you have to specify the return type yourself: 31 \ntemplate<typename Container, typename Index>       // final 32 \nauto                                               // C++11 33 \nauthAndAccess(Container&& c, Index i)              // version 34\n\n#### 第 32 页原文\n\nPage 32 \n \n-> decltype(std::forward<Container>(c)[i]) 1 \n{ 2 \n  authenticateUser(); 3 \n  return std::forward<Container>(c)[i]; 4 \n} 5 \nThe other issue that’s likely to be  nagging at you is my remark at the beginning of 6 \nthis Item that decltype almost always produces the type you expect, that it rarely 7 \nsurprises. Truth be told, you’re unlikely to encounter these exceptions to the rule 8 \nunless you’re a heavy-duty library implementer. 9 \nTo fully understand decltype’s behavior, you’ll have to familiarize yourself with a 10 \nfew special cases. Most of these are to o obscure to warrant discussion in a book 11 \nlike this, but looking at one lends insight into decltype as well as its use. 12 \nApplying decltype to a name yields the declared type for that name, just as I said. 13 \nNames are  lvalue expressions , but that doesn't affec t decltype's behavior. For 14 \nlvalue expressions more complicated than  names, however, decltype ensures 15 \nthat the type reported is always an lvalue reference. That is, if an lvalue expression 16 \nother than a name has type T, decltype reports that type as T&. This rarely has 17 \nany impact , because the type of most lvalue expressions inherently includes an 18 \nlvalue reference qualifier. Functions returning lvalues, for example, always return 19 \nlvalue references.  20 \nThere is an implication of this behavior that is worth being aware of, however. In 21 \nint x = 0; 22 \nx is the name of a variable, so decltype(x) is int. But wrapping the name x in pa-23 \nrentheses—“(x)”—yields an expression more complicated than a name.  Being a 24 \nname, x is an lvalue, and C++ defines (x) to be an lvalue, too . decltype((x)) is 25 \ntherefore int&. Putting parentheses around a name can change the type that 26 \ndecltype reports for it! 27 \nIn C++11, this is little more than a curiosity , but in conjunction with C++14’s su p-28 \nport for decltype(auto), it means that a seemingly trivial change in the way you 29 \nwrite a return statement can affect the deduced type for a function: 30\n\n#### 第 33 页原文\n\nPage 33 \n \ndecltype(auto) f1() 1 \n{ 2 \n  int x = 0; 3 \n  … 4 \n  return x;        // decltype(x) is int, so f1 returns int 5 \n} 6 \ndecltype(auto) f2() 7 \n{ 8 \n  int x = 0; 9 \n  … 10 \n  return (x);      // decltype((x)) is int&, so f2 returns int& 11 \n} 12 \nNote that not only does f2 have a different return type from f1, but it’s also r e-13 \nturning a reference to a local variable! That’s the kind of code that puts you on the 14 \nexpress train to undefined behavior —a train you most assuredly don’t want to be 15 \non. 16 \nThe primary lesson here is to pay very close attention when using 17 \ndecltype(auto). Seemingly insignificant details in the expression whose type is 18 \nbeing deduced can affect the type that decltype(auto) reports. To ensure that 19 \nthe type being deduced is the type you expect, use the techniques described in 20 \nItem 4.  21 \nAt the same time, don’t lose sight of the bigger picture. Sure, decltype (both alone 22 \nand in conjunction with auto) may occasionally yield type -deduction surprises, 23 \nbut that’s not the normal situation. Normally, decltype produces the type you 24 \nexpect. This is especially true when decltype is applied to names, because in that 25 \ncase, decltype does just what it sounds like: it reports that name’s declared type. 26 \nThings to Remember 27 \n decltype almost always yields the type of a variable or expression without 28 \nany modifications. 29 \n For lvalue expressions of type T other than names, decltype always reports a 30 \ntype of T&. 31 \n C++14 supports decltype(auto), which, like auto, deduces a type from its 32 \ninitializer, but it performs the type deduction using the decltype rules. 33\n\n#### 第 34 页原文\n\nPage 34 \n \n Item 4: Know how to view deduced types. 1 \nPeople who want to see the type s that compilers deduce usually fall into one of 2 \ntwo camps. The first are the pragmatists. They’re typically motivated by a beha v-3 \nioral problem in their software (i.e., they’re debugging), and they’re looking for 4 \ninsights into compilation that can help them  identify the source of the problem. 5 \nThe second are the experimentalists. They’re exploring the type deduction rules 6 \ndescribed in Items 1-3. Often, they want to confirm their predictions about the r e-7 \nsults of various type deduction scenarios (“For this code, I think compilers will d e-8 \nduce this type...”), but sometimes they simply want to answer “what if” questions. 9 \n“How,” they might wonder, “do the results of template type deduction change if I 10 \nreplace a universal reference (see Item 26) with an lvalue -reference-to-const pa-11 \nrameter (i.e., replace T&& with const T& in a function template parameter list)?” 12 \nRegardless of the camp you fall into (both  are legitimate ), the tools you have at 13 \nyour disposal depend on the phase of the software development process where 14 \nyou’d like to see the types your compilers have inferred . We’ll explore three possi-15 \nbilities: getting type deduction information as you edit your code, getting it during 16 \ncompilation, and getting it at runtime. 17 \nIDE Editors 18 \nCode editors in IDEs often show the types of program entities (e.g., variables, p a-19 \nrameters, functions, etc.) when you do something like hover your cursor over t he 20 \nentity. For example, given this code, 21 \nconst int theAnswer = 42; 22 \nauto x = theAnswer; 23 \nauto y = &theAnswer; 24 \nan IDE editor would likely show that x’s deduced type was int and y’s was const 25 \nint*.   26 \nFor this to work, your code must be in a more or less compilable state, because 27 \nwhat makes it possible for the IDE to offer this kind of  information is a C++ com-28 \npiler running inside the IDE. If that compiler can’t make enough sense of your code 29 \nto parse it and perform type deduction, it can’t show you what types it deduced. 30\n\n#### 第 35 页原文\n\nPage 35 \n \nCompiler Diagnostics 1 \nAn effective way to get a compiler to show a type it has deduced is to use that type 2 \nin a way that leads to compilation  problems. The error message reporting the 3 \nproblem is virtually sure to mention the type that’s causing it.  4 \nSuppose, for example, we’d like to see the types that were deduced for x and y in 5 \nthe previous example. We first declare a class template that we don’t define. Some-6 \nthing like this does nicely: 7 \ntemplate<typename T>       // declaration only for TD; 8 \nclass TD;                  // TD == \"Type Displayer\" 9 \nAttempts to instantiate this template will elicit an error message , because the re’s 10 \nno template definition to instantiate. To see the types for x and y, just try to instan-11 \ntiate TD with their types: 12 \nTD<decltype(x)> xType;     // elicit errors containing  13 \nTD<decltype(y)> yType;     // x's and y's types; 14 \n                           // see Item 3 for decltype info 15 \nI use variable names of the form variableNameType, because they tend to yield 16 \nquite informative error messages. For the code above, one of my compilers issues 17 \ndiagnositics reading, in part, as follows. (I’ve highlighted the type information 18 \nwe’re looking for.) 19 \nerror: aggregate 'TD<int> xType' has incomplete type and  20 \n    cannot be defined 21 \nerror: aggregate 'TD<const int *> yType' has incomplete type 22 \n    and cannot be defined 23 \nA different compiler provides the same information, but in a different form: 24 \nerror: 'xType' uses undefined class 'TD<int>' 25 \nerror: 'yType' uses undefined class 'TD<const int *>' 26 \nFormatting differences aside, all the compilers I’ve tested produce error messag es 27 \nwith useful type information when this technique is employed. 28 \nRuntime Output 29 \nThe printf approach to displaying type information  (not that I'm recommending 30 \nyou use printf) can’t be employed until runtime, but it offers full control over the 31\n\n#### 第 36 页原文\n\nPage 36 \n \nformatting of the output. The challenge is to create a textual representation of the 1 \ntype you care about  that is suitable for display. “No sweat,” you’re thinking, “it’s 2 \ntypeid and std::type_info::name to the rescue.” In our c ontinuing quest to 3 \nsee the types deduced for x and y, you figure, we can write this: 4 \nstd::cout << typeid(x).name() << '\\n';    // display types for 5 \nstd::cout << typeid(y).name() << '\\n';    // x and y 6 \nThis approach relies on the fact that invoking typeid on an object such as x or y 7 \nyields a std::type_info object, and std::type_info has a member function, 8 \nname, that produces a C -style string (i.e., a const char*) representation of the 9 \nname of the type. 10 \nCalls to std::type_info::name are not guaranteed to return anything sensible, 11 \nbut implementations try to be helpful. The level of helpfulness varies. The GNU and 12 \nClang compilers report that the type of x is “i”, and the type of y is “PKi”, for e x-13 \nample. These results make more sense once you learn that , in output from these 14 \ncompilers, “i” means “int” and “PK” means “pointer to konst const.” (Both com-15 \npilers support a tool, c++filt, that decodes such “mangled” types.) Microsoft’s com-16 \npiler produces less cryptic output: “int” for x and “int const *” for y. 17 \nBecause these results are correct for the types of x and y, you might be tempted to 18 \nview the type-reporting problem as solved, but let’s not be hasty. Consider a more 19 \ncomplex example: 20 \ntemplate<typename T>                 // template function to  21 \nvoid f(const T& param);              // be called 22 \nstd::vector<Widget> createVec();     // factory function 23 \nconst auto vw = createVec();         // init vw w/factory return 24 \nif (!vw.empty()) { 25 \n  f(&vw[0]);                         // call f 26 \n  … 27 \n} 28 \nThis code, which involves a user -defined type ( Widget), an STL container 29 \n(std::vector), and  an auto variable (vw),  is more representative of the situ a-30 \ntions where you might want some visibility into the types your compilers are de-31\n\n#### 第 37 页原文\n\nPage 37 \n \nducing. For example, it’d be nice to know what types are inferred for the template 1 \ntype parameter T and the function parameter param in f. 2 \nLoosing typeid on the problem is straightforward. Just add some code to f to dis-3 \nplay the types you’d like to see: 4 \ntemplate<typename T> 5 \nvoid f(const T& param) 6 \n{ 7 \n  using std::cout; 8 \n  cout << \"T =     \" << typeid(T).name() << '\\n';     // show T 9 \n  cout << \"param = \" << typeid(param).name() << '\\n'; // show 10 \n  …                                                   // param's 11 \n}                                                     // type 12 \nExecutables produced by the GNU and Clang compilers produce this output: 13 \nT =     PK6Widget 14 \nparam = PK6Widget 15 \nWe already know that for these compilers, PK means “pointer to const,” so the 16 \nonly mystery is the number 6. That’s simply the number of characters in the class 17 \nname that follows  (Widget). So these compilers tell us that both T and param are 18 \nof type const Widget*.  19 \nMicrosoft’s compiler concurs: 20 \nT =     class Widget const * 21 \nparam = class Widget const * 22 \nThree independent compilers producing the same information suggests that the 23 \ninformation is accurate. But look more closely. In the template f, param’s declared 24 \ntype is const T&. That being the case, doesn't it seem odd that T and param have 25 \nthe same type? If T were int, for example, param’s type should be  const int&—26 \nnot the same type at all. 27 \nSadly, the results of std::type_info::name are not reliable. In this case, for e x-28 \nample, the type  that all three compilers report for param are incorrect. Further-29 \nmore, they’re essentially required to be incorrect, because the specification for 30 \nstd::type_info::name mandates that the type being processed be treated as if 31\n\n#### 第 38 页原文\n\nPage 38 \n \nit had been passed to a template function as a by -value parameter. As Item 1 ex-1 \nplains, that means that if the type is a reference, its reference -ness is ignored, and 2 \nif the type after reference removal is const, its constness is also ignored. That’s 3 \nwhy param’s type —which is  const Widget * const &—is reported as const 4 \nWidget*. First the type’s reference -ness is removed, and then the constness of 5 \nthe result type is eliminated. 6 \nEqually sadly, the type information displayed by IDE  editors is also not reliable —7 \nor at least not reliably useful. For this same example, one IDE editor I know reports 8 \nT’s type as (I am not making this up): 9 \nconst 10 \nstd::_Simple_types<std::_Wrap_alloc<std::_Vec_base_types<Widget, 11 \nstd::allocator<Widget> >::_Alloc>::value_type>::value_type * 12 \nThe same IDE editor shows param’s type as: 13 \nconst std::_Simple_types<...>::value_type *const & 14 \nThat’s less intimidating than the type for T, but the “...” in the middle is disturb-15 \ning until you realize that it’s the IDE  editor’s way of saying “I’m omitting all that 16 \nstuff that’s part of T’s type.”  17 \nMy understanding is that most of what’s displayed here is typedef cruft and that 18 \nonce you push through the typedefs to get to the underlying type information, 19 \nyou get what you’re looking for, but having to do that work pretty much eliminates 20 \nany utility th e display of the types in the IDE originally promised.  With any luck, 21 \nyour IDE editor does a better job on code like this. 22 \nIn my experience, compiler diagnostics are a more dependable source of info r-23 \nmation about the results of type deduction . Revising the template f’s implementa-24 \ntion to instantiate the declared-but-not-defined template TD yields this: 25 \ntemplate<typename T> 26 \nvoid f(const T& param) 27 \n{ 28 \n  TD<T> TType;                      // elicit errors containing 29 \n  TD<decltype(param)> paramType;    // T's and param's types 30 \n  … 31 \n}  32\n\n#### 第 39 页原文\n\nPage 39 \n \nEach of GNU’s, Clang’s, and Microsoft’s compilers produce error messages with the 1 \ncorrect types for T and param. The exact message contents and formats v ary, but 2 \nas an example, this is what GNU’s compiler issues (after minor reformatting): 3 \nerror: 'TD<const Widget *> TType' has incomplete type 4 \nerror: 'TD<const Widget * const &> paramType' has incomplete 5 \n       type 6 \nBeyond typeid  7 \nIf you want accurate runtime information about deduced types, we’ve seen that 8 \ntypeid is not a reliable route to getting it. One way to work around that is to im-9 \nplement your own mechanism for mapping from a type to its displayable repr e-10 \nsentation. In concept, it’s not difficult: you just use type traits and template me t-11 \naprogramming (see Item 9) to break a type into its various components (using 12 \ntype traits such as std::is_const, std::is_pointer, 13 \nstd::is_lvalue_reference, etc.), and you create a string represen tation of the 14 \ntype from textual representations of each of its parts. (You’ d still be dependent on 15 \ntypeid and std::type_info::name to generate string representations of the 16 \nnames of user-defined classes, though.) 17 \nIf you’d use such a facility often enough to justify the effort needed to write, debug, 18 \ndocument, and maintain it, that’s a reasonable approach. But if you’re willing to 19 \nlive with a little platform -dependent code that’s easy to implement and produces 20 \nbetter results than those based on typeid, it’s worth noting that many compilers 21 \nsupport a language extension that yields a printable representation of the full si g-22 \nnature for a function, including, for functions generated from templates, types for 23 \nboth template and function parameters.  24 \nFor example, the  GNU and Clang compilers support a construct called 25 \n__PRETTY_FUNCTION__, and Microsoft’s compiler offers  __FUNCSIG__.  These 26 \nconstructs represent a variable (for GNU and Clang) or a macro (for Microsoft) 27 \nwhose value is the signature of the containing func tion. If we reimplement our 28 \ntemplate f like this, 29 \ntemplate<typename T> 30 \nvoid f(const T& param) 31 \n{ 32\n\n#### 第 40 页原文\n\nPage 40 \n \n#if defined(__GNUC__)                          // For GNU and 1 \n  std::cout << __PRETTY_FUNCTION__ << '\\n';    // Clang 2 \n#elif defined(_MSC_VER) 3 \n  std::cout << __FUNCSIG__ << '\\n';            // For Microsoft 4 \n#endif 5 \n  … 6 \n} 7 \nand call f as we did before, 8 \nstd::vector<Widget> createVec();     // factory function 9 \nconst auto vw = createVec();         // init vw w/factory return 10 \nif (!vw.empty()) { 11 \n  f(&vw[0]);                         // call f 12 \n  … 13 \n} 14 \nwe get the following result from GNU: 15 \nvoid f(const T&) [with T = const Widget*] 16 \nThis tells us that T has been deduced to be const Widget* (the same thing we got 17 \nvia typeid, but without the “PK” encoding and the “6” in front of the class name), 18 \nbut it also tells us that f’s parameter has type const T&. If we expand T in that 19 \nformulation, we get const Widget * const &. That’s different from what typeid 20 \ntold us, though it’s the same as the type in the error message provoked by use of 21 \nthe declared-but-not-defined TD template. It’s also correct. 22 \nUse of Microsoft’s __FUNCSIG__ produces this output: 23 \nvoid __cdecl f<const classWidget*>(const class Widget *const &) 24 \nThe type inside the angle brackets is the type deduced for T: const Widget*. This, 25 \ntoo, is what we got via typeid. But the type inside parentheses is the type d e-26 \nduced for param: const Widget * const&. That’s not what typeid told us, 27 \nthough, again, it’ s the same as the  (correct) information we’d get during compil a-28 \ntion from use of the TD template. 29\n\n#### 第 41 页原文\n\nPage 41 \n \nClang’s function-signature-reporting facility, despite using the same name as 1 \nGNU’s (__PRETTY_FUNCTION__), is not as forthcoming as GNU’s or Microsoft’s. It 2 \nyields simply: 3 \nvoid f(const Widget *const &) 4 \nThis shows param’s type directly, but it leaves it up to you to deduce that T’s type 5 \nmust have been const Widget* (or to rely on the information provided via 6 \ntypeid). 7 \nIDE editors, compiler error messages,  typeid, and language extensions like 8 \n__PRETTY_FUNCTION__ and __FUNCSIG__ are merely tools you can use to help 9 \nyou figure out what types your compilers are deducing for you. All can be helpful, 10 \nbut at the end of the day, there’s no substitute for understanding the type deduc-11 \ntion information in Items 1-3.   12 \nThings to Remember 13 \n Deduced types can often be seen using IDE editors, compiler error messages,  14 \ntypeid, and language extensions such as __PRETTY_FUNCTION__ and 15 \n__FUNCSIG__. 16 \n The results of such tools may be neither helpful nor accurate, so an unde r-17 \nstanding of C++’s type deduction rules remains essential. 18\n\n#### 第 42 页原文\n\nPage 42 \n \n \n1 \nIt’s been a wild ride for auto. In 1983, Bjarne Stroustrup took this lonely and 2 \nlargely superfluous keyword and gave it new purpose in his C++ compiler : to de-3 \nclare a variable whose type was deduced from its initializing expression. The move 4 \nproved too bold for the C developers who were C++’s target audience, so auto’s 5 \nrevised semantics were rolled back, and the keyword retired to the home for trivia 6 \ntopics. (“What is auto used for?”)  7 \nNearly three decades lat er, it debuted a second time (with the same semantics 8 \nBjarne had instilled in it so many years before) and thereby became C++11’s oldest 9 \nnew feature. It’s also probably C++11’s most frequently used one, because few acts 10 \nare as common as declaring variables . In C++14, its utility gains new ground: 11 \nlambda parameters may incorporate auto, and function return types may be au-12 \nto-deduced. It took a long time for auto to make it into the limelight, but its tenure 13 \nthere now seems secure. 14 \nIn concept, auto is as simpl e as simple can be, but it’s more subtle than it looks. 15 \nUsing it saves typing, sure, but it also prevents correctness and performance issues 16 \nthat can bedevil manual type declarations. Furthermore, some of auto’s type d e-17 \nduction results, while dutifully conf orming to the prescribed algorithm, are, from 18 \nthe perspective of a programmer, just plain wrong. When that’s the case, it’s i m-19 \nportant to know how to guide auto to the right answer, because falling back on 20 \nmanual type declarations is an alternative that’s often best avoided. 21 \nThis brief chapter covers all of auto’s ins and outs. 22 \n Item 5:  Prefer auto to explicit type declarations. 23 \nAh, the simple joy of  24 \nint x; 25 \nWait. Damn. I forgot to initialize x, so its value is  indeterminate.  Maybe. It might 26 \nactually be initialized to zero. Depends on the context. Sigh.  27\n\n#### 第 43 页原文\n\nPage 43 \n \nNever mind. Let’s move on  to the simple joy of declaring a local variable to be in i-1 \ntialized with the value of an iterator: 2 \ntemplate<typename It>    // algorithm to dwim (\"do what I mean\")  3 \nvoid dwim(It b, It e)    // for all elements in range from 4 \n{                        // b to e 5 \n  while (b != e) { 6 \n    typename std::iterator_traits<It>::value_type 7 \n      currValue = *b; 8 \n    … 9 \n  } 10 \n} 11 \nUgh. “ typename std::iterator_traits<It>::value_type” to express the 12 \ntype of the valu e pointed to by an iterator?  Really? I must have blocked out the 13 \nmemory of how much fun that is. Damn. Wait—didn’t I already say that? 14 \nOkay, simple joy (take three): the delight of declaring a local variable whose type is 15 \nthe same as that resulting from a lambda expression. Oh, right. The type of a lam b-16 \nda expression is known only to the compiler, hence can’t be written out. Sigh. 17 \nDamn. 18 \nDamn, damn, damn! Programming in C++ is not the joyous experience it should be! 19 \nWell, it didn’t used to be. But as of C++11, all these issues go away, courtesy of au-20 \nto. auto variables have their type deduced from their initializer, so they must be 21 \ninitialized. That means you can wave goodbye to a host of uninitialized variable 22 \nproblems as you speed by on the C++11 superhighway: 23 \nint x;       // valid in C++ 98/11/14, potentially uninitialized 24 \nauto x;      // error! initializer required 25 \nauto x = 0;  // fine, x's value is well-defined 26 \nSaid highway also paves over the potholes associated with declaring a local varia-27 \nble whose value is determined by an iterator: 28 \ntemplate<typename It>    // as before  29 \nvoid dwim(It b, It e)  30 \n{ 31 \n  while (b != e) { 32 \n    auto currValue = *b; 33 \n    … 34\n\n#### 第 44 页原文\n\nPage 44 \n \n  } 1 \n} 2 \nAnd because auto is based on type deduction, it can represent types known only 3 \nto compilers: 4 \nauto derefUPLess =                        // comparison func. 5 \n  [](const std::unique_ptr<Widget>& p1,   // for Widgets  6 \n     const std::unique_ptr<Widget>& p2)   // pointed to by  7 \n  { return *p1 < *p2; };                  // std::unique_ptrs 8 \nVery cool. In C++1 4, the coolness chills further, because in C++14, parameters to 9 \nlambda expressions may involve auto: 10 \nauto derefLess =                          // comparison func. 11 \n  [](const auto& p1,                      // for values pointed 12 \n     const auto& p2)                      // to by anything 13 \n  { return *p1 < *p2; };                  // pointer-like 14 \nCoolness notwithstanding, perhaps you're thinking that we don't really need auto 15 \nto declare a variable that holds a closure, because we can use a  std::function 16 \nobject: 17 \nstd::function<bool(const std::unique_ptr<Widget>&,  18 \n                   const std::unique_ptr<Widget>&)> 19 \n  derefUPLess = [](const std::unique_ptr<Widget>& p1, 20 \n                   const std::unique_ptr<Widget>& p2) 21 \n                  { return *p1 < *p2; }; 22 \nYou're right, but it's important to recognize that —even setting aside the syntactic 23 \nverbosity and the need to repeat the parameter types —using std::function is 24 \nnot the same thing as using auto. An auto-declared variable holding the result of a 25 \nlambda expr ession has the same type as the lambda expression. The type of a 26 \nstd::function-declared variable holding the result of a lambda expression is 27 \nsome instantiation of the std::function template. The constructor for such i n-28 \nstantiations may allocate heap memory . (Whether it does depends on how much 29 \ndata is captured by the lambda and on how std::function is implemented). au-30 \nto never allocates heap memory. T he std::function object uses more memory 31 \nthan the auto-declared object. And, thanks to implementation details that restrict 32 \ninlining and yield indirect function calls, invoking a lambda’s closure via a 33 \nstd::function object is almost certain to be slower than calling it via  an auto-34 \ndeclared object. In other words, the std::function approach is generally bigger 35\n\n#### 第 45 页原文\n\nPage 45 \n \nand slower than the auto approach, and it may yield out -of-memory exceptions, 1 \ntoo. Plus, as you can see in the examples above, writing  “auto”  is a whole lot less 2 \nwork than writing the type of the std::function instantiation. In the competi-3 \ntion between auto and std::function for holding the result of a lambda expre s-4 \nsion, it’s pretty much game, set, and match for auto. (A similar argument can be 5 \nmade for auto over std::function for holding the result of calls to std::bind, 6 \nthough in  Item 36, I do my best to convince you to use lambdas instead of 7 \nstd::bind, anyway.) 8 \nThe advantages of auto extend beyond the avoidance of uninitialized variables, 9 \nverbose variable declarations, and the ability to directly hold the result of lambda 10 \nexpressions. One is the ability to avoid what I call problems related to “type 11 \nshortcuts.” Here’s something you’ve probably seen—possibly even written: 12 \nstd::vector<int> v; 13 \n… 14 \nunsigned sz = v.size(); 15 \nThe official return type of v.size() is std::vector<int>::size_type, but few 16 \ndevelopers are aware of that. std::vector<int>::size_type is specified to be 17 \nan unsigned integral type, so a lot of programmers figure that unsigned is good 18 \nenough and write code such as the above. This can have some interesting conse-19 \nquences. On 32-bit Windows, for example, both unsigned and 20 \nstd::vector<int>::size_type are the same size, but on 64-bit Windows, un-21 \nsigned is 32 bits, while std::vector<int>::size_type is 64 bits. This means 22 \nthat code that works under 32-bit Windows may behave incorrectly under 64-bit 23 \nWindows, and when porting your application from 32 to 64 bits, who wants to 24 \nspend time on issues like that? 25 \nUsing auto ensures that you don’t have to: 26 \nauto sz = v.size();  // sz's type is std::vector<int>::size_type 27 \nStill unsure about the wisdom of using auto? Then consider this code: 28 \nstd::unordered_map<std::string, int> m;  29 \n… 30\n\n#### 第 46 页原文\n\nPage 46 \n \nfor (const std::pair<std::string, int>& p : m) 1 \n{ 2 \n  …                   // do something with p 3 \n} 4 \nThis looks perfectly reasonable, but there’s a problem. Do you see it? 5 \nRecognizing what’s amiss requires remembering that the key part of a 6 \nstd::unordered_map is const, so the type of std::pair in the hash table 7 \n(which is what a std::unordered_map is) isn’t std::pair<std::string, 8 \nint>, it’s std::pair<const std::string, int>. But that’s not the type d e-9 \nclared for the variable p in the loop above. As a result, compilers will strive to find 10 \na way to convert std::pair<const std::string, int> objects (i.e., what’s in 11 \nthe hash table) to std::pair<std::string, int> objects (the declared type for 12 \np). They’ll succeed by creating a temporary object of the type that p wants to bind 13 \nto by copying each object in m, then binding the reference p to that temporary o b-14 \nject. At the end of each loop iteration, the temporary object will be destroyed.  If 15 \nyou wrote this loop, you’d likely be surprised by this behavior, because you’d a l-16 \nmost certainly intend to simply bind the reference p to each element in m. 17 \nSuch unintentional type mismatches can be autoed away: 18 \nfor (const auto& p : m) 19 \n{ 20 \n …                             // as before 21 \n} 22 \nThis is not only more efficient, it’s also easier to type.  Furthermore, this code has 23 \nthe very attractive characteristic that if you take p’s address, you're sure to get a 24 \npointer to an element within m. In the code not using auto, you'd get a pointer to a 25 \ntemporary object—an object that would be destroyed at the end of the loop iter a-26 \ntion. 27 \nThe last two examples —writing unsigned when you should have written 28 \nstd::vector<int>::size_type and writing std::pair<std::string, int> 29 \nwhen you should have written std::pair<const std::string, int>—30 \ndemonstrate how explicitly specifying types can lead to implicit conversions that 31 \nyou neither want nor expect. Such conversions arise whenever the type of the in i-32 \ntializing expression is  different from the type you specify for the target variable, 33\n\n#### 第 47 页原文\n\nPage 47 \n \nbut an implicit conversion exists from one to the other. It generally arises because 1 \nyour expectations about the type of the initializing expression are incorrect, e.g., 2 \nyou believe that std::vector<int>::size_type is the same as unsigned, or 3 \nyou forget that the first component of the std::pairs in a std::unordered_map 4 \nis const. If you use auto as the type of the target variable, you need not worry 5 \nabout mismatches between the type of variable you’re declaring and the type of 6 \nthe expression used to initialize it. 7 \nThere are thus several reasons to prefer auto over explicit type declarations. Yet 8 \nauto isn’t perfect. The type for each auto variable is deduced from its initializing 9 \nexpression, and some initializing expressions have types that are neither antic i-10 \npated nor desired. The conditions under which such cases arise, and what you can 11 \ndo about them, are discussed in Items 2 and 6, so I won’t address th em here. In-12 \nstead, I’ll turn my attention to a different concern you may have about using auto 13 \nin place of traditional type declarations: the readability of the resulting source 14 \ncode. 15 \nFirst, take a deep breath and relax. auto is an option, not a mandate. If, in your 16 \nprofessional judgment, your code will be clearer or more maintainable or in some 17 \nother way better by using explicit type declarations, you’re free to continue using 18 \nthem. But bear in mind that C++ is far from breaking new ground in adopting what 19 \nis generally kn own in the programming languages world as type inference.  Other 20 \nstatically typed procedural languages (e.g., C#, D, Scala, Visual Basic) have a more 21 \nor less equivalent feature, to say nothing of a variety of statically typed functional 22 \nlanguages (e.g., ML , Haskell, OCaml, F#, etc.). In part, this is due to the success of 23 \ndynamically typed languages such as Perl, Python, and Ruby, where variables are 24 \nessentially never explicitly typed. The software development community has e x-25 \ntensive experience with type inference, and it has demonstrated that there is noth-26 \ning contradictory about such technology and the creation and maintenance of i n-27 \ndustrial-strength, industrial-sized code bases.  28 \nSome developers are disturbed by the fact that using auto eliminates the ability to 29 \ndetermine an object’s type by a quick glance at the source code. However, IDEs’ 30 \nability to show object types often mitigates this problem (even taking into account 31 \nthe IDE type-display issues mentioned in Item 4), and, in many cases, a somewhat 32\n\n#### 第 48 页原文\n\nPage 48 \n \nabstract view of an object’s type is just as useful as the exact type. It often suffices, 1 \nfor example, to know that an object is a con tainer or a counter or a smart pointer, 2 \nwithout knowing exactly what kind of container, counter, or smart pointer it is. 3 \nAssuming well-chosen variable names, such abstract type information should al-4 \nmost always be at hand. 5 \nThe fact of the matter is that writing out types explicitly often does little more than 6 \nintroduce opportunities for subtle errors, either in correctness  or efficiency or 7 \nboth. Furthermore, auto types automatically change if the type of their initializing 8 \nexpression changes, and that means that some refactorings are facilitated by the 9 \nuse of auto. For example, if a function is declared to return an int, but you later 10 \ndecide that a long would be better, the calling code automatically updates itself 11 \nthe next time you compile if the results of calling the function are stored in an au-12 \nto variable. If the results are stored in a variable explicitly declared to be an int, 13 \nyou’ll need to find all the call sites so that you can revise them. 14 \nBy all means, consider the impact on your source code’s readability when choosing 15 \nbetween declaring auto types and explicit types. But before you reject auto, be 16 \nsure you’re doing it for a solid technical reason, not simply because you’re used to 17 \nwriting out types by hand. 18 \nThings to Remember: 19 \n auto variables must be initialized, are generally immune to type mismatches 20 \nthat can lead to portability or efficiency problems, can ease the process of r e-21 \nfactoring, and typically require less typing than variables with explicitly spec i-22 \nfied types. 23 \n auto-typed variables are subject to the pitfalls described in Items 2 and 6.  24 \n Item 6: Be aware of the typed initializer idiom. 25 \nItem 5 explains that using auto to declare variables offers a number of technical 26 \nadvantages over explicitly specifying types, but sometimes auto’s type deduction 27 \nzigs when you want it to zag. For e xample, suppose I have a function that takes a 28 \nWidget and returns a std::vector<bool>, where each bool indicates whether 29 \nthe Widget offers a particular feature: 30\n\n#### 第 49 页原文\n\nPage 49 \n \nstd::vector<bool> features(const Widget& w); 1 \nFurther suppose that bit 5 indicates whether the Widget has high priority. We can 2 \nthus write code like this: 3 \nWidget w; 4 \n… 5 \nbool highPriority = features(w)[5];  // is w high priority? 6 \n… 7 \nprocessWidget(w, highPriority);      // process w in accord 8 \n                                     // with its priority 9 \nThere’s nothing wrong with this code. It’ll work fine. But if we make the seemingly 10 \ninnocuous change of replacing the explicit type for highPriority with auto,  11 \nauto highPriority = features(w)[5];  // is w high priority? 12 \nthe situation changes. All the code will  continue to compile, but its behavior is no 13 \nlonger predictable: 14 \nprocessWidget(w, highPriority);      // undefined behavior! 15 \nAs the comment indicates, the call to processWidget now has undefined beha v-16 \nior. But why? The answer is likely to be surprising. In the code using auto, the type 17 \nof highPriority is no longer bool. Though std::vector<bool> conceptually 18 \nholds bools, operator[] for std::vector<bool> doesn’t return a  reference to 19 \nan element of the container  (which is what std::vector::operator[] returns 20 \nfor every type except bool). Instead, it returns an object of type 21 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference (a class nested inside std::vector<bool>).  22 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference exists because std::vector<bool> is speci-23 \nfied to represent its bools in packed  form, one bit per bool. That creates a pro b-24 \nlem for std::vector<bool>’s operator[], because operator[] for 25 \nstd::vector<T> is supposed to return a  T&, but C++ forbids references to bits. 26 \nNot being able to return a bool&, operator[] for std::vector<bool> returns 27 \nan object that acts like  a bool&. For this act to succeed, 28 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference objects must be usable in essentially all co n-29 \ntexts where bool&s can be. Among the features in 30 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference that make this work is an implicit conve rsion 31\n\n#### 第 50 页原文\n\nPage 50 \n \nto bool. (Not to bool&, to bool. To explain the full set of techniques used by 1 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference to emulate the behavior of a bool& would 2 \ntake us too far afield, so I’ll simply remark that this implicit conversion is only one 3 \nstone in a larger mosaic.) 4 \nWith this information in mind, look again at this part of the original code: 5 \nbool highPriority = features(w)[5];  // declare highPriority’s 6 \n                                     // type explicitly 7 \nHere, features returns a std::vector<bool> object, on which operator[] is 8 \ninvoked. operator[] returns a std::vector<bool>::reference object, which 9 \nis then implicitly converted to the bool that is needed to initialize highPriority.  10 \nhighPriority thus ends up with the value of bit 5 in the std::vector<bool> 11 \nreturned by features, just like it’s supposed to. 12 \nContrast that with what happens in the auto-ized declaration for highPriority: 13 \nauto highPriority = features(w)[5];  // deduce highPriority’s 14 \n                                     // type 15 \nAgain, features returns a std::vector<bool> object, and, again, operator[] 16 \nis invoked on it. operator[] continues to return a 17 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference object, but now there’s a change, because au-18 \nto deduces that as the type of highPriority.  highPriority doesn’t have the 19 \nvalue of bit 5 of the std::vector<bool> returned by features at all.  20 \nThe value it does have depends on how std::vector<bool>::reference is im-21 \nplemented. One implementation is for such objects to contain a pointer to the  ma-22 \nchine word holding the referenced bit, plus the offset into that word for that bit . 23 \nConsider what that means for the initialization of highPriority, assuming that 24 \nsuch a std::vector<bool>::reference implementation is in place.  25 \nThe call to features returns a temporary std::vector<bool> object. This o b-26 \nject has no name, but for purposes of this discussion, I’ll call it temp. operator[] 27 \nis invoked on temp, and the std::vector<bool>::reference it returns co n-28 \ntains a pointer to a word in the data structure holding the bits that are managed by 29 \ntemp, p lus the offset into that word corresponding to bit 5. highPriority is a 30", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.51–p.75", "pages": "p.51–p.75", "raw_text": "#### 第 51 页原文\n\nPage 51 \n \ncopy of this std::vector<bool>::reference object, so highPriority, too, 1 \ncontains a pointer to  a word in temp, plus the offset corresponding to bit 5. At the 2 \nend of the statement, temp is destroyed, becau se it’s a temporary object. Ther e-3 \nfore, highPriority contains a dangling pointer, and that’s the cause of the und e-4 \nfined behavior in the call to processWidget: 5 \nprocessWidget(w, highPriority);    // undefined behavior!  6 \n                                   // highPriority may contain 7 \n                                   // dangling pointer! 8 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference is an example of a proxy class: a class that e x-9 \nists for the purpose of emulating and augmenting the behavior of some other type. 10 \nProxy classes are employed for a variety of purposes. 11 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference exists to offer the illusion that operator[] 12 \nfor std::vector<bool> returns a reference to a bit,  for example, and the Stan d-13 \nard Library’s smart pointer types (see  Chapter 4) are proxy classes that graft r e-14 \nsource management onto raw pointers. The utility of proxy classes is well -15 \nestablished. In fact, the design pattern “Proxy” is one of the most longstanding 16 \nmembers of the software design patterns Pantheon. 17 \nSome proxy classes are designed to be apparent to clients. That’s the case for 18 \nstd::shared_ptr and std::unique_ptr, for example. Other proxy classes are 19 \ndesigned to act more or less invisibly. std::vector<bool>::reference is an 20 \nexample of such “invisible” proxies , as is its C++14 cousin, 21 \nstd::bitset::reference.  22 \nAlso in that camp are some classes in C++ lib raries employing a technique known 23 \nas expression templates. Such libraries were originally developed to improve the 24 \nefficiency of numeric code. Given a class Matrix and Matrix objects m1, m2, m3, 25 \nand m4, for example,  the expression 26 \nMatrix sum = m1 + m2 + m3 + m4; 27 \ncan be computed much more efficiently if operator+ for Matrix objects returns a 28 \nproxy for the result instead of the result itself.  That is, operator+ for two Matrix 29 \nobjects would return an object of a proxy class such as Sum<Matrix, Matrix> in-30 \nstead of a Matrix object. As was the case with std::vector<bool>::reference 31\n\n#### 第 52 页原文\n\nPage 52 \n \nand bool, there’d be an implicit conversion from the proxy class to Matrix, which 1 \nwould permit the initialization of sum from the proxy object produced by the e x-2 \npression on the right side of the “=”.  (The type of that object would traditionally 3 \nencode the entire initialization expression, i.e., be something like 4 \nSum<Sum<Sum<Matrix, Matrix>, Matrix>, Matrix>. That’s definitely a type 5 \nfrom which clients should be shielded.) 6 \nAs a general  rule, “invisible” proxy classes  don’t play well with auto. Objects of 7 \nsuch classes are often not designed to live longer than a single statement, so crea t-8 \ning variables of those types tends to violate fundamental library design assum p-9 \ntions. That’s the case  with std::vector<bool>::reference, and we’ve seen 10 \nthat violating that assumption can lead to undefined behavior.  11 \nYou therefore want to avoid code of this form: 12 \nauto someVar = expression of \"invisible\" proxy class type; 13 \nBut how can you recognize when proxy objects are in use ? The software emplo y-14 \ning them is unlikely to advertise their existence. They’re supposed to be invisible, 15 \nat least conceptually! And once you’ve found them, do you really have to abandon 16 \nauto and the many advantages Item 5 demonstrates for it?  17 \nLet’s take the how -do-you-find-them question first. Although “invisible” proxy 18 \nclasses are designed to fly beneath programmer radar in da y-to-day use, libraries 19 \nusing them often document that they do so. The more you’ve familiarized yourself 20 \nwith the basic design decisions of the libraries you use, the less likely you are to be 21 \nblindsided by proxy usage within those libraries. 22 \nWhere documentation comes up short, header files fill the gap. I t’s rarely possible 23 \nfor source code to fully cloak proxy objects. They’re typically returned from fun c-24 \ntions that clients are expected to call, so function signatures usually reflect the ir 25 \nexistence. Here’s the spec for std::vector<bool>::operator[], for example: 26 \nnamespace std {                    // from C++98/11/14 Standards 27 \n  template <class Allocator> 28 \n  class vector<bool, Allocator> {  29 \n  public: 30\n\n#### 第 53 页原文\n\nPage 53 \n \n    … 1 \n    class reference { … }; 2 \n    reference operator[](size_type n); 3 \n    … 4 \n  }; 5 \n} 6 \nAssuming you know that operator[] for std::vector<T> normally returns a  7 \nT&, the unconventional return type for operator[] in this case is a tip -off that a 8 \nproxy class is in use. Paying careful attention to the interfaces you’re using can of-9 \nten reveal the existence of proxy classes. 10 \nIn practice, many developers discover th e use of proxy classes only when they try 11 \nto track down mystifying compilation problems or debug incorrect unit test r e-12 \nsults. Regardless of how you find them , once auto has been determined to be d e-13 \nducing the type of a proxy class instead of the type being proxied, the solution 14 \nneed not involve abandoning auto. auto itself isn’t the problem. The problem is 15 \nthat auto isn’t deducing the type you want it to deduce.  T he solution is to force a 16 \ndifferent type deduction. The way you do that is  what I call the typed initializer idi-17 \nom. 18 \nThe typed initializer idiom involves declaring a variable with auto, but casting the 19 \ninitialization expression to the type you want auto to deduce. Here’s how it can be 20 \nused to force highPriority to be a bool, for example: 21 \nauto highPriority = static_cast<bool>(features(w)[5]); 22 \nHere, features(w)[5] continues to return a std::vector<bool>::reference 23 \nobject, just as it always has, but the cast ch anges the type of the expression to 24 \nbool, which auto then deduces as the type for highPriority. At runtime, the 25 \nstd::vector<bool>::reference object returned from 26 \nstd::vector<bool>::operator[] executes the conversion to bool that it su p-27 \nports, and as part of  that conversion, the still -valid pointer to the 28 \nstd::vector<bool> returned from features is dereferenced. That avoids the 29 \nundefined behavior we ran into earlier. The index 5 is then applied to the bits 30 \npointed to by the pointer, and the bool value that emerges is used to initialize 31 \nhighPriority. 32\n\n#### 第 54 页原文\n\nPage 54 \n \nFor the Matrix example, the typed initializer idiom would look like this: 1 \nauto sum = static_cast<Matrix>(m1 + m2 + m3 + m4); 2 \nApplications of the idiom aren’t limited to initializers yielding proxy cla ss types. It 3 \ncan also be useful to emphasize that you are deliberately creating a variable of a 4 \ntype that is different from that generated by the initializing expression. For exa m-5 \nple, suppose you have a function to calculate some tolerance value: 6 \ndouble calcEpsilon();            // return tolerance value 7 \ncalcEpsilon clearly returns a double, but suppose you know that for your appli-8 \ncation, the precision of a float is adequate, and you care about the difference in 9 \nsize between floats and doubles. You could declare a float variable to store the 10 \nresult of calcEpsilon, 11 \nfloat ep = calcEpsilon();        // impliclitly convert 12 \n                                 // doublefloat 13 \nbut this hardly announces “I’m deliberately reducing the precision of the value r e-14 \nturned by the function!” A declaration using the typed initializer idiom, however, 15 \ndoes: 16 \nauto ep = static_cast<float>(calcEpsilon()); 17 \nSimilar reasoning applies if you have a floating point expression that you are d e-18 \nliberately storing as an integral value. Suppose yo u need to calculate the index of 19 \nan element in a container with random access iterators (e.g., a std::vector, 20 \nstd::deque, or std::array), and you’re given a double between 0.0 and 1.0 21 \nindicating how far from the beginning of the container the desired element is l o-22 \ncated. (0.5 would indicate the middle of the container.) Further suppose that 23 \nyou’re confident that the resulting index will fit in an int. If the container is c and 24 \nthe double is d, you could calculate the index this way, 25 \nint index = d * c.size(); 26 \nbut this obscures the fact that you’re intentionally converting the double on the 27 \nright to an int. The typed initializer idiom eliminates makes things transparent: 28 \nauto index = static_cast<int>(d * c.size()); 29\n\n#### 第 55 页原文\n\nPage 55 \n \nThings to Remember 1 \n “Invisible” proxy types can cause auto to deduce the “wrong” type for an in i-2 \ntializing expression. 3 \n The typed initializer idiom forces auto to deduce the type you want it to have. 4\n\n#### 第 56 页原文\n\nPage 56 \n \n \n1 \nIf you’re an experienced C++ developer, you unde rstand and are comfortable with 2 \nC++98 and its common programming idioms. Enums, classes, member functions, 3 \ninheritance, templates, const, using objects to manage resources —you’ve been 4 \nthere, you’ve done that. As such, you’re likely to view C++11 as C++98 plus a bunch 5 \nof new features, with C++14 comprising essentially more of the same.  6 \nFor the most part, that’s fine. C++’s legendary commitment to backwards -7 \ncompatibility means that not only does virtually all legacy source code remain va l-8 \nid, the same is true for most C++ software development techniques. Good practices 9 \nin C++98 typically remain good practices in C++11. But there are exceptions . In 10 \nsome cases, C++98 language features should generally be eschewed in favor of 11 \nnewer, similar C++11 features that take advantage of the experience the C++ pr o-12 \ngramming community garnered during the 13 year reign of C++98. Examples i n-13 \nclude using nullptr instead of NULL or 0 (Item 8), preferring alias declarations 14 \nover typedefs ( Item 9), choosing scoped instead of unscoped enums ( Item 10), 15 \nand prohibiting the use of  unwanted member functions by deleteing them i n-16 \nstead of declaring them private (Item 11). In other cases, taking advantage of 17 \nnew language features requires modification of some well -entrenched habits. For 18 \nexample, virtual function overrides should now be declared as such ( Item 12), 19 \nsome parameters are now better passed by value than by reference -to-const 20 \n(Item 17), calls to push_back or push_front or insert should sometimes be re-21 \nplaced with their emplac ement counterparts ( Item 18), functions that can pote n-22 \ntially run during compilation should be declared constexpr (Item 14), and excep-23 \ntion specifications should be more widely employed (Item 16). In still other cases, 24 \nC++11 simply changes the rules of the C++ programming game. Ways this is a p-25 \nparent include that there’s now an important syntactic choice to  be made when 26 \ndeclaring objects ( Item 7),  const_iterators are substantially more useful than 27 \nthey used to be ( Item 13), declaring member functions const implies that they’re 28 \nthread-safe (Item 15), and compiler-generated member functions are governed by 29 \na set of rules that have been significantly revised (Item 19). 30\n\n#### 第 57 页原文\n\nPage 57 \n \nThis chapter covers guidelines that will help you update C++98 habits to bring 1 \nthem into accord with C++11 and C++14, but not all guidelines of that nature are in 2 \nthis chapter. Item 5, for example  (“Prefer auto to explicit type declarations”) , be-3 \nlongs here, too, but it fits more naturally into the chapter on auto, and the over-4 \narching guidance to prefer smart pointers to raw pointers has so many facets , it 5 \ngets its own chapter (Chapter 4). Still, the Items that follow do a good job of sum-6 \nmarizing how effective programming in C++11 and C++14 distinguishes itself from 7 \nC++98 approaches to accomplishing common development tasks.  It demonstrates 8 \nthat being an effective contemporary C++ programmer consists of not just adop t-9 \ning new language features, but also reevaluating techniques and practices proven 10 \nin C++98. 11 \n Item 7: Distinguish () and {} when creating objects. 12 \nDepending on your perspective, syntax choices for object initialization in C++11 13 \nembody either an embarrassment of riches or a confusing mess. As a general rule, 14 \ninitialization values may be specified with parentheses, an equals sign, or braces: 15 \nint x(0);             // initializer is in parentheses 16 \nint y = 0;            // initializer follows \"=\" 17 \nint z{0};             // initializer is in braces 18 \nIn many cases, it’s also possible to use an equals sign and braces together: 19 \nint z = {0};          // initializer uses \"=\" and braces 20 \nFor the remainder of this Item, I'll generally ignore the equals -sign-plus-braces 21 \nsyntax, because C++ usually treats it the same as the braces-only version. 22 \nThe “confusing mess” lobby points out that the use of  an equals sign for initializ a-23 \ntion often misleads C++ newbies into thinking  that an assignment is taking place, 24 \neven though it’s not. For built -in types like int, the difference is academic, but for 25 \nuser-defined types, it’s important to distinguish initial ization from assignment, 26 \nbecause different function calls are involved: 27 \nWidget w1;            // call default constructor 28 \nWidget w2 = w1;       // not an assignment; calls copy ctor 29\n\n#### 第 58 页原文\n\nPage 58 \n \nw1 = w2;              // an assignment; calls copy operator= 1 \nEven with several initialization syntaxes, there were some situations where C++98 2 \nhad no way to express a desired initialization . For example, it wasn’t possible  to 3 \ndirectly indicate that an STL container (e.g., std::vector<int>) should be creat-4 \ned holding a particular set of values (e.g., 1, 3, and 5).  5 \nTo address the confusion of multiple initialization syntaxes, as well as the fact that 6 \nthey don’t cover all initialization s cenarios, C++11 introduce s uniform initializ a-7 \ntion: a single initialization syntax that can be  used anywhere and can express ev e-8 \nrything. It’s based on braces, and for that reason I prefer the term braced initializa-9 \ntion. “Uniform initialization” is a concept. “Braced initialization” is a syntactic con-10 \nstruct. 11 \nBraced initialization lets you express th e formerly inexpressible. Using braces, 12 \nspecifying the initial contents of a container is easy: 13 \nstd::vector<int> v{1, 3, 5};   // v's initial content is 1, 3, 5 14 \nBraces can also be used to specify default initialization values for non -static data 15 \nmembers. T his capability —new to C++11 —is shared with the “ =” initialization 16 \nsyntax, but not with parentheses: 17 \nclass Widget { 18 \n  … 19 \nprivate: 20 \n  int x{0};                    // fine, x's default value is 0 21 \n  int y = 0;                   // also fine 22 \n  int z(0);                    // error! 23 \n};   24 \nOn the other hand, uncopyable objects (e.g., std::atomics) may be initialized u s-25 \ning braces or parentheses, but not using \"=\": 26 \nstd::atomic<int> ai1{0};       // fine 27 \nstd::atomic<int> ai2(0);       // fine 28 \nstd::atomic<int> ai3 = 0;      // error! 29\n\n#### 第 59 页原文\n\nPage 59 \n \nPerhaps now you see why braced initialization is called “uniform.” O f C++’s three 1 \nways to designate an initializing expression (braces, parentheses, and “ =”), only 2 \nbraces can be used everywhere. 3 \nA novel feature of braced initializa tion is that it pr ohibits implicit narrowing con-4 \nversions among built-in types. If the value of an expression in a braced initializer 5 \nisn’t guaranteed to  be expressible in the type of the object being initialized, the 6 \ncode won’t compile: 7 \ndouble x, y, z; 8 \n… 9 \nint sum1{x + y + z};         // error! sum of doubles may 10 \n                             // not be expressible as int 11 \nInitialization using parentheses and “=” doesn’t check for narrowing conversions, 12 \nbecause that could break too much legacy code: 13 \nint sum2(x + y + z);         // okay (value of expression  14 \n                             // truncated to an int) 15 \nint sum3 = x + y + z;        // ditto 16 \nAnother noteworthy characteristic of braced initialization is its immunity to C++’s 17 \nmost vexing parse. A side-effect of C++’s rule that anything that can be parsed as a 18 \ndeclaration must be interpreted as one , the most vexing parse most frequently af-19 \nflicts developers when they want to default -construct an object, but inadvertently 20 \nend up declaring a function, instead. The root of the problem is that if you want to 21 \ncall a constructor with an argument, you can do it like this, 22 \nWidget w1(10);     // call Widget ctor with argument 10 23 \nbut if you try to call a Widget constructor with zero arguments using the anal o-24 \ngous syntax, you declare a function instead of an object: 25 \nWidget w2();       // most vexing parse! declares a function 26 \n                   // named w2 that returns a Widget! 27 \nThis trap is particularly odious, because an empty set of parentheses sometimes 28 \ndoes call a constructor with zero arguments: 29\n\n#### 第 60 页原文\n\nPage 60 \n \nvoid f(const Widget& w = Widget());  // w's default value is a 1 \n                                     // default-constructed  2 \n                                     // Widget 3 \nBraced initialization  eliminates the most vexing parse, yet has no effect on the 4 \nmeaning of initializations that already do what’s desired: 5 \nWidget w1{10};     // as before, calls Widget ctor with arg 10 6 \nWidget w2{};       // now calls Widget ctor with no args 7 \nvoid f(const Widget& w = Widget{});  // as before, w's default 8 \n                                     // value is a default- 9 \n                                     // constructed Widget 10 \nThere’s thus a lot to be said for braced initialization. It’s  the syntax that can be 11 \nused in the widest variety of contexts, it prevents implicit narrowing conversions, 12 \nand it’s immune to C++’s most vexing parse. A trifecta of goodness , right? So why 13 \nisn’t this Item entitled something like “Use braced initialization syntax”?  14 \nThe drawback to braced initialization is the sometimes-surprising behavior that 15 \naccompanies it . Such behavior grows out of the unusually tangled relationship 16 \namong braced initializers, std::initializer_lists, and constructor overload 17 \nresolution. T heir interactions can lead to  code that seems like it should do  one 18 \nthing, but actually does another.  For example, Item 2 explains that when an auto-19 \ndeclared variable has a braced initializer, the type deduced is 20 \nstd::initializer_list, even though other ways of declaring a variable  with 21 \nthe same initializer would cause auto to deduce the type of the initializer: 22 \nauto v1 = -1;         // -1's type is int, and so is v1's 23 \nauto v2(-1);          // -1's type is int, and so is v2's 24 \nauto v3{-1};          // -1's type is still int, but 25 \n                      // v3's type is std::initializer_list<int> 26 \nauto v4 = {-1};       // -1's type remains int, but 27 \n                      // v4's type is std::initializer_list<int> 28 \nIn constructor calls, parentheses and braces  have the same meaning as long as 29 \nstd::initializer_list parameters are not involved: 30 \nclass Widget { 31 \npublic: 32\n\n#### 第 61 页原文\n\nPage 61 \n \n  Widget(int i, bool b);      // ctors not declaring  1 \n  Widget(int i, double d);    // std::initializer_list params 2 \n  … 3 \n}; 4 \nWidget w1(10, true);          // calls first ctor 5 \nWidget w2{10, true};          // also calls first ctor 6 \nWidget w3(10, 5.0);           // calls second ctor 7 \nWidget w4{10, 5.0};           // also calls second ctor 8 \nIf, however,  one or more constructors declares a parameter of type 9 \nstd::initializer_list, calls using the braced initialization syntax strongly 10 \nprefer the overloads taking std::initializer_lists. Strongly. If there’s any 11 \nway for compilers to construe a call using a braced initializer to be to a constructor 12 \ntaking a std::initializer_list, compilers will employ that interpretation. If 13 \nthe Widget class above is augmented with a constructor taking a 14 \nstd::initializer_list<long double>, for example, 15 \nclass Widget { 16 \npublic: 17 \n  Widget(int i, bool b);                           // as before 18 \n  Widget(int i, double d);                         // as before 19 \n  Widget(std::initializer_list<long double> il);   // added 20 \n  … 21 \n}; 22 \nWidgets w2 and w4 will be constructed using the new constructor, even though the 23 \ntype of the std::initializer_list elements (long double) is, compared to 24 \nthe non -std::initializer_list constructors, a worse match for both arg u-25 \nments! 26 \nWidget w1(10, true);       // uses parens and, as before, 27 \n                           // calls first ctor 28 \nWidget w2{10, true};       // uses braces, but now calls  29 \n                           // std::init_list ctor (10 and 30 \n                           // true convert to long double) 31 \nWidget w3(10, 5.0);        // uses parens and, as before, 32 \n                           // calls second ctor 33\n\n#### 第 62 页原文\n\nPage 62 \n \nWidget w4{10, 5.0};        // uses braces, but now calls 1 \n                           // std::init_list ctor (10 and 2 \n                           // 5.0 convert to long double) 3 \nCompilers’ determination to match braced initializers with constructors taking 4 \nstd::initializer_lists is so strong, it prevails even if the best-match 5 \nstd::initializer_list constructor can’t be call ed. For exam ple, consider this 6 \nslightly-revised example: 7 \nclass Widget { 8 \npublic: 9 \n  Widget(int i, bool b);                   // as before 10 \n  Widget(int i, double d);                 // as before 11 \n  Widget(std::initializer_list<bool> il);  // std::init_list 12 \n  …                                        // element type is 13 \n};                                         // now bool 14 \nWidget w{10, 5.0};     // error! requires narrowing conversions 15 \nHere, compilers will ignore the first two constructors (the second of which of fers 16 \nan exact match on both argument types) and try to call the constructor taking a 17 \nstd::initializer_list<bool>. Calling that constructor  would require co n-18 \nverting an int (10) and a double (5.0) to bools. Both conversions would be nar-19 \nrowing (bool can't exactly represent either value), and narrowing conversions are 20 \nprohibited inside braced initializers, so the call is invalid, and the code is rejected. 21 \nIf there’s no way to convert the types of the arguments in a braced initializer to the 22 \ntype taken by a std::initializer_list, compilers fall back on normal overload 23 \nresolution. For example, if we replace the std::initializer_list<bool> con-24 \nstructor with one taking a std::initializer_list<std::string>, the non -25 \nstd::initializer_list constructors become candida tes again, because there 26 \nis no way to convert ints and bools to std::strings: 27 \nclass Widget { 28 \npublic: 29 \n  Widget(int i, bool b);               // as before 30 \n  Widget(int i, double d);             // as before 31 \n  // std::init_list element type is now std::string 32 \n  Widget(std::initializer_list<std::string> il); 33 \n  … 34 \n}; 35\n\n#### 第 63 页原文\n\nPage 63 \n \nWidget w1(10, true);     // uses parens, still calls first ctor 1 \nWidget w2{10, true};     // uses braces, now calls first ctor 2 \nWidget w3(10, 5.0);      // uses parens, still calls second ctor 3 \nWidget w4{10, 5.0};      // uses braces, now calls second ctor 4 \nThere are two additional twists to the tale  of constructor overload resolution and 5 \nbraced initializers that are worth knowing about: 6 \n Empty braces mean no arguments, not an empty std::initializer_list. Speci-7 \nfying constructor arguments with an empty pair of braces is a request to call 8 \nthe default constructor , not a request to call a constructor with an empty 9 \nstd::initializer_list: 10 \nclass Widget { 11 \npublic: 12 \n  Widget();                               // default ctor 13 \n  Widget(std::initializer_list<int> il);  // std::init_list  14 \n  …                                       // ctor 15 \n}; 16 \nWidget w1;         // calls default ctor 17 \nWidget w2{};       // also calls default ctor 18 \n                   // (doesn't create empty std::init_list) 19 \nWidget w3();       // most vexing parse! declares a function! 20 \nIf you want to call a std::initializer_list constructor with an empty 21 \nstd::initializer_list, you do it by making the empty braces a constru c-22 \ntor argument—by putting the empty braces  inside the parentheses or braces 23 \ndemarcating what you’re passing! 24 \nWidget w4({});     // calls std::init_list ctor 25 \n                   // with empty list 26 \nWidget w5{{}};     // ditto 27 \n Copy and move constructors are called as usual.  Creating an object from 28 \nanother object of the same type always invokes the conventional copying and 29 \nmoving functions: 30 \nclass Widget { 31 \npublic: 32\n\n#### 第 64 页原文\n\nPage 64 \n \n  Widget(const Widget& rhs);              // copy ctor 1 \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs);                   // move ctor 2 \n  Widget(std::initializer_list<int> il);  // std::init_list  3 \n                                          // ctor 4 \n  operator int() const;                   // convert to int 5 \n  … 6 \n}; 7 \nauto w6{w5};               // calls copy ctor, not 8 \n                           // std::init_list <int> ctor, even 9 \n                           // though Widget converts to int 10 \nauto w7{std::move(w5)};    // ditto, but for move ctor  11 \n                           // (Item 25 has info on std::move) 12 \nAt this point, with seemingly arcane rules about braced initializers, 13 \nstd::initializer_lists, and constructor overloading burbling about in your 14 \nbrain, you may be wondering how much of this information matters in day-to-day 15 \nprogramming. More than you might think. That’s because one of the classes direc t-16 \nly affected is std::vector. std::vector has a non-std::initializer_list 17 \nconstructor that allows you to specify the initial size of the container and a value 18 \neach of the initial elements should have, but it also has a constructor taking a 19 \nstd::initializer_list that permits you to specify the initial values in the con-20 \ntainer. If you create a std::vector of a numeric type (e.g., a std::vector<int>) 21 \nand you pass  two arguments to the constructor, whether you enclose those arg u-22 \nments in parentheses or braces makes a tremendous difference: 23 \nstd::vector<int> v1(10, 20);  // use non-std::init_list ctor: 24 \n                              // create 10-element std::vector, 25 \n                              // all elements have value of 20 26 \nstd::vector<int> v2{10, 20};  // use std::init_list ctor: 27 \n                              // create 2-element std::vector, 28 \n                              // element values are 10 and 20 29 \nBut let’s step back from std::vector and also from the details of parentheses, 30 \nbraces, and constructor overloading resolution rules . There are two primary take-31 \naways from this discussion. First, as a class author, you need to be aware that if 32 \nyour constructor overloa ds include one or more functions taking a 33 \nstd::initializer_list, client code using braced initialization may see only the 34 \nstd::initializer_list overloads. As a result, it’s best to design your construc-35\n\n#### 第 65 页原文\n\nPage 65 \n \ntors so that the overload called isn’t affected by whether clients use parentheses or 1 \nbraces. In other words, learn from what is now considered to be an error in the 2 \ndesign of the std::vector interface, and design your classes to avoid it. 3 \nAn implication i s that if you have a class with no std::initializer_list con-4 \nstructor and you add one, client code using braced initialization may find that calls 5 \nthat used to resolve to non -std::initializer_list constructors now resolve 6 \nto the new function. Of course, this kind of thing can happen any time you add a  7 \nnew function to a set of overloads: calls that used to resolve to one of the old ove r-8 \nloads might start calling the new one. The difference with 9 \nstd::initializer_list constructor overloads is that a 10 \nstd::initializer_list overload doesn’t just compete with other overloads, it 11 \novershadows them to the point that the other overloads may not even be consi d-12 \nered. So add such overloads only with great deliberation. 13 \nThe second lesson is that as a class client, you must ch oose carefully between p a-14 \nrentheses and braces when creating objects. Most developers end up choosing one 15 \nkind of delimiter as a default, using the other only when they have to. Braces -by-16 \ndefault folks are attracted by their unrivaled breadth of applicability, their preven-17 \ntion of narrowing conversions, and their immunity to  C++’s most vexing parse. 18 \nSuch folks understand that in some cases (e.g., creation of a std::vector with a 19 \ngiven size and initial element value), parentheses are required. In contrast, the go-20 \nparentheses-go crowd embraces parentheses as their default argument delimiter . 21 \nThey’re attracted to its consistency with the  C++98 syntactic tradition , its avoi d-22 \nance of the auto-deduced-a-std::initializer_list problem, and the 23 \nknowledge that their object creation calls  won’t be inadvertently waylaid by 24 \nstd::initializer_list constructors. They concede that sometimes only bra c-25 \nes will do (e.g., when creating a container with particular values). Neither a p-26 \nproach is  rigorously better than the other . M y advi ce is to pick one and apply it 27 \nconsistently.† 28 \n                                                             \n† The examples in this book reveal that I’m a parentheses-by-default person.\n\n#### 第 66 页原文\n\nPage 66 \n \nIf you’re a template author, the parentheses -braces duality for object creation can 1 \nbe especially frustrating, because, in general, it’s not possible to know which form 2 \nshould be used. For example, suppose you’d like to create an object of a n arbitrary 3 \ntype from an arbitrary number of arguments. A variadic template makes this co n-4 \nceptually straightforward: 5 \ntemplate<typename T,                // type of object to create 6 \n         typename... Args>          // types of arguments to use 7 \nvoid doSomeWork(const T& obj, Args&&... args) 8 \n{ 9 \n  create local T object from args... 10 \n  … 11 \n} 12 \nThere are two ways to turn the line of pseudocode into real code  (see Item 27 for 13 \ninformation about std::forward): 14 \nT localObject(std::forward<Args>(args)...);    // using parens 15 \nT localObject{std::forward<Args>(args)...};    // using braces 16 \nSo consider this calling code: 17 \nstd::vector<int> v; 18 \n… 19 \ndoSomeWork(v, 10, 20); 20 \nIf doSomeWork uses parentheses when creating localObject, the result is a 21 \nstd::vector with 10 elements. If doSomeWork uses braces, the result is a 22 \nstd::vector with 2 elements. Which is correct? The author of doSomeWork can’t 23 \nknow. Only the caller can.  24 \nThis is precisely the problem faced by the Standard Library functions 25 \nstd::make_unique and std::make_shared (see Item 23). These functions r e-26 \nsolve the problem by internally using parentheses and documenting this decision 27 \nas part of their interfaces. This is not the only way of dealing with the issue , how-28 \never. Alternative designs permit callers to determine whether parentheses  or 29\n\n#### 第 67 页原文\n\nPage 67 \n \nbraces should be used in functions generated from a template. A common compo-1 \nnent of such designs is tag dispatch, which is described in Item 29.† 2 \nThings to Remember 3 \n Braced initialization is the most widely applicable initialization syntax, it pr e-4 \nvents narrowing conversions, and it’s immune to C++’s most vexing parse. 5 \n As detailed in  Item 2, braced initializers yield std::initializer_lists for 6 \nauto-declared objects. 7 \n During constructor overload resolution, braced initializers are matched to 8 \nstd::initializer_list parameters, even if other construct ors offer see m-9 \ningly better matches. 10 \n An example of where the choice between parentheses and braces can make a 11 \nsignificant difference is creating a std::vector with two arguments. 12 \n Choosing between parentheses and braces for object creation inside templates 13 \ncan be challenging. 14 \n Item 8: Prefer nullptr to 0 and NULL. 15 \nSo here's the deal: 0 is an int, not a pointer. If C++ finds itself looking at 0 in a con-16 \ntext where only a pointer can be used, it'll grudgingly interpret 0 as a null pointer, 17 \nbut that’s a fallback position. C++’s primary policy is that 0 is an int, not a pointer. 18 \nPractically speaking, the same is true of NULL. There is some uncertainty in the de-19 \ntails in NULL’s case, because implementations are allowed to give NULL an integral 20 \ntype other th an int (e.g., long).* That’s not common, but it doesn’t really matter, 21 \nbecause the issue here isn’t the exact type of NULL, it’s that neither 0 nor NULL has 22 \na pointer type.  23 \n                                                             \n† The treatment in Item 29 is general. For an example of how it can be specifically applied \nto functions like doSomeWork, see the 5 June 2013 entry of  Andrzej's C++ blog , “Intuitive \ninterface — Part I.” \n* In C, NULL’s type can be void*, but this has never been legal in C++.\n\n#### 第 68 页原文\n\nPage 68 \n \nIn C++98, the primary implication of this was that overloading on pointer and i n-1 \ntegral types could lead to surprises. Passing 0 or NULL to such overloads never 2 \ncalled a pointer overload: 3 \nvoid f(int);        // three overloads of f 4 \nvoid f(bool); 5 \nvoid f(void*); 6 \nf(0);               // calls f(int) overload, not f(void*) 7 \nf(NULL);            // might not compile, but typically calls 8 \n                    // f(int) overload. Never calls f(void*) 9 \nThe uncertainty regarding the behavior of f(NULL) is a reflection of the leeway 10 \ngranted to implementations regarding the type of NULL. If NULL is defi ned to be, 11 \nsay, 0L (i.e., 0 as a long), the call is ambiguous, because conversion from long to 12 \nint, long to bool, and 0L to void* are considered equally good. The interesting 13 \nthing about that call is the contradiction between the apparent meaning of the 14 \nsource code (“I’m calling f with NULL—the null pointer”) and its actual meaning 15 \n(“I’m calling f with some kind of integer—not the null pointer”).  This counterintu-16 \nitive behavior is what led to the guideline for C++98 programmers to avoid ove r-17 \nloading on point er and integral types. Th at guideline remains valid in C++11, b e-18 \ncause, the advice of this Item notwithstanding, it’s likely that some developers will 19 \ncontinue to use 0 and NULL, even though nullptr is a better choice. 20 \nnullptr’s advantage is that it doesn’t have an integral type. To be honest, it 21 \ndoesn’t have a pointer type, either, but you can think of it as a pointer of all types. 22 \nnullptr’s actual type is std::nullptr_t, and, in a wonderfully circular defin i-23 \ntion, std::nullptr_t is defined to  be the type of nullptr. The type 24 \nstd::nulltpr_t implicitly converts to all raw pointer types, and that’s what 25 \nmakes nullptr act as if it were a pointer of all types. 26 \nCalling the overloaded function f with nullptr calls the void* overload (i.e., the 27 \npointer overload), because nullptr can’t be viewed as anything integral: 28 \nf(nullptr);         // calls f(void*) overload 29\n\n#### 第 69 页原文\n\nPage 69 \n \nUsing nullptr instead of 0 or NULL thus avoids overload resolution surprises, but 1 \nthat’s not its only advantage. It can also improve code clar ity, especially when au-2 \nto variables are involved. For example, suppose you encounter this in a code base: 3 \nauto result = findRecord( /* arguments */ ); 4 \nif (result == 0) { 5 \n  …  6 \n} 7 \nIf you don’t happen to know (or be able to easily find out) what findRecord re-8 \nturns, it may not be clear whether result is a pointer type or an integral type . Af-9 \nter all, 0 (what result is tested against) could go either way. If you see the follow-10 \ning, on the other hand,  11 \nauto result = findRecord( /* arguments */ ); 12 \nif (result == nullptr) { 13 \n  …  14 \n} 15 \nthere’s no ambiguity: result must be a pointer type. 16 \nWhere nullptr truly shines is when templates enter the picture. Suppose you 17 \nhave some functions that should be called only when the appropriate mutex has 18 \nbeen locked. Each function takes a different kind of pointer: 19 \nint    f1(std::shared_ptr<Widget> spw);  // call these only when  20 \ndouble f2(std::unique_ptr<Widget> upw);  // the appropriate 21 \nbool   f3(Widget* pw);                   // mutex is locked 22 \nCalling code that wants to pass null pointers could look like this: 23 \nstd::mutex f1m, f2m, f3m;         // mutexes for f1, f2, and f3 24 \nusing MuxGuard =                  // C++11 typedef; see Item 9 25 \n  std::lock_guard<std::mutex>; 26 \n… 27 \n{ 28 \n  MuxGuard g(f1m);            // lock mutex for f1 29 \n  auto result = f1(0);        // pass 0 as null ptr to f1 30 \n}                             // unlock mutex 31 \n… 32\n\n#### 第 70 页原文\n\nPage 70 \n \n{ 1 \n  MuxGuard g(f2m);            // lock mutex for f2 2 \n  auto result = f2(NULL);     // pass NULL as null ptr to f2 3 \n}                             // unlock mutex 4 \n… 5 \n{ 6 \n  MuxGuard g(f3m);            // lock mutex for f3 7 \n  auto result = f3(nullptr);  // pass nullptr as null ptr to f3 8 \n}                             // unlock mutex 9 \nThe failure to use nullptr in the first two calls in this code is sad, but the code 10 \nworks, and that counts for something. However, the repeated pattern in the calling 11 \ncode—lock mutex, call function, unlock mutex —is more than  sad. It’s disturbing . 12 \nThis kind of source code duplication is one of the things that templates are d e-13 \nsigned to avoid, so let’s templatize the pattern: 14 \ntemplate<typename FuncType, 15 \n         typename MuxType, 16 \n         typename PtrType> 17 \nauto lockAndCall(FuncType func, 18 \n                 MuxType& mutex, 19 \n                 PtrType ptr) -> decltype(func(ptr)) 20 \n{ 21 \n  MuxGuard g(mutex); 22 \n  return func(ptr); 23 \n} 24 \nIf the return type of this function ( auto … -> decltype(func(ptr)) has you 25 \nscratching your head, do your head a favor and navigate to  Item 3, which explains 26 \nwhat’s going on.  There you’ll see that in C++14, the return type could be reduced 27 \nto a simple decltype(auto): 28 \ntemplate<typename FuncType, 29 \n         typename MuxType, 30 \n         typename PtrType> 31 \ndecltype(auto) lockAndCall(FuncType func,        // C++14 only 32 \n                           MuxType& mutex, 33 \n                           PtrType ptr)  34 \n{ 35 \n  MuxGuard g(mutex); 36 \n  return func(ptr); 37 \n} 38 \nGiven the lockAndCall template (either version), callers can write code like this: 39\n\n#### 第 71 页原文\n\nPage 71 \n \nauto result1 = lockAndCall(f1, f1m, 0);          // error! 1 \n… 2 \nauto result2 = lockAndCall(f2, f2m, NULL);       // error!  3 \n… 4 \nauto result3 = lockAndCall(f3, f3m, nullptr);    // fine 5 \nWell, they can write it, but, as the comments indicate, in two of the three cases, the 6 \ncode won’t compile.  The problem in the first call is that when 0 is passed to 7 \nlockAndCall, template type deduction kicks in to figure out its type. The type of 0 8 \nis, was, and always will be int, so that’s the type of the parameter ptr inside the 9 \ninstantiation of this call to lockAndCall. Unfortunately, this means that in the call 10 \nto func inside lockAndCall, an int is being passed, and that’s not compatible 11 \nwith the std::shared_ptr<Widget> parameter that f1 expects. The 0 passed in 12 \nthe call to lockAndCall was intended to represent a null pointer, but what actual-13 \nly got passed was a run -of-the-mill int. Trying to pass this int to f1 as a 14 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> is a type erro r. The call to lockAndCall with 0, 15 \nthen, fails, because inside the template, an int is being passed to a function that 16 \nrequires a std::shared_ptr<Widget>. 17 \nThe analysis for the call involving NULL is essentially the same. When NULL is 18 \npassed to lockAndCall, an integral type is deduced for the parameter ptr, and a 19 \ntype error occurs when ptr—an int or int-like type—is passed to f2, which ex-20 \npects to get a std::unique_ptr<Widget>. 21 \nIn contrast, the call involving nullptr has no trouble. When nullptr is passed to 22 \nlockAndCall, the type for ptr is deduced to be std::nullptr_t (i.e., nullptr’s 23 \ntype). When ptr is passed to f3, there’s an implicit conversion from 24 \nstd::nullptr_t to void*, because std::nullptr_t objects implicitly convert 25 \nto all pointer types. (If the ide a of there being object s—plural—of type  26 \nstd::nullptr_t seems odd, I agree, it is. But don’t worry about it. All objects of 27 \ntype std::nullptr_t represent the null pointer, so they all behave the same way. 28 \nYou can create your own such objects if you want to , but given the universal avai l-29 \nability of nullptr, why would you?) 30\n\n#### 第 72 页原文\n\nPage 72 \n \nThe fact that template type deduction deduces the “wrong” types for 0 and NULL 1 \n(i.e., their true types, rather than their fallback meaning as a representation for a 2 \nnull pointer) is the mos t compelling reason to use nullptr instead of 0 or NULL 3 \nwhen you want to refer to a null pointer. With nullptr, templates pose no special 4 \nchallenge. With 0 and NULL, the challenge is essentially insurmountable: templates 5 \nalways deduce the wrong type. Combined with the fact that nullptr doesn’t suffer 6 \nfrom the overload resolution surprises that 0 and NULL are susceptible to, the case 7 \nis iron-clad. When you want to refer to a null pointer, use nullptr, not 0 or NULL. 8 \nAs a coda, it’s worth noting that lockAndCall is a simplified version of a perfect 9 \nforwarding template, and the inability to forward 0 and NULL as null pointers is an 10 \nexample of a situation where perfect forwarding doesn’t work. For details on pe r-11 \nfect forwarding and the conditions under which it breaks down, consult Item 32. 12 \nThings to Remember 13 \n Prefer nullptr to 0 and NULL. 14 \n Avoid overloading on integral and pointer types. 15 \n Item 9: Prefer alias declarations to typedefs. 16 \nI’m confident we can agree that using STL containers is a good idea, and I hope that 17 \nItem 20 convinces you that using std::unique_ptr is a good idea, but my guess 18 \nis that neither of us is fond of writing types like 19 \n“std::unique_ptr<std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string>> ” 20 \nmore than once. Just thinking about it probably increases the risk of  carpal tunnel 21 \nsyndrome.  22 \nAvoiding such medical tragedies is easy. Just introduce a typedef: 23 \ntypedef 24 \n  std::unique_ptr<std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string>> 25 \n  UPtrMapSS; 26 \nBut typedefs are so…C++98. They work in C++11, sure, but C++11 also offers  ali-27 \nas declarations: 28\n\n#### 第 73 页原文\n\nPage 73 \n \nusing UPtrMapSS =  1 \n  std::unique_ptr<std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string>>; 2 \nGiven that the typedef and the alias declaration do exactly the same thing (in the 3 \nStandard, an alias declaration is actually defined to be an alternative way to create 4 \na typedef), it’s reasonable to wonder whether there is a solid technical reason for 5 \npreferring one over the other. 6 \nThere is, but before I get to it, I want to mention tha t many people find the alias 7 \ndeclaration easier to swallow when dealing with types involving function pointers: 8 \n// FP is a synonym for a pointer to a function taking an int and 9 \n// a const std::string& and returning nothing 10 \ntypedef void (*FP)(int, const std::string&);      // typedef 11 \n// same meaning as above 12 \nusing FP = void (*)(int, const std::string&);     // alias 13 \n                                                  // declaration 14 \nOf course, neither form is particularly easy to choke down, and few people spen d 15 \nmuch time dealing with synonyms for function pointer types, anyway, so this is 16 \nhardly a compelling reason to choose alias declarations over typedefs. 17 \nBut a compelling reason does exist: templates. In particular, alias declarations may 18 \nbe templatized (in which case they’re called alias templates), while typedefs can-19 \nnot.  This gives C++11 programmers a clear, straightforward mechanism for e x-20 \npressing things that in C++98 had to be hacked together with typedefs nested 21 \ninside templatized structs. For example, consider defining a synonym for a linked 22 \nlist that uses a custom allocator, MyAlloc.  With an alias template, it’s  a piece of 23 \ncake: 24 \ntemplate<typename T>                           // MyAllocList<T> 25 \nusing MyAllocList = std::list<T, MyAlloc<T>>;  // is synonym for 26 \n                                               // std::list<T, 27 \n                                               //   MyAlloc<T>> 28 \nMyAllocList<Widget> lw;                        // client code 29 \nWith a typedef, you pretty much have to create the cake from scratch: 30 \ntemplate<typename T>                     // MyAllocList<T>::type 31 \nstruct MyAllocList {                     // is synonym for 32\n\n#### 第 74 页原文\n\nPage 74 \n \n  typedef std::list<T, MyAlloc<T>> type; // std::list<T, 1 \n};                                       //   MyAlloc<T>> 2 \nMyAllocList<Widget>::type lw;            // client code 3 \nIt gets worse. If you want to use the typedef inside a template for the purpose of 4 \ncreating a linked list holding objects of a type specified by a template parameter, 5 \nyou have to precede the typedef name with typename: 6 \ntemplate<typename T> 7 \nclass Widget {                         // Widget<T> contains 8 \nprivate:                               // a MyAllocList<T> 9 \n  typename MyAllocList<T>::type list;  // as a data member 10 \n  … 11 \n}; 12 \nHere, MyAllocList<T>::type refers to a type that’s dependent on a template 13 \ntype parameter (T). MyAllocList<T>::type is thus a dependent type, and one of 14 \nC++’s many endearing rules is that the names of dependent types must be prece d-15 \ned by typename. 16 \nIf MyAllocList is defined as an alias template, this need for typename vanishes 17 \n(as does the cumbersome “::type” suffix): 18 \ntemplate<typename T> 19 \nusing MyAllocList = std::list<T, MyAlloc<T>>;  // as before 20 \ntemplate<typename T> 21 \nclass Widget {  22 \nprivate:  23 \n  MyAllocList<T> list;                         // no \"typename\",  24 \n  …                                            // no \"::type\" 25 \n}; 26 \nTo you, MyAllocList<T> (i.e., use of  the alias template) may look just as depen d-27 \nent on the template parameter T as MyhAllocList<T>::type (i.e., use of the 28 \nnested typedef), but you're not a compiler. When compilers process the Widget 29 \ntemplate and encounter the use of MyAllocList<T> (i.e. use of the alias te m-30 \nplate), they know that MyAllocList<T> is the name of a type, because MyAllo-31 \ncList is an alias  template: it must name a type. MyAllocList<T> is thus a non-32 \ndependent type, and a typename specifier is neither required nor permitted. 33\n\n#### 第 75 页原文\n\nPage 75 \n \nWhen compilers see MyAllocList<T>::type (i.e., use of the nested typedef) in 1 \nthe Widget template, on the other hand, th ey can't know for sure that it names a 2 \ntype, because there might be a specialization of MyAllocList that they haven’t yet 3 \nseen where MyAllocList<T>::type refers to something other than a type. That 4 \nsounds crazy, but don’t blame compilers for this possibili ty. It’s the humans who 5 \nhave been known to produce such code.  6 \nFor example, some misguided soul may have concocted something like this: 7 \nclass Wine { … };   8 \ntemplate<>                       // MyAllocList specialization 9 \nclass MyAllocList<Wine> {        // for T is Wine 10 \nprivate: 11 \n  enum class WineType            // see Item 10 for info on 12 \n  { White, Red, Rose };          // \"enum class\" 13 \n  WineType type;                 // in this class, type is 14 \n  …                              // a data member! 15 \n}; 16 \nAs you can see, MyAllocList<Wine>::type doesn’t refer to a type. If Widget 17 \nwere to be instantiated with Wine, MyAllocList<T>::type inside the Widget 18 \ntemplate would refer to a data member, not a type. Inside the Widget template, 19 \nthen, whether MyAllocList<T>::type refers to a type is honestly dependent on 20 \nwhat T is, and that’s why compilers insist on your asserting that it is a type by pr e-21 \nceding it with typename. 22 \nIf you’ve done any template metaprogramming  (TMP), you’ve almost certainly 23 \nbumped up against the need to take tem plate type parameters and create revised 24 \ntypes from them. For example, given some type T, you might want to strip off  any 25 \nconst-or reference-qualifiers that T contains, i.e., you might want to turn const 26 \nstd::string& into std::string. Or you might want to take a type and add 27 \nconst to it or turn it into an lvalue reference, i.e., turn Widget into const Widget 28 \nor into Widget&. (If you haven’t done any TMP, that’s too bad, because if you want 29 \nto be a truly effective C++ programmer, you n eed to be familiar with at least the 30 \nbasics of this facet of C++. You can see examples of TMP in action, including the 31 \nkinds of type transformations I just mentioned, in Items 25 and 29.)  32", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.76–p.100", "pages": "p.76–p.100", "raw_text": "#### 第 76 页原文\n\nPage 76 \n \nC++11 gives you the tools to perform these kinds of transformations in the form of 1 \ntype traits, an assortment of templates insid e the header <type_traits>. There 2 \nare dozens of type traits in that header,  and not all of them perform type transfo r-3 \nmations, but the ones that do offer a predictable interface . Given a type T to which 4 \nyou’d like to apply a transformation, the resulting ty pe is 5 \nstd::transformation<T>::type. For example: 6 \nstd::remove_const<T>::type           // yields T from const T 7 \nstd::remove_reference<T>::type       // yields T from T& and T&& 8 \nstd::add_lvalue_reference<T>::type   // yields T& from T 9 \nThe comments merely summarize what these transformations do, so don’t take 10 \nthem too literally. Before using them on a project, you’d look up the precise spec i-11 \nfications, I know.  12 \nMy motivation here isn’t to give you a tutorial on type traits, anyway. Rather, note 13 \nthat applicat ion of these transformations entails writing “ ::type” at the end of 14 \neach use. If you apply them to a type parameter inside a template (which is virtual-15 \nly always how you employ them in real code), you’d also have to precede each use 16 \nwith typename. The reaso n for both of these syntactic speed bumps is that the 17 \nC++11 type traits are implemented as nested typedefs inside templatized 18 \nstructs. That’s right, they’re implemented using the type synonym technology 19 \nI’ve been trying to convince you is inferior to alias templates! 20 \nThere’s a historical reason for that, but we’ll skip over it (it’s dull, I promise), b e-21 \ncause the Standardization Committee belatedly recognized that alias templates are 22 \nthe better way to go, and they included such templates in C++14 for all the  C++11 23 \ntype transformations. The aliases have a common form: for each C++11 transfo r-24 \nmation std::transformation<T>::type, there’s a corresponding C++14 alias 25 \ntemplate named std::transformation_t. Examples will clarify what I mean: 26 \nstd::remove_const<T>::type           // C++11: const T  T 27 \nstd::remove_const_t<T>               // C++14 equivalent 28 \nstd::remove_reference<T>::type       // C++11: T&/T&&  T 29 \nstd::remove_reference_t<T>           // C++14 equivalent 30\n\n#### 第 77 页原文\n\nPage 77 \n \nstd::add_lvalue_reference<T>::type   // C++11: T  T& 1 \nstd::add_lvalue_reference_t<T>       // C++14 equivalent 2 \nThe C++11 constructs remain valid in C++14, but I don’t know why you’d want to 3 \nuse them. Even if you don’t have access to C++14, writing the alias templates you r-4 \nself is child’s play. Only C++11 la nguage features are required, and even children 5 \ncan mimic a pattern, right? If you happen to have access to an electronic copy of 6 \nthe C++14 Standard, it’s easier still, because all that’s required is some copying and 7 \npasting. Here, I’ll get you started (via copy-and-paste technology): 8 \ntemplate <class T> 9 \nusing remove_const_t = typename remove_const<T>::type;  10 \ntemplate <class T> 11 \nusing remove_reference_t = typename remove_reference<T>::type; 12 \ntemplate <class T> 13 \nusing add_lvalue_reference_t = 14 \n  typename add_lvalue_reference<T>::type;  15 \nSee? Couldn’t be easier. 16 \nThings to Remember 17 \n typedefs don’t support templatization, but alias declarations do. 18 \n Alias templates avoid the “ ::type” suffix and, in templates, the “ typename” 19 \nprefix often required to refer to typedefs. 20 \n C++14 offers alias templates for all the C++11 type traits transformations. 21 \n Item 10: Prefer scoped enums to unscoped enums. 22 \nAs a general rule, declaring a name inside curly braces limits the visibility of that 23 \nname to the scope defined by the braces. Not so for the enumerators declared in C- 24 \nand C++98-style enums. The names of  such enumerators belong to the scope co n-25 \ntaining the enum, and that means that nothing else in that scope may have the 26 \nsame name: 27 \nenum Color { black, white, red };   // black, white, red are 28 \n                                    // in same scope as Color 29 \nbool white = false;                 // error! white already 30 \n                                    // declared in this scope 31\n\n#### 第 78 页原文\n\nPage 78 \n \nThe fact that these  enumerator names leak into  the scope containing their enum 1 \ndefinition gives rise to the  official name  for this kind of enum: unscoped enums. 2 \nTheir C++11 scoped counterparts, scoped enums, don’t leak names in this way: 3 \nenum class Color { black, white, red };  // black, white, red  4 \n                                         // are scoped to Color 5 \nbool white = false;              // fine, no other 6 \n                                 // \"white\" in scope 7 \nColor c = white;                 // error! no enumerator named 8 \n                                 // \"white\" is in this scope 9 \nColor c = Color::white;          // fine 10 \nauto c = Color::white;           // also fine (and in accord 11 \n                                 // with Item 5's advice) 12 \nBecause scoped enums are declared via “ enum class”, scoped enums are some-13 \ntimes referred to as enum classes. 14 \nThe reduction in namespace pollution offered by scoped enums is reason enough to 15 \nprefer them over their unscoped siblings, but scoped enums have a second compel-16 \nling advantage: their enumerators are much more strongly typed. Enumerators for 17 \nunscoped enums implicitly convert  to integral types  (and, from there, to flo ating 18 \npoint types). Semantic travesties such as th e following are therefore perfectly val-19 \nid: 20 \nenum Color { black, white, red };        // unscoped enum 21 \nstd::vector<std::size_t>                 // func. returning 22 \nprimeFactors(std::size_t x);             // prime factors of x 23 \nColor c = red; 24 \n… 25 \nif (c < 14.5) {                // compare Color to double (!) 26 \n  auto factors =               // compute prime factors 27 \n    primeFactors(c);           // of a color (!) 28 \n  … 29 \n} 30 \nThrow a simple “ class” after “ enum”, however, t hus transforming an unscoped 31 \nenum into a scoped one, and it’s a very different story, because there are no implic-32 \nit conversions from enumerators in a scoped enum to any other type: 33\n\n#### 第 79 页原文\n\nPage 79 \n \nenum class Color { black, white, red };  // enum is now scoped 1 \nColor c = Color::red;                    // as before, but 2 \n…                                        // with scope qualifier 3 \nif (c < 14.5) {                // error! can't compare 4 \n                               // Color and double 5 \n  auto factors =               // error! can't pass Color to 6 \n    primeFactors(c);           // function expecting std::size_t            7 \n  …                  8 \n} 9 \nIf you honestly want to perform a conversion from Color to a different type , do 10 \nwhat you always do to twist the type system to your wanton desires: use a cast: 11 \nif (static_cast<double>(c) < 14.5) {       // odd code, but 12 \n                                           // it's valid 13 \n  auto factors =                                // suspect, but 14 \n    primeFactors(static_cast<std::size_t>(c));  // it compiles 15 \n  …  16 \n} 17 \nIt may seem that scoped enums have a third advantage over unscoped enums, be-18 \ncause scoped enums may be forward -declared, i.e., their names may be declared 19 \nwithout specifying their enumerators: 20 \n  enum Color;               // error!  21 \n  enum class Color;         // fine 22 \nThis is misleading. In C++11, unscoped enums may also be forward -declared, but 23 \nonly after a bit of additional work.  24 \nEvery enum in C++ has an integral underlying type that is determined by compilers. 25 \nFor an unscoped enum like Color, 26 \nenum Color { black, white, red }; 27 \ncompilers might choose char as the underlying type, because there are only three 28 \nvalues to represent. On the other hand, some enums may have a range of values 29 \nthat is much larger, e.g.: 30 \nenum Status { good = 0, 31 \n              failed = 1, 32 \n              incomplete = 100, 33\n\n#### 第 80 页原文\n\nPage 80 \n \n              corrupt = 200, 1 \n              indeterminate = 0xFFFFFFFF 2 \n            }; 3 \nHere the values to be represented range from 0 to 0xFFFFFFFF. Except on very 4 \nunusual machines ( where a char consists of at least 32 bits), compilers will have 5 \nto select an integral type larger than char for the  underlying representation of 6 \nStatus values. 7 \nTo make efficient use of memory, compiler writers want to choose the smallest 8 \nunderlying type for each enum that’s sufficient to represent the enum’s range of 9 \nenumerator values. (In some cases, compilers will optimize for speed instead of 10 \nsize, and in that case, they may not choose the smallest permissible underlying 11 \ntype, but they certainly want to be able to optimize for minimal size.) To make that 12 \npossible, C++98  requires that enums be defined (i.e., list all their enumerators) 13 \nwhere they're declared. Having seen the range of enumerator values that must be 14 \nrepresentable, c ompilers are then in a position to choose a suitable underlying 15 \ntype for that enum type. 16 \nBut the inability to forward -declare enums has drawbacks, too. Perhaps the most 17 \nnotable is the increase in compilation dependencies.  Consider again the Status 18 \nenum: 19 \nenum Status { good = 0, 20 \n              failed = 1, 21 \n              incomplete = 100, 22 \n              corrupt = 200, 23 \n              indeterminate = 0xFFFFFFFF 24 \n            }; 25 \nThis is the kind of enum that's likely to be used throughout a system, hence inclu d-26 \ned in a header file that every  part of the system is dependent on.  If a new status 27 \nvalue is then introduced, 28 \nenum Status { good = 0, 29 \n              failed = 1, 30 \n              incomplete = 100, 31 \n              corrupt = 200, 32 \n              audited = 500,              33 \n              indeterminate = 0xFFFFFFFF 34 \n            }; 35\n\n#### 第 81 页原文\n\nPage 81 \n \nit's likely that the entire system will have to be recompiled, even if only a single 1 \nsubsystem—possibly only a single function! —uses the new enumerator. This is 2 \nthe kind of thing that people hate. And it's the kind of thing  that the ability to fo r-3 \nward-declare enums would eliminate. With forward -declared enums, declarations 4 \nfor enum-manipulating functions would be freed of their dependencies on the enum 5 \ndefinitions. For example, here’s a perfectly valid C++11 declaration of a  scoped 6 \nenum and a function that takes one as a parameter: 7 \nenum class Status;                   // forward declaration 8 \nvoid continueProcessing(Status s);   // use of fwd-declared enum 9 \nWith this design, the header containing these  two declarations requires no 10 \nrecompilation if the enumerators in Status are revised. Furthermore, if the 11 \nenumerators in Status are revised (e.g., to add the new enumerator audited), 12 \nbut continueProcessing’s behavior is unaffected (e.g., because continuePro-13 \ncessing doesn’t care about  the audited value), continueProcessing’s 14 \nimplementation need not be recompiled, either. 15 \nBut if code generation requires knowing the size of the enumerators (and it does), 16 \nhow can C++11’s scoped enums get away with forward declarations when C++98’s 17 \nunscoped enums can’t? That’s easy: compilers always know the underlying type for 18 \nscoped enums.  19 \nAll scoped enums have a default underlying type of int: 20 \nenum class Status;                 // underlying type is int 21 \nIf you don’t like the default, you may specify one explicitly: 22 \nenum class Status: std::uint32_t;  // underlying type for  23 \n                                   // Status is std::uint32_t 24 \n                                   // (from <cstdint>) 25 \nEither way, compilers know the size of the enumerators in a scoped enum. 26 \nBut I said that this isn’t really an advantage of scoped enums over unscoped enums. 27 \nThat’s because  C++11 allows you to specify the underlying type for un scoped 28 \nenums, too. When you do, those enums can be forward -declared, just like their 29 \nscoped compatriots: 30\n\n#### 第 82 页原文\n\nPage 82 \n \nenum Color: std::uint8_t;       // fwd decl for unscoped enum; 1 \n                                // underlying type is  2 \n                                // std::uint8_t 3 \nUnderlying type specifications aren’t restricted to enum declarations. You can put 4 \nthem on the definitions, too: 5 \nenum class Status: std::uint32_t { good = 0, 6 \n                                   failed = 1, 7 \n                                   incomplete = 100, 8 \n                                   corrupt = 200, 9 \n                                   audited = 500,              10 \n                                   indeterminate = 0xFFFFFFFF 11 \n                                 }; 12 \nIt should go without saying that the underlying type you specify when you declare 13 \nan enum must match the underlying type you specify when you define the enum. In 14 \ngeneral, mismatches will be diagnosed during compilation, although there are si t-15 \nuations where compilers are unable to do this. As long as you put all your enum 16 \ndeclarations in header files and dutifully #include those header files everywhere 17 \nyou use the enums, you’ll be fine. If you try to cut corners by skipping an #include 18 \nand manually declaring your enum instead, you may not be fine, and the almost 19 \ninvariably resulting overnight debugging ses sions should serve to remind you not 20 \nto commit such crimes against software engineering. 21 \nIn view of the fact that scoped enums avoid namespace pollution and aren’t suscep-22 \ntible to nonsensical implicit type conversions, it may surprise you to hear that 23 \nthere’s at least one situation in C++11 where unscoped enums are sometimes con-24 \nsidered useful. That’s when referr ing to fields within std::tuples. For example, 25 \nsuppose we have a tuple holding values for the name, email address, and reput a-26 \ntion value for a user at a social networking web site: 27 \nusing UserInfo =                 // type alias; see Item 9  28 \n  std::tuple<std::string,        // name 29 \n             std::string,        // email 30 \n             std::size_t> ;      // reputation 31 \nThough the comments indicate what each field of the tuple represents, that’s 32 \nprobably not very helpful when you encounter code like this in a se parate source 33 \nfile: 34\n\n#### 第 83 页原文\n\nPage 83 \n \nUserInfo uInfo;                  // object of tuple type 1 \n… 2 \nauto val = std::get<1>(uInfo);   // get value of field 1 3 \nAs a programmer, you have a lot of stuff to keep track of . Should you really be 4 \nexpected to remember that field 1 corresponds to the user’s email address? I think 5 \nnot. Use of an unscoped enum avoids the need to: 6 \nenum UserInfoFields { uiName, uiEmail, uiReputation }; 7 \nUserInfo uInfo;                        // as before 8 \n… 9 \nauto val = std::get<uiEmail>(uInfo);   // ah, get value of 10 \n                                       // email field 11 \nYou need to use an unscoped enum here, because std::get requires a 12 \nstd::size_t argument, and that means you need to take advantage of the implic-13 \nit conversion from UserInfoFields to std::size_t that unscoped enums sup-14 \nport.  15 \nThe corresponding code with scoped enums is substantially more verbose: 16 \nenum class UserInfoFields { uiName, uiEmail, uiReputation }; 17 \nUserInfo uInfo;                        // as before 18 \n… 19 \nauto val = 20 \n  std::get<static_cast<std::size_t>(UserInfoFields::uiEmail)> 21 \n    (uInfo); 22 \nThe verbosity can be reduced by writing a function that takes an enumerator and 23 \nreturns its corresponding std::size_t value, but it’s a bit tricky. std::get is a 24 \ntemplate, and the value you provide is a template argument (notice the  use of an-25 \ngle brackets, not parentheses), so the function that transforms an enumerator into 26 \na std::size_t has to produce its result during compilation. As Item 14 explains, 27 \nthat means it must be a constexpr function. In fact, it should really  be a con-28 \nstexpr function template, because it should work with any kind of enum: 29 \ntemplate<typename E> 30 \nconstexpr std::size_t toIndex(E enumerator) 31 \n{ 32\n\n#### 第 84 页原文\n\nPage 84 \n \n  return static_cast<std::size_t>(enumerator); 1 \n} 2 \nThis toIndex template permits us to access a field of the tuple this way: 3 \nauto val = std::get<toIndex(UserInfoFields::uiEmail)>(uInfo); 4 \nIt’s still more to write than use of the unscoped enum, of course, but it also avoids 5 \nnamespace pollution and inadvertent conversions involving enumerators. In many 6 \ncases, you may decide that typing a few extra characters is a reasonable price to 7 \npay for the ability to avoid an enum techno logy that dates to a time when the state 8 \nof the art in digital telecommunications was the 2400-baud modem. 9 \nThings to Remember 10 \n C++98-style enums are now known as unscoped enums. 11 \n Enumerators of scoped enums are visible only within the enum. They convert to 12 \nother types only with a cast. 13 \n Both scoped and unscoped enums support specification of the underlying type. 14 \nThe default underlying type for scoped enums is int. Unscoped enums have no 15 \ndefault underlying type. 16 \n Scoped enums may always be forward -declared. Unscoped enums may be fo r-17 \nward declared only if their declaration specifies an underlying type. 18 \n Item 11: Prefer deleted functions to private undefined ones. 19 \nIf you’re providing code to other developers, and you want to prevent th em from 20 \ncalling a particular function, you generally just don’t declare the function. No func-21 \ntion declaration, no function to call. Easy, peasy. But sometimes C++ declares func-22 \ntions for you, and if you want to prevent clients from calling those functions, th e 23 \npeasy isn’t quite so easy any more.  24 \nThis situation arises only for the special member functions , i.e., the member fun c-25 \ntions that C++ automatically generates when they’re needed. Item 19 discusses 26 \nthese functions in detail, but for now, we’ll worry only about the copy constructor 27 \nand the copy assignment operator. This chapter is devoted to common practices in 28 \nC++98 that have been superseded by better practices in C++11, and in C++98, if 29\n\n#### 第 85 页原文\n\nPage 85 \n \nyou want to suppress use of a member function, it’s  almost always the copy co n-1 \nstructor, the assignment operator, or both. 2 \nThe C++98 approach to preventing use of these functions  is to declare the m pri-3 \nvate and to avoid defining them. For example, near the base of the iostreams hier-4 \narchy in the C++ Standard Library is the class template basic_ios. All istream 5 \nand ostream classes inherit (possibly indirectly) from this class. Copying istreams 6 \nand ostreams  is undesirable, because it's not really clear what such operations 7 \nshould do. An istream object, for example, represents a stream of input values, 8 \nsome of which may have already been read, and some of which will potentially be 9 \nread later. If an istream were to be copied, would that entail copying all the values 10 \nthat had already been read as well as all the values that would be read in the f u-11 \nture? The easiest way to deal with such questions is to define them out of exis t-12 \nence. Prohibiting the copying of streams does just that. 13 \nTo render istream and ostream classes uncopyable, basic_ios is specified  in 14 \nC++98 as follows (including the comments): 15 \ntemplate <class charT, class traits = char_traits<charT> > 16 \nclass basic_ios : public ios_base { 17 \npublic: 18 \n  … 19 \nprivate: 20 \n  basic_ios(const basic_ios& );            // not defined 21 \n  basic_ios& operator=(const basic_ios&);  // not defined 22 \n}; 23 \nDeclaring these functions private prevents clients from calling them. Deliberately 24 \nfailing to define them means that if code that still has access to them (i.e., member 25 \nfunctions or friends of the class) uses them, linking will fail due to  missing func-26 \ntion definitions. 27 \nIn C++11, there’s a better way to achieve essentially the same end: use “=delete” 28 \nto mark the  copy constructor and the copy assignment operator as deleted func-29 \ntions. Here’s the same part of basic_ios as it’s specified in C++11: 30 \ntemplate <class charT, class traits = char_traits<charT> > 31 \nclass basic_ios : public ios_base { 32 \npublic: 33\n\n#### 第 86 页原文\n\nPage 86 \n \n  … 1 \n  basic_ios(const basic_ios& ) = delete; 2 \n  basic_ios& operator=(const basic_ios&) = delete; 3 \n  … 4 \n}; 5 \nThe difference between deleting these functions and declaring them private may 6 \nseem more a matter of fashion than anything else , but there’s greater substance 7 \nhere than you might think. Deleted func tions may not be used in any way, so even 8 \ncode that’s in member and friend functions will fail to compile if it  tries to copy 9 \nbasic_ios objects. That’s an improvement over the C++98 behavior, where  such 10 \nimproper usage wouldn’t be diagnosed until link-time. 11 \nBy convention, deleted functions are declared public, not private. There’s a rea-12 \nson for that. When client code tries to use a member function, C++ checks access i-13 \nbility before =delete status. When client code tries to use a deleted private 14 \nfunction, some c ompilers complain only about the function being private, even 15 \nthough the function’s accessibility doesn’t really affect whether it can be used. It’s 16 \nworth bearing this in mind when revising legacy code to replace private-and-17 \nnot-defined member functions wi th deleted ones, because making the new fun c-18 \ntions public will generally result in superior error messages. 19 \nAn important advantage of deleted functions is that  any function may be deleted, 20 \nwhile only member functions may be private. For example, suppose we have a 21 \nnon-member function that takes an integer and returns whether it’s a lucky nu m-22 \nber: 23 \nbool isLucky(int number); 24 \nC++’s C heritage means that pretty much any type that can be viewed as vaguely 25 \nnumerical will implicitly convert to int, but some calls that  would compile might 26 \nnot make sense: 27 \nif (isLucky('a')) …            // is 'a' a lucky number? 28 \nif (isLucky(true)) …           // is \"true\"? 29 \nif (isLucky(3.5)) …            // should we truncate to 3 30 \n                               // before checking for luckiness? 31\n\n#### 第 87 页原文\n\nPage 87 \n \nIf lucky numbers must really be integers, we’d like to prevent calls such as these 1 \nfrom compiling. 2 \nOne way to accomplish that is to create deleted overloads for the types we want to 3 \nfilter out: 4 \nbool isLucky(int number);            // original function 5 \nbool isLucky(char) = delete;         // reject chars 6 \nbool isLucky(bool) = delete;         // reject bools 7 \nbool isLucky(double) = delete;       // reject doubles and 8 \n                                     // floats  9 \n(The comment on the double overload that says that both doubles and floats 10 \nwill be rejected  may initially surprise you, but your surprise will dissipate once 11 \nyou recall that, given a choice between converting a float to an int or to a dou-12 \nble, C++  prefers the conversion to double. Calling isLucky with a float will 13 \ntherefore call the double overload, not the int one. Well, it’ll try to. The fact that 14 \nthat overload is deleted will prevent the call from compiling.) 15 \nAlthough deleted functions can’t be used, they are part of your program. As such, 16 \nthey are taken into account during overload resolution. That’s why, with the delet-17 \ned function declarations above, the undesirable calls to isLucky will be rejected: 18 \nif (isLucky('a')) …           // error! call to deleted function 19 \nif (isLucky(true)) …          // error! 20 \nif (isLucky(3.5f)) …          // error! 21 \nAnother trick that deleted functions can perform (and that private member func-22 \ntions can’t) is to prevent use of template instantiations that should be disabled. For 23 \nexample, suppose you need a template that works with built -in pointers 24 \n(Chapter 4’s advice to prefer smart pointers to raw pointers notwithstanding): 25 \ntemplate<typename T> 26 \nvoid processPointer(T* ptr); 27 \nThere are two special cases  in the world of pointers . One is void* pointers, b e-28 \ncause there is no way to dereference them, to increment or decrement them , etc.. 29 \nThe other is char* pointers, because they typically represent pointers to C -style 30\n\n#### 第 88 页原文\n\nPage 88 \n \nstrings, not pointers to individual characters. These special cases often call for spe-1 \ncial handling, and , in the case of the processPointer template, let’s assume the 2 \nproper handling is to reject calls using those types . That is, it should not be poss i-3 \nble to call processPointer with void* or char* pointers.  4 \nThat’s easily enforced. Just delete those instantiations: 5 \ntemplate<> 6 \nvoid processPointer<void>(void*) = delete; 7 \ntemplate<> 8 \nvoid processPointer<char>(char*) = delete; 9 \nNow, if calling processPointer with a char* is invalid, it’s probably also invalid 10 \nto call it with a const char*, so that instantiation will typically need to be deleted, 11 \ntoo: 12 \ntemplate<> 13 \nvoid processPointer<const char>(const char*) = delete; 14 \nIf you really want to be thorough, you’ll also delete the const volatile char* 15 \noverload, and then you’ll get to work on the overloads for  pointers to the other 16 \nstandard character types: std::wchar_t, std::char16_t, and std::char32_t. 17 \nInterestingly, if you have a function template inside a class, and you’d like to dis a-18 \nble some instantiations by declaring them private (à la classic C++98 conve n-19 \ntion), you can’t, because it’s not possible to give a member function template sp e-20 \ncialization a different access level from that of the main template. If pro-21 \ncessPointer were a member function template inside Widget, for example, and 22 \nwe wanted to disable calls for void* pointers, this would be the C++98 approach, 23 \nthough it would not compile: 24 \nclass Widget { 25 \npublic: 26 \n  … 27 \n  template<typename T> 28 \n  void processPointer(T* ptr) 29 \n  { … } 30 \nprivate: 31 \n  template<>                                 // error! 32 \n  void processPointer<void>(void*); 33\n\n#### 第 89 页原文\n\nPage 89 \n \n}; 1 \nThe problem is that template specializations must be written at namespace scope, 2 \nnot class scope. Th is issue doesn’t arise for deleted functions, because they don’t 3 \nneed a different access level . T hey can be  deleted outside the class (hence at 4 \nnamespace scope): 5 \nclass Widget { 6 \npublic: 7 \n  … 8 \n  template<typename T> 9 \n  void processPointer(T* ptr) 10 \n  { … } 11 \n  … 12 \n}; 13 \ntemplate<>                                          // still 14 \nvoid Widget::processPointer<void>(void*) = delete;  // public, 15 \n                                                    // but 16 \n                                                    // deleted 17 \nThe truth is that the C++98 practice of declaring functions private and not defin-18 \ning them was really an attempt to achieve what C++11’s deleted functions actually 19 \ndo. As an emulation, the C++98 approach is not as good as the real thing. It doesn’t 20 \nwork outside classes, it doesn’t always work inside classes, and when it does work, 21 \nit may not work until link-time. So stick to deleted functions. 22 \nThings to Remember 23 \n Prefer deleted functions to private undefined ones. 24 \n Any function may be deleted, including non -member functions and template 25 \ninstantiations. 26 \n Item 12: Declare overriding functions override. 27 \nThe world of object-oriented programming in C++ revolves around classes, inher-28 \nitance, and virtual functions. Among the most fundamental ideas in this world is 29 \nthat virtual function implementations in derived classes override the implementa-30 \ntions of their base class counterparts. It’s disheartening, then, to realize how easily 31 \nvirtual function overriding can go wrong. It’s almost as if this part of the language 32\n\n#### 第 90 页原文\n\nPage 90 \n \nwere designed with the idea that Murphy’s Law wasn’t just to be obeyed, it was to 1 \nbe honored. 2 \nBecause “overriding” sounds a lot like “overloading ,” yet is completely unrelated, 3 \nlet me make clear that virtual function overriding is what make s it possible to i n-4 \nvoke a derived class function through a base class interface: 5 \nclass Base {  6 \npublic: 7 \n  virtual void doWork();         // base class virtual function 8 \n  … 9 \n}; 10 \nclass Derived: public Base { 11 \npublic: 12 \n  virtual void doWork();         // overrides Base::doWork 13 \n  …                              // (\"virtual\" is optional 14 \n};                               // here\") 15 \nstd::unique_ptr<Base> upb =      // create base class pointer  16 \n  std::make_unique<Derived>();   // to derived class object; 17 \n                                 // see Item 23 for info on 18 \n…                                // std::make_unique 19 \nupb->doWork();                   // call doWork through base 20 \n                                 // class ptr; derived class 21 \n                                 // function is invoked 22 \nFor overriding to occur, several requirements must be met: 23 \n The base class function must be virtual. 24 \n The base and derived function names must be identical  (except in the case of 25 \ndestructors). 26 \n The parameter types of the base and derived functions must be identical. 27 \n The constness of the base and derived functions must be identical. 28 \nTo these constraints, which have been part of C++ since the beginning, C++11 adds 29 \none more: 30 \n The functions ’ reference qualifiers must be identical.  Member function refe r-31 \nence qualifiers are  one of C++11’s less -publicized features, so don’t be su r-32 \nprised if you’ve never heard of them . They make it possible to limit use of a 33\n\n#### 第 91 页原文\n\nPage 91 \n \nmember function to lvalues only or to rvalues only. Member functions need not 1 \nbe virtual to use them: 2 \nclass Widget { 3 \npublic: 4 \n  … 5 \n  void doWork() &;     // this version of doWork applies only  6 \n                       // when *this is an lvalue 7 \n  void doWork() &&;    // this version of doWork applies only 8 \n};                     // when *this is an rvalue 9 \n… 10 \nWidget makeWidget();   // factory function (returns rvalue) 11 \nWidget w;              // normal object (an lvalue) 12 \n… 13 \nw.doWork();              // calls Widget::doWork for lvalues 14 \n                         // (i.e., Widget::doWork &) 15 \nmakeWidget().doWork();   // calls Widget::doWork for rvalues 16 \n                         // (i.e., Widget::doWork &&) 17 \nI’ll say more about member functions with reference qualifiers later, but for 18 \nnow, simply note that if a virtual function in a base class has a reference qual i-19 \nfier, derived class overrides of that function must have exactly the same refer-20 \nence qualifier. If they don’t, the declared functions will still exist in the derived 21 \nclass, but they won’t override anything in the base class. 22 \nAll these requirements for overriding mean that small mistakes can make a big 23 \ndifference. Code with overriding errors is typically valid, but its meaning isn’t what 24 \nyou intended. As a result, you can’t rely on compilers to notify you if you do some-25 \nthing wrong. For example, the following code is completely l egal and, at first sight, 26 \nlooks reasonable, but it contains no virtual function overrides —not a single d e-27 \nrived class function that i s tied to a base class function. Can you identify the pro b-28 \nlem in each case, i.e., why the derived class function doesn't override the base class 29 \nfunction with the same name? 30 \nclass Base { 31 \npublic: 32 \n  virtual void mf1() const; 33 \n  virtual void mf2(int x); 34\n\n#### 第 92 页原文\n\nPage 92 \n \n  virtual void mf3() &; 1 \n  void mf4() const; 2 \n}; 3 \nclass Derived: public Base { 4 \npublic: 5 \n  virtual void mf1(); 6 \n  virtual void mf2(unsigned int x); 7 \n  virtual void mf3() &&; 8 \n  void mf4() const; 9 \n}; 10 \nNeed some help? 11 \n mf1 is declared const in Base, but not in Derived. 12 \n mf2 takes an int in Base, but an unsigned int in Derived. 13 \n mf3 is lvalue-qualified in Base, but rvalue-qualified in Derived. 14 \n mf4 isn’t declared virtual in Base. 15 \nYou may think, “Hey, in practice, these things will elicit compiler warnings, so I 16 \ndon’t need to worry.” Maybe that’s true. But maybe it’s not. With two of the co m-17 \npilers I checked, the code was accepted without complaint, and  that was with all 18 \nwarnings enabled. (Other compilers provided warnings about some—but not all—19 \nof the issues.) 20 \nBecause declaring derived class overrides is important to get right, but easy to get 21 \nwrong, C++11 gives you a way to make explicit that a derived class function is sup-22 \nposed to override a base class version. Just declare it override. Applying this to 23 \nthe example above would yield this derived class: 24 \nclass Derived: public Base { 25 \npublic: 26 \n  virtual void mf1() override; 27 \n  virtual void mf2(unsigned int x) override; 28 \n  virtual void mf3() && override; 29 \n  virtual void mf4() const override; 30 \n}; 31 \nThis won’t compile, of course, because when written this way, compilers will 32 \nkvetch about all the overriding -related problems. That’s exactly what you want, 33 \nand it’s why you should declare all your overriding functions override.  34\n\n#### 第 93 页原文\n\nPage 93 \n \nThe code using override that does compile looks as follows (assuming that the 1 \ngoal is for all functions in Derived to override virtuals in Base):  2 \nclass Base { 3 \npublic: 4 \n  virtual void mf1() const; 5 \n  virtual void mf2(int x); 6 \n  virtual void mf3() &; 7 \n  virtual void mf4() const; 8 \n}; 9 \nclass Derived: public Base { 10 \npublic: 11 \n  virtual void mf1() const override; 12 \n  virtual void mf2(int x) override;   // note revised param type 13 \n  virtual void mf3() & override;     14 \n  void mf4() const override;          // adding \"virtual\" is OK, 15 \n};                                    // but not necessary 16 \nNote that in this example, part of getting things to work involves declaring mf4 vir-17 \ntual in Base. Most overriding -related errors o ccur in derived classes, but it’s po s-18 \nsible for things to be incorrect in base classes, too. 19 \nA policy of using override on all your derived class overrides can do more than 20 \njust enable compilers to tell you when would -be overrides aren’t overriding an y-21 \nthing. It can also help you gauge the ramifications if you’re contemplating changing 22 \nthe signature of a virtual function  in a base class . If derived classes use override 23 \neverywhere, you can just change the signature, recompile your system, see how 24 \nmuch damage you’ve caused (e.g., how many derived classes fail to compile), then 25 \ndecide whether the signature change is worth the trouble. Without override, 26 \nyou’d have to hope you have comprehensive unit tests in place, because, as we’ve 27 \nseen, derived class virtuals th at are supposed to override base class functions, but 28 \ndon’t, need not elicit compiler diagnostics. 29 \nC++ has always had keywords, but C++11 introduces two contextual keyword s, 30 \noverride and final. These keywords have the characteristic that they are re-31 \nserved, but only in certain contexts. In the case of override, it has a reserved 32 \nmeaning only when it occurs  at the end of a member function declaration. That 33 \nmeans that if you have legacy code that already uses the name override, you 34 \ndon’t need to change it for C++11: 35\n\n#### 第 94 页原文\n\nPage 94 \n \nclass Warning {           // potential legacy class from C++98 1 \npublic: 2 \n  … 3 \n  void override();        // legal in both C++98 and C++11 4 \n  …                       // (with the same meaning) 5 \n};  6 \nThat's all there is to say about override, but it's not all there is to say about 7 \nmember function reference qualifiers. I promised I'd provide more information on 8 \nthem later, and it’s now later.  9 \nIf we want to write a function that accepts only lvalue arguments, we declare an 10 \nlvalue reference parameter: 11 \nvoid doSomething(Widget& w);      // accepts only lvalue Widgets 12 \nIf we want to write a function that accepts only rvalue arguments, we declare an 13 \nrvalue reference parameter: 14 \nvoid doSomething(Widget&& w);     // accepts only rvalue Widgets 15 \nMember fu nction reference qualifiers simply make it possible to draw the same 16 \ndistinction for the object on which a member function is invoked, i.e., *this. It’s 17 \nprecisely analogous to the const at the end of a member function declaration, 18 \nwhich indicates that the object on which the member function is invoked (i.e., 19 \n*this) is const. 20 \nThe need for reference -qualified member functions is not common, but it does 21 \narise. For example, suppose our Widget class has a std::vector data member, 22 \nand we offer an accessor function that gives clients direct access to it: 23 \nclass Widget { 24 \npublic: 25 \n  using DataType = std::vector<double>;      // see Item 9 for 26 \n  …                                          // info on \"using\" 27 \n  DataType& data() { return values; } 28 \n  … 29 \nprivate: 30 \n  DataType values; 31 \n}; 32\n\n#### 第 95 页原文\n\nPage 95 \n \nThis is hardly the most encapsulated design that’s seen the light of day, but  set 1 \nthat aside and consider what happens in this client code: 2 \nWidget w; 3 \n… 4 \nauto vals1 = w.data();               // copy w.values into vals1 5 \nThe return  type of Widget::data is an lvalue reference  (a 6 \nstd::vector<double>&, to be precise), and because  lvalue references are d e-7 \nfined to be lvalues, we’re initializing vals1 from an lvalue. vals1 is thus copy -8 \nconstructed from w.values, just as the comment says. 9 \nNow suppose we have a factory function that creates Widgets, 10 \nWidget makeWidget(); 11 \nand we want to initialize a variable with the std::vector inside the Widget re-12 \nturned from makeWidget: 13 \nauto vals2 = makeWidget().data();    // copy values inside the  14 \n                                     // Widget into vals2 15 \nAgain, Widgets::data returns an lvalue referenc e, and, again, the lvalue refe r-16 \nence is an lvalue, so, again, our new object (vals2) is copy-constructed from val-17 \nues inside the Widget. This time, though, the Widget is the temporary object r e-18 \nturned from makeWidget (i.e., an rvalue), so copying the std::vector inside it is 19 \na waste of time. It’d be preferable to move it, but, because data is returning an 20 \nlvalue reference, the rules of C++ require that compilers generate code for a copy. 21 \n(There’s some wiggle room for optimization through what is known as the “as if 22 \nrule,” but you’d be foolish to rely on your compilers finding a way to take a d-23 \nvantage of it.) 24 \nWhat’s needed is a way to specify that when data is invoked on an rvalue Widget, 25 \nthe result should also be an rvalue. Using reference qualifiers to overload data for 26 \nlvalue and rvalue Widgets makes that possible: 27 \nclass Widget { 28 \npublic: 29 \n  using DataType = std::vector<double>; 30 \n  … 31\n\n#### 第 96 页原文\n\nPage 96 \n \n  DataType& data() &                // for lvalue Widgets,  1 \n  { return values; }                // return lvalue 2 \n  DataType data() &&                // for rvalue Widgets, 3 \n  { return std::move(values); }     // return rvalue 4 \n  … 5 \nprivate: 6 \n  DataType values; 7 \n}; 8 \nNotice the differing return types from the data overloads. The lvalue reference  9 \noverload returns an lvalue reference (i.e., an lvalue), and the rvalue reference over-10 \nload returns a  temporary object (i.e.,  an rvalue). This means that client code now 11 \nbehaves as we’d like: 12 \nauto vals1 = w.data();             // calls lvalue overload for 13 \n                                   // Widget::data, copy- 14 \n                                   // constructs vals1 15 \nauto vals2 = makeWidget().data();  // calls rvalue overload for 16 \n                                   // Widget::data, move- 17 \n                                   // constructs vals2 18 \nThis is certainly nice, but don’t let the warm glow of this happy ending distract you 19 \nfrom the true point of this Item. That point is that whenever you declare a function 20 \nin a derived class that’s meant to override a virtual function in a base class, be sure 21 \nto declare that function override. 22 \nThings to Remember 23 \n Declare overriding functions override. 24 \n Member function reference qualifiers make it possible to treat lvalue and rva l-25 \nue objects (*this) differently. 26 \n Item 13: Prefer const_iterators to iterators. 27 \nconst_iterators are the STL equivalent of pointers -to-const. They point to val-28 \nues that  may not be modified. The standard practice of using const whenever 29 \npossible dictates that you should use const_iterators any time you need an i t-30 \nerator, yet have no need to modify what the iterator points to.  31\n\n#### 第 97 页原文\n\nPage 97 \n \nThat’s as true for C++98 as for C++11 and C++14, but in C++98, const_iterators 1 \nhad only halfhearted support. It wasn’t that easy to create them, and once you had 2 \none, the ways you could use it were limited. For example, suppose you want to 3 \nsearch a std::vector<int> for the first occurrence of 1983 (the year “C++” r e-4 \nplaced “C with Classes” as the name of the programming language), then insert the 5 \nvalue 1998 (the year the first C++ Standard was adopted) at that location. If there’s 6 \nno 1983 in the vector, the insertion should go at the end of the vector. Using iter-7 \nators in C++98, that was easy: 8 \nstd::vector<int> values; 9 \n… 10 \nstd::vector<int>::iterator it = 11 \n  std::find(values.begin(),values.end(), 1983); 12 \nvalues.insert(it, 1998); 13 \nBut iterators aren’t really the proper choice here, because this code never modi-14 \nfies what an iterator points to. Revising the code to use const_iterators 15 \nshould be trivial, but in C++98, it was anything but. Here’s one approach that’s 16 \nconceptually sound, though still not correct: 17 \ntypedef std::vector<int>::iterator IterT;             // type- 18 \ntypedef std::vector<int>::const_iterator ConstIterT;  // defs 19 \nstd::vector<int> values; 20 \n… 21 \nConstIterT ci = 22 \n  std::find(static_cast<ConstIterT>(values.begin()),  // cast 23 \n            static_cast<ConstIterT>(values.end()),    // cast 24 \n            1983); 25 \nvalues.insert(static_cast<IterT>(ci), 1998);    // may not 26 \n                                                // compile; see  27 \n                                                // below 28 \nThe typedefs aren’t required, of course, but they make the casts in the code easier 29 \nto write. (If you’re wondering why I’m showing typedefs instead of following the 30 \nadvice of Item 9 to use alias declarations, it’s because this example shows C++98 31 \ncode, and alias declarations are a feature new to C++11.) 32\n\n#### 第 98 页原文\n\nPage 98 \n \nThe casts in the call to std::find are present because values is a non -const 1 \ncontainer and, in C++98, there was no simple way to get a const_iterator from 2 \na non-const container.  The casts aren’t strictly necessary, because it was possible 3 \nto get const_iterators in other ways, † but regardless of how you did it, the 4 \nprocess of getting const_iterators to elements of a non -const container 5 \ninvolved some amount of contorting. 6 \nOnce you had the const_iterators, matters often got worse, because in C++98, 7 \nlocations for insertions (and er asures) could be specified only by iterators. 8 \nconst_iterators weren’t acceptable. That’s why, in the code above, I cast the 9 \nconst_iterator (that I was so careful to get from std::find) into an 10 \niterator: passing a const_iterator to insert wouldn’t compile.  11 \nTo be honest, the code I’ve shown might not compile, either, because there’s no 12 \nportable conversion from a const_iterator to an iterator, not even with a 13 \nstatic_cast. Even the semantic sledgehammer known as  reinterpret_cast 14 \ncan’t do the job. (That’s not a C++98 restriction. It’s true in C++11 and C++14, too.  15 \nconst_iterators simply don’t convert to iterators, no matter how much it 16 \nmight seem like they should. ) There are some portable ways to generate itera-17 \ntors that point where const_iterators do, but they’re not obvious, not 18 \nuniversally applicable, and not worth discussing in this book. Besides, I hope that 19 \nby now my point is clear: const_iterators were so much trouble in C++98, they 20 \nwere rarely worth the bother. At the end of the day, develo pers don’t use const 21 \nwhenever possible, they use it whenever practical, and in C++98, 22 \nconst_iterators just weren’t that practical. 23 \nAll that changed in C++11. Now const_iterators are both easy to get and easy to 24 \nuse. The container member functions cbegin and cend produce 25 \nconst_iterators, even for non -const containers, and STL member functions 26 \nthat use iterators to identify positions (e.g., insert and erase) actually use 27 \n                                                             \n† For example, you could bind values to a reference-to-const variable, then use th at vari-\nable in place of values in your code.\n\n#### 第 99 页原文\n\nPage 99 \n \nconst_iterators. Revising the original C++98 code that uses iterators to use 1 \nconst_iterators in C++11 is truly trivial: 2 \nstd::vector<int> values;                           // as before 3 \n… 4 \nauto it =                                          // use cbegin 5 \n  std::find(values.cbegin(),values.cend(), 1983);  // and cend 6 \nvalues.insert(it, 1998); 7 \nNow that’s code using const_iterators that’s practical! If we decide we’d prefer 8 \nto do a reverse iteration —inserting 1998 at the location of the last occurrence of 9 \n1983 (or, if there are no such occurrences, at the beginning of the container) —the 10 \nproblem is no more challenging, provided we remember that the way to convert a 11 \nreverse iterator to a “normal” iterator is to call its base member function: 12 \nauto it =                                           // use  13 \n  std::find(values.crbegin(),values.crend(), 1983); // crbegin 14 \n                                                    // and crend 15 \nvalues.insert(it.base(), 1998); 16 \nAbout the only situation in which C++11’s support for const_iterators comes 17 \nup a bit short is when you want to write maximally generic library code. Such code 18 \ntakes into account that some containers and container -like data structures offer 19 \nbegin and end (plus cbegin, cend, rbegin, etc.) as non-member functions, rather 20 \nthan members. This is the case for built -in arrays, for example, and it’s also the 21 \ncase for some third-party libraries with interfaces consisting only of free functions. 22 \nMaximally generic code thus uses non-member functions rather than assuming the 23 \nexistence of member versions. 24 \nFor example, we could generalize the code we’ve been working wit h into a 25 \nfindAndInsert template as follows: 26 \ntemplate<typename C, typename V>                              27 \nvoid findAndInsert(C& container,          // in container, 28 \n                   const V& targetVal,    // find 1st occurrence 29 \n                   const V& insertVal)    // of targetVal, then 30 \n{                                         // insert insertVal 31 \n                                          // there   32\n\n#### 第 100 页原文\n\nPage 100 \n \n  auto it = std::find(std::cbegin(container),  // non-mbr cbegin 1 \n                      std::cend(container),    // non-mbr cend 2 \n                      targetVal); 3 \n  container.insert(it, insertVal); 4 \n} 5 \nThis works fine in C++14, but, sadly, not in C++11. Through a n oversight during 6 \nstandardization, C++11 added the non -member functions begin and end, but it 7 \nfailed to add cbegin, cend, rbegin, rend, crbegin, and crend. C++14 rectifies 8 \nthat oversight.  9 \nIf you’re using C++11, you want to write maximally generic code, and none of the 10 \nlibraries you’re using provides the missing templates for non-member cbegin and 11 \nfriends, you can throw your own implementations together with ease. For exa m-12 \nple, here’s an implementation of non-member cbegin: 13 \ntemplate <class C> 14 \nauto cbegin(const C& container)->decltype(std::begin(container)) 15 \n{ 16 \n  return std::begin(container);         // see explanation below 17 \n} 18 \nYou’re surprised to see that non-member cbegin doesn’t call member cbegin, 19 \naren’t you? I was, too. But follow the logic. This cbegin template accepts any type 20 \nof argument  representing a container -like data structure, C, and it accesses this 21 \nargument through its reference-to-const parameter, container. If C is a conven-22 \ntional container type (e.g., a std::vector<int>), container will be a reference 23 \nto a const version of that container (e.g., a const std::vector<int>&). Invoking 24 \nthe non-member begin function (provided by C++11) on a const container yields 25 \na const_iterator, and that iterator is what this template returns. The advantage 26 \nof implementing things this way is that it works even for containers that offer a 27 \nbegin member function (which, for containers, is what C++11’s non -member 28 \nbegin calls), but fail to offer a cbegin member. You can thus use this non-member 29 \ncbegin on containers that directly support only  begin. 30 \nThis template also works if C is a built -in array type. In that cas e, container be-31 \ncomes a reference to a const array. C++11 provides a specialized version of non-32 \nmember begin for arrays that returns a pointer to the array’s first element. (Itera-33", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.101–p.125", "pages": "p.101–p.125", "raw_text": "#### 第 101 页原文\n\nPage 101 \n \ntors into arrays are pointers.) The elements of  a const array are const, so the 1 \npointer that non-member begin returns for a const array is a pointer -to-const, 2 \nand a pointer-to-const is, in fact, a const_iterator for an array. (For insight i n-3 \nto how a template can be specialized for built-in arrays, consult Item 1’s discussion 4 \nof type deduction in templates that take reference parameters to arrays.) 5 \nBut back to basics. The point of this Item is to encoura ge you to use 6 \nconst_iterators whenever you can. The fundamental motivation —using const 7 \nwhenever it’s meaningful—predates C++11, but in C++98, it simply wasn’t pract i-8 \ncal when working with iterators . In C++11, it’s eminently practical, and C++14 t i-9 \ndies up the few bits of unfinished business that C++11 left behind. 10 \nThings to Remember 11 \n Prefer const_iterators to iterators. 12 \n In maximally generic code, prefer non -member versions of begin, end, 13 \nrbegin, etc., over their member function counterparts. 14 \n Item 14: Use constexpr whenever possible. 15 \nIf there were an award for the m ost confusing new word in C++11, constexpr 16 \nwould probably win it. When applied to objects, it’s essentially a beefed-up form of 17 \nconst, but whe n applied to functions, it has  a quite different meaning. Cutting 18 \nthrough the confusion is worth the trouble, because when constexpr corresponds 19 \nto what you want to express, you definitely want to use it. 20 \nConceptually, constexpr indicates a  value that’s not only  constant, its value is 21 \nknown during compilation. Th e concept is only part of the story , though, because 22 \nwhen constexpr is applied to functions , things are more nuanced than this su g-23 \ngests. Lest I ruin the surprise ending, for now I’ll just say that you can’t assume 24 \nthat the results of constexpr functions are const, nor can you take for granted 25 \nthat their values are known during co mpilation. Perhaps most intriguingly, these 26 \nthings are features. It’s good that constexpr functions need not produce results 27 \nthat are const or known during compilation! 28\n\n#### 第 102 页原文\n\nPage 102 \n \nBut let’s be gin with constexpr objects. Such objects are , in fact, const, and they 1 \ndo, in fact, have values that are known at compile -time. (Technically, their values 2 \nare determined during translation, and translation consists not just of compilation 3 \nbut also of linking. Unless you write compilers or linkers for C++, however, this has 4 \nno effect on you, so you can blithely program as if the values of constexpr objects 5 \nwere determined during compilation.)  6 \nValues known during compilation are privileged. They may be placed in read-only 7 \nmemory, for example, and, especially for developers of embedded sys tems, this 8 \ncan be a feature of considerable importance. Of broader applicability is that inte-9 \ngral values that are constant and known during compilation can be used in co n-10 \ntexts where C++ requires  an integral constant expression . Such contexts include  11 \nspecification of  array sizes, integral template arguments (including lengths of  12 \nstd::array objects), enumerator values, alignment specifiers, and more.  If you 13 \nwant to use a va riable for these kinds of things , you certainly want to declare it 14 \nconstexpr, because then compilers will ensure that it has a compile-time value: 15 \nint sz;                             // non-constexpr variable 16 \n… 17 \nconstexpr auto arraySize1 = sz;     // error! sz's value not 18 \n                                    // known at compilation 19 \nstd::array<int, sz> data1;          // error! same problem 20 \nconstexpr auto arraySize2 = 10;     // fine, 10 is a 21 \n                                    // compile-time constant 22 \nstd::array<int, arraySize2> data2;  // fine, arraySize  23 \n                                    // is constexpr 24 \nNote that const doesn’t offer the same guarantee  as constexpr, because const 25 \nobjects need not be initialized with values known during compilation: 26 \nint sz;                             // as before 27 \n… 28 \nconst auto arraySize = sz;          // fine, arraySize is 29 \n                                    // const copy of sz 30 \nstd::array<int, arraySize> data;    // error! arraySize's value 31 \n                                    // not known at compilation  32\n\n#### 第 103 页原文\n\nPage 103 \n \nSimply put, all constexpr objects are const, but not all const objects are con-1 \nstexpr. If you want compilers to guarantee that a variable has a value that can be 2 \nused in contexts requiring compile -time constants, the too l to reach for is con-3 \nstexpr, not const. 4 \nUsage scenarios for constexpr objects become more interesting when con-5 \nstexpr functions are involved . Such functions produce compile -time constants 6 \nwhen they are called with compile -time constants. If they’re called with values not 7 \nknown until runtime, they produce runtime values. This may sound as if you don’t 8 \nknow what they’ll do, but that’s the wrong way to think about it. The right way to 9 \nview it is this: 10 \n constexpr functions can be used in cont exts that demand compile -time con-11 \nstants. If the values of the arguments you pass to a constexpr function in such 12 \na context are known during compilation, the result will be computed  during 13 \ncompilation. If any of the arguments ’ values is not known during com pilation, 14 \nyour code will be rejected. 15 \n When a constexpr function is called with one or more values that are not 16 \nknown during compilation, it acts like a normal function, computing its result 17 \nat runtime. This means you don’t need two functions to perform the same op-18 \neration, one for compile -time constants and one for all other values. The con-19 \nstexpr function does it all.  20 \nSuppose we need a data structure to hold the results of an experiment that can be 21 \nrun in a variety of ways. For example, the lights can be on or off as the experiment 22 \nruns, as can the heater  or the wind machine , etc. If there are n binary conditions 23 \napplicable to the running of the experiment , the number of combinations is 2 n, so 24 \nwe need a data structure with enough room for 2 n values. Assuming each result is 25 \nan int and that n is known (or can be computed) during compilation, a 26 \nstd::array could be a rea sonable data structure choice . But we’d need a way to 27 \ncompute 2 n during compilation.  The C++ Standard Library provides std::pow, 28 \nwhich is the mathematical functionality we need, but, for our purposes, there are 29 \ntwo problems with it. First, std::pow works on floating point types, and we need 30 \nan integral result. Second, std::pow isn’t constexpr (i.e., isn’t guaranteed to r e-31\n\n#### 第 104 页原文\n\nPage 104 \n \nturn a compile-time result when called with compile -time values), so we can’t use 1 \nit to specify the size of a std::array. 2 \nFortunately, we can write the pow we need. I’ll show how to do that in a moment, 3 \nbut first let’s look at how it’d be declared and used: 4 \nconstexpr int pow(int base, int exp)   // pow's a constexpr func 5 \n{ 6 \n  …                                    // impl is below 7 \n} 8 \nconstexpr auto numConds = 5;                 // # of conditions 9 \nstd::array<int, pow(2, numConds)> results;   // results has 10 \n                                             // 2numConds elements 11 \nRecall that the constexpr in front of pow doesn’t say that pow returns a const 12 \nvalue, it says that if base and exp are compile-time constants, pow’s result may be 13 \nused as  a compile -time constant. If base and/or exp are not compile -time co n-14 \nstants, pow’s result will be computed at runtime. That means that pow can not only 15 \nbe called to do things like compile -time-compute the size of a std::array, it can 16 \nalso be called in runtime contexts such as this: 17 \nauto base = readFromDB(\"base\");       // get these values 18 \nauto exp = readFromDB(\"exponent\");    // at runtime 19 \nauto baseToExp = pow(base, exp);      // call pow function 20 \n                                      // at runtime 21 \nBecause constexpr functions must be able to return  compile-time results when 22 \ncalled with compile-time values, restrictions are imposed on their implementation. 23 \nAmong these is that, in C++11, there may be only a single executable statement: a 24 \nreturn.  That sounds m ore limiting than it is, because two tricks can be used to 25 \nextend the expressiveness of  constexpr functions beyond what you might think . 26 \nFirst, the  conditional “?:” operator can be used in place  of if-else statements, 27 \nand second, recursion can be used inst ead of loops. pow can therefore be impl e-28 \nmented like this: 29 \nconstexpr int pow(int base, int exp) 30 \n{ 31 \n  return (exp == 0 ? 1                   // if (exp==0) return 1 32\n\n#### 第 105 页原文\n\nPage 105 \n \n         : base * pow(base, exp - 1));   // else return  1 \n}                                        // base*baseexp-1 2 \nThis works, but it’s hard to imagine that anybody except a hard -core functional 3 \nprogrammer would consider it pretty.  In C++14, the restrictions on constexpr 4 \nfunctions are substantially looser, so the following implementation is val id in 5 \nC++14: 6 \nconstexpr int pow(int base, int exp)            // C++14 only 7 \n{ 8 \n  if (exp == 0) return 1; 9 \n  auto result = base; 10 \n  for (int i = 1; i < exp; ++i) result *= base; 11 \n  return result; 12 \n} 13 \nconstexpr functions are limited to taking and returning literal types, which essen-14 \ntially means types that can have values determined during compilation. All built-in 15 \ntypes qualify, but user -defined types may be literal, too, because constructors and 16 \nother member functions may be constexpr: 17 \nclass Point { 18 \npublic: 19 \n  constexpr Point(double xVal, double yVal) 20 \n  : x(xVal), y(yVal) 21 \n  {} 22 \n  constexpr double xVal() const { return x; } 23 \n  constexpr double yVal() const { return y; } 24 \n  void setX(double newX) { x = newX; } 25 \n  void setY(double newY) { y = newY; } 26 \nprivate: 27 \n  double x, y; 28 \n}; 29 \nHere, the Point constructor can be declared constexpr, because if the arguments 30 \npassed to it are known during compilation, the value of the data members of the 31 \nconstructed Point can also be known during compilation. Points so initialized 32 \ncould thus be constexpr: 33 \nconstexpr Point p1 { 1, 1 };       // fine, \"runs\" constexpr 34 \n                                   // ctor during compilation 35\n\n#### 第 106 页原文\n\nPage 106 \n \nconstexpr Point p2 { 4, 2 };       // also fine 1 \nSimilarly, the getters xVal and yVal can be constexpr, because if they’re invoked 2 \non a Point object with a value known during compilation (e.g., a constexpr 3 \nPoint object), the values of the data members x and y can be known during co m-4 \npilation. That makes it possible to write constexpr functions that call Point’s 5 \ngetters and to initialize constexpr objects with the results of such functions: 6 \nconstexpr Point midpoint(const Point& p1, const Point& p2) 7 \n{ 8 \n  return { (p1.xVal() + p2.xVal()) / 2,    // call constexpr 9 \n           (p1.yVal() + p2.yVal()) / 2 };  // member functions 10 \n} 11 \nconstexpr auto mid = midpoint(p1, p2);     // init constexpr 12 \n                                           // object w/result of 13 \n                                           // constexpr function 14 \nThis is very exciting. It means that the ob ject mid, though its initialization involves 15 \ncalls to constructors, getters, and a non -member function, can be created in read -16 \nonly memory! It means you could use an expression like mid.xVal() * 10 as an 17 \nargument to a template or to specify the value of an enumerator! It means that the 18 \ntraditionally fairly strict line between work done during compilation and work  19 \ndone at runtime begins to blur, and in turn some computations traditionally done 20 \nat runtime can migrate to compile -time. The more code taking part in the migr a-21 \ntion, the faster your software will run. (Compilation may take longer, however.) 22 \nThe setter functions setX and setY can’t be declared constexpr, because, among 23 \nother things, they conta in an assignment, and assignments are not permitted in 24 \nconstexpr functions, not even in C++14.  (For a detailed treatment of the r e-25 \nstrictions on constexpr function implementations, consult your favorite refe r-26 \nences for C++11 and C++14, bearing in mind that C ++11 imposes more constraints 27 \nthan C++14.) 28 \nThe advice of this Item is to use constexpr whenever possible, and by now I hope 29 \nit’s clear why: both constexpr objects and constexpr functions can be employed 30 \nin a wider range of contexts than non -constexpr objects and functions. By using 31 \nconstexpr whenever possible, you maximize the range of situations in which 32 \nyour objects and functions may be used.  33\n\n#### 第 107 页原文\n\nPage 107 \n \nIt’s important to note that constexpr is part of an object’s or function’s interface. 1 \nconstexpr proclaims “I can be used in a context where C++ requires a constant 2 \nexpression.” If you declare an object or function constexpr, clients may use it in 3 \nsuch contexts. If  you later decide that  your use of constexpr was a mistake  and 4 \nyou remove it, you may cause arbitrarily large amounts of client code to stop com-5 \npiling. (The simple act of adding I /O to a function for debugging or performance 6 \ntuning could lead to such a problem, because I /O statements are generally not 7 \npermitted in constexpr functions.) Part of “whenever possible” in “Use con-8 \nstexpr whenever possible” is your willingness to make a long -term commitment 9 \nto the constraints it imposes on the objects and functions you apply it to. 10 \nThings to Remember 11 \n constexpr objects are const and are initialize d with values known during 12 \ncompilation. 13 \n constexpr functions produce compile -time results when called with arg u-14 \nments whose values are known during compilation. 15 \n constexpr objects and functions may be used in a wider range of contexts 16 \nthan non-constexpr objects and functions. 17 \n constexpr is part of an object’s or function’s interface. 18 \n Item 15: Make const member functions thread-safe. 19 \nIf we’re working in a mathematical domain, we might find it convenient to have a 20 \nclass representing polynomials. Within this class, it woul d probably be useful to 21 \nhave a function to compute the root(s) of the polynomial, i.e., values where the 22 \npolynomial evaluates to zero. Such a function would not modify the polynomial, so 23 \nit’d be natural to declare it const: 24 \nclass Polynomial {    25 \npublic:  26 \n  using RootsType =          // data structure holding values 27 \n    std::vector<double>;     // where polynomial evals to zero                                                                                     28 \n  …                          // (see Item 9 for info on \"using\") 29 \n  RootsType roots() const; 30 \n  … 31\n\n#### 第 108 页原文\n\nPage 108 \n \n}; 1 \nComputing the roots of a polynomial can be expensive, so we don’t want to do it if 2 \nwe don’t have to. And if we do have to do it, we certainly don’t want to do it more 3 \nthan once. We’ll thus cache the root(s)  of the polynomial  if we have to compute 4 \nthem, and we’ll implement roots to return the cached value. Here’s the basic a p-5 \nproach: 6 \nclass Polynomial { 7 \npublic: 8 \n  using RootsType = std::vector<double>; 9 \n  RootsType roots() const  10 \n  { 11 \n    if (!rootsAreValid) {            // if cache not valid 12 \n     …                               // compute roots, 13 \n                                     // store them in rootVals 14 \n     rootsAreValid = true; 15 \n    } 16 \n    return rootVals; 17 \n  } 18 \nprivate: 19 \n  mutable bool rootsAreValid { false };   // see Item 7 for info 20 \n  mutable RootsType rootVals {};          // on initializers 21 \n}; 22 \nConceptually, roots doesn’t change the Polynomial object on which it operates, 23 \nbut, as part of its caching activity, it may need to modify rootVals and rootsAre-24 \nValid. That’s a classic use case for mutable, and that’s why it’s part of the declara-25 \ntions for these data members. 26 \nImagine now that two threads simultaneously call roots on a Polynomial object: 27 \nPolynomial p; 28 \n 29 \n/*-----  Thread 1  ----- */     /*-------  Thread 2  ------- */ 30 \nauto rootsOfP = p.roots();      auto valsGivingZero = p.roots(); 31 \nThis client code is perfectly reasonable . roots is a const member function, and 32 \nthat means it represents a read operation. Having multiple threads perform a read 33 \noperation without synchronization is safe. At least it’s supposed to be. In this case, 34\n\n#### 第 109 页原文\n\nPage 109 \n \nit’s not, because inside roots, one or both of these threads might try to modify the 1 \ndata members rootsAreValid and rootVals. That mean s that this code could 2 \nhave different threads reading and writing the same memory without synchron i-3 \nzation, and that’s the definition of a data race. This code has undefined behavior. 4 \nBut there’s still nothing wrong with the client code. Clients are supposed to be able 5 \nto rely on simultaneous reads being safe, and declaring a member function const 6 \nis tantamount to proclaiming it a read operation. The notion that const member 7 \nfunctions may be safely invoked without synchronization is so deeply engrained in 8 \nC++11, the Standard Library take s it for granted. If you use any part of the Stan d-9 \nard Library on a type where a const member function can’t safely be called wit h-10 \nout synchronization, your program has undefined behavior. 11 \nThe problem here is that roots is decla red const, but it’s not thread -safe. The 12 \nconst declaration is as correct in C++11 as it would be in C++98 (retrieving the 13 \nroots of a polynomial doesn’t change the value of the  polynomial), so what r e-14 \nquires rectification is the lack of thread safety. 15 \nThe easiest way to address the issue is the usual one: employ a mutex: 16 \nclass Polynomial { 17 \npublic: 18 \n  using RootsType = std::vector<double>; 19 \n  RootsType roots() const  20 \n  { 21 \n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> g(m);     // lock mutex 22 \n    if (!rootsAreValid) {                 // if cache not valid 23 \n     …                                    // compute/store roots 24 \n     rootsAreValid = true; 25 \n    } 26 \n    return rootVals;                      // release mutex 27 \n  } 28 \nprivate: 29 \n  mutable std::mutex m; 30 \n  mutable bool rootsAreValid { false }; 31 \n  mutable RootsType rootVals {}; 32 \n}; 33\n\n#### 第 110 页原文\n\nPage 110 \n \nThe std::mutex, m, is declared mutable, because locking and unlocking it are 1 \nnon-const member functions, and within roots (a const member function), m 2 \nwould otherwise be considered a const object. 3 \nIt’s hard to go wrong using a mutex in this kind of context, but sometimes a mutex 4 \nis overkill. For example, if all you’re doing is counting how many times a member 5 \nfunction is called, an atomic variable will often  be a less expensive way to go. 6 \n(Whether it actually is less expensive depends on the hardware you’re running on 7 \nand the implementation of mutexes in your Standard Library.) Here’s how you can 8 \ncount calls using an atomic variable: 9 \nclass Point {                              // 2D point 10 \npublic: 11 \n  … 12 \n  double distanceFromOrigin() const 13 \n  { 14 \n    ++callCount;                           // atomic increment 15 \n    return std::sqrt((x * x) + (y * y)); 16 \n  } 17 \nprivate: 18 \n  mutable std::atomic<unsigned> callCount { 0 }; 19 \n  double x, y; 20 \n}; 21 \nAll operations on atomic types in the Standard Library are guaranteed to be atomic 22 \n(i.e., indivisible as observed by other threads), so even though the increment of 23 \ncallCount is a read-modify-write operation, you can rely on it proceeding atomi-24 \ncally. 25 \nIn view of the fact that operations on atomic variables are often less expensive 26 \nthan mutex acquisition and release, you may be tempted to lean on atomic varia-27 \nbles more heavily than you should. For example, in a class caching an expensive-28 \nto-compute int, you might try to use a pair of atomic variables instead of a mutex: 29 \nclass Widget { 30 \npublic: 31 \n  … 32 \n  int magicValue() const  33 \n  {  34\n\n#### 第 111 页原文\n\nPage 111 \n \n    if (cacheValid) return cachedValue; 1 \n    else { 2 \n      auto val1 = expensiveComputation1(); 3 \n      auto val2 = expensiveComputation2(); 4 \n      cachedValue = val1 + val2;               // uh oh, part 1 5 \n      cacheValid = true;                       // uh oh, part 2 6 \n      return cachedValue; 7 \n    } 8 \n  } 9 \nprivate: 10 \n  mutable std::atomic<bool> cacheValid { false }; 11 \n  mutable std::atomic<int> cachedValue; 12 \n}; 13 \nThis will work, but sometimes it will work a lot harder than it should. Consider: 14 \n A thread calls Widget::magicValue, sees cacheValid as false, performs 15 \nthe two expensive computations and assigns their sum to cachedValue. 16 \n At that point, a second thread calls Widget::magicValue, also sees ca-17 \ncheValid as false and thus carries out  the same expensive computations 18 \nthat the first thread has just finished performing.  (This “second thread” may in 19 \nfact be several other threads.) 20 \nSuch behavior is contrary to the goal  of caching. Reversing the order of the as-21 \nsignments to cachedValue and CacheValid eliminates that problem, but the r e-22 \nsult is even worse: 23 \nclass Widget { 24 \npublic: 25 \n  … 26 \n  int magicValue() const  27 \n  {  28 \n    if (cacheValid) return cachedValue; 29 \n    else { 30 \n      auto val1 = expensiveComputation1(); 31 \n      auto val2 = expensiveComputation2(); 32 \n      cacheValid = true;                        // uh oh, part 1 33 \n      return cachedValue = val1 + val2;         // uh oh, part 2 34 \n    } 35 \n  } 36 \n  … 37 \n}; 38\n\n#### 第 112 页原文\n\nPage 112 \n \nImagine that cacheValid is false, and then: 1 \n One thread calls Widget::magicValue and executes through the point where 2 \ncacheValid is set to true. 3 \n At that moment, a second thread  calls Widget::magicValue and checks ca-4 \ncheValid. Seeing it true, the thread returns cachedValue, even though the 5 \nfirst thread has not yet made an assignment to it.  The returned value is ther e-6 \nfore incorrect. 7 \nThere’s a lesson here. F or a single variable or memory location requiring synchr o-8 \nnization, use of a std::atomic is often adequate, but once you get to two or more 9 \nvariables or memory locations (e.g., distinct elements of a data structure) that r e-10 \nquire manipulation as a unit , you should reach for a mutex. For Widg-11 \net::magicValue, that would look like this: 12 \nclass Widget { 13 \npublic: 14 \n  … 15 \n  int magicValue() const 16 \n  {   17 \n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard(m);   // lock m 18 \n    if (cacheValid) return cachedValue; 19 \n    else { 20 \n      auto val1 = expensiveComputation1(); 21 \n      auto val2 = expensiveComputation2(); 22 \n      cachedValue = val1 + val2; 23 \n      cacheValid = true; 24 \n      return cachedValue; 25 \n    } 26 \n  }                                         // unlock m 27 \n  … 28 \nprivate: 29 \n  mutable std::mutex m; 30 \n  mutable int cachedValue;                  // no longer atomic 31 \n  mutable bool cacheValid { false };        // no longer atomic 32 \n}; 33 \nRegardless of how you make your const member functions thread-safe, it’s essen-34 \ntial that you do. Callers of const member functions have a right to assume that 35 \ntheir calls will succeed without their performing any synchronization. Implemen-36\n\n#### 第 113 页原文\n\nPage 113 \n \ntations of const member functions achieve thread safety either by being bitwise 1 \nconst (i.e., not modifying any bits making up the value of the object) or by using 2 \nsome kind of internal synchronization, e.g., a mutex or an atomic variable. 3 \nThings to Remember 4 \n Make const member functions thread safe. 5 \n Use of atomic variables may offer better performance than a mutex, but they’re 6 \ngenerally only suited for manipulation of a single variable or memory location. 7 \n Item 16: Declare functions noexcept whenever possible. 8 \nIn C++98, exception specifications were rather temperamental creatures. You had 9 \nto summarize the  exception types a function might emit , so i f the function’s im-10 \nplementation was modified, the exception specificat ion might need revision, too. 11 \nChanging an exception specification could break client code, because callers might 12 \nbe dependent on the original exception specification . Compilers typically offered 13 \nno help in maintaining consistency among function implementat ions, exception 14 \nspecifications, and client code. Most programmers ultimately decided that C++98 15 \nexception specifications weren’t worth the trouble.  16 \nInterest in the idea of exception specifications remained strong, however, and as 17 \nwork on C++  progressed, a consensus emerged that the truly meaningful info r-18 \nmation about a function’s exception -emitting behavior was whether it had any. 19 \nBlack or white , either a function might emit an exception or it guaranteed that it 20 \nwouldn’t. This maybe-or-never dichotomy forms  the basis of C++11’s exception 21 \nspecifications, which essentially replace C++98’s. (C++98-style exception specifica-22 \ntions remain valid, but they’re deprecated.) In C++11, noexcept is for functions 23 \nthat guarantee they won’t emit an exception.  24 \nWhether a function should be so declared is fundamentally a matter of interface 25 \ndesign. The exception-emitting behavior of a function is of key interest to  clients. 26 \nCallers can query a function’s noexcept status, and the results of such a query can 27 \naffect the exception safety or efficiency of the calling code. As such, whether a func-28 \ntion is noexcept is as important a piece of information as whether a member func-29\n\n#### 第 114 页原文\n\nPage 114 \n \ntion is const. Failure to declare a function noexcept when you know that it will 1 \nnever emit an exception is simply poor interface specification. 2 \nBut there’s an additional incentive to apply noexcept to functions that won’t pr o-3 \nduce exceptions : it permits compilers to generate better object  code. To under-4 \nstand why, it helps to examine the difference between the C++98 and C++11 ways 5 \nof saying that a function won’t emit exceptions. Consider a function f that promis-6 \nes callers they’ll never receive an exception. The two ways of expressing that are: 7 \nint f(int x) throw();     // no exceptions from f: C++98 style 8 \nint f(int x) noexcept;    // no exceptions from f: C++11 style 9 \nIf, at runtime,  an exception leaves f, f’s exception specification is violated. With 10 \nthe C++98 approach, the call stack is unwound to f’s caller, and, after some actions 11 \nnot relevant here, program execution is terminated.  With the C++11 approach, 12 \nruntime behavior is a bit different: the stack is only possibly unwound before pro-13 \ngram execution is terminated. 14 \nThe difference between unwinding the call stack and possibly unwinding it has a 15 \nsurprisingly large impact on code generation. In a noexcept function, optimizers 16 \nneed not  keep the runtime stack in an unwindable state if an exception would 17 \npropagate out of the function, nor must they ensure that objects in a noexcept 18 \nfunction are destroyed in  the inverse order of construction should an exception 19 \nleave the function. The result is more opportunities for optimization, not only 20 \nwithin the body of a noexcept function, but also at sites where the function  is 21 \ncalled. Such flexibility is present only for noexcept functions. Functions with 22 \n“throw()” exception specifications lack it, as do functions with no exception speci-23 \nfication at all. The situation can be summarized this way: 24 \nRetType function(params) noexcept;     // most optimizable 25 \nRetType function(params) throw();      // less optimizable 26 \nRetType function(params);              // less optimizable 27 \nThis alone should provide sufficient motivation to declare functions noexcept 28 \nwhenever you can.  29\n\n#### 第 115 页原文\n\nPage 115 \n \nFor some functions, the case is even stronger. The move o perations are the 1 \npreeminent example.  Suppose you have a C++98 code base making use of 2 \nstd::vectors of Widgets. Widgets are added to the std::vectors from time to 3 \ntime, perhaps via push_back: 4 \nstd::vector<Widget> vw; 5 \n… 6 \nWidget w; 7 \n…                        // work with w 8 \nvw.push_back(w);         // add w to vw 9 \n… 10 \nAssume this code works fine, and you have no interest in modifying it for C++11. 11 \nHowever, you do want to take advantage of the fact that C++11’s move semantics 12 \ncan improve the performance of legacy code when move -enabled types are i n-13 \nvolved. You therefore ensure that Widget has move operations, either by writing 14 \nthem yourself or by seeing to it that the conditions for their automatic generation 15 \nare fulfilled (see Item 19).  16 \nWhen a new element is added to a std::vector via push_back, it’s possible that 17 \nthe std::vector lacks space for it, i.e., that the std::vector’s size is equal to its 18 \ncapacity. When that happens, the std::vector allocates a new , larger, chunk of 19 \nmemory to hold its elements, and it transfers the elements from the existing chunk 20 \nof memory to the new one. In C++98, the transfer was accomplished by copying  21 \neach element from the old memory to the new memory, then destroy ing the origi-22 \nnals in the old memory. This approach enabled push_back to offer the strong e x-23 \nception safety guarantee: if an exception was thrown during the copying of the el-24 \nements, the state of the std::vector remained unchanged, because none of the 25 \nelements in the original memory was destroyed until all elements had been su c-26 \ncessfully copied into the new memory. 27 \nIn C++11, a natural optimization would be to replace the copying of std::vector 28 \nelements with moves. Unfortunately, doing this runs the risk of violating 29 \npush_back’s exception safety guarantee. If n elements have been moved from the 30 \nold memory and an exception is thrown moving element n+1, the push_back op-31\n\n#### 第 116 页原文\n\nPage 116 \n \neration can’t run to complet ion. But the original std::vector has been modified: 1 \nn of its elements have been moved from. Restoring their original state may not be 2 \npossible, because attempt ing to move each object back into the original memory 3 \nmay itself yield an exception. 4 \nThis is a s erious problem, because the behavior of legacy code could depend on 5 \npush_back’s strong exception safety guarantee. Therefore, C++11 implementa-6 \ntions can’t silently replace copy operations inside push_back with moves.  They 7 \nmust continue to employ copy operat ions. Unless, that is, it’s known that the move 8 \noperations are guaranteed not to emit exceptions. In that case, replacing element 9 \ncopy operations inside push_back with move operations would be safe, and the 10 \nonly side effect would be improved performance.  11 \nstd::vector::push_back takes advantage of this “move if you can, but copy if 12 \nyou must” strategy, and it’s not the only function in the Standard Library that does. 13 \nOther functions sport ing the strong exception safety guarantee in C++98  (e.g., 14 \nstd::vector::reserve, std::deque::insert, etc.) behave the same way. All 15 \nthese functions replace calls to copy operations in C++98 with calls to move opera-16 \ntions in C++11 if (and only if) the move operations are known to not emit excep-17 \ntions. But how can a function know i f a move operation won’t produce an excep-18 \ntion? The answer is obvious: it checks to see if the operation is  declared noex-19 \ncept.*  20 \nswap functions comprise another case where noexcept is particularly desirable.  21 \nswap is a key component of many STL algorithm  implementations, and it’s co m-22 \nmonly employed in copy assignment operators , too. I ts widespread use renders 23 \nthe optimizations that noexcept affords especially worthwhile. Furthermore, 24 \n                                                             \n* The checking is typically rather roundabout. Functions like std::vector::push_back \ncall std::move_if_noexcept, a variation of std::move that conditionally casts to an \nrvalue (see Item 25), depending on whether the type’s move constructor is noexcept. In \nturn, std::move_if_noexcept calls std::is_nothrow_move_constructible, and the \nvalue of this type trait is set by compilers, based on whether the move constructor has a \nnoexcept (or throw()) designation.\n\n#### 第 117 页原文\n\nPage 117 \n \nwhether swaps in the Standard Library are noexcept is sometimes dependent on 1 \nwhether user-defined swaps are noexcept. For example, the declarations for the 2 \nStandard Library’s swaps for arrays and for std::pair are: 3 \ntemplate <class T, size_t N> 4 \nvoid swap(T (&a)[N], 5 \n          T (&b)[N]) noexcept(noexcept(swap(*a, *b))); 6 \ntemplate <class T1, class T2> 7 \nstruct pair { 8 \n  … 9 \n  void swap(pair& p) noexcept(noexcept(swap(first, p.first)) && 10 \n                              noexcept(swap(second, p.second))); 11 \n  … 12 \n}; 13 \nThese functions are conditionally noexcept: whether they are noexcept depends 14 \non whether the expressions inside the noexcepts are noexcept. Given two arrays 15 \nof Widget, for example, swapping them is noexcept only if swapping individual 16 \nelements from the arrays is noexcept, i.e., if swap for Widget is noexcept. The 17 \nauthor of Widget’s swap thus determines whether swapping arrays of Widget is 18 \nnoexcept. That, in turn, determines whether other swaps, such as the one for  ar-19 \nrays of arrays of Widget, are noexcept. S imilarly, whether swapping two 20 \nstd::pair objects containing Widgets is noexcept depends on whether swap for 21 \nWidgets is noexcept. The fact that swapping higher -level data structures can 22 \ngenerally be noexcept only if swapping their lower -level constituents is noex-23 \ncept is the reason why you should strive to offer noexcept swap functions. 24 \nBy now, I hope you’re excited about the optimization opportunities that noexcept 25 \naffords. Alas, I must temper your enthusiasm . Optimization is important, but co r-26 \nrectness is more important. I noted at the beginning of this Item  that noexcept is 27 \npart of a function’s interface, so you should declare a function noexcept only if 28 \nyou are willing to commit to a noexcept implementation over the long term. If 29 \nyou declare a function noexcept and later regret that decision , your options a re 30 \nbleak. You can remove noexcept from the function’s declaration  (i.e., change its 31 \ninterface), thus running the risk of breaking  client code. You can change the i m-32 \nplementation such that an exception could escape , but keep the original (now i n-33 \ncorrect) exception specification. If you do that, your program will be terminated if 34\n\n#### 第 118 页原文\n\nPage 118 \n \nan exception tries to leave the function. Or you can resign yourself to your existing 1 \nimplementation, abandoning whatever motivat ed your desire  to change the i m-2 \nplementation in the first place. None of these options is appealing. 3 \nThe fact of the matter is that most functions are exception-neutral. Such functions 4 \nthrow no exceptions themselves, but functions they call might emit one. When that 5 \nhappens, the calling function allows the emitted exception to pass  through on its 6 \nway to a  handler further up the call chain . Exception-neutral functions are  never 7 \nnoexcept, because they may emit such “just passing through” exceptions . Most 8 \nfunctions, therefore, quite properly lack the noexcept designation. 9 \nSome functions, however, have natural implementations that emit no exceptions, 10 \nand for a few more—notably the move operations and swap—being noexcept has 11 \nsuch a significant payoff, it’s worth implementing them in a noexcept manner if at 12 \nall possible.† When you can honestly say that a function should never emit exce p-13 \ntions, you should definitely declare it noexcept.  14 \nPlease note that I said some functions have natural noexcept implementations. 15 \nTwisting a function’s implementation to permit a noexcept declaration is the tail 16 \nwagging the dog. Is putting the cart before the horse. Is not seeing the forest for 17 \nthe trees. Is…choose your favorite metaphor. If a straightforward function impl e-18 \nmentation might yield exceptions (e.g., by invoking a funct ion that might throw), 19 \nthe hoops you’ll jump through to hide that from callers (e.g., catching all exce p-20 \ntions and replacing them with status codes or special return values) will not only 21 \ncomplicate your function’s implementation, it will typically complica te code at call 22 \nsites, too (e.g., code there may have to check for status codes or special return va l-23 \n                                                             \n† The prescribed declarations for move operations on containers in the Standard Library \nlack noexcept. However, implement ers are permitted to strengthen exception specific a-\ntions for Standard Library functions, and, in practice, it is common for at least some co n-\ntainer move operations to be declared noexcept. That practice exemplifies this Item’s a d-\nvice. Having found that it’ s possible to write container move operations such that exce p-\ntions never need to be emitted, implementers often declare the operations noexcept, even \nthough the Standard does not require them to do so.\n\n#### 第 119 页原文\n\nPage 119 \n \nues). The runtime cost of those complications (e.g., extra branches, larger functions 1 \nthat put more pressure on instruction caches, etc.) could exceed any speedup 2 \nyou’d hope to achieve via noexcept, plus you’d be saddled with source code that’s 3 \nmore difficult to comprehend and maintain. That’d hardly be exemplary software 4 \nengineering. As a general rule, the only time it makes sense to actively search for a 5 \nnoexcept algorithm is when you’re implementing the move functions or swap. 6 \nTwo more points about noexcept functions are worth mentioning. First, in C++98, 7 \nit was considered bad style to permit the memory deallocation functions (i.e., op-8 \nerator delete and operator delete[]) and destructors to emit exceptions, and 9 \nin C++11, this style rule has been all but upgraded to a language rule. By default, all 10 \nmemory deallocation functions and all destructors—both user -defined and co m-11 \npiler-generated—are implicitly noexcept. There’s thus no need to declare t hem 12 \nnoexcept. (Doing so doesn’t hurt anything, it’s  just unconventional.) The only 13 \ntime a destructor is not implicitly noexcept is when a data member of the class 14 \n(including inherited members and those contained inside other data members) is 15 \nof a type that expressly states that its destructor may emit exceptions ( e.g., d e-16 \nclares it “noexcept(false)”). Such destructors are uncommon. There are none in 17 \nthe Standard Library. 18 \nSecond, let me elaborate  on my earlier observati on that compilers typically offer 19 \nno help in identifying inconsistencies between function implementations and their 20 \nexception specifications. Consider this code, which is perfectly legal: 21 \nvoid setup();           // functions defined elsewhere 22 \nvoid cleanup(); 23 \nvoid doWork() noexcept 24 \n{ 25 \n  setup();              // set up work to be done 26 \n  …                     // do the actual work 27 \n  cleanup();            // perform cleanup actions 28 \n} 29 \nHere, doWork is declared noexcept, even though it calls the non -noexcept func-30 \ntions setup and cleanup. This seems contradictory, but it could be that setup 31 \nand cleanup document that they never emit exceptions, even though they’re not 32\n\n#### 第 120 页原文\n\nPage 120 \n \ndeclared that way. There could be good reasons for their non-noexcept declara-1 \ntions. For example, they might be part of a library written in C.  (Even functions 2 \nfrom the C Standard Library that have been moved into the std namespace lack 3 \nexception specifications, e.g., std::strlen isn’t declared noexcept.) Or they 4 \ncould be part of a C++98 library that decided not to use C++98 exception specifica-5 \ntions and hasn’t yet been revised for C++11. 6 \nBecause there are legitimate reasons for noexcept functions to rely on code lack-7 \ning the noexcept guarantee, C++ permits such code, and compilers generally don’t 8 \nissue warnings about it.  9 \nThings to Remember 10 \n noexcept is part of a function’s interface, so callers may depend on it. 11 \n noexcept functions are more optimizable than non-noexcept functions.  12 \n noexcept is particularly valuable for the move operations and for swap. 13 \n Most functions are exception-neutral rather than noexcept. 14 \n Item 17: Consider pass by value for cheap-to-move parame-15 \nters that are always copied. 16 \nSome functions take parameters that are, at l east under normal circumstances, a l-17 \nways copied. Setters are a good example. Setter functions typically take a value and 18 \nstore it in a data member of an object. For such functions to operate with maximal 19 \nefficiency, they should copy lvalue arguments and move rvalue arguments: 20 \nclass Widget { 21 \npublic: 22 \n  void setName(const std::string& newName)    // take lvalue; 23 \n  { name = newName; }                         // copy it 24 \n  void setName(std::string&& newName)         // take rvalue; 25 \n  { name = std::move(newName); }              // move it 26 \n  … 27 \nprivate: 28 \n  std::string name; 29 \n}; 30\n\n#### 第 121 页原文\n\nPage 121 \n \nThis works, but it requires writing two functions that do essentially the same 1 \nthing. That chafes a bit: two functions to declare, two functions to implement, two 2 \nfunctions to document, two functions to maintain. Ugh.  3 \nFurthermore, there will be two functions in the object code—something you might 4 \ncare about if you're concerned about your program's footprint. In this case, both 5 \nfunctions will probably be inlined, and that's likely to eliminate any  bloat issues 6 \nrelated to the existence of two functions, but if these functions aren't inlined ev e-7 \nrywhere, you really will get two functions in your object code. (Inlining might not 8 \noccur due to the creation of pointers to these functions, calls to these f unctions oc-9 \ncurring inside complicated calling contexts, or building the system with inlining 10 \ndisabled.) 11 \nAn alternative approach is to make setName a template function taking a universal 12 \nreference (see Item 26): 13 \nclass Widget { 14 \npublic: 15 \n  template<typename T> 16 \n  void setName(T&& newName)             // take lvalues and 17 \n  { name = std::forward<T>(newName); }  // rvalues; copy  18 \n                                        // lvalues, move rvalues 19 \n  … 20 \n}; 21 \nThis reduces the source code you have to deal with, though, assuming this function 22 \nis called with both lvalues and rvalues, there will still be two functions in the o b-23 \nject code. (The t emplate will be instantiated differently for lvalues and rvalues.) 24 \nFurthermore, the use of universal references has drawbacks. As Item 32 explains, 25 \nnot all argument types can be passed via universal reference, and, as noted in 26 \nItem 29, if clients pass improper argument types to functions taking  universal ref-27 \nerences, compiler error messages can be, er, challenging.  28 \nWouldn’t it be nice if there were a way to write functions like setName such that 29 \nlvalues were copied, rvalues were moved, there was only one function to deal with 30 \n(in both source and  object code), and the idiosyncrasies of universal references 31 \nwere avoided? As it happens, there is. All you have to do is abandon one of the very 32 \nfirst rules you probably learned as a C++ programmer. That rule was to avoid pass-33\n\n#### 第 122 页原文\n\nPage 122 \n \ning objects by value. For pa rameters like newName in functions like setName, pass 1 \nby value may be exactly what you want. 2 \nBefore we discuss why pass-by-value is probably a good fit for newName and set-3 \nName, let's see how it would be implemented. 4 \nclass Widget { 5 \npublic: 6 \n  void setName(std::string newName)    // take lvalue or  7 \n  { name = std::move(newName); }       // rvalue; move it 8 \n  … 9 \n}; 10 \nThe only non -obvious part of this code is the application of std::move to the pa-11 \nrameter, newName. Typically, std::move is used with rvalue references (see 12 \nItem 27), but in this case, we know that (1) newName is a completely independent 13 \nobject from whatever the caller passed in, so changing newName won't affect cal l-14 \ners and (2) this is the final use of newName, so moving from it won't have any i m-15 \npact on the rest of the function.  16 \nThe fact that there's only one setName function explains how we avoid code dupli-17 \ncation—both in the source file as well as the corresponding object file. We’re not 18 \nusing a universal reference, so this approach doesn't lead to odd failure cases or 19 \nconfounding error messages. The question remaining regards the efficiency of this 20 \ndesign. We're passing by value. Isn't that expensive? 21 \nIn C++98, it was a reasonable bet that it was, because no matter what callers 22 \npassed in, the parameter newName would be created by copy construction . In 23 \nC++11, newName will be copy -constructed only for lvalues. For rvalues , it will be 24 \nmove-constructed. Here, look: 25 \nWidget w; 26 \n… 27 \nstd::string widgetID(\"Bart\"); 28 \nw.setName(widgetID);               // call setName with lvalue 29 \n… 30\n\n#### 第 123 页原文\n\nPage 123 \n \nw.setName(widgetID + \"Jenne\");     // call setName with rvalue 1 \n                                   // (see below) 2 \nIn the first call to setName (when widgetID is passed), the parameter newName is 3 \ninitialized with an lvalue. newName is thus copy-constructed, just like it would be in 4 \nC++98. In the second call, newName is initialized with the std::string object re-5 \nsulting from a call to operator+ for std::string (i.e., the append operation). 6 \nThat object is an rvalue, and newName is therefore move-constructed. 7 \nIn sum, when callers pass lvalues, they're copied into newName, and when callers 8 \npass rvalues, they're moved into newName, just like we want. Neat, huh?  9 \n“It can’t be that simple,” I hear you thinking. “There’s got to be a catch.”  Generally, 10 \nthere’s not, but there are some conditions you need to keep in mind. Doing that 11 \nwill be easier if we recap the three versions of setName we’ve considered: 12 \nclass Widget {                               // Approach 1: 13 \npublic:                                      // overload for 14 \n  void setName(const std::string& newName)   // lvalues and 15 \n  { name = newName; }                        // rvalues 16 \n  void setName(std::string&& newName)  17 \n  { name = std::move(newName); }  18 \n  … 19 \nprivate: 20 \n  std::string name; 21 \n}; 22 \nclass Widget {                               // Approach 2: 23 \npublic:                                      // use universal 24 \n  template<typename T>                       // reference 25 \n  void setName(T&& newName)  26 \n  { name = std::forward<T>(newName); }  27 \n  … 28 \n}; 29 \nclass Widget {                               // Approach 3: 30 \npublic:                                      // pass by value 31 \n  void setName(std::string newName)  32 \n  { name = std::move(newName); } 33 \n  … 34 \n}; 35 \nAnd here are the two calling scenarios we’ve examined: 36\n\n#### 第 124 页原文\n\nPage 124 \n \nWidget w; 1 \n… 2 \nstd::string widgetID(\"Bart\"); 3 \nw.setName(widgetID);                         // pass lvalue 4 \n… 5 \nw.setName(widgetID + \"Jenne\");               // pass rvalue 6 \nNow consider the cost, in terms of copy and move operations, of setting a Widget’s 7 \nname for the two calling scenarios and each of the three setName implementations 8 \nwe’ve discussed: 9 \n Overloading: Regardless of whether an lvalue or an rvalue is passed, the cal l-10 \ner’s argument is bound to a reference called newName. That costs nothing, in 11 \nterms of copy and move operations. In the lvalue overload, newName is copied 12 \ninto Widget::name. In t he rvalue overload, it’s moved. Cost summary: 1 copy 13 \nfor lvalues, 1 move for rvalues. 14 \n Using a universal reference:  As with overloading, the caller’s argument is 15 \nbound to the reference newName. This is a no -cost operation. Due to the use of 16 \nstd::forward (see Item 25) lvalue arguments are copied into Widg-17 \net::name, while rvalue arguments are moved. The cost summary is the same 18 \nas with overloading: 1 copy for lvalues, 1 move for rvalues. This is to be e x-19 \npected. A template taking a universal reference parameter instantiates into 20 \ntwo functions, one taking an lvalue reference  parameter and one taking its 21 \nrvalue reference counterpart. 22 \n Passing by value: Regardless of whether an lvalue or an rvalue is passed, the 23 \nparameter newName must be constructed. If an lvalue is passed, this costs a 24 \ncopy operation. If an rvalue is passed, it typically costs a move. In the body of 25 \nthe function, newName is then unconditionally moved into Widget::name. The 26 \ncost summary is thus 1 copy plus 1 move for lvalues, and 2 moves for rvalues. 27 \nLook again at this Item’s title: 28 \nConsider pass by value for cheap-to-move parameters that are always copied. 29 \nIt’s worded the way it is for a reason. Three reasons, in fact. 30\n\n#### 第 125 页原文\n\nPage 125 \n \nFirst, you should only consider using pass by value. Yes, it requires writing only 1 \none function. Yes, it generates only one function in the object code. Yes, it avoids 2 \nthe interface issues associated with universal references (i.e., failure cases and u n-3 \npleasant error messages). But it h as a higher cost than the alternatives. In partic u-4 \nlar, it costs an extra move for both lvalue and rvalue arguments.  5 \nWhich brings me to the second caveat. Consider pass by value only for cheap-to-6 \nmove parameters. When moves are cheap, the cost of the extra  move is likely to be 7 \nnegligible. But when moves are not cheap, that extra move can incur an expense 8 \nyou can’t afford. Item 31 explains that not all t ypes are cheap to move —not even 9 \nall types in the Standard Library. When moves are not cheap, performing an u n-10 \nnecessary move is analogous to performing an unnecessary copy, and the i m-11 \nportance of avoiding unnecessary copy operations is what lead to the C++98  rule 12 \nabout avoiding pass by value in the first place! 13 \nFinally, you should consider pass by value only for parameters that are always cop-14 \nied. For setter functions (as well as for constructors taking initialization arguments 15 \nfor an object’s data members), t his condition is typically fulfilled, but consider a 16 \nfunction that adds a value to a data structure only if the value satisfies a constraint. 17 \nUsing pass by value, it could be written like this: 18 \nclass Widget { 19 \npublic: 20 \n  bool insert(std::string s) 21 \n  { 22 \n    if ((s.length() >= MinLen) && (s.length() <= MaxLen)) { 23 \n      values.insert(s); 24 \n      return true; 25 \n    } 26 \n    else { 27 \n      return false; 28 \n    } 29 \n  } 30 \n  … 31 \nprivate: 32 \n  std::unordered_set<std::string> values; 33 \n}; 34 \nIn this function, we’ll pay to construct s, even  if it’s not copied, e.g., if the value 35 \npassed in is too short or too long. For example, given this code, 36", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.126–p.150", "pages": "p.126–p.150", "raw_text": "#### 第 126 页原文\n\nPage 126 \n \nWidget w; 1 \n 2 \nstd::string finalGWTWWords(\"Tomorrow is another day\"); 3 \n… 4 \nauto status = w.insert(finalGWTWWords); 5 \nwe'll pay to copy finalGWTWWords, even if its length is less than MinLen or great-6 \ner than MaxLen. (Note that we'll really pay for a copy, not a move, because final-7 \nGWTWWords is an lvalue.) This is hardly an efficiency win. 8 \nEven when you're dealing with a function performing an unconditional copy on a 9 \ntype that's known to be cheap to move, there are times when pass by value may 10 \nnot be a suitable design decision. For code that has to be as fast as possible, avoi d-11 \ning even cheap moves can be important. Besides, it's not always clear how many 12 \nmoves are truly being performed. In our Widget::setName example, pass by va l-13 \nue incurs only a single extra move operation, but suppose that Widget::setName 14 \ncalled Widget::validateName, and this function also passed by value. (Presu m-15 \nably it has a reason for always copyi ng its parameter, e.g., to store it in a data 16 \nstructure of all values it validates.) And suppose that validateName called a third 17 \nfunction that also passed by value…  18 \nYou can see where this is headed. When there are chains of function calls, each of 19 \nwhich employs pass by value because “it costs only one inexpensive move,” the 20 \ncost for the entire chain of calls may not be something you can tolerate. Using by -21 \nreference parameter passing (as is the case with lvalue and rvalue overloads and 22 \nthe use of universal references), chains of calls don’t incur this kind of accumulated 23 \noverhead. 24 \nIf you were paying particularly close attention during this Item, you probably n o-25 \nticed my comment that there’s “generally” no catch to using pass by value, provi d-26 \ned the constraint s we’ve discussed are satisfied, i.e., that moving the parameter 27 \ntype is cheap and that the parameter is unconditionally copied. “Generally?,” you 28 \nprobably wondered. “Generally?!” “What’s up with ‘generally’?!” 29 \nWhat’s up with “generally” is the slicing problem. Pass by value is susceptible to it. 30 \nPass by reference isn’t. This is well-trod C++98 ground, so I won’t dwell on it, but if 31\n\n#### 第 127 页原文\n\nPage 127 \n \nyou have a function that is designed to accept a parameter of a base class type or 1 \nany type derived from it, you don’t want to  declare a pass -by-value parameter of 2 \nthat type, because you’ll “slice off” the derived -class characteristics of any derived 3 \ntype object that may be passed in: 4 \nclass Widget { … };                          // base class 5 \nclass SpecialWidget: public Widget { … };    // derived class 6 \nvoid processWidget(Widget w);   // func for any kind of Widget, 7 \n                                // including derived types; 8 \n…                               // suffers from slicing problem 9 \nSpecialWidget sw; 10 \n… 11 \nprocessWidget(sw);              // processWidget sees a  12 \n                                // Widget, not a SpecialWidget! 13 \nIf you’re not familiar with the slicing problem, search engines and the Internet are 14 \nyour friend; there’s lots of information available . You’ll find that the existence of 15 \nthe slicing problem is another reason (on top of the efficiency hit) why pass by 16 \nvalue has a shady reputation in C++98. There are good reasons why one of the first 17 \nthings you probably learned about C++ programming was to  avoid passing objects 18 \nby value. 19 \nC++11 doesn’t fundamentally change the C++98 wisdom regarding pass by value. 20 \nIn general, pass by value still entails a performance hit you’d prefer to avoid, and 21 \npass by value can still lead to the slicing problem.  What’s new in C++11 is the di s-22 \ntinction between lvalue and rvalue arguments. Declaring functions that take a d-23 \nvantage of move semantics for rvalues requires either writing multiple functions 24 \n(i.e., overloading for lvalues and rvalues) or using universal references,  both of 25 \nwhich have drawbacks. For the special case of cheap -to-move types passed to 26 \nfunctions that always copy them and where slicing is not a concern, pass by value 27 \noffers an easy -to-implement alternative that’s nearly as efficient as its pass -by-28 \nreference competitors, but avoids their disadvantages. 29\n\n#### 第 128 页原文\n\nPage 128 \n \nThings to Remember 1 \n For cheap-to-move parameters that are unconditionally copied, pass by value 2 \nis nearly as efficient as pass by reference, it’s easier to implement, and it can 3 \ngenerate less object code. 4 \n Pass by value is subject to the slicing problem, so it’s typically inappropriate 5 \nfor base class parameter types. 6 \n Item 18: Consider emplacement instead of insertion. 7 \nIf you have a container holding, say, std::strings, it seems logical that when you 8 \nadd a new element via  an insertion function (i.e., insert, push_front,  9 \npush_back, or, for std::forward_list, insert_after), the type of element 10 \nyou'll pass to the function will be std::string. After all, that's what the container 11 \nhas in it.  12 \nLogical though this may be, it’s not always true. Consider this code: 13 \nstd::vector<std::string> vs;         // container of std::string 14 \nvs.push_back(\"xyzzy\");               // add string literal 15 \nHere, the container holds std::strings, but what you have in hand—what you’re 16 \nactually trying t o push_back—is a string literal  (i.e., a sequence of characters i n-17 \nside quotes). A string literal is not a std::string, and that means that the arg u-18 \nment you’re passing to push_back is not of the type held by the container.  19 \npush_back for std::vector is overloaded for lvalues and rvalues as follows: 20 \ntemplate <class T,                           // from the C++11 21 \n          class Allocator = allocator<T> >   // Standard 22 \nclass vector { 23 \npublic: 24 \n  … 25 \n  void push_back(const T& x);                // insert lvalue 26 \n  void push_back(T&& x);                     // insert rvalue 27 \n  … 28 \n}; 29 \nIn the call  30 \nvs.push_back(\"xyzzy\"); 31\n\n#### 第 129 页原文\n\nPage 129 \n \ncompilers see a mismatch between the type of the argument ( const char[6]—1 \nsee Item 1) and the type of the parameter taken by push_back (std::string). 2 \nThey address the mismatch by generating code to create a temporary 3 \nstd::string object from the string literal, and they pass that temporary object to 4 \npush_back. In other words, they treat the call as if it had been written like this: 5 \nvs.push_back(std::string(\"xyzzy\"));  // create temp. std::string 6 \n                                     // and pass it to push_back 7 \nThe code compiles and runs, and everybody go es home happy. Everybody except 8 \nthe performance freaks, that is, because the performance freaks recognize that this 9 \ncode isn’t as efficient as it should be.  10 \nTo create a new element in a container of std::strings, they understand, a 11 \nstd::string constructor is going to have to be called, but the code above doesn’t 12 \ncall just one constructor. It calls two. Furthermore, it tacks on a call to the 13 \nstd::string destructor, which seems not just gratuitous, but downright, well, 14 \ntacky. 15 \nHere’s what happens at runtime in the call to push_back: 16 \n1. A temporary std::string object is created from the string literal, \"xyzzy\". 17 \nThis object has no name; we’ll call it temp. Construction of temp is the first 18 \nstd::string construction. Because it’s a temporary object,  temp is an rvalue. 19 \n2. temp is passed to the rvalue overload for push_back, where it’s bound to the 20 \nrvalue reference parameter, x. A copy of x is then constructed in the memory 21 \nfor the std::vector. This construction —the second one—is what actually 22 \ncreates a new ob ject inside the std::vector. (The constructor that’s used to 23 \ncopy x into the std::vector is the move constructor, because x, being an 24 \nrvalue reference, gets cast to an rvalue before it’s copied. For information 25 \nabout the casting of rvalue reference parameters to rvalues, see Item 27.) 26 \n3. When push_back returns, temp is destroyed, thus calling the std::string 27 \ndestructor. 28 \nThe performance freaks can’t help b ut notice that if there were a way to take the 29 \nstring literal and pass it directly to the code in step 2 that constructs the 30\n\n#### 第 130 页原文\n\nPage 130 \n \nstd::string object inside the std::vector, i.e., that performs the only co n-1 \nstruction that’s actuall y required, we coul d avoid constructing and destroying 2 \ntemp. That would be maximally efficient, and even the performance freaks would 3 \ngo home happy. 4 \nBecause you’re a C++ programmer, there’s an abo ve-average chance you’re a pe r-5 \nformance freak. If you’re not, you’re still probably sympathetic to their point of 6 \nview. (If you’re not at all interested in performance, shouldn’t you be in the Python 7 \nroom down the hall?) So I’m pleased to tell you that ther e is a way to do exactly 8 \nwhat is needed for maximal efficiency in the call to push_back. It’s to not call 9 \npush_back. push_back is the wrong function. The function you’re looking for is 10 \nemplace_back. 11 \nemplace_back does exactly what we want: it uses whatever argument is passed to 12 \nit to construct a new std::string directly inside the std::vector. No temp o-13 \nraries are involved: 14 \nvs.emplace_back(\"xyzzy\");   // construct std::string inside 15 \n                            // vs directly from \"xyzzy\" 16 \nemplace_back uses perfect forwarding, so, as long as you don’t bump into one of 17 \nperfect forwarding’s limitations (see Item 32), you can pass any number of arg u-18 \nments of any combination of types through emplace_back. For example, if you’d 19 \nlike to create a std::string in vs via the std::string constructor taking a 20 \ncharacter and a repeat count, this would do it: 21 \nvs.emplace_back(50, 'x');   // insert std::string consisting 22 \n                            // of 50 'x' characters 23 \nemplace_back is available for every standard container that supports 24 \npush_back. Similarly, every standard container that supports push_front sup-25 \nports emplace_front. And every standard container that supports insert 26 \n(which is all but std::forward_list and std::array) supports emplace. The 27 \nassociative containers offer emplace_hint to complement their insert functions 28 \nthat take a “hint” iterator, and std::forward_list has emplace_after to match 29 \nits insert_after. 30\n\n#### 第 131 页原文\n\nPage 131 \n \nAll emplacement functions work the same way as emplace_back: they perfect -1 \nforward their arguments to the code that creates a new object in the container. All 2 \navoid the cost of type conversions that arise through use of the insertion functions 3 \nwhen the type of the passed-in argument doesn't match the type stored in the con-4 \ntainer. Hence: 5 \nstd::vector<std::string> vs;         // as before 6 \n… 7 \nauto midPoint = vs.begin() + vs.size() / 2; 8 \nvs.insert(midPoint, \"hello\");        // create temp std::string, 9 \n                                     // move temp into vector, 10 \n…                                    // destroy temp 11 \nmidPoint =                           // recompute midpoint 12 \n  vs.begin() + vs.size() / 2; 13 \nvs.emplace(midPoint, \"world\");       // create std::string 14 \n                                     // directly in vector 15 \nOne of the nicest things about the emplacement functions is that when the type 16 \npassed is the same as the type stored in the container (i.e., when there’s no type 17 \nmismatch), they’re no less efficient than calling their insertion counterparts. This 18 \nmeans that you can safely fall into the habit of using the emplacement functions all 19 \nthe time. They’re often more efficient than their insert/push_back/push_front 20 \nsiblings, and they’re never less efficient. 21 \nAt the same time, emplacement functions have a couple o f sharp edges, and if 22 \nyou’re not careful, you can cut yourself. For example, suppose you have a container 23 \nof std::shared_ptr<Widget>s, 24 \nstd::list<std::shared_ptr<Widget>> ptrs; 25 \nand you want to add a std::shared_ptr to a Widget that should be released via 26 \na custom deleter (see  Item 21). Item 23 explains that you should use 27 \nstd::make_shared to create std::shared_ptrs whenever you can, but it also 28 \nconcedes that there are situations where you can’t. One such situation is when you 29 \nwant to specify a custom deleter. In that case, you must call new directly to get the 30 \nraw pointer to be managed by the std::shared_ptr. 31 \nIf the custom deleter is this function, 32\n\n#### 第 132 页原文\n\nPage 132 \n \nvoid killWidget(Widget* pWidget); 1 \nthe code using an insertion function could look like this: 2 \nptrs.push_back(std::shared_ptr<Widget>(new Widget, killWidget)); 3 \nIt could also look like this, though the meaning would be the same: 4 \nptrs.push_back( { new Widget, killWidget } ); 5 \nEither way, a temporary std::shared_ptr would be constructed before c alling 6 \npush_back. push_back’s parameter is a reference to a std::shared_ptr, so 7 \nthere has to be a std::shared_ptr for this parameter to refer to.  8 \nThe creation of the temporary std::shared_ptr is what emplace_back would 9 \navoid, but in this case, that temporary is worth far more than it costs. Consider the 10 \nfollowing potential sequence of events: 11 \n1. In either call above, a temporary std::shared_ptr<Widget> is constructed 12 \nto hold the raw pointer resulting from “new Widget”. Call this object p.  13 \n2. push_back takes p by reference. During allocation of a list node to hold a copy 14 \nof p, an out-of-memory exception gets thrown. 15 \n3. As the exception propagates out of push_back, p is destroyed. Being the sole 16 \nstd::shared_ptr referring to the Widget it’s managing, it automatically r e-17 \nleases that Widget, in this case by calling killWidget. 18 \nEven though an exception occurred, nothing leaks: the Widget created via “new 19 \nWidget” in the call to push_back is released in the destructor of the 20 \nstd::shared_ptr that was created to manage it (p). Life is good. 21 \nBut now consider what happens if emplace_back is called instead of push_back: 22 \nptrs.emplace_back(new Widget, killWidget); 23 \n1. The raw pointer resulting from “new Widget” is perfect-forwarded to the point 24 \ninside push_back where a list node is to be allocated. That allocation fails, and 25 \nan out-of-memory exception is thrown. 26\n\n#### 第 133 页原文\n\nPage 133 \n \n2. As the exception propagates out of push_back, the raw pointer that was the 1 \nonly way to get at the Widget on the heap is lost. That Widget (and any re-2 \nsources it owns) is leaked. 3 \nIn this scenario, life is not good, and the fault doesn’t lie with std::shared_ptr. 4 \nThe same kind of problem can arise through the use of std::unique_ptr with a 5 \ncustom deleter. Fundamentally, the effectiveness of resource-managing classes like 6 \nstd::shared_ptr and std::unique_ptr is predicated on resources (such as 7 \nraw pointers returned from new) being immediately passed to the constructors of 8 \nthe resource -managing objects. The fact that functions like std::make_shared 9 \nand std::make_unique automate this is one of the reasons they’re so important.  10 \nIn calls to the insertion functions of containers holding resource -managing objects 11 \n(e.g., std::list<std::shared_ptr<Widget>>), the functions’ parame ter types 12 \ngenerally ensure that nothing gets between acquisition of a resource (e.g., use of 13 \nnaked new) and construction of the object managing the resource. In the emplac e-14 \nment functions, perfect -forwarding defers the creation of the resource -managing 15 \nobjects until they can be constructed in the container’s memory, and that opens a 16 \nwindow during which exceptions can lead to resource leaks.  When working with 17 \ncontainers of resource-managing objects, you must take care to ensure that if you 18 \nchoose an emplacement function over its insertion counterpart, you’re not paying 19 \nfor improved code efficiency with diminished exception safety. 20 \nThe fundamental difference between insertion and emplacement functions is that 21 \ninsertion functions take objects to be inserted , while emplacement functions take 22 \nconstructor arguments for objects to be inserted. One consequence of this—the one 23 \ndriving this Item—is that emplacement functions can be more efficient, but a se c-24 \nond consequence is that you may get less benefit from explicit constructors 25 \nthan you expect. For example, suppose, in celebration of C++11’s support for reg u-26 \nlar expressions, you create a container of regular expression objects: 27 \nstd::vector<std::regex> regexes; 28 \nDistracted by your colleagues’ quarreling over th e ideal number of times per day 29 \nto check one’s Facebook account, you accidently write the following seemingly 30 \nmeaningless  code: 31\n\n#### 第 134 页原文\n\nPage 134 \n \nregexes.emplace_back(nullptr);    // add nullptr to container 1 \n                                  // of regexes? 2 \nYou don’t notice the error as you type it, and your compilers accept the code wit h-3 \nout complaint, so you end up wasting a bunch of time debugging. At some point, 4 \nyou discover that you appear to have inserted a null pointer into your container of 5 \nregular expressions. But how is that possible? Pointers aren’t regular expressions, 6 \nand if you tried to do something like this, 7 \nstd::regex r = nullptr;           // error! won't compile 8 \ncompilers would reject your code . Interestingly , they would also reject it if you 9 \ncalled push_back instead of emplace_back: 10 \nregexes.push_back(nullptr);       // error! won't compile 11 \nThe curious behavior you’re experiencing stems from the fact that std::regex 12 \nobjects can be constructed from character strings, i.e., from const char* pointers. 13 \nThat’s what makes useful code like this legal: 14 \nstd::regex upperCaseWord(\"[A-Z]+\"); 15 \nHowever, because nullptr implicitly converts to all pointer types, the em-16 \nplace_back call that unexpectedly compiles is treated by compilers more or less 17 \nas if you’d written this: 18 \nregexes.emplace_back(static_cast<const char*>(nullptr)); 19 \nCreation of a std::regex from a character string can exact a comparatively large 20 \nruntime cost, so, to minimize the likelihood that such an expense will be incurred 21 \nunintentionally, the std::regex constructor taking a const char* pointer is ex-22 \nplicit. That’s why these lines don’t compile: 23 \nstd::regex r = nullptr;           // error! won't compile 24 \nregexes.push_back(nullptr);       // error! won't compile 25 \nIn both cases, we’re requesting an implicit convers ion from a pointer to a 26 \nstd::regex, and the explicitness of that constructor prevents such conve r-27 \nsions.  28\n\n#### 第 135 页原文\n\nPage 135 \n \nIn the call to emplace_back, however, we’re not claiming to pass a std::regex 1 \nobject. Instead, we’re passing a constructor argument  for a std::regex object. 2 \nThat’s not considered an implicit conversion request. Rather, it’s viewed as if you’d 3 \nwritten this code: 4 \nstd::regex r(nullptr);           // compiles 5 \nIf the laconic comment “compiles” suggests a lack of enthusiasm, that’s good, b e-6 \ncause this code, tho ugh it will compile, has undefined behavior. The std::regex 7 \nconstructor taking a const char* pointer requires that the pointed-to string com-8 \nprise a valid regular expression, and the null pointer fails that requirement. If you 9 \nwrite and compile such code, t he best you can hope for is that it crashes at 10 \nruntime. If you’re not so lucky, you and your debugger could be in for a special 11 \nbonding experience. 12 \nSetting aside push_back, emplace_back, and bonding for a moment, notice how 13 \nthese very similar initialization syntaxes yield different results: 14 \nstd::regex r1 = nullptr;         // error! won't compile 15 \nstd::regex r2(nullptr);          // compiles 16 \nIn the official terminology of the Standard, there are two kinds of initialization: 17 \ncopy initialization  and direct initialization. The syntax used to initialize r1 (em-18 \nploying the equals-sign) is defined to be copy initialization. The syntax used to in i-19 \ntialize r2 (with the parentheses, although braces may also be used) is defined to be 20 \ndirect initialization. Copy initialization is not permitted to use explicit construc-21 \ntors. Direct initialization is. That's why the line initializing r1 doesn’t compile, but 22 \nthe line initializing r2 does.  23 \nBut back to push_back and emplace_back (and, more generally, the insertion 24 \nfunctions versus the emplacement functions). Insertion functions employ copy in i-25 \ntialization, which means they can’t make use of explicit constructors. Emplace-26 \nment functions use direct initialization, so they can.  Hence: 27 \nregexes.emplace_back(nullptr);  // fine, direct init permits 28 \n                                // use of explicit 29 \n                                // pointerstd::regex ctor 30\n\n#### 第 136 页原文\n\nPage 136 \n \nregexes.push_back(nullptr);     // error! copy init forbids  1 \n                                // use of explicit 2 \n                                // pointerstd::regex ctor 3 \nThe lesson to take away  is that when you use an emplacement function, be esp e-4 \ncially careful to make sure you're passing the correct arguments, because even ex-5 \nplicit constructors will be considered by compilers as  they try to find a way to 6 \ninterpret your code as valid. 7 \nThings to Remember 8 \n Emplacement functions are often more efficient than their insertion counte r-9 \nparts, and they’re never less efficient. 10 \n For containers of resource-managing objects, emplacement functions may suf-11 \nfer resource leaks that would not arise through use of insertion functions. 12 \n Emplacement functions may perform type conversions that would be rejected 13 \nby insertion functions. 14 \n Item 19: Understand special member function generation. 15 \nIn official C++ parlance, the “special member functions” are the ones that  C++ gen-16 \nerates on its own. C++98  has four such functions : the default constructor, the d e-17 \nstructor, the cop y constructor, and the copy assignment operator. There’s fine 18 \nprint, of course. These functions are generated only if they’re needed, i.e., if some 19 \ncode uses them  without their being expressly declared in the class . A default con-20 \nstructor is generated only if the class declares no constructors at all. (This pr e-21 \nvents compilers from creating a default constructor for a class where you’ve spec i-22 \nfied that constructor arguments are required. ) Generated special member fun c-23 \ntions are implicitly inline, and they’re nonvirtual unless the function in question 24 \nis a destructor in a derived class inheriting from a base class with a virtual destruc-25 \ntor. In that case, the compiler-generated destructor is also virtual.  26 \nBut you already know these things . Yes, yes, ancient histo ry: Mesopotamia, the 27 \nShang dynasty, FORTRAN, C++98. But times have changed, and the rules for special 28 \nmember function generation in C++ have changed with them. It’s important to be 29 \naware of the new rules, because few things are as central to effective C++ pro-30\n\n#### 第 137 页原文\n\nPage 137 \n \ngramming as knowing when compilers silently insert member functions into your 1 \nclasses. 2 \nAs of C++11, the special member function s club has two more inductees: the move 3 \nconstructor and the move assignment operator. For a class Widget, t heir sign a-4 \ntures are: 5 \nclass Widget { 6 \npublic: 7 \n  … 8 \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs);              // move constructor 9 \n  Widget& operator=(Widget&& rhs);   // move assignment operator 10 \n  … 11 \n}; 12 \nThe rules governing their generation and behavior are analogous to those for their 13 \ncopying siblings. The move operations are generated only if they’ re needed, and if 14 \nthey are generated, they perform “memberwise moves” on the data members of 15 \nthe class. That means that the move constructor move -constructs each data me m-16 \nber of the class from the cor responding member of its parameter, rhs, and the 17 \nmove assignment operator move -assigns each data member of the class from the 18 \ncorresponding member of its parameter, rhs. Furthermore, the move constructor 19 \nmove-constructs its base class parts (if there are a ny), and the move assignment 20 \noperator move-assigns its base class parts, too. 21 \nNow, when I refer to a move operation move -constructing or move -assigning a 22 \ndata member or base class, there is no guarantee that a move will actually take 23 \nplace. “Memberwise mov es” are, in reality, more like memberwise move requests, 24 \nbecause types that aren’t move-enabled (i.e., that offer no special support for move 25 \noperations, e.g., most C++98 legacy classes) will be “moved” via their copy oper a-26 \ntions. The heart of each memberwi se “move” is application of std::move to the 27 \nsource to be moved from, and the result of this application is used during function 28 \noverload resolution to determine whether a move or a copy should be performed. 29 \nItem 25 covers this process in detail. For this Item, simply remember that a me m-30 \nberwise move consists of move operations on data members and base classes that 31 \nsupport move operations, but for data members and base classes that offer only 32 \ncopy operations, a memberwise “move” entails making a copy. 33\n\n#### 第 138 页原文\n\nPage 138 \n \nAs is the case with the copy operations, the move operations aren’t generated if 1 \nyou declare them yourself . However, the precise conditions under which the y are 2 \ngenerated differ a bit from those for the copy operations. 3 \nThe two copy operations are independent: declaring one doesn’t prevent compi l-4 \ners from generating the other. So if you declare a copy constructor , but no copy 5 \nassignment operator, then write  code that requires copy assignment, compilers 6 \nwill generate the copy assignment operator for you. Similarly, if you declare a copy 7 \nassignment operator, but no copy constructor, yet your code requires copy co n-8 \nstruction, compilers will generate the copy const ructor for you.  That was true in 9 \nC++98, and it’s still true in C++11. 10 \nThe two move operations are not independent. If you declare either, that prevents 11 \ncompilers from generating the other . The rationale is that if you declare, say, a 12 \nmove constructor for your class, you’re indicating that there’s something about 13 \nhow move construction should be implemented that’s different from the default 14 \nmemberwise move that compilers would generate. And if there’s something wrong 15 \nwith memberwise move construction, compilers reason, there’d probably be 16 \nsomething wrong with memberwise move assignment, too. So declaring a move 17 \nconstructor prevents a move assignment operator from being generated, and d e-18 \nclaring a move assign ment operator prevents compilers from generating a move 19 \nconstructor. 20 \nFurthermore, move operations won’t be generated for any class that explicitly d e-21 \nclares a copy operation. The justification is that declaring a copy operation (co n-22 \nstruction or assignment) indicates that the normal approach to copying an object 23 \n(memberwise copy) isn’t appropriate for the class, and compilers figure that if 24 \nmemberwise copy isn’t appropriate for the copy operations, memberwise move 25 \nprobably isn’t appropriate for the move operations.  26 \nThis goes in the other direction, too. Declaring a move operation (construction or 27 \nassignment) in a class prevents compilers from generating copy operations. After 28 \nall, if memberwise move isn’t the proper way to move an object, there’s no reason 29 \nto expect that memberwise copy is the proper way to copy it. This may sound like 30 \nit could break C++98 code, because the conditions under which the copy oper a-31\n\n#### 第 139 页原文\n\nPage 139 \n \ntions are generated are more constrained in C++11 than in C++98, but further r e-1 \nflection reveals that this is not the case. C++98 code can’t have move operations, 2 \nbecause there was no such thing as “moving” objects in C++98. The only way a leg-3 \nacy class can have user-declared move operations is if they were added for C++11, 4 \nand classes that are modified to take advantage of move semantics have to play by 5 \nthe C++11 rules for special member function generation. 6 \nPerhaps you’ve heard of a guideline known as the Rule of Three. The Rule of Three 7 \nemerged fairly early in the C++ era (the early 1990s), and it states that if you d e-8 \nclare any of a copy constructor, copy assignment operator, or destructor, you 9 \nshould declare all three. It grew out of the observation that the need to take over 10 \nthe meaning of a copy operation almost always stemmed from the class perfor m-11 \ning some kind of resource management, and that almost always implied that (1) 12 \nwhatever resource management was being done in one copy operation probably 13 \nneeded to be done in the other copy operation and (2) the class destructor would 14 \nalso be participating in management of the resource (e.g., releasing it).  The classic 15 \nresource to be managed was memory, and this is why all the Standard Library  16 \nclasses that manage memory (e.g., the STL containers that perform dynamic 17 \nmemory management) all declare “the big three :” both copy operations and a d e-18 \nstructor. 19 \nA consequence of the Rule of Three is that the presence of a user-declared destruc-20 \ntor indicates that simple memberwise copy is unlikely to be appropriate for the 21 \ncopying operations in the class. That, in turn, sugg ests that if a class declares a d e-22 \nstructor, the copy operations probably shouldn’t be automatically generated, b e-23 \ncause they wouldn’t do the right thing. At the time C++98 was adopted, the signifi-24 \ncance of this line of reasoning was not fully appreciated,  so in C++98, the existence 25 \nof a user-declared destructor had no impact on compilers’ willingness to generate 26 \ncopy operations. That continues to be the case in C++11, but only because restrict-27 \ning the conditions under which the copy operations are generated would break too 28 \nmuch legacy code.  29 \nThe reasoning behind the Rule of Three remains valid, however, and combined 30 \nwith the observation that declaration of a copy operation precludes the implicit 31\n\n#### 第 140 页原文\n\nPage 140 \n \ngeneration of the move operations, C++11 does not generate move operations for a 1 \nclass with a user-declared destructor.  2 \nSo move operations are generated for classes (when needed) only if  all of these 3 \nthree things are true: 4 \n No copy operations are declared in the class. 5 \n No move operations are declared in the class. 6 \n No destructor is declared in the class. 7 \nAt some point, analogous rules may be extended to the copy operations , because 8 \nC++11 deprecates the automatic generation of copy operations for classes decla r-9 \ning copy operations or a destructor.  This puts such function generation on death 10 \nrow. Feature deprecation is the Standard’s way of issuing a warning that behavior 11 \nvalid in the current Standard is officially frowned upon, and the feature may be 12 \nremoved in a future Standard.  13 \nIn practice, this means that if you have code that depends on the generation of 14 \ncopy operations in classes declaring a destructor or one  of the copy operations, 15 \nyou should think about upgrading these classes to eliminate the dependence. Pro-16 \nvided the behavior of the compiler -generated functions is correct (i.e, if membe r-17 \nwise copying of the class’s nonstatic data members is what you want), your job is 18 \neasy, because C++11’s “= default” lets you say that explicitly: 19 \nclass Widget { 20 \npublic: 21 \n  … 22 \n  ~Widget();                             // user-declared dtor 23 \n 24 \n  …                                      // default copy-ctor                                                 25 \n  Widget(const Widget&) = default;       // behavior is OK                                                26 \n  Widget&                                // default copy-assign 27 \n    operator=(const Widget&) = default;  // behavior is OK 28 \n  … 29 \n};                            30 \nIn fact, you may want to adopt a policy of manually declaring all the copy and move 31 \nfunctions you want your class to support, then using “ = default” to force compil-32\n\n#### 第 141 页原文\n\nPage 141 \n \ners to generate the default implementations for the functions where these imple-1 \nmentations would be appropriate. It’s a bit more work than having compilers s i-2 \nlently generate those functions on their own, but it makes your intentions clearer, 3 \nand it can help you side -step some fairly subtle bugs. For example, suppose you 4 \nhave a class representing a string table, i.e., a data structure that permits fast 5 \nlookups of string values via an integer ID: 6 \nclass StringTable { 7 \npublic: 8 \n  StringTable() {} 9 \n  …                 // functions for insertion, erasure, lookup, 10 \n                    // etc., but no copy/move/dtor functionality 11 \nprivate: 12 \n  std::map<int, std::string> values;  13 \n}; 14 \nAssuming that the class declares no copy operations, no move operations, and no 15 \ndestructor, compilers will automatically generate these functions if they are used. 16 \nThat’s very convenient.  17 \nBut suppose that sometime later, it’s decided that logging the default construction 18 \nand the destruction of such objects  would be useful . Adding that functionality is 19 \neasy: 20 \nclass StringTable { 21 \npublic: 22 \n  StringTable()  23 \n  { makeLogEntry(\"Creating StringTable object\"); }     // added 24 \n  ~StringTable()                                       // also 25 \n  { makeLogEntry(\"Destroying StringTable object\"); }   // added 26 \n  …                                     // other funcs as before 27 \nprivate: 28 \n  std::map<int, std::string> values;    // as before 29 \n}; 30 \nThis seems innocuous, but it has a potentially significant side effect: it prevents the 31 \nmove operations from being generated. To be specific, the existence of the user -32 \ndeclared destructor precludes their generation. That's why I've highlighted only 33 \nthe destructor declaration above. The modificatio n to the default constructor  and 34 \nthe body of the destructor is irrelevant for this discussion. 35\n\n#### 第 142 页原文\n\nPage 142 \n \nAlthough the addition of the destructor prevents the move operations from coming 1 \ninto existence, generation of the class's copy operations is unaffected. Your code is 2 \ntherefore likely to compile, run, and pass all your functional testing. That includes 3 \ntesting its move functionality, because even though this class is no longer move -4 \nenabled, requests to move it will compile and run. Such requests will, as noted ear-5 \nlier in this Item, cause copies to be made . Which means that code “moving” 6 \nStringTable objects actually copies them , i.e., copies the underlying  7 \nstd::vector<std::string> objects. And copying a 8 \nstd::vector<std::string> is likely to be orders of magnitude  slower than 9 \nmoving it . The simple act of adding a destructor to the class could thereby have 10 \nintroduced a significant performance problem! 11 \nFixing the problem is easy: just tell your compilers to start generating the move 12 \noperations again: 13 \nclass StringTable { 14 \npublic: 15 \n  StringTable() { … }                               // as before 16 \n  ~StringTable() { … }                              // as before 17 \n  StringTable(StringTable&&) = default;             // move ctor 18 \n  StringTable& operator=(StringTable&&) = default;  // move op= 19 \n  …                                     // other funcs as before 20 \nprivate: 21 \n  std::map<int, std::string> values;    // as before 22 \n}; 23 \nBut as long as you’re doing that, you might as well do the same for the copy oper a-24 \ntions. That clearly expresses that you desire the compiler-generated copy behavior 25 \nfor this class, and it also future-proofs the class against the day when copy opera-26 \ntions are no longer created for classes with user -declared destructors. The result 27 \nlooks like this: 28 \nclass StringTable { 29 \npublic: 30 \n  StringTable() { … }                               // as before 31 \n  ~StringTable() { … }                              // as before 32 \n  StringTable(const StringTable&) = default;        // copy ctor 33 \n  StringTable(StringTable&&) = default;             // move ctor 34\n\n#### 第 143 页原文\n\nPage 143 \n \n  StringTable& 1 \n    operator=(const StringTable&) = default;        // copy op= 2 \n  StringTable& operator=(StringTable&&) = default;  // move op= 3 \n  …                                                 // as before 4 \nprivate: 5 \n  std::map<int, std::string> values;                // as before 6 \n}; 7 \nNow, h aving endured  my endless blathering about the rules governing the copy 8 \nand move operations in C++11, you may wonder when I’ll turn my attention to the 9 \ntwo other special member functions, the de fault constructor and the destructor. 10 \nThat time is now, but only for this sentence, because nothing has changed for these 11 \nmember functions: the rules in C++11 are the same as in C++98.  12 \nThe C++11 rules governing the special member functions are thus: 13 \n Default constructor : Same rules as C++98. Generated only if the class co n-14 \ntains no user-declared constructors.  15 \n Destructor: Same rules as C++98. Virtual only if a base class destructor is vi r-16 \ntual. 17 \n Copy constructor: Same runtime b ehavior as C++98. Performs member wise 18 \ncopying of (nonstatic) class data members. Generated only if the class contains 19 \nneither a user-declared copy constructor nor any move operations. Generation 20 \nof this function in a class with a user-declared copy assignment operator or de-21 \nstructor is deprecated. 22 \n Copy assignment operator : Same runtime b ehavior as C++98. Performs 23 \nmemberwise copying of (nonstatic) class data members. Generated only if  the 24 \nclass contains neither a user-declared copy assignment operator nor any move 25 \noperations. Generation of t his function in a class with a user -declared copy 26 \nconstructor or destructor is deprecated. 27 \n Move constructo r and move assignment operator : Performs memberwise 28 \nmoving of (nonstatic) class data members. Generated only if the class contains 29 \nneither user-declared copy operations, move operations, nor destructor. 30\n\n#### 第 144 页原文\n\nPage 144 \n \nNote, by the way, that there’s nothing in the rules about the existence of a member 1 \nfunction template preventing compilers from generating the special member fun c-2 \ntions. That means that if Widget looks like this, 3 \nclass Widget { 4 \n  … 5 \n  template<typename T>                // copy-construct Widget 6 \n  Widget(const T& rhs);               // from anything 7 \n  template<typename T>                // copy-assign Widget 8 \n  Widget& operator=(const T& rhs);    // from anything 9 \n  … 10 \n}; 11 \ncompilers will still generate copy and move operations for Widget (assuming the 12 \nusual conditions governing their generation are fulfilled), even though these te m-13 \nplates could be instantiated to produce the signatures for the copy constructor and 14 \ncopy assignment operator. (That would be the case when T is Widget.) In all lik e-15 \nlihood, this will strike you as an edge case barely worth acknowledging, but there’s 16 \na reason I’m mentioning it. As Item 28 explains, it can have important consequenc-17 \nes. 18 \nThings to Remember 19 \n The special member functions are those compilers may generate on their own :  20 \nthe default constructor, destructor, copy operations, and move operations. 21 \n Move operations are generated only for classes lacking expli citly-declared 22 \nmove operations, copy operations, and a destructor. 23 \n The copy constructor  is generated only for classes lacking an explicitly -24 \ndeclared copy constructor and the move operations. The copy assignment o p-25 \nerator is generated only for classes lacking an explicitly -declared copy assign-26 \nment operator and the move operations. Generation of the copy operations in 27 \nclasses with an explicitly-declared destructor is deprecated,   28 \n Member function templates never suppress generation of special member 29 \nfunctions. 30\n\n#### 第 145 页原文\n\nPage 145 \n \n \n1 \nPoets and songwriters have a thing about love. And sometimes about counting. 2 \nOccasionally both. Inspired by the rather different takes on love and countin g by 3 \nElizabeth Barrett Browning  (“How do I love thee? Let me count the ways.”) and 4 \nPaul Simon (“There must be 50 ways to leave your lover”), we might try to en u-5 \nmerate the reasons why a raw pointer is hard to love: 6 \n1. Its declaration doesn’t indicate whether it points to a single object or to an a r-7 \nray. 8 \n2. Its declaration reveals nothing about whether you  should destroy what it 9 \npoints to when you’re done using it, i.e., if the pointer owns the thing it points 10 \nto. 11 \n3. If you somehow determine that you should destroy what the pointer points to, 12 \nthere’s no way to tell how. Should you use delete, or is there a different d e-13 \nstruction mechanism (e.g., a dedicated destruction function the pointer should 14 \nbe passed to)? 15 \n4. Even if you manage to find out that delete is the way to go , Reason 1 means 16 \nit’s rarely possible to know whether to use the object form (“ delete”) or the 17 \narray form (“delete []”). If you use the wrong form, results are undefined. 18 \n5. Assuming you ascertain that the pointer owns what it points to and you di s-19 \ncover how to destroy it, it’s difficult to ensure that you perform the destruction 20 \nexactly once along every control path in your co de (including those due to e x-21 \nceptions). Missing a path leads to resource leaks, and doing the destruction 22 \nmore than once leads to undefined behavior. 23 \n6. There’s typically no way to tell if the pointer dangles, i.e., points to memory 24 \nthat no longer holds the o bject the pointer is supposed to point to. Dangling 25 \npointers arise when objects are destroyed while pointers still point to them. 26 \nRaw pointers are powerful tools, to be sure, but decades of experience have 27 \ndemonstrated that with only the slightest lapse in  concentration or discipline, 28 \nthese tools can turn on their ostensible masters. Surely we can do better.  29\n\n#### 第 146 页原文\n\nPage 146 \n \nSmart pointers are one way to address these issues . Smart pointers are wrappers 1 \naround raw pointers that act much like the raw pointers they wrap, but  that avoid 2 \nmany of their pitfalls. You should therefore prefer smart pointers to raw pointers . 3 \nSmart pointers can do virtually everything raw pointers can, but with far fewer 4 \nopportunities for error.  5 \nThere are four smart pointers in C++11: std::auto_ptr, std::unique_ptr, 6 \nstd::shared_ptr, and std::weak_ptr. All are designed to help manage the lif e-7 \ntime of dynamically allocated objects, i.e., to avoid resource leaks by ensuring that 8 \nsuch objects are destroyed in the appropriate manner at the appropriate time  (in-9 \ncluding in the event of exceptions). 10 \nstd::auto_ptr is a deprecated leftover from C++98. It was an attempt to stan d-11 \nardize what later became C++11’s std::unique_ptr. Doing the job right required 12 \nmove semantics, but C++98 didn’t have them. As a workaround , std::auto_ptr 13 \nco-opted its copy operations for moves. This led to surprising code (copying a 14 \nstd::auto_ptr sets it to null!) and frustrating usage restrictions (e.g., it’s not 15 \npossible to store std::auto_ptrs in containers). 16 \nstd::unique_ptr does everything std::auto_ptr does, plus more. It does it as 17 \nefficiently, and it does it without warping what it means to copy an object. It’s be t-18 \nter than std::auto_ptr in every way. The only legitimate use case for 19 \nstd::auto_ptr is a need to compile code with C++98 compilers. Unless you have 20 \nthat constraint, you should replace std::auto_ptr with std::unique_ptr and 21 \nnever look back. 22 \nThe smart pointer APIs are remarkably varied. About the only functionality co m-23 \nmon to all is default  construction. Because comprehensive references for these 24 \nAPIs are widely available in both electronic and print form, I’ll focus my discus-25 \nsions on information that’s often missing from API overviews, e.g., noteworthy use 26 \ncases, runtime cost analyses, etc. Mastering such information can be the difference 27 \nbetween merely using these smart pointers and using them effectively. 28\n\n#### 第 147 页原文\n\nPage 147 \n \n Item 20: Use std::unique_ptr for exclusive-ownership re-1 \nsource management. 2 \nWhen you reach for a smart pointer, std::unique_ptr should generally be  the 3 \none closest at hand. By default, std::unique_ptrs are the same size as raw 4 \npointers, and for most operations (including dereferencing), they execute exactly 5 \nthe same instructions. This means you can use them even in situations where 6 \nmemory and cycles are tight. If a raw pointer is small enough and fast enough for 7 \nyou, a std::unique_ptr almost certainly is, too. 8 \nLike std::auto_ptr before it, std::unique_ptr embodies exclusive ownership 9 \nsemantics. A non-null std::unique_ptr always owns what it points to.  Moving a 10 \nstd::unique_ptr transfers ownership from the source pointer to the destination 11 \npointer. (The source pointer is set to null.) Copying a std::unique_ptr isn’t al-12 \nlowed, because if you could copy a std::unique_ptr, you ’d end up with two 13 \nstd::unique_ptrs to the same resource, each thinking it owned (and should 14 \ntherefore destroy) that resource. std::unique_ptr is thus a move-only type. Up-15 \non destruction, a non -null std::unique_ptr destroys its resource. By default, 16 \nresource destruction is accompl ished by applying delete to the raw pointer i n-17 \nside the std::unique_ptr. 18 \nA common use for std::unique_ptr is as a factory function return type for ob-19 \njects in a hierarchy. Suppose we have a hierarchy for types of investments (e.g., 20 \nstocks, bonds, real estate, etc.) with a base class Investment.  21 \n22 \nA factory function for such a hierarchy typically allocates an object on the heap and 23 \nreturns a pointer to it, with the caller being responsible for deleting the object 24 \nclass Investment { … }; \nclass Stock: \n  public Investment { … }; \nclass Bond: \n  public Investment { … }; \nclass RealEstate: \n  public Investment { … };\n\n#### 第 148 页原文\n\nPage 148 \n \nwhen it’s no longer needed. That’s a perfect match for std::unique_ptr, because 1 \nthe caller acquires responsibility for the resource returned by the factory  (i.e., ex-2 \nclusive ownership of it) , and the std::unique_ptr automatically deletes what it 3 \npoints to when it’s de stroyed. A factory function  for the Investment hierarchy 4 \ncould be declared like this: 5 \ntemplate<typename... Ts>          // return std::unique_ptr  6 \nstd::unique_ptr<Investment>       // to an object created  7 \n  makeInvestment(Ts&&... args);   // from the given args 8 \nCallers could use the returned std::unique_ptr in a single scope, as follows, 9 \n{ 10 \n  … 11 \n  auto pInvestment =              // pInvestment is of type 12 \n    makeInvestment( arguments );  // std::unique_ptr<Investment> 13 \n  … 14 \n}                                 // destroy *pInvestment 15 \nbut they  could also use it in ownership -migration scenarios, such as when the 16 \nstd::unique_ptr returned from the factory is moved into a container, the co n-17 \ntainer element is subsequently moved into a data member of an object, and that 18 \nobject is later destroyed. When that happens, the object’s std::unique_ptr data 19 \nmember would also be destroyed, and its destruction would cause the resource 20 \nreturned from the factory to be destroyed. If the ownership chain got interrupted 21 \ndue to an exception or other atypical control flow (e.g., premature function return 22 \nor break from a loop), the std::unique_ptr owning the managed resource 23 \nwould eventually have its destructor called ,† and the resource it was managing 24 \nwould thereby be destroyed. 25 \nBy default, that destruction would take place via delete, but, during construction, 26 \nstd::unique_ptr objects can be configured t o use custom deleters : arbitrary 27 \n                                                             \n† There are a few exceptions to this rule . All stem from abnormal program termination. If \nan exception propagates out of a thread’s primary function (e.g., main, for the program’s \ninitial thread) or if a noexcept specification is violated (see Item 16), local objects may not \nbe destroyed, and if std::abort is called, they definitely won’t be.\n\n#### 第 149 页原文\n\nPage 149 \n \nfunctions (or function objects, including those arising from lambda expressions) to 1 \nbe invoked when it’s time for their resources to be d estroyed. If the object created 2 \nby makeInvestment shouldn’t be directly deleted, but instead should first have a 3 \nlog entry written, makeInvestment could be implemented as follows. (An expl a-4 \nnation follows the code, so don’t worry if you see something whose motivation is 5 \nless than obvious.) 6 \nauto delInvmt = [](Investment* pInvestment)       // custom  7 \n                {                                 // deleter 8 \n                  makeLogEntry(pInvestment);      // (a lambda 9 \n                  delete pInvestment;             // expression) 10 \n                }; 11 \ntemplate<typename... Ts>                          // revised 12 \nstd::unique_ptr<Investment, decltype(delInvmt)>   // return type 13 \nmakeInvestment(Ts&&... args)                 14 \n{ 15 \n  std::unique_ptr<Investment, decltype(delInvmt)> // ptr to be 16 \n    pInv(nullptr, delInvmt);                      // returned 17 \n  if ( /* a Stock object should be created */ ) 18 \n  { 19 \n    pInv.reset(new Stock(std::forward<Ts>(args)...)); 20 \n  } 21 \n  else if ( /* a Bond object should be created */ ) 22 \n  { 23 \n    pInv.reset(new Bond(std::forward<Ts>(args)...)); 24 \n  } 25 \n  else if ( /* a RealEstate object should be created */ ) 26 \n  { 27 \n    pInv.reset(new RealEstate(std::forward<Ts>(args)...)); 28 \n  } 29 \n  return pInv;                               30 \n}                                           31 \nIn a moment, I’ll explain how this works, but first consider how things look if 32 \nyou’re a caller. Assuming you store the result of the  makeInvestment call in an 33 \nauto variable, you frolic in blissful ignorance of the fact that the resource you’re 34 \nusing requires special treatment during deletion. In fact, you veritably bathe in 35 \nbliss, because the use of std::unique_ptr means you need not concern yourself 36 \nwith when the resource  should be destroyed, much less ensure that the destru c-37 \ntion happens exactly once along every control path in the program. 38\n\n#### 第 150 页原文\n\nPage 150 \n \nstd::unique_ptr takes car e of all those things automatically. From a client’s 1 \nperspective, makeInvestment’s interface is sweet. 2 \nThe implementation is pretty nice, too, once you understand the following: 3 \n delInvmt is the custom deleter for the object returned from makeInvest-4 \nment. All custom deletion functions accept a raw pointer to the object to be d e-5 \nstroyed, then do what is necessary to destroy that object. In this case, the a c-6 \ntion is to call makeLogEntry and then apply delete. Using a lambda expre s-7 \nsion to create delInvmt is convenient, but, as we’ll see shortly, it’s also more 8 \nefficient than writing a conventional function. 9 \n When a custom deleter is to be used, its type must be specified as the second 10 \ntype argument to std::unique_ptr. In this case, that’s the type of delInvmt, 11 \nand that’s why the return type of makeInvestment is 12 \nstd::unique_ptr<Investment, decltype(delInvmt)>. (For information 13 \nabout decltype, see Item 3.) 14 \n The basic strategy of makeInvestment is to create a null  std::unique_ptr, 15 \nmake it point to an object of the appropriate type, and then return it.  To ass o-16 \nciate the custom deleter ( delInvmt) with pInv, we pass that as its second 17 \nconstructor argument. 18 \n As Item 23 explains, it’s preferable to use std::make_unique rather than new 19 \nto create an object for a std::unique_ptr to point to, yet this code uses new 20 \nin several places.  That’s because std::make_unique offers no  support for 21 \ncustom deleters. (Of course, it would be possible to encapsulate the use of new 22 \ninside a std::make_unique-like template that supported the specification of 23 \na custom deleter. If you’ve been waiting for an opportunity to write some code 24 \nwhose development is “left as an exercise for the reader,” here’s your chance.) 25 \n Attempting to assign a raw pointer ( e.g., from new) to a std::unique_ptr 26 \nwon’t compile, because it would constitute an implicit conversion from a raw 27 \nto a smart pointer . Such implicit conversions can be problematic, so C++11’s 28 \nsmart pointers prohibit them. That’s why reset is used to have pInv assume 29 \nownership of the object created via new. 30", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.151–p.175", "pages": "p.151–p.175", "raw_text": "#### 第 151 页原文\n\nPage 151 \n \n With each use of new, we perfect -forward the arguments passed to 1 \nmakeInvestment (see Item 27). This makes al l the information provided by 2 \ncallers available to the constructors of the objects being created.  3 \n The custom deleter takes a parameter of type Investment*.  Regardless of the 4 \nactual type of object created inside makeInvestment (i.e., Stock, Bond, or Re-5 \nalEstate), it will ultimately be deleted inside the lambda expression as an 6 \nInvestment* object. This means we’ll be  deleting a derived class ob ject via a 7 \nbase class pointer. For that to work, the base class —Investment—must have 8 \na virtual destructor: 9 \nclass Investment { 10 \npublic: 11 \n  …                                           // essential 12 \n  virtual ~Investment();                      // design 13 \n  …                                           // component! 14 \n}; 15 \nIn C++14, the existence of function return type deduction (see Item 3) means that  16 \nmakeInvestment could be implemented in this simpler and more encapsulated 17 \nfashion: 18 \ntemplate<typename... Ts>  19 \nauto makeInvestment(Ts&&... args)                // C++14 only 20 \n{ 21 \n  auto delInvmt = [](Investment* pInvestment)    // this is now 22 \n                  {                              // inside 23 \n                    makeLogEntry(pInvestment);   // make- 24 \n                    delete pInvestment;          // Investment 25 \n                  }; 26 \n  std::unique_ptr<Investment, decltype(delInvmt)>   // as 27 \n    pInv(nullptr, delInvmt);                        // before 28 \n  if ( … )                                       // as before 29 \n  { 30 \n    pInv.reset(new Stock(std::forward<Ts>(args)...)); 31 \n  } 32 \n  else if ( … )                                  // as before 33 \n  { 34 \n    pInv.reset(new Bond(std::forward<Ts>(args)...)); 35 \n  } 36 \n  else if ( … )                                  // as before 37 \n  { 38 \n    pInv.reset(new RealEstate(std::forward<Ts>(args)...)); 39\n\n#### 第 152 页原文\n\nPage 152 \n \n  } 1 \n  return pInv;                                   // as before 2 \n}  3 \nAt the beginning of this Item , I emphasize d that, when using the default deleter 4 \n(i.e., delete), std::unique_ptr objects are the same size as raw pointers. When 5 \ncustom deleters enter the picture, this may no longer be the case. Deleters that are 6 \nfunction pointers generally cause the size of the std::unique_ptr to grow from 7 \none word to two. For deleters that are func tion objects, the change in size depends 8 \non how much state is stored in the function object. Stateless function objects (e.g., 9 \nfrom lambda expressions with no captures) incur no size penalty , and this means 10 \nthat when a custom deleter can be implemented as either a function or a capture-11 \nless lambda expression, the lambda is preferable: 12 \nauto delInvmt1 = [](Investment* pInvestment)      // custom  13 \n                 {                                // deleter 14 \n                   makeLogEntry(pInvestment);     // as  15 \n                   delete pInvestment;            // stateless 16 \n                 };                               // lambda 17 \ntemplate<typename... Ts>                          // return type 18 \nstd::unique_ptr<Investment, decltype(delInvmt1)>  // has size of 19 \nmakeInvestment(Ts&&... args);                     // Investment* 20 \n 21 \nvoid delInvmt2(Investment* pInvestment)           // custom 22 \n{                                                 // deleter 23 \n  makeLogEntry(pInvestment);                      // as function 24 \n  delete pInvestment; 25 \n} 26 \ntemplate<typename... Ts>                 // return type has 27 \nstd::unique_ptr<Investment,              // size of Investment* 28 \n                void (*)(Investment*)>   // plus at least size 29 \nmakeInvestment(Ts&&... args);            // of function pointer! 30 \nFunction object deleters with extensive state can yield std::unique_ptr objects 31 \nof significant size. However, large function objects are often ill -advised on other 32 \ngrounds, and there are ways to make them smaller; see Error! Reference source 33 \nnot found. . If you find yourself with a custom deleter with significant state, you 34 \nprobably need to change your design. 35 \nFactory functions are not th e only common use case for std::unique_ptrs. 36 \nThey’re even more popular as a mechanism for implementing the Pimpl Idiom. The 37\n\n#### 第 153 页原文\n\nPage 153 \n \ncode for that is n’t complicated, but in some cases it’s less than  straightforward, so 1 \nI’ll refer you to Item 24, which is dedicated to the topic. 2 \nstd::unique_ptr comes in two forms, one for individual objects 3 \n(std::unique_ptr<T>) and one for arrays ( std::unique_ptr<T[]>). As a r e-4 \nsult, there’s never any ambiguity about what kind of entity a std::unique_ptr 5 \npoints to. The std::unique_ptr API is designed to match the form you’re us ing. 6 \nFor example, t here’s no indexing operator ( operator[]) for the single -object 7 \nform, while the array form lacks dereferencing operators (operator* and opera-8 \ntor->).  9 \nThe existence of std::unique_ptr for arrays should be of only intellectual inte r-10 \nest to you, because std::array, std::vector, and std::string are virtually 11 \nalways better data structure choices than raw arrays. The only situation I can co n-12 \nceive of when a std::unique_ptr<T[]> would make sense would be when 13 \nyou’re using a C -like API that returns a raw pointer to a heap array that you a s-14 \nsume ownership of.  15 \nstd::unique_ptr is the C++11 way to express exclusive ownership, but one of its 16 \nmost attractive features is that it easily and efficiently converts to a 17 \nstd::shared_ptr: 18 \nstd::shared_ptr<Investment> sp =    // converts std::unique_ptr 19 \n  makeInvestment( arguments );      // to std::shared_ptr 20 \nThis is a key part of why std::unique_ptr is so well suited as a factory function 21 \nreturn type. Factory functions can’t know whether callers will want to use excl u-22 \nsive-ownership semantics for the object they return or whether shared ownership 23 \n(i.e., std::shared_ptr) would be more appropriate. By returning a 24 \nstd::unique_ptr, factories provide callers with the most efficient smart pointer, 25 \nbut they don’t impede callers from replacing it with its more flexible sibling. (For 26 \ninformation about std::shared_ptr, proceed to Item 21.) 27 \nThings to Remember 28 \n std::unique_ptr is a small, fast, move -only smart pointer for managing r e-29 \nsources with exclusive-ownership semantics. 30\n\n#### 第 154 页原文\n\nPage 154 \n \n By default, resource destruction takes place via delete, but custom deleters 1 \ncan be specified . Stateful deleters and function pointers as deleters increase 2 \nthe size of std::unique_ptr objects. 3 \n Converting a std::unique_ptr to a std::shared_ptr is easy. 4 \n Item 21: Use std::shared_ptr for shared-ownership re-5 \nsource management. 6 \nProgrammers using languages with garbage collection point and laugh at what C++ 7 \nprogrammers go through  to prevent resource leaks . “How primitive!”, they jeer. 8 \n“Didn’t you get the memo from Lisp in the 1960s? Machines should manage r e-9 \nsource lifetimes , not humans.” C++ developers roll their eyes . “You mean the 10 \nmemo where the only resource is memory and the timing of resource reclamat ion 11 \nis nondeterministic?  We prefer the generality and predictability of destructors, 12 \nthank you.” But our bravado is part bluster. Garbage collection really is convenient, 13 \nand manual lifetime management really can seem akin to constructing a mnemonic 14 \nmemory circuit using stone knives and bear skins. Why can’t we have the best of 15 \nboth worlds: a system that works automatically ( like garbage collec tion), yet a p-16 \nplies to all resources and has predictable timing (like destructors)? 17 \nstd::shared_ptr is the C++11 way  of binding these worlds together. An object 18 \naccessed via std::shared_ptrs ha s its  lifetime managed by those pointers 19 \nthrough shared ownership. No specific std::shared_ptr owns the object. Instead, 20 \nall std::shared_ptrs pointing to it collaborate to ensure its destruction at the 21 \npoint where it’s no longer needed. When the last std::shared_ptr pointing to an 22 \nobject stops pointing there (e.g., because the std::shared_ptr is destroyed or 23 \nmade to point to a different object ), that std::shared_ptr destroys the object it 24 \npoints to . As with garbage collection, client s need not concern themselves with 25 \nmanaging the lifetime of pointed -to objects, but as with destructors, the timing of 26 \nthe objects’ destruction is deterministic. 27 \nA std::shared_ptr can tell whether  it’s the last one pointing to a resource by 28 \nconsulting the resource’s reference count: a value associated with the resource that 29 \nkeeps track of how many std::shared_ptrs point to it. std::shared_ptr con-30 \nstructors increment this count  (usually—see below), std::shared_ptr destruc-31\n\n#### 第 155 页原文\n\nPage 155 \n \ntors decrement it, and copy assignment operators do both. ( If sp1 and sp2 are 1 \nstd::shared_ptrs, the assignment “sp1 = sp2” modifies sp1 such that it points 2 \nto the object pointed to by sp2. The net effect of the assignment, then, is that the 3 \nreference count for the object originally pointed to by sp1 is decremented, while 4 \nthat for the object pointed to by sp2 is incremented.) If a std::shared_ptr sees a 5 \nreference count of zero after perform ing a decrement, no more 6 \nstd::shared_ptrs point to the resource, so the std::shared_ptr destroys it. 7 \nThe existence of the reference count has performance implications: 8 \n std::shared_ptrs are twice the size of a raw pointer, because they internally 9 \ncontain a raw pointer to the resource as well as a raw pointer to the resource’s 10 \nreference count.†  11 \n Memory for the reference count must be dynamically allocated. Conceptu-12 \nally, the reference count is associated with the object being pointed to, but 13 \npointed-to objects know nothing about this. They thus have no place to store a 14 \nreference count. Storage for the count is hence dynamically allocated. Item 23 15 \nexplains that the cost of this allocation (but not the allocation itself) is avoided 16 \nwhen the std::shared_ptr is created by std::make_shared, but there are 17 \nsituations where std::make_shared can’t be used. Either way, the reference 18 \ncount is stored as dynamically allocated runtime data. 19 \n Increments and decrements of the reference count must be atomic , b e-20 \ncause there can be simultaneous readers and write rs in different threads. For 21 \nexample, a std::shared_ptr pointing to a resource in one thread could be 22 \nexecuting its destructor (hence decrementing the reference count for the r e-23 \nsource it points to), while, in a different thread, a std::shared_ptr to the 24 \nsame object could be copied (and therefore incrementing the same reference 25 \ncount). Atomic operations are typically slower than non-atomic operations, so 26 \n                                                             \n† This implementation is not required by the Standard, but there are sound technical re a-\nsons why every Standard Library implementation I’m familiar with employs it.\n\n#### 第 156 页原文\n\nPage 156 \n \neven though reference counts are usually only a word in size, you should a s-1 \nsume that reading and writing them is comparatively costly. 2 \nDid I pique your curiosity when I wrote that std::shared_ptr constructors only 3 \n“usually” increment the reference count for the object they point to ? Creating a 4 \nnew std::shared_ptr pointing to an object always yields one more 5 \nstd::shared_ptr pointing to that object, so why mustn’t we always increment 6 \nthe reference count?  7 \nMove construction, that’s why . Move-constructing a std::shared_ptr from an-8 \nother std::shared_ptr sets the source std::shared_ptr to null, and that 9 \nmeans that th e old std::shared_ptr stops pointing to the resource at the mo-10 \nment the new std::shared_ptr starts. As a result, no reference count manipul a-11 \ntion is required. Moving std::shared_ptrs is therefore faster than copying 12 \nthem: copying requires incrementing the reference count, but moving doesn’t. This 13 \nis as true for assignment as for construction, so move construction is faster than 14 \ncopy construction, and move assignment is faster than copy assignment.  15 \nLike std::unique_ptr (see Item 20), std::shared_ptr uses delete as its d e-16 \nfault resource -destruction mechanism, but it also supports custom deleters . T he 17 \ndesign of this support  differs from that for std::unique_ptr, however . For 18 \nstd::unique_ptr, the type of the deleter is part of the type of the smart pointer. 19 \nFor std::shared_ptr, it’s not: 20 \nauto loggingDel = [](Widget *pw)        // custom deleter 21 \n                  {                     // (as in Item 20)  22 \n                    makeLogEntry(pw); 23 \n                    delete pw; 24 \n                  }; 25 \nstd::unique_ptr<                        // deleter type is 26 \n  Widget, decltype(loggingDel)          // part of ptr type 27 \n  > upw(new Widget, loggingDel); 28 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget>                 // deleter type is not 29 \n  spw(new Widget, loggingDel);          // part of ptr type 30 \nThe std::shared_ptr design is more flexible. Consider two 31 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget>s, each with a custom deleter of a unique type (e.g., 32 \nbecause the custom deleters are specified via lambda expressions): 33\n\n#### 第 157 页原文\n\nPage 157 \n \nauto customDeleter1 = [](Widget *pw) { … };  // custom deleters, 1 \nauto customDeleter2 = [](Widget *pw) { … };  // each with a  2 \n                                             // different type 3 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> pw1(new Widget, customDeleter1); 4 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> pw2(new Widget, customDeleter2); 5 \nBecause pw1 and pw2 have the same type, they can be placed in a container of o b-6 \njects of that type: 7 \nstd::vector<std::shared_ptr<Widget>> vpw { pw1, pw2 }; 8 \nThey could also be assigned to one another, and they could each be passed to a 9 \nfunction taking a paramete r of type std::shared_ptr<Widget>. None of these 10 \nthings can be done with std::unique_ptrs that differ in the types of their cu s-11 \ntom deleters, because the type of the custom deleter would affect the type of the 12 \nstd::unique_ptr. 13 \nIn another difference from std::unique_ptr, specifying a custom deleter doesn’t 14 \nchange the size of a std::shared_ptr object. Regardless of deleter, a 15 \nstd::shared_ptr object is two pointers in size.  That’s great news, but it should 16 \nmake you vaguely uneasy. Custom deleters can be func tion objects, and function 17 \nobjects can contain arbitrary amounts of data. That means they can be arbitrarily 18 \nlarge. How can a std::shared_ptr refer to a deleter of arbitrary size without u s-19 \ning any more memory? 20 \nIt can’t. It may have to use more memory. However, that memory isn’t part of the 21 \nstd::shared_ptr object. I t’s on the heap  (or, if the creator of the 22 \nstd::shared_ptr took advantage of std::shared_ptr support for custom allo-23 \ncators, it’s wherever the memory managed by that allocator is located). I remarked 24 \nearlier that a std::shared_ptr object contains a pointer to the reference count 25 \nfor the object  it points to . That’s true, but it’s a bit misleading, because the refe r-26 \nence count is part of a larger data structure known as the control block. There’s a 27 \ncontrol block for each object managed by std::shared_ptrs. The control block 28 \ncontains, in addition to the reference count, a copy of the custom deleter, if one has 29 \nbeen specified. I f a custom allocator was specified, the control block contains a 30 \ncopy of that, too. (As Item 23 explains, it also contains a secondary reference count 31 \nknown as the weak count, but we’ll ignore that in this Item.) 32\n\n#### 第 158 页原文\n\nPage 158 \n \nThe control block is created by the function creating the first std::shared_ptr to 1 \nthe object. At least that’s what’s supposed to happen. In general, it’s impossible for 2 \na function creating a std::shared_ptr to an object to know whether some other 3 \nstd::shared_ptr already points to that object, so the following rules for control 4 \nblock creation are used: 5 \n std::make_shared (see Item 23) always creates a control block . It manu-6 \nfactures a new object to point to, so there is certainly no control block for that 7 \nobject at the time std::make_shared is called. 8 \n When a std::shared_ptr constructor is called with a raw pointer, it creates 9 \na control block for the object the raw pointer points to.  The thinking here 10 \nis that if you wanted to create a std::shared_ptr from an object that already 11 \nhad a control block, you’d use a std::shared_ptr or a std::weak_ptr (see 12 \nItem 22) as a constructor argument, not a raw pointer. std::shared_ptr 13 \nconstructors taking std::shared_ptrs or std::weak_ptrs as constructor 14 \narguments don’t create new control block s, because they know that the smart 15 \npointers passed to them already point to control blocks.  16 \nIf a std::shared_ptr is created from a raw pointer that’s null, by the way, 17 \nthere’s no object to associate the control block with, so no control block is cr e-18 \nated.  19 \nA consequence of these rules is that constructing more than one 20 \nstd::shared_ptr from a single raw pointer gives you a complimentary ride on 21 \nthe particle accelerator of undefined behavior, because the pointed -to object will 22 \nhave multiple control blocks. M ultiple control blocks means multiple reference 23 \ncounts, and multiple reference counts means the object will be destroyed multiple 24 \ntimes (once for each reference count). That means that code like this is bad, bad, 25 \nbad: 26 \nauto pw = new Widget; 27 \n… 28 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw1(pw, loggingDel);  // create control 29 \n                                               // block for *pw 30 \n… 31\n\n#### 第 159 页原文\n\nPage 159 \n \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw2(pw, loggingDel);  // create 2nd 1 \n                                               // control block 2 \n                                               // for *pw! 3 \nThe creation of the raw pointer pw to a dynamically allocated object is bad, b e-4 \ncause it runs contrary to the advice behind this entire chapter : to prefer smart 5 \npointers to raw pointers. (If you’ve forgotten the motivation for that advice, turn to 6 \npage 148 to refresh your memory.) But set that aside. The line creating pw is a sty-7 \nlistic abomination, but at le ast it doesn’t cause undefined program behavior. The 8 \nreal problem here is that the constructor for spw1 is called with a raw pointer, so it 9 \ncreates a control block (and thereby a reference count) for what’s pointed to. In 10 \nthis case, that’s *pw (i.e., the o bject pointed to by pw). In and of itself, that’s okay, 11 \nbut the constructor for spw2 is called with the same raw pointer, so it also creates 12 \na control block (hence a reference count) for *pw. *pw thus has two reference 13 \ncounts, each of which will eventually  become zero, and that will ultimately lead to 14 \nan attempt to destroy *pw twice. The second destruction is responsible for the un-15 \ndefined behavior. 16 \nThere are at least two lessons regarding std::shared_ptr use here. First, try to 17 \navoid passing raw pointers to a std::shared_ptr constructor. The usual alterna-18 \ntive is to use std::make_shared (see Item 23), but in the example above, we’re 19 \nusing custom deleters, and that’s not possible with std::make_shared. Second, if 20 \nyou must pass a raw pointer to a std::shared_ptr constructor, pass the result of 21 \nnew directly instead of going through a raw pointer variable.  If the first part of the 22 \ncode above were rewritten like this, 23 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw1(new Widget,    // direct use of new 24 \n                             loggingDel); 25 \nit’d be a lot harder to create a second std::shared_ptr from the same raw 26 \npointer. Instead, the author of the code creating spw2 would naturally use spw1 as 27 \nan initialization argument (i.e., would call the std::shared_ptr copy construc-28 \ntor), and that would pose no problem whatsoever: 29 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw2(spw1);     // spw2 uses same 30 \n                                        // control block as spw1 31\n\n#### 第 160 页原文\n\nPage 160 \n \nAn especially surprising way that using raw pointer variables as 1 \nstd::shared_ptr constructor arguments can lead to multiple control blocks i n-2 \nvolves the this pointer. Suppose our program uses std::shared_ptrs to ma n-3 \nage Widget objects, and we have a data structure that keeps track of Widgets that 4 \nhave been processed: 5 \nstd::vector<std::shared_ptr<Widget>> processedWidgets; 6 \nFurther suppose that Widget has a member function that does the processing: 7 \nclass Widget { 8 \npublic: 9 \n  … 10 \n  void process(); 11 \n  … 12 \n}; 13 \nHere’s a reasonable-looking approach for Widget::process: 14 \nvoid Widget::process() 15 \n{ 16 \n  …                                       // process the Widget 17 \n  processedWidgets.emplace_back(this);    // add it to list of 18 \n}                                         // processed Widgets; 19 \n                                          // this is wrong! 20 \nThe comment about this being wrong says it all —or at least most of it. (The part 21 \nthat’s wrong is the passing of this, not the use of emplace_back. If you’re not f a-22 \nmiliar with emplace_back, see Item 18.) This code will compile, but it’s passing a 23 \nraw pointer (this) to a container of std::shared_ptrs. The std::shared_ptr 24 \nthus constructed will create a new control block for the pointed -to Widget 25 \n(*this). That doesn’t sound harmful until you realize that if there are 26 \nstd::shared_ptrs outside the member function that already point to that Widg-27 \net, it’s game, set, and match for undefined behavior. 28 \nThe std::shared_ptr API includes a facility for just this kind of situation. It has 29 \nprobably the oddest of all names in the Standard C++ Library: 30 \nstd::enable_shared_from_this. That’s a template for a base class you inherit 31 \nfrom if you want a class managed by std::shared_ptrs to be able to safely cr e-32\n\n#### 第 161 页原文\n\nPage 161 \n \nate a std::shared_ptr from a this pointer. In our example, Widget would in-1 \nherit from std::enable_shared_from_this as follows: 2 \nclass Widget: public std::enable_shared_from_this<Widget> { 3 \npublic: 4 \n  … 5 \n  void process(); 6 \n  … 7 \n}; 8 \nAs I said, std::enable_shared_from_this is a base class template. Its type p a-9 \nrameter is always the name of the class being derived, so Widget inherits from 10 \nstd::enable_shared_from_this<Widget>. If the idea of a derived class inhe r-11 \niting from a base class templatized on the derived class makes yo ur head hurt, try 12 \nnot to think about it. The code is completely legal, and the design pattern behind it 13 \nis so well established, it has a standard name, albeit one that’s almost as odd as 14 \nstd::enable_shared_from_this. The name is The Curiously Recurring Te m-15 \nplate Pattern (CRTP ). If you’d like to learn more about it, unleash your search e n-16 \ngine, because here we need to get back to std::enable_shared_from_this. 17 \nstd::enable_shared_from_this defines a member function that creates a 18 \nstd::shared_ptr to the current object, but it does it without duplicating control 19 \nblocks. Th e member function is shared_from_this, and you use it in member 20 \nfunctions whenever you want a std::shared_ptr that points to the same object 21 \nas the this pointer. Here’s a safe implementation of Widget::process: 22 \nvoid Widget::process() 23 \n{ 24 \n  // as before, process the Widget 25 \n  … 26 \n  // add std::shared_ptr to current object to processedWidgets 27 \n  processedWidgets.emplace_back(shared_from_this()); 28 \n}                               29 \nInternally, shared_from_this looks up the control block for the current object, 30 \nand it creates a new std::shared_ptr that refers to that control block. The d e-31 \nsign relies on the current object having an associated control block. For that to be 32 \nthe case, there must be an existing std::shared_ptr (e.g., one outside the me m-33 \nber function calling shared_from_this) that points to the current object. If no 34\n\n#### 第 162 页原文\n\nPage 162 \n \nsuch std::shared_ptr exists (i.e., if the current object has no associated control 1 \nblock), shared_from_this throws an exception. 2 \nAt this po int, you may only dimly recall that our discussion of control blocks was 3 \nmotivated by a desire to understand the costs associated with 4 \nstd::shared_ptrs. Now that we understand how to avoid creating too many 5 \ncontrol blocks, let’s return to the original topic.  6 \nA control block is typically only a few words in size , although custom deleters and 7 \nallocators may make it larger. The usual control block implementation is more so-8 \nphisticated than you might expect. It makes use of inheritance, and there’s even a 9 \nvirtual function . (It’s used to ensure that the pointed-to object is properly de-10 \nstroyed.) That means  that using std::shared_ptrs also incurs the cost of the 11 \nmachinery for the virtual function used by the control block. 12 \nHaving read about dynamically allocated co ntrol blocks, arbitrarily large deleters  13 \nand allocators, virtual function machinery , and atomic reference count manipul a-14 \ntions, your enthusiasm for std::shared_ptrs may have waned somewhat. That’s 15 \nfine. They’re not the best solution to every resource management problem. But for 16 \nthe functionality they provide , std::shared_ptrs exact a very reasonable cost. 17 \nUnder typical conditions, where the default deleter and default allocator are used 18 \nand where the std::shared_ptr is created by std::make_shared, the control 19 \nblock is only about three words in size, and its allocation is essentially free . (It’s 20 \nincorporated into the memory allocation for the object being pointed to.  For d e-21 \ntails, see Item 23.) Dereferencing a std::shared_ptr is no more expensive than  22 \ndereferencing a raw pointer. Performing an operation requiring a reference count 23 \nmanipulation (e.g., copy construction or copy assignment, destruction) entails one 24 \nor two atomic operation s, but the se operations typically map to individual ma-25 \nchine instructions, so although the y may be expensive compared to non -atomic 26 \ninstructions, they’re still just single instructions. The virtual function machinery in 27 \nthe control block is used only once per object managed by std::shared_ptrs: 28 \nwhen the object is destroyed.   29 \nIn exchange for these rather modest costs, you get automatic lifetime management 30 \nof dynamically allocated resources. Most o f the time, using std::shared_ptr is 31\n\n#### 第 163 页原文\n\nPage 163 \n \nvastly preferable to trying to manage the lifetime of an object with shared owne r-1 \nship by hand . If you find yourself doubting whether you can afford use of 2 \nstd::shared_ptr, reconsider whether you really need shared owner ship. If e x-3 \nclusive ownership will do or even may do, std::unique_ptr is a better choice. Its 4 \nperformance profile is close to that  for raw pointers, and “upgrading” from 5 \nstd::unique_ptr to std::shared_ptr is easy , because  a std::shared_ptr 6 \ncan be created from a std::unique_ptr. 7 \nThe reverse is not true. Once you’ve turned lifetime management of a resource 8 \nover to a std::shared_ptr, there’s no changing your mind. Even if the reference 9 \ncount is one, you can’t reclaim ownership of the resource in order to, say, have a 10 \nstd::unique_ptr manage it. The ownership contract between a resource and the 11 \nstd::shared_ptrs that point to it is  of the ’ til-death-do-us-part variety. No d i-12 \nvorce, no annulment, no exceptions. 13 \nSomething else  std::shared_ptrs can’t do is work with arrays. In yet another 14 \ndifference from std::unique_ptr, std::shared_ptr has an API that’s designed 15 \nonly for pointers to single objects. There’s no std::shared_ptr<T[]>. From time 16 \nto time, “clever” programmers stumble on the idea of using a 17 \nstd::shared_ptr<T> to point to an array, specifying a custom deleter to perform 18 \nan array delete (i.e., delete []). This can be made to compile, but it’s a  horrible 19 \nidea. For one thing, std::shared_ptr offers no operator[], so indexing into the 20 \narray requires awkward expressions based on pointer arithmetic . For another, 21 \nstd::shared_ptr supports derived-to-base pointer conversions that make sense 22 \nfor single objects, but that open holes in the type system when applied to arrays.  23 \n(For this reason, the std::unique_ptr<T[]> API prohibits such conversions .) 24 \nMost importantly, given the variety of C++11 alternatives to built -in arrays (e.g., 25 \nstd::array, std::vector, std::string), declaring a smart pointer to a  dumb 26 \narray is almost always a sign of bad design. 27 \nThings to Remember 28 \n std::shared_ptrs offer convenience approaching that of garbage collection 29 \nfor the shared lifetime management of arbitrary resources. 30\n\n#### 第 164 页原文\n\nPage 164 \n \n Compared to std::unique_ptr, std::shared_ptr objects are twice as big, 1 \nincur overhead for control blocks, and require atomic reference count manipu-2 \nlations. 3 \n Default resource destruction is via delete, but custom deleters are supported. 4 \nThe type of the deleter is independent of the type of the std::shared_ptr. 5 \n Avoid creating std::shared_ptrs from variables of raw pointer type. 6 \n Item 22: Use std::weak_ptr for std::shared_ptr-like 7 \npointers that can dangle. 8 \nParadoxically, it can be convenient to have a smart pointer that acts like a 9 \nstd::shared_ptr (see Item 21), but that doesn’t participate in the shared own-10 \nership of the pointed -to resource. In other words, a pointer  like 11 \nstd::shared_ptr that doesn’t affect an object’s reference count. This kind of  12 \nsmart pointer has to contend with a problem unknown to std::shared_ptrs: the 13 \npossibility that what it points to has been destroyed.  A truly smart pointer would 14 \ndeal with this problem by tracking when it dangles, i.e., when the object i t is sup-15 \nposed to point to  no longer exists . That’s precisely the kind of smart pointer 16 \nstd::weak_ptr is.  17 \nYou may be wondering how a std::weak_ptr could be useful . Y ou’ll probably 18 \nwonder even more when you examine the std::weak_ptr API. It looks anything 19 \nbut smart. std::weak_ptrs can’t be dereferenced, nor can they be tested for null-20 \nness. That’s because  std::weak_ptr isn’t a standalone smart pointer. It’s an 21 \naugmentation of std::shared_ptr.  22 \nThe relationship begins at birth. std::weak_ptrs are typically created from 23 \nstd::shared_ptrs. They point to the same place as the std::shared_ptrs ini-24 \ntializing them, but they don’t affect the reference count of the object they point to: 25 \nauto spw =                       // after spw is constructed, 26 \n  std::make_shared<Widget>();    // the pointed-to Widget's 27 \n                                 // ref count (RC) is 1. (See 28 \n                                 // Item 23 for info on 29 \n                                 // std::make_shared.) 30 \n… 31\n\n#### 第 165 页原文\n\nPage 165 \n \nstd::weak_ptr<Widget> wpw(spw);  // wpw points to same Widget 1 \n                                 // as spw. RC remains 1 2 \n… 3 \nspw = nullptr;                   // RC goes to 0, and the 4 \n                                 // Widget is destroyed. 5 \n                                 // wpw now dangles 6 \nstd::weak_ptrs that dangle are said to have expired. You can test for this direc t-7 \nly, 8 \nif (!wpw.expired()) …            // if wpw points to an object… 9 \nbut often what you desire is a check to see if a  std::weak_ptr has expired and, if 10 \nit hasn’t (i.e., if it’s not dangling), to access the object it points to. Alas, this is easier 11 \ndesired than done . Because std::weak_ptrs lack s dereferencing operations, 12 \nthere’s no way to write the code. Even if there were, separating the check and the 13 \ndereference would introduce a race condition: between the call to expired and 14 \nthe dereferencing action, another thread might reassign or destroy  the last 15 \nstd::shared_ptr pointing to the object, thus causing that object to be destroyed. 16 \nIn that case, your dereference would yield undefined behavior. 17 \nWhat you need is an atomic operation that checks to see if the std::weak_ptr has 18 \nexpired and, if not, gives you access to the object it points to. This is done by crea t-19 \ning a std::shared_ptr from the std::weak_ptr. The operation comes in two 20 \nforms, depending on what you’d like to have h appen if the std::weak_ptr has 21 \nexpired when you try to create a std::shared_ptr from it. One form is 22 \nstd::weak_ptr::lock, which returns a std::shared_ptr. The 23 \nstd::shared_ptr is null if the std::weak_ptr has expired: 24 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw1 = wpw.lock();  // if wpw's expired, 25 \n                                            // spw1 is null 26 \nauto spw2 = wpw.lock();                     // same as above,  27 \n                                            // but uses auto 28 \nThe other form is the std::shared_ptr constructor taking a std::weak_ptr as 29 \nan argument.  In this case, if the std::weak_ptr has expired, an exception is 30 \nthrown: 31\n\n#### 第 166 页原文\n\nPage 166 \n \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw3(wpw);    // if wpw's expired, 1 \n                                      // throw std::bad_weak_ptr 2 \nBut you’re probably still wondering about how std::weak_ptrs can be useful . 3 \nConsider a factory function that produces smart pointers to read -only objects 4 \nbased on a unique ID. In accord with Item 20’s advice regarding factory function  5 \nreturn types, it returns a std::unique_ptr:  6 \nstd::unique_ptr<const Widget> loadWidget(WidgetID id); 7 \nIf loadWidget is an expensive call (e.g., because it performs file or d atabase I/O) 8 \nand it’s common for IDs to be used repeatedly, a reasonable optimization would be 9 \nto write a function that does what loadWidget does, but also caches its results. 10 \nClogging the cache with  every Widget that has ever been requested can lead to 11 \nperformance problems of its own, however, so another reasonable optimization 12 \nwould be to destroy cached Widgets when they’re no longer in use. 13 \nFor this caching factory function, a std::unique_ptr return type is not a good fit. 14 \nThe caller should certainly rec eive a smart pointer to the cached object, and the 15 \ncaller should certainly determine the lifetime of that object, but the cache needs a 16 \npointer to the object, too. The cache’s pointer needs to be able to detect when it 17 \ndangles, because when a factory clien t is finished using an object returned by the 18 \nfactory, that object will be destroyed, and th e corresponding cache entry will dan-19 \ngle. The cached pointer should therefore be a std::weak_ptr—a pointer that can 20 \ndetect when it dangles . That means that th e factory’s return type should be a 21 \nstd::shared_ptr, because std::weak_ptrs can detect when they dangle only 22 \nwhen an object’s lifetime is managed by std::shared_ptrs.  23 \nHere’s a quick-and-dirty implementation of a caching version of loadWidget: 24 \nstd::shared_ptr<const Widget> fastLoadWidget(WidgetID id) 25 \n{ 26 \n  using SPType = std::shared_ptr<const Widget>; 27 \n  using WPType = std::weak_ptr<const Widget>; 28 \n  static std::unordered_map<WidgetID, WPType> cache; 29 \n  auto retPtr = cache[id].lock();     // retPtr points to cached 30 \n                                      // object or is null 31\n\n#### 第 167 页原文\n\nPage 167 \n \n  if (!retPtr) {                                   // if not in 1 \n                                                   // cache, 2 \n    retPtr = static_cast<SPType>(loadWidget(id));  // load it  3 \n    cache[id] = static_cast<WPType>(retPtr);       // cache it 4 \n  } 5 \n  return retPtr; 6 \n} 7 \nThis implementation employs one of C++11’s hash table containers 8 \n(std::unordered_map), though it doesn’t show the WidgetID hashing and equal-9 \nity-comparison functions that would also have to be present.  10 \nThere are two smart pointer type conversions in the code , both made obvious by 11 \nthe use of static_casts. In the assignment to retPtr (a std::shared_ptr), the 12 \nstd::unique_ptr returned from loadWidget is used to create a temporary 13 \nstd::shared_ptr (via the alias SPType) to act as the source of the assignment. In 14 \nthe following line, retPtr (still a std::shared_ptr) is used to create a temp o-15 \nrary std::weak_ptr (via the alias WPType) that is moved into the cache (via a s-16 \nsignment to cache[id]). Neither cast is doing anything underhanded. Each simply 17 \ncauses a smart pointer object of one type to be constructed from a smart pointer 18 \nobject of a different type. 19 \nThis implementation of fastLoadWidget ignores the fact that  the cache may ac-20 \ncumulate expired std::weak_ptrs corresponding to Widgets that are no longer 21 \nin use (and have therefore been destroyed) . The implementation can be refined, 22 \nbut rather than spend time on an issue that lends no additional insight into 23 \nstd::weak_ptrs, let’s consider a s econd use case : the Observer design pattern. 24 \nThe primary components of this pattern are subjects (objects whose state may 25 \nchange) and observers (objects to be notified when state changes occur).  In most 26 \nimplementations, each subject contains a data member holding pointers to its ob-27 \nservers. That makes it easy for subjects to issue state change notifications. Subjects 28 \nhave no interest in controlling the lifetime of their observers (i.e., when they’re 29 \ndestroyed), but they have a great in terest in making sure that if an observer gets 30 \ndestroyed, subjects don’t try to subsequently access it. A reasonable design is for 31 \neach subject to hold a container of std::weak_ptrs to its observers, thus making 32\n\n#### 第 168 页原文\n\nPage 168 \n \nit possible for the subject to ensure that t he pointers don’t dangle when it tries to 1 \nuse them. 2 \nAs a final example of std::weak_ptrs utility, consider a data structure with o b-3 \njects A, B, and C in it, where A and C share ownership of B and therefore hold 4 \nstd::shared_ptrs to it: 5 \n6 \nSuppose it’d be useful to also have a pointer from B back to A. What kind of pointer 7 \nshould this be?  8 \n9 \nThere are three choices: 10 \n A raw pointer.  With this approach, if A is destroyed, but C continues to point 11 \nto B, B will contain a pointer to A that will dangle. B won’t be able to detect that, 12 \nso B may inadvertently dereference the dangling pointer. That would yield un-13 \ndefined behavior. 14 \n A std::shared_ptr. In this design, A and B contain  std::shared_ptrs to one 15 \nanother. The resulting std::shared_ptr cycle (A points to B and B points to 16 \nA) will prevent both A and B from being destroyed. Even if A and B are u n-17 \nreachable from other program data structures  (i.e., because C no longer points 18 \nto B), each will have a reference count of one. If that happens, A and B will have 19 \nbeen leaked, for all practical purposes: it will be impossible for the program to 20 \naccess them, yet their resources will never be reclaimed. 21 \n A std::weak_ptr. This avoids both problems above. If A is destroyed, B’s point-22 \ner back to it will dangle, but B will be able to detect that. Furthermore, though 23 \nA and B will point to one another, B’s pointer won’t affect A’s reference count, 24 \nhence can’t keep A from being destroyed when std::shared_ptrs no longer 25 \npoint to it. 26 \nA \n B \n C \nstd::shared_ptr \n std::shared_ptr \nA \n B \n C \nstd::shared_ptr \n std::shared_ptr \n \n ???\n\n#### 第 169 页原文\n\nPage 169 \n \nThe std::weak_ptr is clearly the best of these choices. However, it’s worth no t-1 \ning that the need to employ std::weak_ptrs to break prospective cycles of 2 \nstd::shared_ptrs is not terr ibly common. In strictly hierarchal data structures 3 \nsuch as trees, child nodes are typically owned only by their parents. When a parent 4 \nnode is destroyed, its child nodes should be destroyed, too. Links from parents to 5 \nchildren are thus generally best represented by std::unique_ptrs. Back -links 6 \nfrom children to parents can be safely implemented as raw pointers, because a 7 \nchild node should never have a lifetime longer than its parent . There’s thus no risk 8 \nof a child node dereferencing a dangling parent pointer. 9 \nOf course, not all pointer -based data structures are strictly hierarchical, and when 10 \nthat’s the case, as well as in situations such as caching and the implementation of 11 \nlists of observers, it’s nice to know that std::weak_ptr stands at the ready. 12 \nFrom an efficiency perspective, the std:weak_ptr story is essentially the same as 13 \nthat for  std::shared_ptr. std::weak_ptr objects are the same size as 14 \nstd::shared_ptr objects, they make use of the same control blocks as 15 \nstd::shared_ptrs (see Item 21), and operations such as construction, destru c-16 \ntion, and assignment involve atomic reference count manipulations. That probably 17 \nsurprises you, because I wrote at the beginning of this Item that std::weak_ptrs 18 \ndon’t  participate in reference counting. Except that’s not quite what I wrote. What 19 \nI wrote was that std::weak_ptrs don’t participate in the shared ownership of ob-20 \njects and hence don’t affect the pointed-to object’s reference count. There’s actually 21 \na second reference count in the control block, and it’s this second reference count 22 \nthat std::weak_ptrs manipulate. For details, continue on to Item 23. 23 \nThings to Remember 24 \n Use std::weak_ptr for std::shared_ptr-like pointers that can dangle. 25 \n Potential use cases for std::weak_ptr include caching, observer lists, and the 26 \nprevention of std::shared_ptr cycles. 27\n\n#### 第 170 页原文\n\nPage 170 \n \n Item 23: Prefer std::make_unique and std::make_shared 1 \nto direct use of new. 2 \nLet’s begin by leveling the playing field for std::make_unique and 3 \nstd::make_shared. std::make_shared is part of C++11, but, sadly, 4 \nstd::make_unique isn’t. It joined the Standard Library as of C++14. If you’re us-5 \ning C++11, never fear , because a  basic version of std::make_unique is easy to 6 \nwrite yourself. Here, look: 7 \ntemplate<typename T, typename... Args> 8 \nstd::unique_ptr<T> make_unique(Args&&... args) 9 \n{ 10 \n  return std::unique_ptr<T>(new T(std::forward<Args>(args)...)); 11 \n} 12 \nAs you can see, make_unique simply perfect-forwards its arguments to  the con-13 \nstructor of the object being created , constructs a std::unique_ptr from the raw 14 \npointer new produces, and returns the std::unique_ptr so created. This form of 15 \nthe function  doesn’t support arrays or custom deleters (see Item 20), but it 16 \ndemonstrates that, with only a little effort, you can create make_unique yourself, 17 \nif you need to.† 18 \nstd::make_unique and std::make_shared are two of the three make functions: 19 \nfunctions that take an arbitrary set of arguments, perfect-forward them to the con-20 \nstructor for a dynamically-allocated object, and return a smart pointer to th at ob-21 \nject. The third make function is std::allocate_shared. It acts just like 22 \nstd:make_shared, except its first argument is an allocator object to be used for 23 \nthe dynamic memory allocation. 24 \nEven the most trivial comparison of smart pointer creation using and not using a 25 \nmake function reveals the first reason why using such functions is preferable. Con-26 \nsider: 27 \n                                                             \n† To create a full -featured make_unique with the smallest effort possible, search for the \nstandardization document that gave rise to  it, then copy the implementation you’ll find \nthere. The document you want is N3588 by Stephan T. Lavavej, dated 2013 -03-15.\n\n#### 第 171 页原文\n\nPage 171 \n \nauto upw1(std::make_unique<Widget>());      // with make func 1 \nstd::unique_ptr<Widget> upw2(new Widget);   // without make func 2 \n 3 \nauto spw1(std::make_shared<Widget>());      // with make func 4 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw2(new Widget);   // without make func 5 \nI’ve highlighted the essen tial difference: the versions using new repeat the type 6 \nbeing created, but the  make functions don’t. Repeating types  runs afoul of a key 7 \ntenet of software engineering: code duplication  should be avoided . Duplication in 8 \nsource code increases compilation times, can lead to bloated object code, and ge n-9 \nerally renders a code base more difficult to work with. It often evolves into incon-10 \nsistency in a code base, and inconsistency in a code base often leads to bugs. Be-11 \nsides, typing something twice takes more effort than typing it once, and who’s not 12 \na fan of reducing their typing burden? 13 \nThe second reason to prefer make functions has to do with exception safety. Su p-14 \npose we have a function to process a Widget in accord with some priority: 15 \nvoid processWidget(std::shared_ptr<Widget> spw, int priority); 16 \nPassing the std::shared_ptr by value may look suspicious, but Item 17 explains 17 \nthat if processWidget always makes a copy of the std::shared_ptr (e.g., by 18 \nstoring it in a data structure tracking Widgets that have been processed), this can 19 \nbe a reasonable design choice. 20 \nNow suppose we have a function to compute the relevant priority,  21 \nint computePriority(); 22 \nand we use that in a call to processWidget that uses new instead of 23 \nstd::make_shared: 24 \nprocessWidget(std::shared_ptr<Widget>(new Widget),  // potential 25 \n              computePriority());                   // resource 26 \n                                                    // leak! 27 \nAs the comment indicates, this code could leak the Widget conjured up by new. But 28 \nhow? Both the calling code and the called function are using std::shared_ptrs, 29 \nand std::shared_ptrs are designed to prevent resource leaks. They automat i-30\n\n#### 第 172 页原文\n\nPage 172 \n \ncally destroy what they point to when the last std::shared_ptr pointing there 1 \ngoes away.  If everybody is using std::shared_ptrs everywhere, how can this 2 \ncode leak? 3 \nThe answer has to do with compilers’ translation of source code into object code. 4 \nAt runtime, the arguments for a function must be evaluated before the function can 5 \nbe invoked, so in  the call to processWidget, the following things must occur b e-6 \nfore processWidget can begin execution: 7 \n The expression “new Widget” must be evaluated, i.e., a Widget must be creat-8 \ned on the heap. 9 \n The constructor for the std::shared_ptr<Widget> responsible for mana g-10 \ning the pointer produced by new must be executed.  11 \n computePriority must run. 12 \nCompilers are not required to generate code that executes them in this order. “new 13 \nWidget” must be executed before the std::shared_ptr constructor may be 14 \ncalled, because the result of that new is used as an argument to that constructor, 15 \nbut computePriority may be executed before those calls, after them, or, crucial-16 \nly, between them. That is, compilers may emit code to execute the operations 17 \nabove in this order: 18 \n1. Perform “new Widget”. 19 \n2. Execute computePriority. 20 \n3. Run std::shared_ptr constructor. 21 \nIf such code is generated and, at runtime, computePriority produces an excep-22 \ntion, the dynamically allocated Widget from Step 1 will be leaked, because it w ill 23 \nnever be stored in the std::shared_ptr that’s supposed to start  managing it in 24 \nStep 3. 25 \nUsing std::make_shared avoids this problem. Calling code would look like this: 26 \nprocessWidget(std::make_shared<Widget>(),   // no potential 27 \n              computePriority());           // resource leak 28\n\n#### 第 173 页原文\n\nPage 173 \n \nAt runtime, either std::make_shared or computePriority will be called first. If 1 \nit’s std::make_shared, the raw pointer to the dynamically allocated Widget is 2 \nsafely stored in the returned std::shared_ptr before computePriority is 3 \ncalled. If computePriority then yields an exception, the std::shared_ptr de-4 \nstructor will see to it that the Widget it owns is destroyed.  And if computePrior-5 \nity is called first and yields an exception, std::make_shared will not be invoked, 6 \nand there will hence be no dynamically allocated Widget to worry about. 7 \nIf we replace std::shared_ptr and std::make_shared with 8 \nstd::unique_ptr and std::make_unique, exactly the same reasoning appl ies. 9 \nUsing std::make_unique instead of new is thus just as important in writing e x-10 \nception-safe code as using std::make_shared. 11 \nA special feature of std::make_shared (compared to direct use of new) is i m-12 \nproved efficiency. Using std::make_shared allows compilers to generate smaller, 13 \nfaster code that  employs leaner data structures. Consider the following direct use 14 \nof new: 15 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw(new Widget); 16 \nIt’s obvious that this code entails a memory allocation, but it actually requires two. 17 \nItem 21 explains that every std::shared_ptr points to a control block contai n-18 \ning, among other things, the reference count for the pointed -to object. Memory for 19 \nthis control block is allocated in the std::shared_ptr constructor. Direct use of 20 \nnew, then, requires one memory allocation for the Widget and a second allocation 21 \nfor the control block. 22 \nIf std::make_shared is used instead, 23 \nauto spw = std::make_shared<Widget>(); 24 \none allocation suffices. That’s because std::make_shared allocates a single 25 \nchunk of memory to hold both the Widget object and the control block. This opt i-26 \nmization reduces the static size of the program, because the code contains only 27 \none memory allocation call, and it increases the speed of the executable code, b e-28 \ncause memory is allocated only once. Furthermore, using std::make_shared ob-29\n\n#### 第 174 页原文\n\nPage 174 \n \nviates the need for some of the bookkeeping information in the control block, thus 1 \nreducing the total memory footprint for the program.  2 \nThe efficiency analysis for std::make_shared is equally applicable to 3 \nstd::allocate_shared, so the performance advantages of std::make_shared 4 \nextend to that make function, as well. 5 \nClearly, the argument  for preferring make functions over direct use of new is a 6 \nstrong one. Despite the ir software engineering, exception-safety, and efficiency 7 \nadvantages, however, this Item’s guidance is to prefer the make functions, not to 8 \nrely on them exclusively. That’s because there are circumstances where they can’t 9 \nor shouldn’t be used.  10 \nFor example, none of the make functions permit the specification of custom 11 \ndeleters (see Items 20 and 21), but both std::unique_ptr and 12 \nstd::shared_ptr have constructors that do. Given a custom deleter for a Widg-13 \net, 14 \nauto widgetDeleter = [](Widget* pw) { … }; 15 \ncreating a smart pointer using it is straightforward using new: 16 \nstd::unique_ptr<Widget, decltype(widgetDeleter)> 17 \n  upw(new Widget, widgetDeleter); 18 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw(new Widget, widgetDeleter); 19 \nThere’s no way to do the same thing with a make function. 20 \nItem 7 explains that when creating an object whose type overloads constructors 21 \nboth with and without std::initializer_list parameters, creating the object 22 \nusing braces calls the std::initializer_list constructor, while creating the 23 \nobject using parentheses calls the non-std::initializer_list constructor. The 24 \nmake functions perfect-forward their parameters to an object’s constructor, but do 25 \nthey do so using parentheses or using braces? For some types, this seemingly trivi-26 \nal syntactic detail can make a big difference. For example, in these calls, 27 \nauto upv = std::make_unique<std::vector<int>>(10, 20); 28 \nauto spv = std::make_shared<std::vector<int>>(10, 20); 29\n\n#### 第 175 页原文\n\nPage 175 \n \ndo the resulting smart pointers point to std::vectors with 10 elements, each of 1 \nvalue 20, or to std::vectors with two elements, one with value 10 and the other 2 \nwith value 20? Or is the result indeterminate? 3 \nThe good news is that it’s not indeterminate: both calls create std::vectors of 4 \nsize 10 with all values set to 20. That means that within the make functions, the 5 \nperfect forwarding code uses parentheses, not braces. The bad news is that if you 6 \nwant to achieve the effect of usin g braces, you must use new directly. Using a make 7 \nfunction would require the ability to perfect-forward a braced initializer through a 8 \nfunction, but, as Item 32 explains, braced initializers can’t be perfect-forwarded. 9 \nFor std::unique_ptr, these two scenarios (custom deleters and braced initializ-10 \ners) are the only ones where its make function can’t be used . For  11 \nstd::shared_ptr and its make functions, there are two more. Both are edge cas-12 \nes, but some developers live on the edge, and you may be one of them. 13 \nSome classes define their own versions of operator new and operator delete. 14 \nThe presence of these functions implies that the global memor y allocation and 15 \ndeallocation routines for objects of these types are inappropriate. Often, these 16 \nclass-specific routines are designed only to allocate and deallocate chunks of 17 \nmemory of precisely the size of objects of the class, e.g., operator new and opera-18 \ntor delete for class Widget are often designed only to handle allocation and 19 \ndeallocation of chunks of memory of exactly size sizeof(Widget). Such routines 20 \nare a poor fit for std::shared_ptr’s support for custom allocation (via 21 \nstd::allocate_shared) and  deallocation (via custom deleters), because the 22 \namount of memory that std::allocate_shared requests isn’t the size of the d y-23 \nnamically allocated object, it’s the size of that object plus the size of a  control 24 \nblock. Consequently, using make functions to c reate objects of types with class -25 \nspecific versions of operator new and operator delete is typically a poor idea. 26 \nThe size and speed  advantages of std::make_shared vis-à-vis direct use of new 27 \nstem from  std::shared_ptr’s control block being placed in the same chunk of 28 \nmemory as the managed object. When that object’s reference count goes to zero, 29 \nthe object is destroyed (i.e., its destructor is called). However, the memory it occ u-30", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.176–p.200", "pages": "p.176–p.200", "raw_text": "#### 第 176 页原文\n\nPage 176 \n \npies can’t be rel eased until the control block has also been destroyed, because the 1 \nsame chunk of dynamically allocated memory contains both. 2 \nAs I noted, the control block contains bookkeeping information beyond just the 3 \nreference count itself. The reference count tracks how many std::shared_ptrs 4 \nrefer to the control block, but the co ntrol block contains a second reference count, 5 \none that tallies how many std::weak_ptrs refer to the control block. This second 6 \nreference count is known as the weak count.† When a std::weak_ptr checks to 7 \nsee if it has expired (see Item 21), it does so by examining the reference count (not 8 \nthe weak count) in the control block that it refers to. If the reference count is zero  9 \n(i.e., if the pointed-to object has no std::shared_ptrs referring to it and has thus 10 \nbeen destroyed), the std::weak_ptr has expired. Otherwise, it hasn’t.  11 \nAs long as std::weak_ptrs refer to a control block (i.e., the weak count is greater 12 \nthan zero), that control block must continue to exist. And as long as a control block 13 \nexists, the dynamically allocated memory containing it must remain allocated. The 14 \nmemory allocated by a std::shared_ptr make function, then, can’t be dealloca t-15 \ned until the last std::shared_ptr and the last std::weak_ptr referring to it has 16 \nbeen destroyed. 17 \nWhen the final std::shared_ptr referring to a control block is destroyed, the 18 \nobject it referred to is destructed, but the memory that object occupied remains 19 \nallocated until the final std::weak_ptr referring to the control block is  also de-20 \nstroyed. If the object type is quite large and the time between destruction of the 21 \nlast std::shared_ptr and the last std::weak_ptr is significant, the result could 22 \nbe notable lags between when an object is destroyed and wh en the memory it o c-23 \ncupied is freed: 24 \n                                                             \n† In practice, the value of the weak count isn’t always equal to the number of outstanding \nstd::weak_ptrs, because library implementers have found ways to encode additional \ninformation that permits the generation of better code. For purposes of this Item, we’ll i g-\nnore this discrepancy and assume that the weak count’s value is the number of \nstd::weak_ptrs referring to the control block.\n\n#### 第 177 页原文\n\nPage 177 \n \nclass ReallyBigType { … }; 1 \nauto pBigObj =                          // create very large 2 \n  std::make_shared<ReallyBigType>();    // object via 3 \n                                        // std::make_shared 4 \n…            // create std::shared_ptrs and std::weak_ptrs to 5 \n             // large object, use them to work with it 6 \n…            // final std::shared_ptr to object destroyed here, 7 \n             // but std::weak_ptrs to it remain 8 \n…            // during this period, memory formerly occupied 9 \n             // by large object remains allocated 10 \n…            // final std::weak_ptr to object destroyed here; 11 \n             // memory for control block and object is released 12 \nWith a direct use of new, the memory for the ReallyBigType object can be r e-13 \nleased as soon as the last std::shared_ptr to it is destroyed: 14 \nclass ReallyBigType { … };              // as before 15 \nstd::shared_ptr<ReallyBigType> pBigObj(new ReallyBigType); 16 \n                                        // create very large 17 \n                                        // object via new 18 \n…            // as before, create std::shared_ptrs and  19 \n             // std::weak_ptrs to object, use them with it 20 \n…            // final std::shared_ptr to object destroyed here, 21 \n             // but std::weak_ptrs to it remain; 22 \n             // memory for object is deallocated 23 \n…            // during this period, only memory for the 24 \n             // control block remains allocated 25 \n…            // final std::weak_ptr to object destroyed here; 26 \n             // memory for control block is released 27 \nShould you find yourself in a situation where use of std::make_shared is impos-28 \nsible or inappropriate, you’ll want to guard yourself against the kind of exception -29 \nsafety problems we saw earlier. The best way to do that is to make sure tha t when 30 \nyou use new directly, you pass the result to a smart pointer constructor in a state-31 \nment that does nothing else . This prevents compilers from generating code corr e-32 \nsponding to operations being inserted between the use  of new and the call of the 33 \nconstructor for the smart pointer that will manage the newed object.  34\n\n#### 第 178 页原文\n\nPage 178 \n \nAs an example, consider a minor revision to the exception -unsafe call to the pro-1 \ncessWidget function we examined earlier. This time, we’ll specify a custom 2 \ndeleter: 3 \nvoid cusDel(Widget *ptr);                      // custom deleter 4 \nHere’s the exception-unsafe call: 5 \nprocessWidget(                                   // as before, 6 \n  std::shared_ptr<Widget>(new Widget, cusDel),   // potential 7 \n  computePriority()                              // resource 8 \n);                                               // leak! 9 \nRecall: if computePriority is called after “ new Widget” but before the 10 \nstd::shared_ptr constructor, and if computePriority yields an exception, the 11 \ndynamically allocated Widget will be leaked. 12 \nIn this example , t he need for a custom deleter precludes use of 13 \nstd::make_shared, so the way to avoid the problem is to put the allocation of the 14 \nWidget and the construction of the std::shared_ptr into their own statement, 15 \nthen call processWidget with the std::shared_ptr object: 16 \nstd::shared_ptr<Widget> spw(new Widget, cusDel); 17 \nprocessWidget(spw, computePriority()); 18 \nThis works, because a std::shared_ptr assumes ownership of the raw pointer 19 \npassed to its constructor, even if that constructor yields an exception. In this e x-20 \nample, if pw’s constructor throws an exception (e.g., due to an inability to dynam i-21 \ncally allocate memory for a control block), it’s still guaranteed that cusDel will be 22 \ninvoked on the pointer resulting from “new Widget”. 23 \nThat’s interesting and worth knowing , but it’s also typically irrelevant, because in 24 \nmost cases, there’s no reason not to use a make function. And unless you have a 25 \ncompelling reason for doing otherwise, using a make function is what you should 26 \ndo. 27\n\n#### 第 179 页原文\n\nPage 179 \n \nThings to Remember 1 \n Compared to direct use of new, the make functions eliminate source code d u-2 \nplication,  improve exception safety, and, for std::make_shared and 3 \nstd::allocate_shared, generate code that’s smaller and faster. 4 \n Situations where use of the make functions is inappropriate include the need to 5 \nspecify custom deleters and a desire to pass braced initializers. 6 \n For std::shared_ptrs, additional situations where make functions may be 7 \nill-advised include (1) classes with custom memory management and (2) sy s-8 \ntems with memory concerns, very large objects, and std::weak_ptrs that 9 \noutlive the corresponding std::shared_ptrs. 10 \n Item 24: When using the Pimpl Idiom, define special mem-11 \nber functions in the implementation file. 12 \nIf you’ve ever had to combat excessive build times, you’re familiar with the Pimpl 13 \n(“pointer to implementation”) Idiom. That’s the technique whereby you replace the 14 \ndata members of a class with a pointer to an implementation cl ass (or struct), put 15 \nthe data members that used to be in the primary class into the implementation 16 \nclass, and access those data members indirectly through the pointer. For example, 17 \nsuppose Widget looks like this: 18 \nclass Widget {                     // in header \"widget.h\" 19 \npublic: 20 \n  Widget(); 21 \n  … 22 \nprivate: 23 \n  std::string name; 24 \n  std::vector<double> data; 25 \n  Gadget g1, g2, g3; 26 \n}; 27 \nBecause Widget’s data members are of types std::string, std::vector, and 28 \nGadget, headers for those types must be present for Widget to compile, and that 29 \nmeans that Widget clients must #include <string>, <vector>, and gadget.h. 30 \nThose headers increase the compilation time for Widget clients, plus they make 31 \nthose clients dependent on the contents of the headers. If a header ’s content 32 \nchanges, Widget clients must recompile. The standard headers <string> and 33\n\n#### 第 180 页原文\n\nPage 180 \n \n<vector> don’t change very often, but it could be that gadget.h is subject to fre-1 \nquent revision. 2 \nApplying the Pimpl Idiom in C++98 could have Widget replace its data members 3 \nwith a raw pointer to a struct that has been declared, but not defined: 4 \nclass Widget {                 // still in header \"widget.h\" 5 \npublic: 6 \n  Widget();  7 \n  ~Widget();                   // dtor is needed—see below 8 \n  … 9 \nprivate: 10 \n  struct Impl;                 // declare implementation struct 11 \n  Impl *pImpl;                 // and pointer to it 12 \n}; 13 \nBecause Widget no longer mentions the types std::string, std::vector, and 14 \nGadget, Widget clients no longer need to #include the headers for these types. 15 \nThat speeds compilation, and it also means that if something in these headers 16 \nchanges, Widget clients are unaffected. 17 \nA type that has been declared, but not defined, is known as an incomplete type . 18 \nWidget::Impl is such a type. There are very few things you can do with an i n-19 \ncomplete type, but declaring a pointer to it is one of them . The Pimpl Idiom takes 20 \nadvantage of that.  21 \nPart 1 of the Pimpl Idiom is the declaration of a data member that’s a pointer to an 22 \nincomplete type. Part 2 is the dynamic allocation and deallocation o f the object 23 \nthat holds the data members that used to be in  the original class.  The allocation 24 \nand deallocation code goes in the implementation file, e.g., for Widget, in widg-25 \net.cpp: 26 \n#include \"widget.h\"            // in impl. file \"widget.cpp\" 27 \n#include \"gadget.h\" 28 \n#include <string> 29 \n#include <vector> 30 \nstruct Widget::Impl {          // definition of Widget::Impl 31 \n  std::string name;            // with data members formerly 32 \n  std::vector<double> data;    // in Widget 33 \n  Gadget g1, g2, g3; 34 \n}; 35\n\n#### 第 181 页原文\n\nPage 181 \n \nWidget::Widget()               // allocate data members for 1 \n: pImpl(new Impl)              // this Widget object 2 \n{} 3 \nWidget::~Widget()              // destroy data members for 4 \n{ delete pImpl; }              // this object 5 \nHere I’m showing #include directives to make clear that the overall depende n-6 \ncies on the headers for std::string, std::vector, and Gadget continue to e x-7 \nist. However, these dependencies have been moved from widget.h (which is visi-8 \nble to and used by Widget clients) to widget.cpp (which is visible to and used 9 \nonly by the Widget implementer). I’ve also highlighted the code that dynamically 10 \nallocates and deallocates the Impl object. The need to deallocate this object when 11 \na Widget is destroyed is what necessitates the Widget destructor. 12 \nBut I’ve shown you C++98 code, and that reeks of a bygone millennium. It uses raw 13 \npointers and raw new and raw delete and it’s all just so…raw. This chapter is built 14 \non the idea that smart pointers are preferable to raw pointers, and if what we want 15 \nis to dynamically allocate a Widget::Impl object inside the Widget constructor 16 \nand have it destroyed at the same time the Widget is, std::unique_ptr (see 17 \nItem 20) is  precisely the tool we  need. Replacing the raw pImpl pointer with a 18 \nstd::unique_ptr yields this code for the header file, 19 \nclass Widget {                      // in \"widget.h\" 20 \npublic: 21 \n  Widget();  22 \n  … 23 \nprivate: 24 \n  struct Impl;  25 \n  std::unique_ptr<Impl> pImpl;      // use smart pointer 26 \n};                                  // instead of raw pointer 27 \nand this for the implementation file: 28 \n#include \"widget.h\"                 // in \"widget.cpp\" 29 \n#include \"gadget.h\" 30 \n#include <string> 31 \n#include <vector> 32 \nstruct Widget::Impl {               // as before 33 \n  std::string name;  34 \n  std::vector<double> data;  35\n\n#### 第 182 页原文\n\nPage 182 \n \n  Gadget g1, g2, g3; 1 \n}; 2 \nWidget::Widget()                    // per Item 23, create 3 \n: pImpl(std::make_unique<Impl>())   // std::unique_ptr 4 \n{}                                  // via std::make_unique 5 \nYou’ll note that the Widget destructor is no longer present . That’s because we 6 \nhave no code to put into it. std::unique_ptr automatically deletes what it points 7 \nto when it  (the std::unique_ptr) is destroyed, so we need not delete anything 8 \nourselves. That’s one of th e attractions of smart pointers: they eliminate the need 9 \nfor us to sully our hands with manual resource release. 10 \nThis code compiles, but, alas, the most trivial client use doesn’t: 11 \n#include \"widget.h\" 12 \nWidget w;                           // error! 13 \nThe error message you get depends on the compiler you’re using, but the text ge n-14 \nerally mentions something a bout applying sizeof or delete to an incomplete 15 \ntype. Those operations aren’t among the very few things you can do with such 16 \ntypes. 17 \nThis apparent failure of the Pimpl Idiom using std::unique_ptrs is alarming, 18 \nbecause (1) std::unique_ptr is advertised as  s upporting incomplete types, and 19 \n(2) the Pimpl Idiom is one of std::unique_ptrs most common use cases. Fort u-20 \nnately, getting the code to work is easy. All that’s required is a basic understanding 21 \nof the cause of the problem. 22 \nThe issue arises due to the code that’s generated when w is destroyed (e.g., goes 23 \nout of scope). At that point, its destructor is called. In the class definition using 24 \nstd::unique_ptr, we didn’t declare a destructor, because we didn’t have any 25 \ncode to put into it. In accord with the usual  rules for compiler -generated special 26 \nmember functions (see Item 19), the compiler generates a destructor for us. Wit h-27 \nin that destructor, the compiler inserts code to call the destructor for Widget’s da-28 \nta member, pImpl. pImpl is a std::unique_ptr<Widget::Impl>, i.e., a 29 \nstd::unique_ptr using the default deleter . The default deleter is a function that  30 \nuses delete on the raw pointer inside the std::unique_ptr. Prior to using de-31\n\n#### 第 183 页原文\n\nPage 183 \n \nlete, however, implementations typically have the default deleter employ C++11’s 1 \nstatic_assert to ensure that the raw pointer doesn’t point to an incomplete 2 \ntype. When the compiler generates code for the destruction of the Widget w, then, 3 \nit encounters a static_assert that fails, and that’s what leads to the error mes-4 \nsage.† This message is a ssociated with the point where w is destroyed, because 5 \nWidget’s destructor, like all compiler -generated special member functions, is i m-6 \nplicitly inline. The message itself often refers to the line where w is created, b e-7 \ncause it’s the source code explicitly creating the object that leads to its later implic-8 \nit destruction. 9 \nFixing the problem is easy. You just need to make sure that at the point where the 10 \ncode to destroy the std::unique_ptr<Widget::Impl> is generated, Widg-11 \net::Impl is a complete type. The type becomes complete when its definition has 12 \nbeen seen, and Widget::Impl is defined inside widget.cpp. The key to succes s-13 \nful compilation, then , is to have the compiler see the body of Widget’s destructor 14 \n(i.e., the place where the  compiler will generate code to destroy the 15 \nstd::unique_ptr data member) only inside widget.cpp after Widget::Impl 16 \nhas been defined.  17 \nArranging for that is simple. Declare (but don’t define!) Widget’s destructor in 18 \nwidget.h, 19 \nclass Widget {                     // as before, in \"widget.h\" 20 \npublic: 21 \n  Widget();  22 \n  ~Widget();                       // declaration only! 23 \n  … 24 \nprivate:                           // as before 25 \n  struct Impl;  26 \n  std::unique_ptr<Impl> pImpl;  27 \n};   28 \n                                                             \n† Implementations need not have a static_assert in the default deleter. Nor, for that \nmatter, are they required to  reject code deleting an incomplete type. But all implement a-\ntions I’m familiar with take steps to ensure that code involving the destruction of a \nstd::unique_ptr to an incomplete type won’t compile.\n\n#### 第 184 页原文\n\nPage 184 \n \nand define it in widget.cpp after Widget::Impl has been defined: 1 \n#include \"widget.h\"                // as before, in \"widget.cpp\" 2 \n#include \"gadget.h\" 3 \n#include <string> 4 \n#include <vector> 5 \nstruct Widget::Impl {              // as before, definition of 6 \n  std::string name;                // Widget::Impl 7 \n  std::vector<double> data;  8 \n  Gadget g1, g2, g3; 9 \n}; 10 \nWidget::Widget()                   // as before 11 \n: pImpl(std::make_unique<Impl>())  12 \n{} 13 \nWidget::~Widget()                  // ~Widget definition 14 \n{} 15 \nThis works well, and it requires the least typing, but if you want to emphasize that 16 \nthe compiler-generated destructor would do the right thing—that the only reason 17 \nyou declared it was to cause its definition to be generated in Widget’s implemen-18 \ntation file, you can define the destructor body with =default: 19 \nWidget::~Widget() = default;       // same effect as above 20 \nClasses using the Pimpl Idiom are natural candidates for move support, because 21 \ncompiler-generated move operations do exactly what’s desired : perform a move 22 \non the underlying std::unique_ptr. As Item 19 explains, the declaration of a de-23 \nstructor in Widget prevents compilers from generating the move operations, so if 24 \nyou want move support, you must declare the functions yourself. Given that the 25 \ncompiler-generated versions would behave correctly, you might be tempted to im-26 \nplement them thusly: 27 \nclass Widget {                                 // still in 28 \npublic:                                        // \"widget.h\" 29 \n  Widget();  30 \n  ~Widget(); 31 \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs) = default;              // right idea, 32 \n  Widget& operator=(Widget&& rhs) = default;   // wrong code! 33 \n  … 34\n\n#### 第 185 页原文\n\nPage 185 \n \nprivate:                                       // as before 1 \n  struct Impl;  2 \n  std::unique_ptr<Impl> pImpl;  3 \n};   4 \nThis approach lead s to the same kind of problem as declaring the class without a 5 \ndestructor, and for the same fundamental reason. The compiler -generated move 6 \nassignment operator needs to destroy the object pointed to by pImpl before reas-7 \nsigning it, but in the Widget header file, pImpl points to an incomplete type.  The 8 \nsituation is different for the move constructor. The problem there is that compilers 9 \ntypically generate code to destroy pImpl in the event that an exception arises i n-10 \nside the move constructor, and destroying pImpl requires that Impl be complete.  11 \nBecause the problem is the same as before, so is the fix: move the definition of the 12 \nmove operations into the implementation file: 13 \nclass Widget {                       // still in \"widget.h\" 14 \npublic:  15 \n  Widget();  16 \n  ~Widget(); 17 \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs);              // declarations 18 \n  Widget& operator=(Widget&& rhs);   // only 19 \n  … 20 \nprivate:                             // as before 21 \n  struct Impl;  22 \n  std::unique_ptr<Impl> pImpl;  23 \n};   24 \n 25 \n#include <string>                    // as before,  26 \n…                                    // in \"widget.cpp\" 27 \nstruct Widget::Impl { … };           // as before 28 \nWidget::Widget()                     // as before 29 \n: pImpl(std::make_unique<Impl>())  30 \n{} 31 \nWidget::~Widget() = default;         // as before 32 \nWidget::Widget(Widget&& rhs) = default;              // defini- 33 \nWidget& Widget::operator=(Widget&& rhs) = default;   // tions 34\n\n#### 第 186 页原文\n\nPage 186 \n \nThe Pimpl Idiom is a way to reduce compilation dependencies between a class’s 1 \nimplementation and the class’s clients, but, conceptually, use of the idiom doesn’t 2 \nchange what the class represents. The original Widget class contained 3 \nstd::string, std::vector, and Gadget data members, and, assuming that 4 \nGadgets, like std::strings and s td::vectors, can be copied, it would make 5 \nsense for Widget to support the copy operations. We have to write these functions 6 \nourselves, because (1) compilers won’t generate copy operati ons for classes with 7 \nmove-only types like std::unique_ptr and (2) even if they did, the generated 8 \nfunctions would copy only the std::unique_ptr (i.e., perform a shallow copy ), 9 \nand we want to copy what the pointer points to (i.e., perform a deep copy). 10 \nIn a ritual that is by now familiar, we declare the functions in the header file and 11 \nimplement them in the implementation file: 12 \nclass Widget {                       // still in \"widget.h\" 13 \npublic:  14 \n  …                                  // other funcs, as before 15 \n  Widget(const Widget& rhs);              // declarations 16 \n  Widget& operator=(const Widget& rhs);   // only 17 \nprivate:                                  // as before 18 \n  struct Impl;  19 \n  std::unique_ptr<Impl> pImpl;  20 \n};   21 \n 22 \n#include \"widget.h\"                  // as before,  23 \n…                                    // in \"widget.cpp\" 24 \nstruct Widget::Impl { … };           // as before 25 \nWidget::~Widget() = default;         // other funcs, as before 26 \nWidget::Widget(const Widget& rhs)              // copy ctor 27 \n: pImpl(std::make_unique<Impl>(*rhs.pImpl)) 28 \n{} 29 \nWidget& Widget::operator=(const Widget& rhs)   // copy operator= 30 \n{ 31 \n  if (this != &rhs) {                // detect self-assignment 32 \n    *pImpl = *rhs.pImpl; 33 \n  } 34\n\n#### 第 187 页原文\n\nPage 187 \n \n  return *this; 1 \n} 2 \nBoth function implementations are conventional. In each case, we simply copy  the 3 \nfields of the Impl struct from the source object ( rhs) to the destination  object 4 \n(*this). Rather than copy the fields one by one, we take advantage of the fact that 5 \ncompilers will create the copy operations for Impl, and these operations will copy 6 \neach field automatically. We thus implement Widget’s copy operations by calling 7 \nWidget::Impl’s compiler -generated copy operations. In the copy constructor, 8 \nnote that we still follow the advice of Item 23 to prefer use of std::make_unique 9 \nover direct use of new, and in the copy assignment operator, we adhere to common 10 \nC++ convention by doing nothing if we detect that an o bject is being assigned to 11 \nitself. 12 \nFor purposes of implementing the Pimpl Idiom, std::unique_ptr is the smart 13 \npointer to use, because the pImpl pointer inside an object (e.g., inside a Widget) 14 \nhas exclusive ownership of the corresponding implementation obj ect (e.g.,  the 15 \nWidget::Impl object). Still, it’s interesting to note that if we  were to use 16 \nstd::shared_ptr instead of std::unique_ptr for pImpl, we’d find that the ad-17 \nvice of this Item no longer applied. There’d be no need to declare a destructor in 18 \nWidget, and with out a user-declared destructor, compilers would happily gener-19 \nate the move operations, which would do exactly what we’d want them to. That is, 20 \ngiven this code in widget.h, 21 \nclass Widget {                     // in \"widget.h\" 22 \npublic: 23 \n  Widget();  24 \n  …                                // no declarations for dtor 25 \n                                   // or move operations 26 \nprivate: 27 \n  struct Impl;  28 \n  std::shared_ptr<Impl> pImpl;     // std::shared_ptr 29 \n};                                 // instead of std::unique_ptr 30 \nand this client code that #includes widget.h, 31 \nWidget w1; 32 \nauto w2(std::move(w1));            // move-construct w2 33 \nw1 = std::move(w2);                // move-assign w1 34\n\n#### 第 188 页原文\n\nPage 188 \n \neverything would compile and run as we’d hope: w1 would be default-constructed, 1 \nits value would be moved into w2, that value would be moved back into w1, and 2 \nthen both w1 and w2 would be destroyed (thus causing the pointed -to Widg-3 \net::Impl object to be destroyed). 4 \nThe difference in behavior between std::unique_ptr and std::shared_ptr 5 \nfor pImpl pointers stems from the differing ways that these smart pointers  sup-6 \nport custom deleters. For std::unique_ptr, the type of the deleter is part of the 7 \ntype of the smart pointer, and this makes it possible for compilers to generate 8 \nsmaller runtime data structures and faster runtime code. A  consequence of this 9 \ngreater efficiency is that pointed -to types must be complete when compiler -10 \ngenerated special functions (e.g., destructors or move operations) are used. For 11 \nstd::shared_ptr, the type of the deleter is not part of the type of the smart 12 \npointer. This necessitates larger runtime data structures and somewhat slower 13 \ncode, but pointed-to types need not be complete when compiler -generated special 14 \nfunctions are employed.  15 \nFor the Pimpl Idiom, the re’s not really a trade -off between the characteristics of 16 \nstd::unique_ptr and std::shared_ptr, because the relationship between 17 \nclasses like Widget and classes like Widget::Impl is exclusive ownership, and 18 \nthat makes std::unique_ptr the proper tool for the job. Nevertheless, it’s worth 19 \nknowing that in other situations —situations where shared ownership exists (and 20 \nstd::shared_ptr is hence a good design choice), there’s no need to jump 21 \nthrough the function-definition hoops that use of std::unique_ptr entails. 22 \nThings to Remember 23 \n The Pimpl Idiom decreases build times by reducing compilation dependencies 24 \nbetween class implementations and class clients. 25 \n For std::unique_ptr pImpl pointers, declare special member functions in 26 \nthe class header, but implement them in the implementation file. Do this even 27 \nif the default function implementations are acceptable. 28 \n The above advice applies to std::unique_ptr, but not to std::shared_ptr. 29\n\n#### 第 189 页原文\n\nPage 189 \n \n \n1 \n2 \nWhen you first learn about  them, move semantics and perfect forwarding seem 3 \npretty straightforward: 4 \n Move semantics makes it possible for compilers to replace expensive copying 5 \noperations with less expensive moves. In the same way that copy constructors 6 \nand copy assignment operators give developers control over what it means to 7 \ncopy objects, move constructors and move assignment operators offer control 8 \nover the semantics of moving.  9 \n Perfect forwarding  makes it possible to write function templates that take 10 \narbitrary arguments and forward them to other functions such that the target 11 \nfunctions receive exactly the same arguments as were passed to the forwar d-12 \ning functions.   13 \nRvalue references are the glue that ties these two rather disparate features togeth-14 \ner. They’re the underlying language mechanism that makes both move semantics 15 \nand perfect forwarding possible.  16 \nThe more experience you have with these features, the more you realize that your 17 \ninitial impr ession was based on only the metaphorical tip of the proverbial ic e-18 \nberg. The world of move semantics, perfect forwarding, and rvalue references is 19 \nmore nuanced than it appears.  std::move doesn’t move anything, for example, 20 \nand perfect forwarding is  imperfect. Move operations aren’t always cheaper than 21 \ncopying; when they are, they’re not always as cheap as you’d expect ; and they’re 22 \nnot always called in a context where moving is valid. The construct T&& doesn’t 23 \nalways represent an rvalue reference.  24 \nNo matter how far you dig into these features, it can seem that there’s always more 25 \nto uncover. Fortunately, there is a limit to the depths of move semantics, perfect 26 \nforwarding, and rvalue references. This chapter will take you to the bedrock. Once 27 \nyou arrive, this part of C++11  will make a lot more sense. You’ll know the usage 28 \nconventions for std::move and std::forward, for example . You’ll be comfort a-29\n\n#### 第 190 页原文\n\nPage 190 \n \nble with the ambiguous nature of “T&&”. You’ll understand the reasons for the su r-1 \nprisingly varied behavioral profiles of move operations. All those pieces will come 2 \ntogether and fall into place. At that point, you’ll be back where you started, because 3 \nmove semantics, perfect forwarding, and rvalue references will once again seem 4 \npretty straightforward. But this time, they’ll stay that way. 5 \nIn the Items in this chapter, it ’s especially important to bear in mind that a param-6 \neter is always an lvalue, even if its type is an rvalue reference. That is, given 7 \nvoid f(Widget&& w); 8 \nthe parameter w is an l value, even though its type is rvalue -reference-to-Widget. 9 \n(If this surprises you, please review the overview of lvalues and rvalues that b e-10 \ngins on page Error! Bookmark not defined..) 11 \n Item 25: Understand std::move and std::forward. 12 \nIt’s useful to approach std::move and std::forward in terms of what they don’t 13 \ndo. std::move doesn’t move anything . std::forward doesn’t forward anything. 14 \nAt runtime, neither does anything at all. That’s because they generate no execut a-15 \nble code. Not a single byte.  16 \nstd::move and std::forward are merely functions that perform casts. 17 \nstd::move unconditionally casts its argument to an rvalue , while std::forward 18 \nperforms this cast only if a particular condition is  fulfilled. That’s it. The explana-19 \ntion leads to a new set of questions, but, fundamentally, that’s the complete story. 20 \nTo make the story more concrete, h ere’s a sample implementation of std::move 21 \nin C++11. It’s not fully conforming to the details of the Standard, but it’s very close. 22 \ntemplate<typename T>                       // in namespace std 23 \ntypename remove_reference<T>::type&& 24 \nmove(T&& param) 25 \n{ 26 \n  using ReturnType =                       // alias declaration; 27 \n    typename remove_reference<T>::type&&;  // see Item 9 28 \n  return static_cast<ReturnType>(param); 29 \n} 30\n\n#### 第 191 页原文\n\nPage 191 \n \nI’ve highlighted two parts of the code for you. One is the name of the function, b e-1 \ncause the return type specification is  rather noisy, and I don’t want you to lose 2 \nyour bearings in the din . The other thing is the cast that comprises the essence of 3 \nthe function. As you can see, std::move takes a reference to an object (a universal 4 \nreference, to be precise (see Item 26)) and it returns a reference to the same o b-5 \nject.  6 \nThe “&&” part of the function’s return type implies that std::move returns an 7 \nrvalue reference, but, as Item 30 explains, if the type T happens to be an lvalue ref-8 \nerence, T&& would become an lvalue reference. To prevent this from happening, 9 \nthe type trait (see Item 9) std::remove_reference is applied to T, thus ensuring 10 \nthat “ &&” is applied to a type that isn’t a reference. That guarantees that 11 \nstd::move truly returns an rvalue reference, and that’s important, because rvalue 12 \nreferences returned from functions are  rvalues. (Why? Because the C++ Standard 13 \nsays they are.)  Thus, std::move casts its argument to an rvalue , and that’s all it 14 \ndoes. 15 \nAs an aside, std::move can be implemente d with less fuss in C++14. Thanks to 16 \nfunction return type deduction  (see Item 3) and to the Standard Library ’s alias 17 \ntemplate std::remove_reference_t (see Item 9), std::move can be written 18 \nthis way: 19 \ntemplate<typename T>                       // C++14 only; still 20 \ndecltype(auto) move(T&& param)             // in namespace std 21 \n{ 22 \n  using ReturnType = remove_reference_t<T>&&; 23 \n  return static_cast<ReturnType>(param); 24 \n} 25 \nEasier on the eyes, no? 26 \nBecause std::move does nothing but cast its argument to an rvalue, there have 27 \nbeen suggestions that a better name for it might be something like rvalue_cast. 28 \nBe that as it may , the name we have is std::move, so it’s important to remember 29 \nwhat std::move does and doesn’t do. It does cast. It doesn’t move. 30 \nOf course, r values are candidates for moving, so applying std::move to an object 31 \ntells the compiler that that object is eligible to be  moved from . T hat’s why  32\n\n#### 第 192 页原文\n\nPage 192 \n \nstd::move has the name it does: to make it easy to designate objects that may be 1 \nmoved from. 2 \nActually, rvalues are only usually candidates for moving.  Suppose you’re writing a 3 \nfunction taking a std::string parameter, and you know that inside your fun c-4 \ntion, you’ll copy that parameter. Flush with the advice proffered by Item 17, you 5 \ndeclare a by-value parameter: 6 \nvoid f(std::string s);       // s to be copied, so per Item 17, 7 \n                             // pass by value 8 \nBut suppose you  also know that inside f, you only need to read s’s value; you’ll 9 \nnever need to modify it. In accord with the time -honored tradition of using const 10 \nwhenever possible, you revise your declaration such that s is const: 11 \nvoid f(const std::string s);     12 \nFinally, assume that at the end of f, s will be copied into some data structure. E x-13 \ncept that you don’t want to pay for the copy, so, again in accord with Item 17, you 14 \napply std::move to s to turn it into an rvalue: 15 \nstruct SomeDataStructure { 16 \n  std::string name; 17 \n  … 18 \n}; 19 \nSomeDataStructure sds; 20 \nvoid f(const std::string s) 21 \n{ 22 \n  …                         // read operations on s 23 \n  sds.name = std::move(s);  // \"move\" s into sds.name; this code 24 \n}                           // doesn't do what it seems to! 25 \nThis code compiles. This code links. This code runs.  This code sets sds.name to 26 \nthe value you expect. The only thing separating this code from a perfect realization 27 \nof your vision is that s is not moved into sds.name, it’s copied. Sure, s is cast to an 28 \nrvalue by std::move, but s is declared as a const std::string, so before the 29 \ncast, s is an lvalue const std::string, and the result of  the cast is an rvalue 30 \nconst std::string, but throughout it all, s remains const.  31\n\n#### 第 193 页原文\n\nPage 193 \n \nConsider the effect that has when compilers have to determine which 1 \nstd::string assignment operator to call. There are two possibilities: 2 \nclass string {            // std::string is actually a  3 \npublic:                   // typedef for std::basic_string<char> 4 \n  … 5 \n  string& operator=(const string& rhs);  // copy assignment 6 \n  string& operator=(string&& rhs);       // move assignment 7 \n  … 8 \n}; 9 \nIn our function f, the result of std::move(s) is an rvalue of type const 10 \nstd::string. That rvalue can’t be passed to std::string’s move assignment 11 \noperator, because the move assignment operator takes an rvalue reference to a 12 \nnon-const std::string. The rvalue can, however, be passed to the copy assig n-13 \nment operator, because  an lvalue reference -to-const is permitted to bind to a 14 \nconst rvalue. The statement 15 \n  sds.name = std::move(s); 16 \ntherefore invokes the copy assignment operator in std::string, even though s 17 \nhas been cast to an rvalue! Such behavior is  essential to maintainin g const-18 \ncorrectness. Moving a value out of an object generally modifies the object, so the 19 \nlanguage should not permit const objects to be passed to functions (such as move 20 \nassignment operators) that could modify them. 21 \nThere are two lessons to be drawn from  this example. First, don’t declare objects 22 \nconst if you want to be able to move from them. Move requests on const objects 23 \nare silently transformed into copy operations.  24 \nSecond, std::move not only doesn’t actually move anything, it doesn’t even gua r-25 \nantee that the object it’s casting will be eligible to be moved. The only thing you 26 \nknow for sure about an object that has been std::moved is that it’s an rvalue. 27 \nThe story for std::forward is similar to that for std::move, but where as 28 \nstd::move unconditionally casts its argument to an rvalue, std::forward does it 29 \nonly under certain conditions. std::forward is a conditional cast. To understand 30 \nwhen it casts and when it doesn’t, recall how std::forward is typically used. The 31\n\n#### 第 194 页原文\n\nPage 194 \n \nmost common scenario is a function template taking a universal reference parame-1 \nter that is to be passed to another function: 2 \nvoid process(const Widget& lvalParam);   // process lvalues 3 \nvoid process(Widget&& rvalParam);        // process rvalues 4 \ntemplate<typename T>                     // template that passes 5 \nvoid logAndProcess(T&& param)            // param to process 6 \n{  7 \n  auto now =                             // get current time 8 \n    std::chrono::system_clock::now(); 9 \n  makeLogEntry(\"Calling 'process'\", now); 10 \n  process(std::forward<T>(param)); 11 \n   12 \n} 13 \nConsider two calls to logAndProcess, one with an lvalue, the other with an rva l-14 \nue: 15 \nWidget w; 16 \nlogAndProcess(w);                  // call with lvalue 17 \nlogAndProcess(std::move(w));       // call with rvalue 18 \nInside logAndProcess, the parameter param is passed to the function process. 19 \nprocess is overloaded for lvalues and rvalues. When we call logAndProcess 20 \nwith an lvalue, we naturally expect that lvalue to be forwarded to  process as an 21 \nlvalue, and when we call logAndProcess with an rvalue, we ex pect the rvalue 22 \noverload of process to be invoked.  23 \nBut param, like all function parameters, is an lvalue. Every call to process inside 24 \nlogAndProcess will thus want to invoke the lvalue overload for process. To pre-25 \nvent this, we need a mechanism for param to be cast to an rvalue if and only if the 26 \nargument with which param was initialized —the argument passed to lo-27 \ngAndProcess—was an rvalue. This is precisely what std::forward does. That's 28 \nwhy std::forward is a conditional cast: it casts to an rvalue only if its argument 29 \nwas initialized with an rvalue. 30 \nYou may wonder how std::forward can know whether its argument was initia l-31 \nized with an rvalue. In the code above, for example, how can std::forward tell 32 \nwhether param was initialized with an lvalue or an rvalue? The brief answer is that 33\n\n#### 第 195 页原文\n\nPage 195 \n \nthat information is encoded in logAndProcess´s template parameter T. That pa-1 \nrameter is then passed to std::forward, which recovers the encoded info r-2 \nmation. Item 30 covers all the details, but for now, simply accept that T will be an 3 \nlvalue reference only if param was initialized with an lvalue. That means that 4 \nstd::forward can, in concept, be implemented something like this: 5 \ntemplate<typename T>                   // conceptual impl. of 6 \nT&&                                    // std::forward (in 7 \nforward(T&& param)                     // namespace std) 8 \n{ 9 \n  if (is_lvalue_reference<T>::value) { // if T indicates lvalue 10 \n    return param;                      // return param as lvalue 11 \n  } else {                             // else 12 \n    return move(param);                // return param as rvalue 13 \n  } 14 \n} 15 \nDon’t be misled by forward’s return type declarat ion into thinking that it returns 16 \nan rvalue reference. Item 30 will make clear that when an lvalue is being returned 17 \nfrom this function, std::forward’s return type will be an lvalue reference. 18 \nNote that this is a conceptual implementation. This code won’t even compile for 19 \nsome uses, and when it does compile, it’ll generate runtime code. Real 20 \nstd::forward implementations compile for all uses, and they never generate 21 \nruntime code. For purposes of understanding how std::forward determines 22 \nwhether to cast to an rvalue, however, this suffices. 23 \nGiven that both std::move and std::forward boil down to casts, the only differ-24 \nence being that std::move always casts, while std::forward only sometimes 25 \ndoes, you may wonder whether we can  dispense with  std::move and just use 26 \nstd::forward everywhere. From a purely technical perspective, the answer is 27 \nyes: std::forward can do it all . std::move isn’t necessary . (Strictly speaking, 28 \nneither function is necessary, because we could write our own  casts everywhere, 29 \nbut I hope we agree that that would be, well, yucky.) 30 \nstd::move’s advantage is convenience. Consider a conventional move constructor 31 \nfor a class with a single data member of type std::string: 32 \nclass Widget { 33 \npublic: 34\n\n#### 第 196 页原文\n\nPage 196 \n \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs)                       // conventional  1 \n  : s(std::move(rhs.s)) {}                   // move constructor 2 \n  … 3 \nprivate: 4 \n  std::string s; 5 \n}; 6 \nIf we wanted to implement the same behavior  using std::forward instead of 7 \nstd::move, we’d have to write it this way:  8 \nclass Widget { 9 \npublic: 10 \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs)                       // move ctor using 11 \n  : s(std::forward<std::string>(rhs.s)) {}   // std::forward 12 \n  …                                          // instead of  13 \n};                                           // std::move 14 \nNote first that std::move requires only a function argument (i.e., rhs.s), while 15 \nstd::forward requires both a  function argument (rhs.s) and  a template type 16 \nargument (std::string). Note second that the type we pass  to std::forward 17 \nshould be a non-reference, because that’s the standard mechanism for encoding 18 \nthat the argument being passed is an rvalue  (see Item 30). Together, this means 19 \nthat std::move requires less typing  than std::forward, and it spares us the 20 \ntrouble of passing a  type argument that encodes th at the argument we’re passing 21 \nis an rvalue. 22 \nMore importantly, the use of std::move conveys the use of an unconditional cast  23 \nto an rvalue, while the use of std::forward indicates a cast to an rvalue only for 24 \nreferences to which rvalues have been bound. Thos e are two very different a c-25 \ntions. The first one typically sets up a move, while the second one just passes —26 \nforwards—an object to another function in a way that retains its original lvalu e-27 \nness or rvalueness. Because these actions are so different, it’s good  that we have 28 \ntwo different functions (and function names) to distinguish them. 29 \nThings to Remember 30 \n std::move performs an unconditional cast to an rvalue. In and of itself, it 31 \ndoesn’t move anything. 32\n\n#### 第 197 页原文\n\nPage 197 \n \n std::forward casts its argument to an rvalue only if that argument is bound 1 \nto an rvalue.  2 \n Neither std::move nor std::forward do anything at runtime. 3 \n Item 26: Distinguish universal references from rvalue ref-4 \nerences. 5 \nIt’s been said that the truth shall set you free,  but under the right circumstances, a 6 \nwell-chosen lie can be equally liberating. This entire Item is such a lie . Because 7 \nwe’re dealing with software , however, let’s eschew the word “lie” and instead say 8 \nthat this Item comprises an abstraction. 9 \nTo declare an rvalue ref erence to some type T, you write T&&. It thus seems rea-10 \nsonable to assume that if you see “T&&” in source code, you’re looking at an rvalue 11 \nreference. Alas, it’s not quite that simple: 12 \nvoid f(Widget&& param);             // rvalue reference 13 \nWidget&& var1 = Widget();           // rvalue reference 14 \nauto&& var2 = var1;                 // not rvalue reference 15 \ntemplate<typename T> 16 \nvoid f(std::vector<T>&& param);     // rvalue reference 17 \ntemplate<typename T> 18 \nvoid f(T&& param);                  // not rvalue reference 19 \nIn fact, “T&&” has two different meanings. One is rvalue reference, of course. Such 20 \nreferences behave exactly the way you expect: they bind only to rvalues, and their 21 \nprimary raison d’être is to identify objects that may be moved from.  22 \nThe other possible meaning for “T&&” is either rvalue reference or lvalue reference. 23 \nSuch references look like rvalue references in the source code (i.e., “T&&”), but they 24 \ncan behave as if they were lvalue references (i.e., “ T&”). Their dual nature permits 25 \nthem to bind to rvalues (like rvalue references) as well as  lvalues (like lvalue re f-26 \nerences). Furthermore, they can bind to const or non-const objects, to volatile 27 \nor non-volatile objects, even to objects that are both const and volatile. They 28 \ncan bind to virtuall y anything. Such unprecedentedly flexible references deserve a 29 \nname of their own. I call them universal references.  30\n\n#### 第 198 页原文\n\nPage 198 \n \nUniversal references arise in two contexts. The most common is function template 1 \nparameters, such as this example from the sample code above: 2 \ntemplate<typename T> 3 \nvoid f(T&& param);             // param is a universal reference 4 \nThe second context is auto declarations, including this one from the sample code 5 \nabove: 6 \nauto&& var2 = var1;            // var2 is a universal reference 7 \nWhat these contexts have in common is the presence of type deduction. In the tem-8 \nplate f, the type of param is being deduced, and in the declaration for var2, var2’s 9 \ntype is being deduced.  Compare that with the  following examples (also from the 10 \nsample code above ), where type deduction is missing. If you see “ T&&” without 11 \ntype deduction, you’re looking at an rvalue reference: 12 \nvoid f(Widget&& param);        // no type deduction;  13 \n                               // param is an rvalue reference 14 \nWidget&& var1 = Widget();      // no type deduction;  15 \n                               // var1 is an rvalue reference 16 \nBecause universal references are references, they must be initialized. The initiali z-17 \ner for a universal reference determines whether it represents an rvalue reference 18 \nor an lvalue reference.  If the initializer is an rvalue, the universal reference  corre-19 \nsponds to an rvalue reference. If the initializer is an lvalue, the universal reference 20 \ncorresponds to an lvalue ref erence. For universal references that are function p a-21 \nrameters, the initializer is provided at the call site: 22 \ntemplate<typename T>  23 \nvoid f(T&& param);     // param is a universal reference 24 \nWidget w; 25 \nf(w);                  // lvalue passed to f; param's type is 26 \n                       // Widget& (i.e., an lvalue reference) 27 \nf(std::move(w));       // rvalue passed to f; param's type is 28 \n                       // Widget&& (i.e., an rvalue reference) 29 \nFor a reference to be universal, type deduction is necessary, but it’s not sufficient.  30 \nThe form of the reference declaration must also be correct, and that form is quite 31\n\n#### 第 199 页原文\n\nPage 199 \n \nconstrained. It must be precisely “T&&”.  Look again at this example from the sa m-1 \nple code we saw earlier: 2 \ntemplate<typename T> 3 \nvoid f(std::vector<T>&& param);  // param is an rvalue reference 4 \nWhen f is invoked, the type  T will be deduced (unless the caller explicitly specifies 5 \nit, an edge case we’ll not concern ourselves with). But the form of param’s type 6 \ndeclaration isn’t “T&&,” it’s “ std::vector<T>&&.” That rules out the possibility 7 \nthat param is a universal reference. param is therefore an rvalue reference, some-8 \nthing that your compilers will be happy to confirm for you if you try to pass an 9 \nlvalue to f: 10 \nstd::vector<int> v; 11 \nf(v);                             // error! can't bind lvalue to 12 \n                                  // rvalue reference 13 \nEven the simple presence of a const qualifier is enough to disqualify a reference 14 \nfrom being universal: 15 \ntemplate<typename T>  16 \nvoid f(const T&& param);         // param is an rvalue reference 17 \nIf you’re in a template and you see a function parameter of type “ T&&,” you might 18 \nthink you can assume that it’s a universal reference. You can’t . That’s because be-19 \ning in a template doesn’t guarantee the presence of type deduction.  Consider  this 20 \npush_back member function in std::vector: 21 \ntemplate<class T, class Allocator = allocator<T>>  // from C++ 22 \nclass vector {                                     // Standard 23 \npublic:                                          24 \n  void push_back(T&& x);  25 \n  … 26 \n}; 27 \npush_back’s parameter certainly has the right form for a universal reference, but 28 \nthere’s no type deduction in this case. That’s because push_back can’t exist with-29 \nout a particular vector instantiation for it to be part of, and the type of that i n-30 \nstantiation fully determines the declaration for push_back. That is, saying 31 \nstd::vector<Widget> v; 32 \ncauses   33\n\n#### 第 200 页原文\n\nPage 200 \n \nclass vector<Widget, allocator<Widget>> { 1 \npublic: 2 \n  void push_back(Widget&& x);               // rvalue reference 3 \n  … 4 \n}; 5 \nto be generated, and now you can see clearly t hat push_back employs no type de-6 \nduction. This push_back for vector<T> (there are two—the function is overload-7 \ned) always declares a parameter of type rvalue-reference-to-T. 8 \nIn contrast, the conceptually similar emplace_back member function in 9 \nstd::vector does employ type deduction: 10 \ntemplate<class T, class Allocator = allocator<T>>  // still from 11 \nclass vector {                                     // C++ 12 \npublic:                                            // Standard 13 \n  template <class... Args>  14 \n  void emplace_back(Args&&... args); 15 \n  … 16 \n}; 17 \nHere, the type parameter Args is independent of vector’s type parameter T, so 18 \nArgs must be deduced each time emplace_back is called. (Okay, Args is really a 19 \nparameter pack, not a type parameter, but for purposes o f this discussion, we can 20 \ntreat it as if it were a type parameter.) 21 \nThe fact that emplace_back’s type parameter is named Args, yet it’s still a un i-22 \nversal reference, reinforces my earlier comment that it’s the form of a universal 23 \nreference that must be “T&&”.  There’s no requirement that you use the name T. For 24 \nexample, the following template takes a universal reference, because the form 25 \n(“type&&”) is right, and param’s type will be deduced (again, excluding the corner 26 \ncase where the caller explicitly specifies the type): 27 \ntemplate<typename MyTemplateType>         // param is a 28 \nvoid someFunc(MyTemplateType&& param);    // universal reference 29 \nI remarked earlier that auto variables can also be universal references. To be 30 \nmore precise, variables declared wi th the type auto&& are universal references, 31 \nbecause type deduction takes place and they have the correct form (“ T&&”). auto 32 \nuniversal references are not as common as universal references used for function 33 \ntemplate parameters, but they do crop up from time  to time in C++11. They crop 34", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.201–p.225", "pages": "p.201–p.225", "raw_text": "#### 第 201 页原文\n\nPage 201 \n \nup a lot more in C++14, because C++14 lambda expressions may declare auto&& 1 \nparameters. For example, if you wanted to write a  C++14 lambda to record the 2 \ntime taken in an arbitrary function invocation, you could do this: 3 \nauto timeFuncInvocation = [](auto&& func, auto&&... args) 4 \n  { 5 \n    start timer; 6 \n    std::forward<decltype(func)>(func)(        // invoke func on 7 \n      std::forward<decltype(args)>(args)...    // args in C++14 8 \n      );  9 \n    stop timer and record elapsed time; 10 \n  }; 11 \nIf your reaction to the “ std::forward<decltype(blah blah blah)>” code i n-12 \nside the lambda is, “What the…?!”, that probably just means you haven’t yet read 13 \nItem 35. Don’t worry about it. The important thing in this Item is the auto&& pa-14 \nrameters that the lambda declares. func is a universal reference that can be bound 15 \nto any callable entity, lvalue or rv alue. args is one or more universal references 16 \n(i.e., a universal reference parameter pack) that can be bound to any number of 17 \nobjects of arbitrary types. The result, thanks to auto universal references, is that 18 \ntimeFuncInvocation can time pretty much any function execution. ( For info r-19 \nmation on the difference between “any” and “pretty much any,” turn to Item 32.) 20 \nBear in mind that  this entire Item —the foundation of universal references —is a 21 \nlie…er, an abstraction . The underlying truth is known as reference-collapsing, a 22 \ntopic to which Item 30 is dedicated. But the truth doesn’t make the abstraction any 23 \nless useful. Distinguishing between rvalue references and universal references will 24 \nhelp you read source code more accurately (“Does that “T&&” I’m looking at bind to 25 \nrvalues only or to everything?”), and it will avoid ambiguities when you commun i-26 \ncate with your colleagues (“I’m using a universal reference here, not an rvalue re f-27 \nerence…”). It will also allow you to make sense of Items  27 and 28, which rely on 28 \nthe distinction.  So embrace the abstraction. Revel in it. Just as Newton’s laws of 29 \nmotion (which are technically incorrect) are typically just as useful as and easier to 30 \napply than Einstein’s theory of general relativity (“the truth”), so is the notion of 31 \nuniversal references normally preferable to working through  the details of refer-32 \nence-collapsing. 33\n\n#### 第 202 页原文\n\nPage 202 \n \nThings to Remember 1 \n If a variable or parameter has type T&& for some deduced type T, it’s a unive r-2 \nsal reference.  3 \n If the form of the type isn’t precisely T&&, or if type deduction does not occur, 4 \nT&& denotes an rvalue reference. 5 \n Universal references correspond to rvalue references if they’re initialized with 6 \nrvalues. They correspond to lvalue references if they’re initialized with lvalues. 7 \n Item 27: Use std::move on rvalue references, std::forward 8 \non universal references. 9 \nRvalue references bind only to objects that are candidates for moving . If you have 10 \nan rvalue reference parameter, you know that the object it ’s bound to may be 11 \nmoved:  12 \nclass Widget { 13 \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs);        // rhs definitely refers to an 14 \n  …                            // object eligible for moving 15 \n}; 16 \nThat being the case, you’ll want to pass such objects to other functions in a way 17 \nthat permits those functions to take advantage of the object’s  rvalueness. The way 18 \nto do that is to cast parameters bound to such objects to rvalues . As Item 25 ex-19 \nplains, that’s not only what std::move does, it’s what it was created for: 20 \nclass Widget { 21 \npublic: 22 \n  Widget(Widget&& rhs)               // rhs is rvalue reference  23 \n  : name(std::move(rhs.name)), 24 \n    p(std::move(rhs.p)) 25 \n    { … } 26 \n  … 27 \nprivate: 28 \n  std::string name; 29 \n  std::shared_ptr<SomeDataStructure> p; 30 \n}; 31 \nA universal reference, on the other han d (see Item 26), might be bound to an o b-32 \nject that’s eligible for moving. Universal references should be cast to rvalues only if 33\n\n#### 第 203 页原文\n\nPage 203 \n \nthey were initialized with rvalues. Item 25 explains that this is precisely what 1 \nstd::forward does: 2 \nclass Widget { 3 \npublic: 4 \n  template<typename T> 5 \n  void setName(T&& newName)               // newName is 6 \n  { name = std::forward<T>(newName); }    // universal reference  7 \n  …  8 \n}; 9 \nIn short, rvalue references  should be unconditionally cast  to rvalues (via 10 \nstd::move) when forwarding them to other functions , because they’re always 11 \nbound to rvalues, and universal references  should be conditionally cast to rvalues 12 \n(via std::forward) when forwarding them , because they’re only sometimes 13 \nbound to rvalues. 14 \n Item 25 explains that using std::forward on rvalue references can be made to 15 \nexhibit the proper behavior, but the source code is  wordy and unidiomatic, so you 16 \nshould avoid using std::forward with rvalue references. Even worse is the idea 17 \nof using std::move with universal references, because that can have the effect of 18 \nunexpectedly modifying lvalues (e.g., local variables): 19 \nclass Widget { 20 \npublic: 21 \n  template<typename T> 22 \n  void setName(T&& newName)         // universal reference 23 \n  { name = std::move(newName); }    // compiles, but is 24 \n  …                                 // bad, bad, bad! 25 \nprivate: 26 \n  std::string name; 27 \n  std::shared_ptr<SomeDataStructure> p; 28 \n}; 29 \nstd::string getWidgetName();        // factory function 30 \nWidget w; 31 \nauto n = getWidgetName();           // n is local variable 32 \nw.setName(n);                       // moves n into w! 33 \n…                                   // n's value now unknown 34\n\n#### 第 204 页原文\n\nPage 204 \n \nHere, the local variable n is passed to w.setName, which the caller can be forgiven 1 \nfor assuming is a read -only operation. But because setName internally uses 2 \nstd::move to unconditionally cast its reference parameter to an rvalue, n’s value 3 \nwill be moved into w.name, and n will come back from the call to  setName with an 4 \nunspecified value. That’s the kind of behavior that can move callers to despair —5 \npossibly to violence. 6 \nYou might argue that setName shouldn’t have declared its parameter to be a un i-7 \nversal reference.  Such references can't be const (see Item 26), yet setName sure-8 \nly shouldn't modify its parameter. You might point out that if setName had simply 9 \nbeen overloaded for const lvalues and for rvalues, the whole problem could have 10 \nbeen avoided. Like this: 11 \nclass Widget { 12 \npublic: 13 \n  void setName(const std::string& newName)      // set from 14 \n  { name = newName; }                           // const lvalue 15 \n  void setName(std::string&& newName)           // set from 16 \n  { name = std::move(newName); }                // rvalue 17 \n  … 18 \n}; 19 \nThat would certainly work in this case, but there are drawbacks. First, it’s more 20 \nsource code to write and maintain (two functions instead of a single template). 21 \nSecond, it can be less efficient.  For example, consider this use of setName: 22 \nw.setName(\"Adela Novak\"); 23 \nWith the version of setName taking a universal reference, the string literal \"Adela 24 \nNovak\" would be passed to setName, where it would be conveyed to the construc-25 \ntor for the std::string inside w. w's name data member would thus be construct-26 \ned directly from the string literal; no temporary std::string objects would arise. 27 \nWith the overloaded versions of setName, however, a temporary std::string 28 \nobject would be created for setName's parameter to bind to, and  this temporary 29 \nstd::string would then be moved into w's data member. A call to setName 30 \nwould thus entail execution of one std::string constructor (to create the te m-31 \nporary), one std::string move constructor (to move newName into w.name), and 32\n\n#### 第 205 页原文\n\nPage 205 \n \none std::string destructor (to destroy the temporary). That's almost certainly a 1 \nmore expensive execution sequence tha n invoking only the std::string con-2 \nstructor taking a const char* pointer. The additional cost  is likely to vary from 3 \nimplementation to implementation, a nd whether that cost is worth worrying 4 \nabout will vary from application to application and library to library, but the fact is 5 \nthat replacing a template taking a universal reference with a pair of functions 6 \noverloaded on lvalue references and rvalue refere nces is likely to incur a runtime 7 \ncost in some cases.  If we generalize the example such that Widget’s data member 8 \nmay be of an arbitrary type (rather than knowing that it ’s std::string), the per-9 \nformance gap can widen considerably, because not all types are as cheap to move 10 \nas std::string (see Item 31). 11 \nThe most serious problem with overloadi ng on lvalues and rvalues, however, isn't 12 \nthe volume or idiomaticity of the source code, nor is it the code’s runtime perfor-13 \nmance. It’s its lack of  scalability. Widget::setName takes only one parameter, so 14 \nonly two overloads are necessary  in this example , but for functions taking more 15 \nparameters, each of which could be an lvalue or an rvalue, the number of ove r-16 \nloads grows geometrically: n parameters necessitates 2n overloads. And that's not 17 \nthe worst of it . S ome functions —function templates, actually —take an unlimited 18 \nnumber of parameters, each of which could be an lvalue or rvalue. The poster chil-19 \ndren for such functions are std::make_shared, and , as of  C++14, 20 \nstd::make_unique (see Item 23). Check out the declarations of their most co m-21 \nmonly-used overloads: 22 \ntemplate<class T, class... Args>                 // from C++11 23 \nshared_ptr<T> make_shared(Args&&... args);       // Standard 24 \ntemplate<class T, class... Args>                 // from C++14 25 \nunique_ptr<T> make_unique(Args&&... args);       // Standard 26 \nFor functions like this, overloading on lvalues and rvalues is not an option: unive r-27 \nsal references are the only way to go. And inside such functions, I assure you, 28 \nstd::forward is applied to the universal reference parameters when they're 29 \npassed to other functions. Which is exactly what you should do. 30 \nWell, usually. Eventually. But not necessarily initially. In some cases, you'll want to 31 \nuse the object bound to an rvalue reference or a universal references more than 32\n\n#### 第 206 页原文\n\nPage 206 \n \nonce in a single function, and you'll want to make sure that it's not moved from 1 \nuntil you're otherwise done with it. In th at case, you'll want to apply std::move 2 \n(for rvalue references) or std::forward (for universal references) to only the 3 \nfinal use of the reference. For example: 4 \ntemplate<typename T>                       // text is  5 \nvoid setSignText(T&& text)                 // univ. reference 6 \n{ 7 \n  sign.setText(text);                      // use text, but 8 \n                                           // don't modify it  9 \n  auto now =                               // get current time 10 \n    std::chrono::system_clock::now(); 11 \n  signHistory.add(now, 12 \n                  std::forward<T>(text));  // conditionally cast 13 \n}                                          // text to rvalue 14 \nHere, we want to make sure that text's value doesn't get changed by 15 \nsign.setText, because we want to use that value when we call signHisto-16 \nry.add. Ergo the use of std::forward on only the final use of the universal re f-17 \nerence. 18 \nFor std::move, the same thinking applies (i.e., apply std::move to an rvalue ref-19 \nerence the last time it's  used), but it's important to note that in a few  cases, you’ll 20 \nwant to call std::move_if_noexcept instead of std::move. To learn when and 21 \nwhy, consult Item 16. 22 \nIf you're in a function that returns by value, and you're returning an object bound 23 \nto an rvalue reference or a universal reference, you'll want to apply std::move or 24 \nstd::forward when you return the reference. To see why, consider an opera-25 \ntor+ function to add two matrices together, where the left -hand matrix is known 26 \nto be an rvalue (and can hence have its storage reused to hold the sum of the m a-27 \ntrices):  28 \nMatrix operator+(Matrix&& lhs,     // by-value return 29 \n                 const Matrix& rhs)  30 \n{  31 \n  lhs += rhs; 32 \n  return std::move(lhs);           // move lhs into return value 33 \n}   34\n\n#### 第 207 页原文\n\nPage 207 \n \nBy casting lhs to an rvalue in the return statement (via std::move), lhs will be 1 \nmoved into the function's return value location. If the call to std::move were 2 \nomitted, 3 \nMatrix operator+(Matrix&& lhs,     // as above 4 \n                 const Matrix& rhs)  5 \n{  6 \n  lhs += rhs; 7 \n  return lhs;                      // copy lhs into return value 8 \n}   9 \nthe fact that lhs is an lvalue would force compilers to instead copy it into the r e-10 \nturn value location.  Assuming that the Matrix type supports move construction 11 \nthat is more efficient than copy construction, using std::move in the return 12 \nstatement yields more efficient code.  13 \nIf Matrix does not support moving, casting it to an rvalue won't hurt, because the 14 \nrvalue will simply be copied by Matrix’s copy constructor (see Item 25). If Matrix 15 \nis later revised to support moving, operator+ will benefit from that change the 16 \nnext time it ( operator+) is compiled. That being the case, there's nothing to be 17 \nlost (and much to be gained) by applying std::move to rvalue references being 18 \nreturned from functions that return by value. 19 \nThe situation is similar for universal references  and std::forward. Consider a 20 \nfunction (really a function template)  reduceAndCopy that takes a possibly -21 \nunreduced Fraction object, reduces it, and then returns a copy of the reduced 22 \nvalue. If the original object is an rvalue, its value should be moved into the return 23 \nvalue (thus avoiding the expense of making a copy), but if the original is an lvalue, 24 \nan actual copy must be created. Hence: 25 \ntemplate<typename T>               // universal reference param, 26 \nFraction reduceAndCopy(T&& frac)   // by-value return 27 \n{ 28 \n  frac.reduce(); 29 \n  return std::forward<T>(frac);    // move rvalue into return 30 \n}                                  // value, copy lvalue 31 \nIf the call to std::forward were omitted, frac would be unconditionally copied  32 \ninto reduceAndCopy's return value. 33\n\n#### 第 208 页原文\n\nPage 208 \n \nSome programmers take the information above and try to extend it to cover situ a-1 \ntions where it doesn't apply. “If applying std::move to an rvalue reference p a-2 \nrameter being copied into a return value turns a copy construction into a move 3 \nconstruction,” they reason, “I can perform the same optimization on local variables 4 \nthat I’m returning .”  In other words, they figure that given a function returning a 5 \nlocal variable by value, such as this, 6 \nWidget makeWidget()        // \"Copying\" version of makeWidget 7 \n{ 8 \n  Widget w;                // local variable 9 \n  …                        // configure w 10 \n  return w;                // \"copy\" w into return value 11 \n} 12 \nthey can “optimize” it by turning the “copy” into a move: 13 \nWidget makeWidget()        // Moving version of makeWidget 14 \n{ 15 \n  Widget w; 16 \n  … 17 \n  return std::move(w);     // move w into return value 18 \n} 19 \nMy liberal use of quotation marks should have tipped you off that this line of re a-20 \nsoning is flawed. But why is it flawed?  21 \nIt’s flawed, because the Standardization Committee is way ahead of such pr o-22 \ngrammers when it comes to this kind of optimization. It was recognized long ago 23 \nthat the “copying” version of makeWidget can avoid the need to copy the local var-24 \niable w by constructing it in the memory set aside for the function’s return value. 25 \nThis is kno wn as the return value optimization  (RVO), and  it’s been expressly 26 \nblessed by the C++ Standard for as long as there’s been one.  27 \nWording such a blessing is finicky business, because you want to permit such copy 28 \nelision only in places where it won’t affect the observable behavior of the software. 29 \nParaphrasing the legalistic (arguably toxic) prose of the Standard, this particular 30 \nblessing says that compilers may elide the copying (or the moving) of a local var i-31 \nable (or a parameter that was passed by value)  in a function returning by value  if 32 \n(1) the type of the  local variable is the same as that returned by the function and 33\n\n#### 第 209 页原文\n\nPage 209 \n \n(2) the local variable is what’s being returned. With that in mind , look again at the 1 \n“copying” version of makeWidget: 2 \nWidget makeWidget()        // \"Copying\" version of makeWidget 3 \n{ 4 \n  Widget w; 5 \n  … 6 \n  return w;                // \"copy\" w into return value 7 \n} 8 \nBoth conditions are fulfilled here, and  you can trust me when I tell you that  every 9 \ndecent C++ compiler will employ the RVO to avoid copying w.  That means that the 10 \n“copying”  version of makeWidget doesn’t, in fact, copy anything —at least not in a 11 \nrelease build. 12 \nThe moving version of makeWidget does just what its name says it does (assuming 13 \nWidget offers a move constructor): it moves the contents of w into makeWidget’s 14 \nreturn value location. But why don’t compilers use the RVO to eliminate the move, 15 \nagain constructing w in the memory set aside for the function’s return value? The 16 \nanswer is simple: they’re not allowed to. Condition (2) stipulates that the RVO may 17 \nbe performed only if what’s being returned is a local variable, but that’s not what 18 \nthe moving version of makeWidget is doing.  Look again at its return statement: 19 \nreturn std::move(w); 20 \nWhat’s being returned he re isn’t w, it’s the result of calling a function , namely, 21 \nstd::move. Returning the result of a function call doesn’t satisfy the conditions 22 \nrequired for the RVO to be valid, so compilers  must move w into the function’s re-23 \nturn value location. Developers trying to help their compilers optimize by applying 24 \nstd::move to a local variable that’s being returned are actually limiting the opt i-25 \nmization options available to the compilers! There are situations where applying 26 \nstd::move to a local variable can be a reas onable thing to do (i.e., when  you’re 27 \npassing it to a function and  you know you won’t be using the variable any more) , 28 \nbut as part of a  return statement when the RVO would otherwise be available is 29 \nassuredly not one of them. 30\n\n#### 第 210 页原文\n\nPage 210 \n \nThings to remember 1 \n Apply std::move to rvalue references and std::forward to universal refe r-2 \nences the last time each is used. 3 \n Do the same thing for rvalue references and universal references being r e-4 \nturned from functions that return by value. 5 \n Never apply std::move or std::forward to local objects (including by-value 6 \nparameters) if they would otherwise be eligible for  the return value optimiz a-7 \ntion. 8 \n Item 28: Avoid overloading on universal references. 9 \nSuppose you need to write a function that takes a  name as a  parameter, logs the 10 \ncurrent date an d time, then adds the name to a global data structure . That is, you 11 \nneed to write a function that looks something like this: 12 \nstd::set<std::string> names;            // global data structure  13 \nvoid logAndAdd(const std::string& name)  14 \n{ 15 \n  auto now =                            // get current time 16 \n    std::chrono::system_clock::now(); 17 \n  log(now, \"logAndAdd\");                // make log entry 18 \n  names.emplace(name);                  // add name to global  19 \n}                                       // structure; Item 18  20 \n                                        // has info on emplace 21 \nThis isn’t unreasonable code, but it’s not as efficient as it could be.  Consider three 22 \npotential calls: 23 \nstd::string petName(\"Darla\"); 24 \nlogAndAdd(petName);                   // pass lvalue std::string 25 \nlogAndAdd(std::string(\"Persephone\")); // pass rvalue std::string 26 \nlogAndAdd(\"Patty Dog\");               // pass string literal 27 \nIn the first call, logAndAdd’s parameter name is bound to the variable petName. 28 \nWithin logAndAdd, name is ultimately passed to names.emplace. Because name is 29\n\n#### 第 211 页原文\n\nPage 211 \n \nan lvalue, it is copied into names.  There’s no way to avoid that  copy, because an 1 \nlvalue (petName) was passed into logAndAdd in the first place. 2 \nIn the second call, the parameter name is bound to an rvalue (the temporary 3 \nstd::string explicitly created from \"Persephone\"). name itself is an lvalue, so 4 \nit’s copied into names, but we recognize that, in principle, its value could be moved 5 \ninto names. In this call, we pay for a copy, but we should be able to get by with only 6 \na move. 7 \nIn the third call, the parameter name is again bound to an rvalue, but this time it ’s 8 \nto a temporary std::string that’s implicitly created from \"Patty Dog\". As in the 9 \nsecond call, name is copied into  names, but in this case, the argument originally 10 \npassed to logAndAdd was a string literal. Had that string literal been passed d i-11 \nrectly to the call to emplace, there would have been no need to create a temporary 12 \nstd::string at all. Instead, emplace would have used the string literal to create 13 \nthe std::string object directly inside the std::set. In this third call, then, 14 \nwe’re paying to copy a std::string, yet there’s really no reason to pay even for a 15 \nmove, much less a copy. 16 \nWe can eliminate the inefficiencies in the second and third calls by rewriting lo-17 \ngAndAdd to take a universal reference  (see Item 26) and, in accord with Item 27,    18 \nstd::forwarding this reference to emplace. The results speak for themselves: 19 \ntemplate<typename T>  20 \nvoid logAndAdd(T&& name) 21 \n{ 22 \n  auto now = std::chrono::system_clock::now(); 23 \n  log(now, \"logAndAdd\"); 24 \n  names.emplace(std::forward<T>(name));  25 \n} 26 \nstd::string petName(\"Darla\");          // as before 27 \nlogAndAdd(name);                       // as before, copy 28 \n                                       // lvalue into set 29 \nlogAndAdd(std::string(\"Persephone\"));  // move rvalue instead 30 \n                                       // of copying it 31 \nlogAndAdd(\"Patty Dog\");                // create std::string 32 \n                                       // in set instead of  33\n\n#### 第 212 页原文\n\nPage 212 \n \n                                       // copying a temporary 1 \n                                       // std::string 2 \nHurray, optimal efficiency! 3 \nWere this the end of the s tory, we could stop here and go home happy, but I ha-4 \nven’t told you that clients don’t always have direct access to the names that lo-5 \ngAndAdd requires. Some clients have only an index that logAndAdd uses to look 6 \nup the corresponding name in a table. To support such clients, logAndAdd is over-7 \nloaded: 8 \nstd::string nameFromIdx(int idx);      // return name 9 \n                                       // corresponding to idx 10 \nvoid logAndAdd(int idx)  11 \n{ 12 \n  auto now = std::chrono::system_clock::now(); 13 \n  log(now, \"logAndAdd\"); 14 \n  names.emplace(nameFromIdx(idx)); 15 \n} 16 \nResolution of calls to the two overloads works as expected: 17 \nstd::string petName(\"Darla\");          // as before 18 \nlogAndAdd(petName);                    // as before, these  19 \nlogAndAdd(std::string(\"Persephone\"));  // calls all invoke  20 \nlogAndAdd(\"Patty Dog\");                // the T&& overload 21 \nlogAndAdd(22);                         // calls int overload 22 \nActually, resolution works as expected only if you  don’t expect too much . Suppose 23 \na client has a short holding an index and passes that to logAndAdd: 24 \nshort nameIdx; 25 \n…                                      // give nameIdx a value 26 \nlogAndAdd(nameIdx);                    // error! 27 \nThe comment  on th e last line  isn’t terribly illuminatin g, so let me explain what 28 \nhappens here. 29 \nThere are two logAndAdd overloads. The one taking a universal reference can de-30 \nduce T to be short, thus yielding an exact match . T he overload with an int pa-31 \nrameter can match the short argument only with a promotion. P er the normal 32\n\n#### 第 213 页原文\n\nPage 213 \n \noverload resolution rules, an exact match beats a match with a promotion, so the 1 \nuniversal reference overload is invoked. 2 \nWithin that overload, the parameter name is bound to the short that’s passed in .  3 \nname is then std::forwarded to the emplace member function on a 4 \nstd::set<std::string>, which, in turn, dutifully forwards it to the 5 \nstd::string constructor. There is no constructor for std::string that takes a 6 \nshort, so the std::string constructor call inside the call to set::emplace in-7 \nside the call to logAndAdd fails. All because the universal reference overload was a 8 \nbetter match for a short argument than an int. 9 \nFunctions taking universal references are the greediest functions in C++.  They i n-10 \nstantiate to create exact matches for almost a ny type of argument. (The few kinds 11 \nof arguments where this isn’t the case are described in Item 32.) This is why co m-12 \nbining overloading and universal  references is almost always a bad idea: the un i-13 \nversal reference overload vacuums up far more argument types than the developer 14 \ndoing the overloading generally expects. 15 \nAn easy way to topple into this pit is to write a perfect forwarding constructor. A 16 \nsmall modification to the logAndAdd example demonstrates the problem. Instead 17 \nof writing a function that can take either a std::string or an index that can be 18 \nused to look up a std::string, imagine a class Person to which we can pass e i-19 \nther a std::string or an index: 20 \nclass Person { 21 \npublic: 22 \n  template<typename T>          23 \n  explicit Person(T&& n)           // perfect forwarding ctor; 24 \n  : name(std::forward<T>(n)) {}    // initializes data member 25 \n  explicit Person(int idx)         // int ctor 26 \n  : name(nameFromIdx(idx)) {}  27 \n  … 28 \nprivate: 29 \n  std::string name; 30 \n}; 31 \nAs was the case with logAndAdd, passing an integral type other than int (e.g., 32 \nstd::size_t, short, long, etc.) will call the universal reference constructor 33\n\n#### 第 214 页原文\n\nPage 214 \n \noverload instead of the int overload, and that will lead to compilation failures. 1 \nThe problem is much worse in this case, however, because there’s more overloa d-2 \ning present in Person that meets the eye. Item 19 explains that, under the appr o-3 \npriate conditions, C++ will generate both copy and move constructors, and this is 4 \ntrue even if the class contains a templatized constructor that could be instant iated 5 \nto produce the signature of the copy or move constructor. I f the copy and move 6 \nconstructors for Person are thus generated, Person will effectively look like this: 7 \nclass Person { 8 \npublic: 9 \n  template<typename T>        // perfect forwarding ctor 10 \n  explicit Person(T&& n); 11 \n  explicit Person(int idx);   // int ctor 12 \n  Person(const Person& rhs);  // copy ctor (compiler-generated) 13 \n  Person(Person&& rhs);       // move ctor (compiler-generated) 14 \n  … 15 \n}; 16 \nThis leads to behavior that’s intuitive only if you’ve spent  so much time around 17 \ncompilers and compiler-writers, you’ve forgotten what it’s like to be human: 18 \nPerson p(\"Bart\"); 19 \nauto clone(p);                // create new Person from p; 20 \n                              // this won't compile! 21 \nHere we’re trying to create a Person from another Person, which seems like 22 \nabout as obvious a case for copy construction as one can get. (p’s an lvalue, so we 23 \ncan banish any thoughts we might have about the “copying” being accomplished 24 \nthrough a move opera tion.) But this code won’t call the copy constructor. It will 25 \ncall the perfect -forwarding constructor . That function will then try to initialize 26 \nPerson’s std::string data member with a Person object (p). std::string 27 \nhaving no constructor taking a Person, your compilers will throw up their hands 28 \nin exasperation, possibly punishing you with a long and incomprehensible error 29 \nmessage as an expression of their displeasure. 30 \n“Why,” you might wonder, “does the perfect -forwarding constructor get called i n-31 \nstead of th e copy constructor? We’re initializing a Person with another Person!” 32\n\n#### 第 215 页原文\n\nPage 215 \n \nIndeed we are, but compilers are sworn to uphold the rules of C++, and the rules of 1 \nrelevance here are the ones governing the resolution of calls to overloaded fun c-2 \ntions. 3 \nCompilers reaso n as follows. clone is being initialized with a non -const lvalue 4 \n(p), and that means that the templatized  constructor can be instantiated to take a 5 \nnon-const lvalue of type Person. After such instantiation, the Person class looks 6 \nlike this: 7 \nclass Person { 8 \npublic: 9 \n  explicit Person(Person& n);  // instantiated from perfect- 10 \n                               // forwarding template 11 \n  explicit Person(int idx);    // as before 12 \n  Person(const Person& rhs);   // copy ctor (compiler-generated) 13 \n  … 14 \n}; 15 \nIn the statement, 16 \nauto clone(p); 17 \np could be passed to either the copy constructor or the instantiated template. Call-18 \ning the copy constructor would require adding const to p to match the copy co n-19 \nstructor’s parameter’s type, but calling the instantiated template requires no such 20 \naddition. The overload generated from the template is thus a better match, so 21 \ncompilers do what they’re designed to do : generate a call to the better -matching 22 \nfunction. “Copying” non-const lvalues of type Person is thus handled by the pe r-23 \nfect-forwarding constructor, not the copy constructor.  24 \nIf we change the example slightly so that the object to be copied is const, we hear 25 \nan entirely different tune: 26 \nconst Person cp(\"Bart\");      // object is now const 27 \nauto clone(cp);               // calls copy constructor! 28\n\n#### 第 216 页原文\n\nPage 216 \n \nBecause the object to be copied is now const, it’s an exact match for the param e-1 \nter taken by the copy constructor. The templatized constructor can be instantiated 2 \nto have the same signature, 3 \nclass Person { 4 \npublic: 5 \n  explicit Person(const Person& n);    // instantiated from 6 \n                                       // template 7 \n  Person(const Person& rhs);           // copy ctor 8 \n                                       // (compiler-generated) 9 \n  … 10 \n}; 11 \nbut this doesn’t matter, because one of the ove rloading-resolution rules in C++ is 12 \nthat in situations where a template instantiation and a  non-template function (i.e., 13 \na “normal” function) are equally good matches for a function call, the normal func-14 \ntion is preferred.  The copy constructor (a normal function) thereby trumps an in-15 \nstantiated template with the same signature.  16 \n(If you’re wondering why compilers generate a copy constructor when they could 17 \ninstantiate a templatized constructor to get the signature that the copy constructor 18 \nwould have, review Item 19.) 19 \nThe interaction among perfect -forwarding constructors and compiler -generated 20 \ncopy and move operations develops even more wrinkles when inheritance enters 21 \nthe picture. In particular, the con ventional implementations of derived class copy 22 \nand move operations behave quite surprisingly. Here, take a look: 23 \nclass SpecialPerson: public Person { 24 \npublic: 25 \n  SpecialPerson(const SpecialPerson& rhs)  // copy ctor; calls 26 \n  : Person(rhs)                            // base class  27 \n  { … }                                    // forwarding ctor! 28 \n  SpecialPerson(SpecialPerson&& rhs)       // move ctor; calls 29 \n  : Person(std::move(rhs))                 // base class  30 \n  { … }                                    // forwarding ctor! 31 \n}; 32 \nAs the comments indicate, the derived class copy and move constructors don’t call 33 \ntheir base class’s copy and move constructors, they call the base class’s perfect -34 \nforwarding constructor! To understand why, note that the derived class fun ctions 35\n\n#### 第 217 页原文\n\nPage 217 \n \nare using arguments of type SpecialPerson to pass to their base class (they actu-1 \nally pass const SpecialPersons, but in this case,  const is immaterial) , then 2 \nwork through the template instantiation and overloading-resolution consequences 3 \nfor the constructors  in class Person. Ultimately, the code won’t compile, because 4 \nthere’s no std::string constructor taking a SpecialPerson. 5 \nI hope that by now I’ve convinced you that overloading on universal reference p a-6 \nrameters is something you should a void if at all possible . But if overloading on 7 \nuniversal references is a bad idea, what do you do if you need a function that fo r-8 \nwards most argument types, yet needs to treat some argument types in a special 9 \nfashion? That egg can be unscrambled in a number  of ways. So many, in fact, that 10 \nI’ve devoted an entire Item to them. It’s Item 29. The next Item. Keep reading , 11 \nyou’ll bump right into it. 12 \nThings to Remember 13 \n Overloading on universal references almost always leads to the universal re f-14 \nerence overload being called more frequently than expected. 15 \n Perfect-forwarding constructors are especially problematic, because they’re 16 \ntypically better matches than copy constructors for non-const lvalues, and they 17 \ncan hijack derived class calls to base class copy and move constructors. 18 \n Item 29: Familiarize yourself with alternatives to overload-19 \ning on universal references. 20 \nItem 28 explains that overloading on universal references can lead to a variety of 21 \nproblems, both for freestanding and for member functions  (especially constru c-22 \ntors). Yet it also gives examples where such overloading could be useful. If only it 23 \nwould behave the way we’d like ! This Item explores ways to achieve the desired 24 \nbehavior, either through designs that avoid overloading on universal references or 25 \nby employing them in ways that constrain the types of arguments they can match. 26 \nThe discussion that follows builds on the examples introduced in Item 28. If you 27 \nhaven’t read that Item recently, you’ll want to review it before continuing.  28\n\n#### 第 218 页原文\n\nPage 218 \n \nAbandoning overloading 1 \nThe first example in Item 28, logAndAdd, is representative of the many functions 2 \nthat can avoid the drawbacks of overloading on universal references by simply us-3 \ning different names for the would-be overloads. The two logAndAdd overloads, for 4 \nexample, could be broken into logAndAddName and logAndAddNameIdx. Alas, this 5 \napproach won’t work for the second example we considered, the Person construc-6 \ntor, because constructor names are fixed by the language . Besides, who wants to 7 \ngive up overloading?  8 \nPassing by const T& 9 \nAn alternative is to revert to C++98 and replace pass -by-universal-reference with 10 \npass-by-(lvalue)-reference-to-const. In fact, that’s the first approach Item 28 con-11 \nsiders (on page 213). The drawback is that the design isn’t as efficient as we’d pre-12 \nfer. Knowing what we now know about the interaction of universal references and 13 \noverloading, giving up some efficiency to keep things simple might be a more a t-14 \ntractive trade-off than it initially appeared. 15 \nPassing by value 16 \nAn approach that often allows you to dial up performance without any increase in 17 \ncomplexity is to replace pass -by-reference parameters with, counterintuitively, 18 \npass-by-value. “Pass by reference” here refers to any kind of reference. This strate-19 \ngy can supplant both  C++98’s pass -by-lvalue-reference-to-const and C++11’s 20 \npass-by-universal-reference. The design adheres to the advice in Item 17 to pass 21 \nobjects by va lue when you know you’ll copy them, so I’ll defer to that Item for a 22 \ndetailed discussion of how things work  and how efficient they are . Here, I’ll just 23 \nshow how the technique could be used in the Person example: 24 \nclass Person { 25 \npublic: 26 \n  Person(std::string n)          // replaces T&& ctor; see 27 \n  : name(std::move(n)) {}        // Item 17 for use of std::move 28 \n  Person(int idx)                // as before 29 \n  : name(nameFromIdx(idx)) {}  30 \n  … 31\n\n#### 第 219 页原文\n\nPage 219 \n \nprivate: 1 \n  std::string name; 2 \n}; 3 \nBecause there’s no std::string constructor taking only an integer, all int and 4 \nint-like arguments to a Person constructor (e.g., std::size_t, short, long, 5 \netc.) get funneled to the int overload. Similarly, all arguments of type 6 \nstd::string (and things from which std::strings can be created , e.g., literals 7 \nsuch as \"Ruth\") get passed to the constructor taking a std::string. There are 8 \nthus no surpr ises for callers. You could argue , I suppose,  that some people might 9 \nbe surprised that using 0 or NULL to indicate a null pointer would invoke the int 10 \noverload, but such people should be referred to Item 8 and required to read it re-11 \npeatedly until the thought of using 0 or NULL as a null pointer makes them recoil. 12 \nTag dispatch 13 \nNeither pass by lvalue-reference-to-const nor pass by value offer support for per-14 \nfect forwarding. If your motivation for the use of a universal reference is perfect 15 \nforwarding, you have to use a universal reference; there’s no other choice . Yet we 16 \ndon’t want to  abandon overloading. So if we don’t  give up overloading and we 17 \ndon’t give up universal references, how can we avoid overloading on universal re f-18 \nerences? 19 \nIt’s actually not that hard. Calls to overloaded functions are resolved by looking at 20 \nall the parameters of all the overloads as well as all the arguments at the call site, 21 \nthen choosing the function with the best overall match —taking into account all 22 \nparameter/argument combinations. A universal reference parameter generally  23 \nprovides an exact match for whatever’s passed in, but if the univer sal reference is 24 \npart of a parameter list containing other parameters that are not universal refer-25 \nences, sufficiently poor matches on the non -universal reference parameters can 26 \nknock an overload with a universal reference out of the running. That’s the bas is 27 \nbehind the tag dispatch approach, and an example will make the foregoing descrip-28 \ntion easier to understand.  29 \nWe’ll apply tag dispatch to the logAndAdd example from page  214. Here’s that 30 \ncode, to save you the trouble of looking it up: 31\n\n#### 第 220 页原文\n\nPage 220 \n \nstd::set<std::string> names;           // global data structure  1 \ntemplate<typename T>                   // make log entry, add 2 \nvoid logAndAdd(T&& name)               // name to data structure 3 \n{ 4 \n  auto now = std::chrono::system_clock::now(); 5 \n  log(now, \"logAndAdd\"); 6 \n  names.emplace(std::forward<T>(name));  7 \n} 8 \nBy itself, this function works fine, but were we to introduce the overload taking an 9 \nint that’s used to look up objects by index , we’d be back in the troubled land of 10 \nItem 28. T he goal of this Item is to avoid that. Rather than adding the overload, 11 \nwe’ll reimplement logAndAdd to delegate to two other functions, one for integral 12 \nvalues and one for everything else. logAndAdd itself will accept all argument 13 \ntypes, both integral and non-integral.  14 \nThe two functions doing the real work  will be named  logAndAddImpl, i.e., we’ll 15 \nuse overloading. One of the functions will take a universal reference. So we’ll have 16 \nboth overloading and universal references. But each function will also take a se c-17 \nond parameter, one that indicates whether the argument being pa ssed is integral. 18 \nThis second parameter is what will prevent us from tumbling into the morass d e-19 \nscribed in Item 28, because we’ll arrange it so that t he second parameter will be 20 \nthe factor that determines which overload is selected. 21 \nYes, I know, “Blah, blah, blah. Stop talking and show me the code!” No problem. 22 \nHere’s an almost- correct version of the updated logAndAdd:  23 \ntemplate<typename T> 24 \nvoid logAndAdd(T&& name)  25 \n{  26 \n  logAndAddImpl(std::forward<T>(name),       27 \n                std::is_integral<T>());     // not quite correct 28 \n} 29 \nThis function forwards its parameter to logAndAddImpl, but it also passes an a r-30 \ngument indicating whether the type it received (T) is integral. At least, that's what 31 \nit's supposed to do. For integral arguments that are rvalues, it ’s also what it does. 32 \nBut, as Item 30 explains, if an lvalue argument is passed to the universal reference 33 \nname, the type deduced for T will be an lvalue reference. So if an lvalue of type int 34 \nis passed to logAndAdd, T will be deduced to be int&. int& is not an integral type, 35\n\n#### 第 221 页原文\n\nPage 221 \n \nbecause references aren't integral types. That means that std::is_integral<T> 1 \nwill be false for any lvalue argument, even if the argument really does represent an 2 \nintegral value. 3 \nRecognizing the problem is tantamount to identifying the solution, because the 4 \never-handy Standard C++ Library has a type trait, std::remove_reference, that 5 \ndoes both what its name suggests and what we need: remove any reference qual i-6 \nfiers from a type. The proper way to write logAndAdd is therefore: 7 \ntemplate<typename T> 8 \nvoid logAndAdd(T&& name)  9 \n{  10 \n  logAndAddImpl( 11 \n    std::forward<T>(name),       12 \n    std::is_integral<typename std::remove_reference<T>::type>() 13 \n  );  14 \n} 15 \nThis does the trick . (In C++14, you can save a few keystrokes by using 16 \nstd::remove_reference_t<T> in place of the highlighted text. For details, see 17 \nItem 9.) 18 \nWith that taken care of, we can shift our attention to the function being called, lo-19 \ngAndAddImpl. There are two overloads, and the first  is applicable only to non -20 \nintegral types (i.e., to types where  std::is_integral<typename 21 \nstd::remove_reference<T>::type> is false): 22 \ntemplate<typename T>                             // non-integral 23 \nvoid logAndAddImpl(T&& name, std::false_type)    // argument:  24 \n{                                                // add it to  25 \n  auto now = std::chrono::system_clock::now();   // global data 26 \n  log(now, \"logAndAdd\");                         // structure 27 \n  names.emplace_back(std::forward<T>(name));  28 \n}                                        29 \nThis is straightforward code, once you understand the mechanics behind the  high-30 \nlighted parameter. Conceptually, logAndAdd passes a boolean to logAndAddImpl 31 \nindicating whether a n integral type was passed to logAndAdd, but true and 32 \nfalse are runtime values, and we need to use overload resolution—a compile-time 33 \nphenomenon—to choose the correct logAndAddImpl overload. That means we 34 \nneed a type that corresponds to true and a different type that corresponds to 35\n\n#### 第 222 页原文\n\nPage 222 \n \nfalse. This need is common enough that the Standard Library provides what is 1 \nrequired under the names  std::true_type and std::false_type. The arg u-2 \nment passed to logAndAddImpl by logAndAdd is an object of a type th at inherits 3 \nfrom std::true_type if T is integral and from std::false_type if T is not inte-4 \ngral. The net result is that this logAndAddImpl overload is a viable candidate for 5 \nthe call in logAndAdd only if T is not an integral type. 6 \nThe second overload covers  the opposite case: when T is an integral type. In that 7 \nevent, logAndAddImpl simply finds the name corresponding to the passed -in in-8 \ndex and passes that name back to logAndAdd: 9 \nstd::string nameFromIdx(int idx);             // as in Item 28 10 \nvoid logAndAddImpl(int idx, std::true_type)   // integral 11 \n{                                             // argument: look 12 \n  logAndAdd(anyFromIdx(idx));                 // up name and 13 \n}                                             // call logAndAdd 14 \n                                              // with it 15 \nBy having logAndAddImpl for an index look up the corresponding  name and pass 16 \nit to logAndAdd (from where it will be std::forwarded to the other logAn-17 \ndAddImpl overload), we avoid the need to put the logging code in both logAn-18 \ndAddImpl overloads. 19 \nIn this design, the types std::true_type and std::false_type are “tags” 20 \nwhose only purpose is to force overload resolution to go the way we want. Notice 21 \nthat we don’t even name those parameters. They serve no purpose at runtime, and 22 \nin fact we hope that compilers will re cognize that tag parameters are  unused and 23 \nwill optimize them ou t of the program's execution image . (Some compilers do, at 24 \nleast some of the time.) The call to the overloaded implementation functions inside 25 \nlogAndAdd “dispatches” the work to the correct overload by causing the proper 26 \ntag object to be created. Hence the  name for this design: tag dispatch. It’s a stan d-27 \nard building block of template metaprogramming, and the more you look at code 28 \ninside contemporary C++ libraries, the more often you’ll encounter it.  29 \nFor our purposes, what’s important about tag dispatch is less how it works and 30 \nmore how it permits us to combine universal references and overloading without 31 \nthe problems described in Item 28. The dispatching function—logAndAdd—takes 32\n\n#### 第 223 页原文\n\nPage 223 \n \nan unconstrained universal reference parameter, but this function is not overloa d-1 \ned. The implementation functions —logAndAddImpl—are overloaded , and one 2 \ntakes a universal reference parameter, but resolution of calls to these functions 3 \ndepends not just on the universal reference parameter, but also on the tag parame-4 \nter, and the tag values are designed so that no more than one overload will ever be 5 \na viable match. As a result, it’s the tag that determines which overload gets called . 6 \nThe fact that the universal reference parameter will always generate an exact 7 \nmatch for its argument is immaterial. 8 \nTag dispatch constructors 9 \nA keystone of tag dispatch is the existenc e of a single (unoverloaded) function as 10 \nthe client API. This single function dispatches the work to be done to the impl e-11 \nmentation functions. Creating an unoverloaded dispatch  function is usually easy, 12 \nbut the second problem case Item 28 considers, that of the perfect -forwarding 13 \nconstructor for the Person class (see page 216), is an exception . Compilers may 14 \ngenerate copy and move constructors themselves, so even if you write only one 15 \nconstructor and use tag dispatch within it, some c onstructor calls may be handled 16 \nby compiler-generated functions that bypass the tag dispatch system.  17 \nFrankly, the real problem is not that the compiler -generated functions sometimes 18 \nbypass the tag dispatch design, it’s that they don’t always pass it by. We virtually 19 \nalways want the copy constructor for a class to handle requests to copy lvalues of 20 \nthat type, but, as Item 28 demonstrates, providing a constructor taking a universal 21 \nreference causes the universal reference constructor (instead of the copy construc-22 \ntor) to be called when copying non-const lvalues.  23 \nWhat we need is a way to tell compilers, “Look, when I’m constructing an object  24 \nfrom another object of the same type, just do the normal thing: copy each member 25 \nof an lvalue object, and move each member of an rvalue object. Don’t worry about 26 \nwhether the initializing object is const or not, just do a normal copy or move.” 27 \nBrace yourself for good news, because there is, in fact, a way to say that. 28 \nBefore we see  what it is , let’s look at a tag-dispatching Person constructor. The 29 \ndesign is identical to that we saw for logAndAdd, but we’ll apply  a minor twist. 30 \nWe’ll take advantage of C++11’s constructor delegation to have other constructors 31\n\n#### 第 224 页原文\n\nPage 224 \n \nact as  implementation functions. Using constructor delegation  isn’t necessary in 1 \nthis example, but it’s a good habit to get into  in contexts such as this , because it 2 \nalso works with const and reference data members, which may be initialized only 3 \nduring construction. The constructors we’ll delegate to aren’t designed to be called 4 \ndirectly, so we’ll declare them private. 5 \nHere’s the code, where I’ m employing C++14’s std::remove_reference_t<T> 6 \ninstead of C++11’s wordier “typename std::remove_reference<T>::type”: 7 \nclass Person { 8 \npublic: 9 \n  template<typename T>               // dispatch to other ctors 10 \n  explicit Person(T&& initVal)       // via ctor delegation 11 \n  : Person( 12 \n      initVal, 13 \n      std::is_integral<std::remove_reference_t<T>>()  // C++14 14 \n    )                                                 // only 15 \n  {}  16 \n  … 17 \nprivate: 18 \n  template<typename T>                      // private ctor for 19 \n  Person(T&& n, std::true_type)             // integral args  20 \n  : Person(nameFromIdx(std::forward<T>(n)))  21 \n  {} 22 \n  template<typename T>                      // private ctor for  23 \n  Person(T&& n, std::false_type)            // non-integral args 24 \n  : name(std::forward<T>(n))  25 \n  {} 26 \n  std::string name;                         // as in Item 28 27 \n}; 28 \nOur goal is  to modify this class so that the universal reference const ructor never 29 \ngets invoked if a Person is created from another Person.  30 \nOur approach is based on three observations. First, a class may contain variants of 31 \nthe copy operations. In particular, classes may specify different copy constructors 32 \nfor const and non-const objects. Second, =default can be used to get the “nor-33 \nmal” implementation of any special member function (see Item 19), including all 34 \ncopy operation variants. Third, as Item 28 explains, if a template can be instantiat-35\n\n#### 第 225 页原文\n\nPage 225 \n \ned with the same signature as a non-template function, calls to that function signa-1 \nture invoke the normal function, not the template instantiation.  2 \nSo what we’ll do is manually declare the copy and move operation  signatures we 3 \nwant to behave “normally,” (i.e., to not use tag dispatch), and we’ll implement them 4 \nusing =default. The existence of these functions will prevent the universal refe r-5 \nence constructor from being instantiated with their signatures. Hence: 6 \nclass Person { 7 \npublic: 8 \n  template<typename T>                // as before,  9 \n  explicit Person(T&& initVal);       // tag-dispatching ctor 10 \n  Person(const Person&) = default;    // copy-construct from 11 \n                                      // const lvalue 12 \n  Person(Person&) = default;          // copy-construct from 13 \n                                      // non-const lvalue 14 \n  Person(Person&&) = default;         // move-construct 15 \n                                      // from (non-const) rvalue 16 \n  … 17 \n}; 18 \nYou might wonder why there’s only a si ngle move constructor here, i.e., why 19 \nthere’s no “move constructor” taking a const Person&&. That’s because the only 20 \nvalid move constructor signature is the one above; there’s no const variant. 21 \nItem 25 explains that it makes no sense to try to move from a const object, so C++ 22 \ndoesn’t recognize a constructor taking a const rvalue reference as a special me m-23 \nber function.  24 \nBecause this Item is already too long, I’ll leave it to you to verify that when Person 25 \nis defined like this, creation of a Person from another Person will bypass the uni-26 \nversal reference constructor and do the “normal” thing, regardless of whether the 27 \nsource object is an lvalue or an rvalue and regardless of whether it’s const. 28 \nItem 28 points out that a second problem with tag-dispatching constructors is that 29 \nthey interact poorly with inheritance, and it gives this example:  30 \nclass SpecialPerson: public Person { 31 \npublic: 32 \n  SpecialPerson(const SpecialPerson& rhs)  // copy ctor; calls 33", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.226–p.250", "pages": "p.226–p.250", "raw_text": "#### 第 226 页原文\n\nPage 226 \n \n  : Person(rhs)                            // base class  1 \n  { … }                                    // forwarding ctor! 2 \n  SpecialPerson(SpecialPerson&& rhs)       // move ctor; calls 3 \n  : Person(std::move(rhs))                 // base class  4 \n  { … }                                    // forwarding ctor! 5 \n}; 6 \nThe good news is that if you can live with the compiler -generated copy and move 7 \noperations, this problem doesn’t arise. Such operations will call  their base class 8 \ncounterparts, just as you’d hope.  9 \nIf you need to implement these functions yourself, you have to make sure that you 10 \npass base class arguments to your base class constructors. That’s not what ha p-11 \npens in the code above. There, both SpecialPerson constructors pass rhs as an 12 \nargument to the Person constructor, but rhs’s basic type is SpecialPerson, not 13 \nPerson. That will cause the universal reference constructor in Person to instanti-14 \nate and “steal” what you’d like to be calls to the Person copy and move constru c-15 \ntors. The solution is to cast away the derived -ness of the argument you pass to the 16 \nbase class by casting it to a base class reference: 17 \nclass SpecialPerson: public Person { 18 \npublic: 19 \n  SpecialPerson(const SpecialPerson& rhs)          // now calls 20 \n  : Person(static_cast<const Person&>(rhs))        // base copy 21 \n  { … }                                            // ctor 22 \n  SpecialPerson(SpecialPerson&& rhs)               // now calls  23 \n  : Person(static_cast<Person&&>(std::move(rhs)))  // base move 24 \n  { … }                                            // ctor 25 \n}; 26 \nNote how the copy constructor casts rhs to an lvalue reference, while the move 27 \nconstructor casts it to an rvalue reference. Subtilties such as this are one of the 28 \nreasons you should really try to follow Item 19’s advice to avoid writing your own 29 \ncopy and move operations. 30 \nTrade-offs 31 \nThe first three techniques considered in this Item —abandoning overloading, pass-32 \ning by const T&, and pass ing by value—specify a type for each parameter in the 33 \nfunction(s) to be called. Tag dispatch uses perfect forwarding, hence doesn’t speci-34\n\n#### 第 227 页原文\n\nPage 227 \n \nfy types for the parameters. This fundamental decision —to specify a type or not —1 \nhas important consequences. 2 \nAs a rule, perfect forwarding is more efficient, because it avoids the creation of 3 \ntemporary objects solely for the purpose of conforming to the typ e of a parameter 4 \ndeclaration. In the case of the Person constructor, perfect forwarding permits a 5 \nstring literal such as \"Nancy\" to be forwarded to the constructor for the 6 \nstd::string inside Person, whereas techniques not using perfect forwarding 7 \nmust create a temporary std::string object from the string literal to satisfy the 8 \nparameter specification for the Person constructor.  9 \nBut perfect forwarding has drawbacks. One is that some kinds of arguments can’t 10 \nbe perfect-forwarded, even though they can be pass ed to functions taking specific 11 \ntypes. Item 32 explores these perfect forwarding failure cases.  12 \nA second issue is the comprehensibility of error messages when clients pass inva-13 \nlid arguments. Suppose, for example,  a client creating a Person object passes a 14 \nstring literal made up of char16_ts (a type introduced in C++11  to represent 16-15 \nbit characters) instead of chars (which is what a std::string consists of): 16 \nPerson p(u\"Konrad Zuse\");    // \"Konrad Zuse\" consists of 17 \n                             // characters of type std::char16_t 18 \nWith the first three approaches examined in this Item, compilers will see that the 19 \navailable constructors take either int or std::string, and they'll produce a 20 \nmore or less  straightforward message explaining that there's no conversion from 21 \nchar16_t* to int or std::string.  22 \nWith an approach based on universal reference parameters , however, the 23 \nchar16_t* pointer gets bound to the constructor ’s parameter without complaint. 24 \nFrom there it ’s forwarded to the constructor of Person’s std::string data 25 \nmember, and it’s only at that point that the mismatch between what the caller 26 \npassed in (essentially a char16_t* pointer) and what’s required (any type a c-27 \nceptable to the std::string constructor) is discovered. The resulting error me s-28 \nsage is likely to be, er, impressive. With one of the compilers I use, it’s 158 lines 29 \nlong. In this  example, the  universal ref erence is forwarded only once  (from the 30 \nPerson constructor to the std::string constructor), but the more complex the 31\n\n#### 第 228 页原文\n\nPage 228 \n \nsystem, the more likely that a universal reference is forwarded through several 1 \nlayers of function calls before finally arriving at a site that knows what argument 2 \ntype(s) are acceptable.  The more times the universal reference is forwarded, the 3 \nmore baffling the error message when something goes wrong.  Many developers 4 \nfind that this issue alone is grounds to reserve universal reference parame ters for 5 \ninterfaces where performance is a foremost concern. 6 \nThings to Remember 7 \n Alternatives to the combination of universal references and overloading i n-8 \nclude the use of distinct function names, passing parameters by (lvalue) -9 \nreference-to-const, passing parameters by value, and using tag dispatch. 10 \n Universal reference parameters often have efficiency advantages, but they also 11 \nhave usability disadvantages. 12 \n Item 30: Understand reference collapsing. 13 \nItem 25 remarks that when an argument is passed to a template function , the de-14 \nduced type for the template parameter encodes whether that argument is an 15 \nlvalue or an rvalue. The Item fails to mention that this happens only when the a r-16 \ngument is used to initialize a parameter that’s a universal reference, but there’s a 17 \ngood reason for the omission: universal references aren’t introduced until Item 26. 18 \nTogether, these observations about universal references and lvalue/rvalue enco d-19 \ning mean that for this template, 20 \ntemplate<typename T> 21 \nvoid func(T&& param); 22 \nthe deduced template parameter T will encode wh ether the argument passed to 23 \nparam was an lvalue or an rvalue.  24 \nThe encoding mechanism is simple. When an lvalue is passed as an argument, T is 25 \ndeduced to be an lvalue reference. When an rvalue is passed, T is deduced to be a 26 \nnon-reference. (Note the asymmetry: lvalues are encoded as lvalue references, but 27 \nrvalues are encoded as non-references.) Hence: 28 \nWidget widgetFactory();     // function returning rvalue 29\n\n#### 第 229 页原文\n\nPage 229 \n \nWidget w;                   // a variable (an lvalue) 1 \nfunc(w);                    // call func with lvalue; T deduced 2 \n                            // to be Widget& 3 \nfunc(widgetFactory());      // call func with rvalue; T deduced 4 \n                            // to be Widget 5 \nIn both calls to func, a Widget is passed, yet because one Widget is an lvalue and 6 \none is an rvalue, different types are deduced for the template parameter T.  This, as 7 \nwe shall soon see, is what determines whether universal references become rvalue 8 \nreferences or lvalue references, and it’s also the underlying mechanism through 9 \nwhich std::forward does its work. 10 \nBefore we can look more closely at std::forward and universal references , we 11 \nmust observe that references to references  are illegal in C++ . Should you be so 12 \nbold as to try to declare one, your compilers will reprimand you: 13 \nint x; 14 \n… 15 \nauto& & rx = x;   // error! can't declare reference to reference 16 \nBut consider what happens when an lvalue is passed to a template function taking 17 \na universal reference: 18 \ntemplate<typename T> 19 \nvoid func(T&& param);    // as before 20 \nfunc(w);                 // invoke func with lvalue;  21 \n                         // T deduced as Widget& 22 \nIf we take the type deduced for T (i.e., Widget&) and use it to instantiate the specif-23 \nic function we’re calling, we get this: 24 \nvoid func(Widget& && param); 25 \nA reference to a reference! And yet compilers issue no protest. We know from Item 26 \n26 that because the universal reference param is being initialized  with an lvalue, 27 \nparam’s type is supposed to be an lvalue reference, but how does the compiler get 28 \nfrom the result of taking the deduced type for T and substituting it into the te m-29 \nplate to the following, which is the ultimate function signature? 30 \nvoid func(Widget& param); 31\n\n#### 第 230 页原文\n\nPage 230 \n \nThe answer is reference collapsing . Yes, you are forbidden from declaring refer-1 \nences to references, but compilers may do it in particular contexts, template instan-2 \ntiation being among them . When compilers generate references to references, ref-3 \nerence collapsing dictates what happens next. 4 \nThere are two kinds of references (lvalue and rvalue), so there are four possible 5 \nreference-reference combinations (lvalue to lvalue, lvalue to rvalue, rvalue to 6 \nlvalue, and rvalue to rvalue). If a reference to a reference arises in a context where 7 \nthis is permitted (e.g., during template instantiation), the references collapse to a 8 \nsingle reference according to this simple rule: 9 \n If either reference is an lvalue reference, the result is an lvalue reference. Oth-10 \nerwise the result is an rvalue reference. 11 \nIn our example above, substitution of the deduced type Widget& into the template 12 \nfunc yields an rvalue reference to an lvalue reference , and the reference -13 \ncollapsing rule tells us that the result is an lvalue reference.  14 \nReference collapsing is a key part of what makes std::forward work. Item 25 15 \nintroduces the following conceptual implementation:  16 \ntemplate<typename T>                   // conceptual impl.  17 \nT&& forward(T&& param)                 // of std::forward 18 \n{                                      // (in namespace std) 19 \n  if (is_lvalue_reference<T>::value) { // if T indicates lvalue 20 \n    return param;                      // return param as lvalue 21 \n  } else {                             // else 22 \n    return move(param);                // return param as rvalue 23 \n  } 24 \n} 25 \nA real std::forward implementation looks more like this: 26 \ntemplate<typename T>                   // in namespace std 27 \nT&& forward(T&& param) 28 \n{ 29 \n  return static_cast<T&&>(param); 30 \n} 31 \nThis isn’t quite standards-conformant (I’ve omitted a few interface details), but it 32 \nmakes clear that, as Item 25 claims, std::forward is simply a cast. 33\n\n#### 第 231 页原文\n\nPage 231 \n \nBut how does this work? As explained in Item 27, std::forward is generally used 1 \non universal reference parameters, so a typical use case could look like this: 2 \ntemplate<typename T> 3 \nvoid f(T&& fParam) 4 \n{ 5 \n  …                                  // do some work 6 \n  func(std::forward<T>(fParam));     // forward fParam to func 7 \n} 8 \nBecause fParam is a universal reference, we know that the type parameter T will 9 \nencode whether the argument passed to f (i.e., the expression used to initialize 10 \nfParam) was an lvalue or an rvalue. std::forward’s job is to cast fParam (an 11 \nlvalue) to an rvalue if and only if T encodes that the argument passed to f was an 12 \nrvalue, i.e., if T is a non-reference type. Let’s go through this step by step. 13 \nSuppose that the argument passed to f is an lvalue of type Widget. In that case, T 14 \nwill be  deduced as  Widget&. The call to std::forward will instantiate as 15 \nstd::forward<Widget&>. Plugging Widget& into  the above implementation 16 \nyields 17 \nWidget& && forward(Widget& && param)  18 \n{ return static_cast<Widget& &&>(param); } 19 \nwhich, after reference collapsing, becomes: 20 \nWidget& forward(Widget& param) 21 \n{ return static_cast<Widget&>(param);  } 22 \nSo when an lvalue argument is passed to the function template f, std::forward 23 \nis instantiated to take and return an lvalue reference. Lvalue references are, by 24 \ndefinition, lvalues, so an lvalue argument passed to f will be forwarded to func as 25 \nan lvalue, just like it’s supposed to be. 26 \nNow suppose that the argument passed to f is an rvalue of type Widget. In this 27 \ncase, the deduced type for the f’s type parameter, T,  will simply be Widget. The 28 \ncall inside f to std::forward will thus be to std::forward<Widget>. Substitut-29 \ning Widget for T in std::forward gives this: 30 \nWidget&& forward(Widget&& fwdParam) 31 \n{ return static_cast<Widget&&>(param); } 32\n\n#### 第 232 页原文\n\nPage 232 \n \nNo references to references arise in this case, so no reference collapsing  occurs. 1 \nBecause rvalue references returned from functions are defined to be rvalues, this 2 \nmeans that std::forward will turn f’s parameter fParm (an lvalue) into an rva l-3 \nue. The end result is that an rvalue argu ment passed to f will be forwarded to 4 \nfunc as an rvalue, which is precisely what is supposed to happen. 5 \nReference collapsing occurs in four contexts.  The first and most common is te m-6 \nplate instantiation. The second is type generation for auto variables. The details 7 \nare essentially the same as for templates, because type deduction for auto varia-8 \nbles is essentially the same as type deduction for templates  (see Item 2).  Consider 9 \nagain this example from earlier in the Item: 10 \ntemplate<typename T> 11 \nvoid func(T&& param); 12 \nWidget widgetFactory();     // function returning rvalue 13 \nWidget w;                   // a variable (an lvalue) 14 \nfunc(w);                    // call func with lvalue; T deduced 15 \n                            // to be Widget& 16 \nfunc(widgetFactory());      // call func with rvalue; T deduced 17 \n                            // to be Widget 18 \nThis example can be mimicked in auto form. The declaration 19 \nauto&& w1 = w; 20 \ninitializes w1 with an lvalue, thus deducing the type Widget& for auto. Plugging 21 \nWidget& in for auto in the declaration for w1 yields this reference -to-reference 22 \ncode, 23 \nWidget& && w1 = w; 24 \nwhich, after reference collapsing, becomes  25 \nWidget& w1 = w; 26 \nAs a result, w1 is an lvalue reference. 27 \nOn the other hand, this declaration, 28 \nauto&& w2 = widgetFactory(); 29\n\n#### 第 233 页原文\n\nPage 233 \n \ninitializes w2 with an rvalue, causing the non-reference type Widget to be deduced 1 \nfor auto. Substituting Widget for auto gives us this: 2 \nWidget&& w2 = widgetFactory(); 3 \nThere are no references to references here, so we’re done: w2 is an rvalue refe r-4 \nence.  5 \nWe’re now in a position to truly understand the universal references introduced in 6 \nItem 26. A universal reference isn’t a new kind of reference, it’s simply an rvalue 7 \nreference in a reference collapsing context. The concept of universal references is 8 \nuseful, because it frees you from having to recognize the existence of reference col-9 \nlapsing contexts, to mentally deduce different types for lvalues and rvalues, and to 10 \napply the reference collapsing rule after mentally substituting the deduced types 11 \ninto the contexts in which they occur. 12 \nI said there were four such contexts, but we’ve discussed only  two: template i n-13 \nstantiation and auto type generation. The third is  the generation and use  of 14 \ntypedefs and alias declarations (see Item 9). If, during creation or evaluation of a 15 \ntypedef, references to references arise, reference collapsing intervenes to elim i-16 \nnate them. For example, suppose we have a Widget class template with an e m-17 \nbedded typedef for an rvalue reference type, 18 \ntemplate<typename T> 19 \nclass Widget { 20 \npublic: 21 \n  typedef T&& RvalueRefToT; 22 \n  … 23 \n}; 24 \nand suppose we instantiate Widget with an lvalue reference type: 25 \nWidget<int&> w; 26 \nSubstituting int& for T in the Widget template gives us the following typedef:  27 \ntypedef int& && RvalueRefToT; 28 \nReference collapsing reduces it to this, 29 \ntypedef int& RvalueRefToT; 30\n\n#### 第 234 页原文\n\nPage 234 \n \nwhich makes clear that the name we chose for the typedef is perhaps not as d e-1 \nscriptive as we’d hoped: RvalueRefToT is a typedef for an lvalue reference when 2 \nWidget is instantiated with an lvalue reference type. 3 \nThe final context in which reference collapsing takes place is uses of decltype. If, 4 \nduring evaluation of an expression involving decltype, a reference to reference 5 \narises, reference collapsing will kick in to eliminate it. 6 \nThings to Remember 7 \n Reference collapsing occurs in four contexts: template instantiation, auto type 8 \ngeneration, creation and use of typedefs and alias declarations , and 9 \ndecltype. 10 \n When compilers generate a reference to a reference  in a reference collapsing 11 \ncontext, the resu lt becomes a single reference. If either of the original refe r-12 \nences is an lvalue reference, the result is an lvalue reference. Otherwise it’s an 13 \nrvalue reference. 14 \n Universal references are rvalue references in reference collapsing contexts. 15 \n Item 31: Assume that move operations are not present, not 16 \ncheap, and not used. 17 \nMove semantics is arguably the premier feature of C++11 .  “Moving containers is 18 \nnow as cheap as copying pointers!,” you’re likely to hear, and “ Copying temporary 19 \nobjects is now so efficient, coding to avoid it is tantamount to premature optimiza-20 \ntion!” Such  sentiments are easy to understand. Move semantics is truly an i m-21 \nportant feature. It doesn’t just allow compilers to replace expensive copy oper a-22 \ntions with comparatively cheap moves, it actua lly requires that they do so  (when 23 \nthe proper conditions are fulfilled). Take your C++98 code  base, recompile with a 24 \nC++11-conformant compiler and Standard Library, and—shazam!—your software 25 \nruns faster!  26 \nMove semantics can really pull that off, and that grants the feature an aura worthy 27 \nof legend. Legends, however, are generally the result of exaggeration. The purpose 28 \nof this Item is to keep your expectations grounded.  29\n\n#### 第 235 页原文\n\nPage 235 \n \nLet’s begin with the observation that many types fail to support move semantics. 1 \nThe entire C++98 Standard Library was overhauled for C++11 to add move opera-2 \ntions for types where moving could be implemented faster than copying, and the 3 \nimplementation of the library components was revised to take advantage of these 4 \noperations, but chances are that you’re working with a code base that has not been 5 \ncompletely revised to take advantage of C++11.  For types in your applications (or 6 \nin the libraries you use) where no modifications for C++11 have been made, the 7 \nexistence of move support in your comp ilers is likely to do you little good. True, 8 \nC++11 is willing to  generate move operations for classes that lack them, but that 9 \nhappens only for classes declaring no copy operations, move  operations, or de-10 \nstructors (see Item 19). Data members or base classes that can’t be moved will also 11 \nsuppress compiler-generated move operations. For types without explicit support 12 \nfor moving and that don’t qualify for compiler-generated move operations, there is 13 \nno reason to expect C++11 to deliver any kind of performance im provement over 14 \nC++98. 15 \nEven types with explicit move support may not benefit as much as you’d hope. All 16 \ncontainers in the standard C++11 librar y support moving, for example, but it 17 \nwould be a mistake to assume that moving all containers is cheap.  For some co n-18 \ntainers, this is because there’s no truly cheap way to move their contents. For ot h-19 \ners, it’s because the truly cheap move operations the co ntainers offer c ome with 20 \ncaveats the container elements can’t satisfy.  21 \nConsider std::array, a new container in C++11. std::array is essentially a 22 \nbuilt-in array with an STL interface.  This is fundamentally different from the other 23 \nstandard containers, ea ch of which store their contents on the heap . Objects of 24 \nsuch container types hold (as data members) , conceptually, only a pointer to the 25 \nheap memory storing the contents of the container . (The reality is more complex, 26 \nbut for purposes of this analysis, the differences are not important.) The existence 27 \nof this pointer makes it possible to move the contents of an entire container in 28 \nconstant time: just move  the pointer to the container’s contents from the source 29 \ncontainer to the target, and set the source’s pointer to null: 30\n\n#### 第 236 页原文\n\nPage 236 \n \n 1 \nstd::array objects lack such a pointer, because the data for a std::array’s con-2 \ntents are stored directly in the std::array object: 3 \n 4 \nNote that the elements in aw1 are moved into aw2. Assuming that Widget is a type 5 \nwhere moving is faster than copying, moving a std::array of Widget will be 6 \nfaster than copying the same std::array. So std::array certainly offers move 7 \nsupport. Yet both moving and copying a std::array have linear-time computa-8 \ntional complexity, because each element in the container must be copied or moved. 9 \nThis is far from the “moving a container is now as cheap as assigning a couple of 10 \npointers” claim that one sometimes hears.  11 \nOn the other hand, std::string offers constant-time moves and linear-time cop-12 \nies. That makes it sound like moving is faster than copying, but that may not be the 13 \ncase. Many string implementations employ the small string optimization  (SSO).  14 \nWith the SSO, “small” strings (typically those with a capacity of no more than 15 15 \ncharacters) are stored in a buffer  within the std::string object; no heap -16 \nstd::vector<Widget> vw1;  \n// put data into vw1 \n… \n \n// move vw1 into vw2. Runs in \n// constant time. Only ptrs  \n// in vw1 and vw2 are modified   \nauto vw2 = std::move(vw1); \nWidgets \n \nvw1 \nWidgets \n null \nvw1 \n  \nvw2 \nstd::array<Widget, 10000> aw1; \n// put data into aw1 \n… \n \n// move aw1 into aw2. Runs in  \n// linear time. All elements in  \n// aw1 are moved into aw2 \nauto aw2 = std::move(aw1);   \nWidgets \naw1 \nWidgets (moved from) \naw1 \nWidgets (moved to) \naw2\n\n#### 第 237 页原文\n\nPage 237 \n \nallocated storage is used. Moving small strings using an SSO-based implementation 1 \nis no faster than copying them, because the copy -only-a-pointer trick that general-2 \nly underlies the performance advantage of moves over copies isn’t applicable.  3 \nThe motivation for the SSO is extensive evidence that for many applications, short 4 \nstrings are the norm. Using an internal buffer to store such strings’ values elim i-5 \nnates the need to dynamically allocate m emory for them, and that’s typically an 6 \nefficiency win. An implication of the win, however, is that moves are no faster than 7 \ncopies, though one could just as well take a glass -half-full approach and say that 8 \nfor such strings, copying is no slower than moving. 9 \nEven for types supporting speedy move operations, some seemingly sure -fire 10 \nmove situations can  still end up making copies . Item 16 explains that s ome co n-11 \ntainer operations in the Standard Library offer the strong exception safety guaran-12 \ntee, and that to ensure that legacy C++98 code dependent on these exception saf e-13 \nty guarantees isn’t broken when upgrading to C++11, the underlying copy oper a-14 \ntions may be replaced with move operations only if the move operations are 15 \nknown not to throw. A consequence is that even if a type offers move operations 16 \nthat are more efficient than the corresponding copy operations, and even if, at a 17 \nparticular point in the code,  a move operation would generally be appropriate 18 \n(e.g., if the source object is an rvalue), compilers might still be forced to invoke a 19 \ncopy operation, because the corresponding move operation isn’t declared noex-20 \ncept. 21 \nThere are thus several scenarios in which C++11’s move semantics do you no 22 \ngood: 23 \n No move operations: The object to be moved from fails to offer move oper a-24 \ntions. The move request therefore becomes a copy request. 25 \n Move not faster:  The object to be moved from has move operations that are 26 \nno faster than its copy operations. 27 \n Move not usable: The context in which the moving would take place requires 28 \na nothrow move operation, but that operation isn’t declared noexcept. 29\n\n#### 第 238 页原文\n\nPage 238 \n \nIt’s worth mentioning, too,  another scenario where move semantics offers no eff i-1 \nciency gain: 2 \n Source object  is lvalue: With very few exceptions (see e.g., Item 27) only 3 \nrvalues may be used as the source of a move operation. 4 \nBut the title of this Item is to assume that move operations are not present, not 5 \ncheap, and not used. This is typically the case in generic code, e.g., when writing 6 \ntemplates, because you don’t know all the types you’re working with. In such ci r-7 \ncumstances, you must be as conservative about copying objects as you were in 8 \nC++98—before move semantics existed.  This is also the case for “unstable” code, 9 \ni.e., code where the characteristics of the types being used are subject to relatively 10 \nfrequent modification. 11 \nOften, however, you know the types your code uses , and you can rely on their 12 \ncharacteristics not changing (e.g., whether they sup port inexpensive move oper a-13 \ntions).  When that’s the case, you don’t need to make assumptions. You can simply 14 \nlook up the move support details for the types you’re using. If those types offer 15 \ncheap move operations, and if you’re using objects in contexts wh ere those move 16 \noperations will be invoked, there’s no need for assumptions: you can safely rely on  17 \nmove semantics to replace copy operations with their less expensive move coun-18 \nterparts. 19 \nThings to remember 20 \n Assume that move operations are not present, not cheap, and not used. 21 \n In code with known types or support for move semantics, there is no need for 22 \nassumptions. 23 \n Item 32: Familiarize yourself with perfect forwarding fail-24 \nure cases. 25 \nOne of the features most prominently emblazoned on the C++11 box is perfect 26 \nforwarding. Perfect forwarding. It’s perfect! Alas, tear the box open, and you’ll find 27 \nthat there’s “perfect” (the ideal), and then there’s “perfect” (the reality). C++11’s 28 \nperfect forwarding is very good, but it achieves true p erfection only if you’re wil l-29\n\n#### 第 239 页原文\n\nPage 239 \n \ning to overlook an epsilon or two.  This Item is devoted to familiarizing you with 1 \nthe epsilons. 2 \nBefore embarking on  our epsilon exploration , it’s worthwhile to review what ’s 3 \nmeant by “perfect forwarding.” “Forwarding” just mean s that one function pas s-4 \nes—forwards—its parameters to another function. The goal is for the second fun c-5 \ntion (the one being forwarded to) to receive the same objects that the first function 6 \n(the one doing the forwar ding) received. That rules out by -value parameters, be-7 \ncause they’re copies of what the original caller passed in; we want the forwarded-8 \nto function  to be able to  work with the originally-passed-in objects . Pointer p a-9 \nrameters are also ruled out, because we don’t want to force callers to pass point-10 \ners. When it comes to general -purpose forwarding, we’ll be dealing with param e-11 \nters that are references. 12 \nPerfect forwarding means we don’t just forward objects, we also forward their sa-13 \nlient characteristics: their types, whether they’re lvalues or rvalues, and whether 14 \nthey’re const or volatile. In conjunction with the observation that we’ll be deal-15 \ning with reference parameters, this implies that we’ll be using universal references 16 \n(see Item 26), because only universal reference parameters encode information 17 \nabout the lvalueness and rvalueness of the arguments that are passed to them.  18 \nLet’s assume we have some function f, and we’d like to write a function (in truth, a 19 \nfunction template) that forwards to it. The core of what we need looks like this: 20 \ntemplate<typename T>  21 \nvoid fwd(T&& param)                  // accept any argument 22 \n{ 23 \n  f(std::forward<T>(param));         // forward it to f 24 \n} 25 \nForwarding functions are, by their nature, generic. The fwd template, for example, 26 \naccepts any type of argument, and it forwards whatever it gets . A logical extension 27 \nof this genericity is for forwarding functions to be not just templates, but variadic 28 \ntemplates, thus accepting any number of arguments. The variadic form for fwd 29 \nlooks like this: 30 \ntemplate<typename... Ts> 31 \nvoid fwd(Ts&&... params)             // accept any arguments 32 \n{ 33\n\n#### 第 240 页原文\n\nPage 240 \n \n  f(std::forward<Ts>(params)...);    // forward them to f 1 \n} 2 \nThis is the form you’ll see in , among other places,  the standard containers’ e m-3 \nplacement functions (see Item 18) and the smart pointer factory functions, 4 \nstd::make_shared and std::make_unique (see Item 23). 5 \nGiven our target function f and our forwarding function fwd, perfect forwarding 6 \nfails if calling f with a particular argument does one thing, but calling fwd with the 7 \nsame argument does something different: 8 \nf( expression );      // if this does one thing, 9 \nfwd( expression );    // but this does something else, fwd fails 10 \n                      // to perfectly forward expression to f 11 \nSeveral kinds of arguments lead to this kind of failure. Knowing what they are and 12 \nhow to work around them is importan t, so let’s tour the arguments that can’t be 13 \nperfect-forwarded. 14 \nBraced initializers 15 \nSuppose f is declared like this: 16 \nvoid f(const std::vector<int>& v); 17 \nIn that case, calling f with a braced initializer compiles, 18 \nf({ 1, 2, 3 });       // fine, \"{1, 2, 3}\" implicitly 19 \n                      // converted to std::vector<int> 20 \nbut passing the same braced initializer to fwd doesn’t compile: 21 \nfwd({ 1, 2, 3 });     // error! doesn't compile 22 \nThat’s because the use of a braced initializer is a perfect forwarding failure cases. 23 \nAll such failure cases have the same cause. In a direct call to f (such as 24 \nf({ 1, 2, 3 })), compiler s see the types of arguments passed at the call site, 25 \nand they see the types of the parameters declared by f. They compare the arg u-26 \nments at the cal l site to the parameter declarations to see if they’re compatible, 27 \nand, if necessary, they perform implicit type conversions to make the call succeed. 28\n\n#### 第 241 页原文\n\nPage 241 \n \nIn the example above, they generate a temporary std::vector<int> object from 1 \n{ 1, 2, 3 } so that f’s parameter v has a std::vector<int> object to bind to.  2 \nWhen calling f indirectly through the forwarding function template fwd, compilers 3 \nno longer compare the arguments passed at fwd’s call site to the parameter decla-4 \nrations in f. Instead, they deduce the types of the arguments being passed to fwd, 5 \nand they compare the deduced types to f’s parameter declarations. Perfect fo r-6 \nwarding fails when either of the following occurs: 7 \n Compilers are unable to deduce a type for one or more of fwd’s parameters. 8 \nIn this case, the code fails to compile. 9 \n Compilers deduce the “wrong” type  for one or more of fwd’s parameters. 10 \nHere, “wrong” could mean that fwd’s instantiation won’t compile with the 11 \ntypes that were deduced, but it could also mean that the call to f using fwd’s 12 \ndeduced types behaves differently from a direct call to f with the arguments 13 \nthat were passed to fwd. One source of such divergent behavior would be if f 14 \nwere an overloaded function name, and, due to “incorrect” type deduction, the 15 \noverload of f called inside fwd were different from the overload that would be 16 \ninvoked if f were called directly. 17 \nIn the “fwd({ 1, 2, 3 })” call above, the problem is that passing a braced in i-18 \ntializer to a  function template parameter that’s not declared to be a 19 \nstd::initializer_list is decreed to be , as the Standard puts it,  a “ non-20 \ndeduced context.” In plain English, that means that compilers are forbidden from 21 \ndeducing a type for the expression { 1, 2, 3 } in the call to fwd, because fwd’s 22 \nparameter isn’t declared to be a std::initializer_list. Being prevented from 23 \ndeducing a type for fwd’s parameter, compilers must understandably reject the 24 \ncall.  25 \nInterestingly, Item 2 explains that type deduction succeeds for auto variables ini-26 \ntialized with a braced initializer. Such variables are deemed to be 27 \nstd::initializer_list objects, and this affords a simple workaround for cases 28 \nwhere the type the forwarding function should deduce is a 29\n\n#### 第 242 页原文\n\nPage 242 \n \nstd::initializer_list: declare a local variable using auto, then pass the local 1 \nvariable to the forwarding function: 2 \nauto il = { 1, 2, 3 };     // il's type deduced to be 3 \n                           // std::initializer_list<int> 4 \nfwd(il);                   // fine, perfect-forwards il to f 5 \n0 or NULL as null pointers 6 \nItem 8 explains that when you try to pass 0 or NULL as a null pointer to a template, 7 \ntype deduction goes awry, deducing an integral type (typically int) instead of a 8 \npointer type for the argument you pass. The result is that neith er 0 nor NULL can 9 \nbe perfect-forwarded as a null pointer. The fix is easy, however: pass nullptr in-10 \nstead of 0 or NULL. For details, consult Item 8. 11 \nDeclaration-only integral static const data members 12 \nAs a general rule, there’s no need to define integral static const data members 13 \nin classes; declarations alone suffice. That’s because com pilers perform const-14 \npropagation on such members’ value s, thus eliminating the need to set aside 15 \nmemory for them. For example, consider this code: 16 \nclass Widget { 17 \npublic: 18 \n  static const std::size_t MinVals = 28; // MinVals' declaration 19 \n  …  20 \n}; 21 \n…                                        // no defn. for MinVals 22 \nstd::vector<int> widgetData; 23 \nwidgetData.reserve(Widget::MinVals);     // use of MinVals 24 \nHere, we’re using Widget::MinVals (henceforth simply  MinVals) to specify 25 \nwidgetData’s initial capacity, even though MinVals lacks a definition. Compilers 26 \nwork around the missing definition (as they are required to do) by plopping the 27 \nvalue 28 into all places where MinVals is mentioned; the fact that no storage has 28 \nbeen set aside for MinVals’ value is unproblematic. If MinVals’ address were to 29 \nbe taken (e.g., if somebody created a pointer to MinVals), then MinVals would 30 \nrequire storage  (so that the pointer had something to point to) , and the code 31\n\n#### 第 243 页原文\n\nPage 243 \n \nabove, though it would compile, would fail at link -time until a definition for Min-1 \nVals was provided. 2 \nWith that in mind, imagine that f (the function fwd forwards its argument to) is 3 \ndeclared like this: 4 \nvoid f(std::size_t val); 5 \nCalling f with MinVals is fine, because compilers will just replace MinVals with 6 \nits value: 7 \nf(Widget::MinVals);         // fine, treated as \"f(28)\" 8 \nAlas, things may not go so smoothly if we try to call f through fwd: 9 \nfwd(Widget::MinVals);       // error! shouldn't link 10 \nThis code will compile, but it shouldn’t link. If that reminds you of what hap pens if 11 \nwe write code that takes MinVals’ address, that’s good, because the underlying 12 \nproblem is the same.  13 \nAlthough nothing in the source code takes MinVals’ address, fwd’s parameter is a 14 \nuniversal reference, and references, in the code generated by comp ilers, are usual-15 \nly treated like pointers. In the program’s underlying binary code (and on the 16 \nhardware), pointers and references are essentially the same thing. At this level, 17 \nthere’s truth to the adage that references are simply pointers that are automatical-18 \nly dereferenced.  That being the case, passing MinVals by reference is effectively 19 \nthe same as passing it by pointer, and as such, there has to be some memory for 20 \nthe pointer to point to. Passing integral static const data members by reference, 21 \nthen, generally requires that they be defined, and that requirement can cause code 22 \nusing perfect forwarding to fail where the equivalent code without perfect fo r-23 \nwarding succeeds. 24 \nBut perhaps you noticed the weasel words I sprinkled through the preceding di s-25 \ncussion.  The code “shouldn’t” link. References are “usually” treated like pointers. 26 \nPassing integral static const data member by reference “generally” requires 27 \nthat they be defined. It’s almost like I know something I don’t really want to tell 28 \nyou… 29\n\n#### 第 244 页原文\n\nPage 244 \n \nThat’s because I do. According to the Standard, passing MinVals by reference r e-1 \nquires that it be defined. But not all build systems (i.e., compiler -linker combina-2 \ntions) enforce this requirement. So, depending on your build system, you may find 3 \nthat you can perfect -forward integral static const data members that haven’t 4 \nbeen defined. If you do, congratulations, but there is no reason to expect such code 5 \nto port. To make it portable, simply provide a definition for the integral static 6 \nconst data member in question. For MinVals, that’d look like this: 7 \nconst std::size_t Widget::MinVals;     // in Widget's .cpp file 8 \nNote that the definition doesn’t repeat the initializer (28, in the case of MinVals). 9 \nDon’t stress over this detail, however. If you forget and provide the init ializer in 10 \nboth places, your compilers will complain, thus reminding you to specify it only 11 \nonce. 12 \nOverloaded function names and template names 13 \nSuppose our function f (the one we keep wanting to forward arguments to via 14 \nfwd) can have its behavior customized  by passing it a function that does some of 15 \nits work. Assuming this function takes and returns ints, f could be declared like 16 \nthis: 17 \nvoid f(int (*pf)(int));         // pf = \"processing function\" 18 \nIt’s worth noting that f could also be declared using a simpler non-pointer syntax. 19 \nSuch a declaration would look like this, though it’d have the same meaning as the 20 \ndeclaration above: 21 \nvoid f(int pf(int));            // declares same f as above 22 \nEither way, now suppose we have an overloaded function, processVal: 23 \nint processVal(int value); 24 \nint processVal(int value, int priority); 25 \nWe can pass processVal to f, 26 \nf(processVal);                  // fine 27 \nbut it’s something of a surprise that we can. f demands a pointer to a function as 28 \nits argument, but processVal isn’t a function pointer or even a function , it’s the 29\n\n#### 第 245 页原文\n\nPage 245 \n \nname of two different functions. However, compilers know which processVal 1 \nthey need: the one matching f’s parameter type. They thus choose the pro-2 \ncessVal taking one int, and they pass that function’s address to f. 3 \nWhat makes this work is that f’s declaration lets compilers figure out which ver-4 \nsion of processVal is required. fwd, however, being a function template, doesn’t 5 \nhave any information about what type it needs, an d that makes it impossible for 6 \ncompilers to determine which overload should be passed: 7 \nfwd(processVal);            // error! which processVal? 8 \nprocessVal alone has no type. Without a type, there can be no type deduction, 9 \nand without type deduction, we’re l eft with another perfect forwarding failure 10 \ncase. 11 \nThe same problem arises if we try to use a function template instead of (or in addi-12 \ntion to) an overloaded function name . A function template doesn’t represent one 13 \nfunction, it represents many functions: 14 \ntemplate<typename T> 15 \nvoid workOnVal(T param)     // template for processing values 16 \n{ … } 17 \nfwd(workOnVal);             // error! which workOnVal 18 \n                            // instantiation? 19 \nThe way to get a perfect -forwarding function like fwd to accept an overloaded 20 \nfunction name or a template name is to manually specify the overload or instantia-21 \ntion you want to have forwarded. For example, you can create a function pointer of 22 \nthe same type as f’s parameter, initialize that pointer with processVal or wor-23 \nkOnVal (thus causing the proper version of processVal to be selected or the 24 \nproper instantiation of workOnVal to be generated), and pass the pointer to fwd: 25 \nusing ProcessFuncType =                        // make typedef;  26 \n  int (*)(int);                                // see Item 9 27 \nProcessFuncType processValPtr = processVal;    // specify needed 28 \n                                               // signature for  29 \n                                               // processVal                                          30 \nfwd(processValPtr);                            // fine 31\n\n#### 第 246 页原文\n\nPage 246 \n \nfwd(static_cast<ProcessFuncType>(workOnVal));  // also fine 1 \nOf course, this requires that you know the type of function pointer that fwd is for-2 \nwarding to. It’s not unreasonable to assume that a perfect-forwarding function will 3 \ndocument that. After all, perfect -forwarding functions are desi gned to accept any-4 \nthing, so if there’s no documentation telling you what to pass, how would you 5 \nknow? 6 \nBitfields 7 \nThe final failure case for perfect forwarding is when a bitfield is used as a function 8 \nargument. To see what this means in practice, observe that an IPv4 header can be 9 \nmodeled as follows:† 10 \nstruct IPv4Header { 11 \n  std::uint32_t version:4, 12 \n                IHL:4, 13 \n                DSCP:6, 14 \n                ECN:2, 15 \n                totalLength:16; 16 \n  … 17 \n}; 18 \nIf our long -suffering function f (the perennial target of our forwarding function 19 \nfwd) is declared to take a std::size_t parameter, calling it with, say, the total-20 \nLength field of an IPv4Header object compiles without fuss: 21 \nvoid f(std::size_t sz);        // function to call 22 \nIPv4Header h;  23 \n… 24 \nf(h.totalLength);              // fine 25 \nTrying to forward h.totalLength to f via fwd, however, is a different story: 26 \nfwd(h.totalLength);            // error! 27 \n                                                             \n† This assumes that bitfields are laid out lsb (least significant bit) to msb (most significant \nbit). C++ doesn’t guarantee that, but compilers often provide a mechanism that allows pr o-\ngrammers to control bitfield layout.\n\n#### 第 247 页原文\n\nPage 247 \n \nThe problem is that fwd’s parameter is a reference, and h.totalLength is a non-1 \nconst bitfield. That may not sound so bad, but the C++ Standard condemns the 2 \ncombination in unusually clear prose: “ A non-const reference shall not be bound 3 \nto a bit-field.” There’s an excellent reason for the prohibition. Bitfields may consist 4 \nof arbitrary parts of machine words (e.g., bits 3 -5 of a 32-bit int), but there’s no 5 \nway to directly address such things. I mentioned earlier that references and poin t-6 \ners are the same thing at the hardware level, and just as there’s no way to create a 7 \npointer to arbitrary bits (C++ dictates that the smallest thing you can point to is a 8 \nchar), there’s no way to bind a reference to arbitrary bits, either. 9 \nWorking around the impossibility of perfect-forwarding a bitfield is easy, once you 10 \nrealize that any function that accept s a bitfield as an argument will receive a copy 11 \nof the bitfield’s value. After all, no function  can bind a reference  to a bitfield, nor 12 \ncan any function accept pointers to bitfields, because pointers to bitfields don’t 13 \nexist. The only kinds of parameters to which a bitfield can be passed are by -value 14 \nparameters and , counterintuitively, references-to-const. In the case of by -value 15 \nparameters, the called function obviously receives a copy of the value in the bi t-16 \nfield, and it turns out that in the case of a reference-to-const parameter, the 17 \nStandard requires that the reference actually bind to a copy of the bitfield’s value 18 \nthat’s stored in an object of some standard integral type (e.g., int). References-to-19 \nconst don’t bind to bitfields, they bind to “normal” objects into which the values 20 \nof the bitfields have been copied. 21 \nThe key to passing a bitfield into a perfect-forwarding function, then, is to take ad-22 \nvantage of the fact that the forwarded-to function will always receive a copy of the 23 \nbitfield’s value. You can thus make a copy yourself and call the forwarding function 24 \nwith the copy. In the case of our example with IPv4Header, this code would do the 25 \ntrick: 26 \n// copy bitfield value; see Item 6 for info on init. form 27 \nauto length = static_cast<std::uint16_t>{h.totalLength}; 28 \nfwd(length);                        // forward the copy  29\n\n#### 第 248 页原文\n\nPage 248 \n \nUpshot 1 \nIn most cases, perfect forwar ding works exactly as advertised. You rarely  have to 2 \nthink about it. But when it doesn’t work —when reasonable-looking code fails to 3 \ncompile or, worse, compiles , but doesn’t behave the way you anticipate, it’s i m-4 \nportant to know about perfect forwarding’s imperfections. Equally important is 5 \nknowing how to work around them. In most cases, this is straightforward. 6 \nThings to remember 7 \n Perfect forwarding fails when template type deduction fails or when it deduces 8 \nthe wrong type. 9 \n The kinds of arguments that lead to perfect forwarding failure are braced ini-10 \ntializers, null pointers expressed as 0 or NULL, declaration-only integral const 11 \nstatic data members, template and overloaded function names, and bitfields. 12\n\n#### 第 249 页原文\n\nPage 249 \n \n \n1 \nLambda expressions—lambdas—are a game -changer in C++ programming. That’s 2 \nsomewhat surprising, because they bring no new expressive power to the la n-3 \nguage. Everything a lambda can do  is something you can do by hand with a bit 4 \nmore typing. But lambdas are such a convenient way to create function objects, the 5 \nimpact on day -to-day C++ software development is enormous. Without lambdas, 6 \nthe STL “ _if” algorithms (e.g., std::find_if, std::remove_if, 7 \nstd::count_if, etc.) tend to be employed with only the most trivial predicates, 8 \nbut when lambdas are available, use of these algorithms with nontrivial conditions 9 \nblossoms. The same is true of algorithms that can be customized with comparison 10 \nfunctions (e.g., std::sort, std::nth_element, std::lower_bound, etc.). Ou t-11 \nside the STL, lambdas make it possible to quickly create custom deleters for 12 \nstd::unique_ptr and std::shared_ptr (see Items 20 and 21), and the y make 13 \nthe specification of predicates for condition variables in the threading API equally 14 \nstraightforward. Beyond the Standard Library, lambdas facilitate the on -the-fly 15 \nspecification of callback functions, interface adaption functions , and context -16 \nspecific functions for one-off calls. Lambdas really make C++ a more pleasant pr o-17 \ngramming language. 18 \nThe Items in this chapter cover important dos and don’ts for effective software 19 \ndevelopment with lambdas. The chapter begins with an admonition to steer clear 20 \nof what initially looks like a n attractive feature: default capture modes. It then a d-21 \ndresses how to accomplish move capture in C++14 (where it’s more or less direc t-22 \nly supported) and C++11 (where the way is more circuitous) . That’s followed by a 23 \nC++14-specific Item on implementing perfect -forwarding with generic lambdas, 24 \nand the chapter concludes with an Item explaining why programmers accustomed 25 \nto std::bind should switch to lambdas.  26 \nThe vocabulary associated with lambdas can be confusing. Here’s a brief refresher: 27 \n A lambda expression is just that: an expression. It’s part of the source code. In 28 \nstd::find_if(container.begin(), container.end(), 29 \n             [](auto val) { return 0 < val && val < 10; }); 30\n\n#### 第 250 页原文\n\nPage 250 \n \nthe highlighted expression is the lambda. 1 \n A closure is the runtime object created by a lambda. Closures hold copies of all 2 \ncaptured data (although by -reference captures are typically optimized away) . 3 \nIn the call to std::find_if above, the closure is the object that’s passed at 4 \nruntime as the third argument to std::find_if. (The first two arguments are 5 \nthe begin and end iterators for container.) 6 \n A closure class  is a class from which a closure is instantiated. Each lambda 7 \ncauses compilers to generate a unique closure class.  The statements inside a 8 \nlambda become executable instructions in the member functions of its closure 9 \nclass (modulo inlining and other optimizations). 10 \nA lambda is often used to create a closure that’s used only as an argument to a 11 \nfunction. That’s the case in the call to std::find_if above. However, closures 12 \nmay generally be copied, so it’s usually possible to have multiple closures  of a clo-13 \nsure type corresponding to a single lambda. For example, in the following code, 14 \n{                                       // begin a scope 15 \n  int x;                                // x is local variable 16 \n  … 17 \n  auto c1 =                             // c1 is copy of the 18 \n    [x](int y) { return x * y = 5; };   // closure produced 19 \n                                        // by the lambda 20 \n  auto c2 = c1;                         // c2 is copy of c1 21 \n  auto c3 = c2;                         // c3 is copy of c2 22 \n  … 23 \n}                                       // end the scope 24 \nc1, c2, and c3 are all copies of the closure produced by the lambda. 25 \nInformally, it’s perfectly acceptable to blur the lines between  lambdas, closures, 26 \nand closure classes. But in the Items that follow, it’s often important to distinguish 27 \nwhat exists during compilation (lambdas and closure classes), what exists at 28 \nruntime (closures), and how they relate to one another. 29", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.251–p.275", "pages": "p.251–p.275", "raw_text": "#### 第 251 页原文\n\nPage 251 \n \n Item 33: Avoid default capture modes. 1 \nThere are two default capture modes in C++11: by -reference and by-value. Default 2 \nby-reference capture can lead to easy-to-overlook dangling references. Default by-3 \nvalue capture lures you into thinking you’re immune to that problem (you’re not), 4 \nand it lulls you into thinking your closures are self-contained (they may not be).  5 \nThat’s the executive summary for this Item . If you’re more engineer than exec u-6 \ntive, you’ll want some meat on those bones, so let’s start with the danger of default 7 \nby-reference capture.  8 \nA by-reference capture causes the code inside the lambda to refer to a local vari a-9 \nble or a parameter that’s available in the scope where the lambda is defined. I f the 10 \nlifetime of a closure created from that lambda exceeds the lifetime of the local var-11 \niable or parameter, the reference in the code will dangle. For example, suppose we 12 \nhave a container of filtering functions, each of which takes an int and returns a 13 \nbool indicating whether a passed-in value satisfies the filter: 14 \nusing FilterContainer =                      // typedef;  15 \n  std::vector<std::function<bool(int)>>;     // see Item 9 16 \nFilterContainer filters;                     // filtering funcs 17 \nWe could add a filter for multiples of 5 like this: 18 \nfilters.emplace_back(                        // see Item 18 for 19 \n  [](int value) { return value % 5 == 0; }   // info on  20 \n);                                           // emplace_back 21 \nHowever, it may be that we need to compute the divisor at runtime, i.e., we can’t 22 \njust hard-code 5 into the lambda. So adding the filter might look more like this: 23 \nvoid addDivisorFilter() 24 \n{ 25 \n  auto calc1 = computeSomeValue1(); 26 \n  auto calc2 = computeSomeValue2(); 27 \n  auto divisor = computeDivisor(calc1, calc2); 28 \n  filters.emplace_back(                              // danger! 29 \n    [&](int value) { return value % divisor == 0; }  // divisor 30 \n  );                                                 // might 31 \n}                                                    // dangle! 32\n\n#### 第 252 页原文\n\nPage 252 \n \nThis code is a problem waiting to happen. The lambda refers to the local variable 1 \ndivisor, but that variable ceases to exist when addDivisorFilter returns. 2 \nThat’s immediately after filters.emplace_back returns, so the function that’s 3 \nadded to filters is essentially dead on arrival . Using that filter yields undefined 4 \nbehavior from virtually the moment it’s created. 5 \nNow, the same problem would exist if divisor’s by -reference capture were e x-6 \nplicit, 7 \n  filters.emplace_back( 8 \n    [&divisor](int value)                // danger! divisor can 9 \n    { return value % divisor == 0; }     // still dangle! 10 \n  ); 11 \nbut with an explicit capture, it’s easier to see that the viability of the lambda is d e-12 \npendent on divisor’s lifetime. Also, writing out the name, “divisor,” reminds us to 13 \nensure that divisor lives at least as long as the lambda’s closures. That’s a more 14 \nspecific memory jog than the general “make sure nothing dangles” admonition that 15 \n“[&]” conveys. 16 \nIf you know that the lambda will be used on ly once (e.g., in a call to an STL alg o-17 \nrithm), there is no risk that its closure will outlive the local variables and parame-18 \nters in the environment where the lambda is created. In that case, you might argue, 19 \nthere’s no risk of dangling references, hence no  reason to avoid a default by -20 \nreference capture mode. For example, our filtering lambda might be used only as 21 \nan argument to C++11’s std::all_of, which returns whether all elements in a 22 \nrange satisfy a condition:  23 \ntemplate<typename C>                          24 \nvoid workWithContainer(const C& container) 25 \n{ 26 \n  auto calc1 = computeSomeValue1();             // as above 27 \n  auto calc2 = computeSomeValue2();             // as above 28 \n  auto divisor = computeDivisor(calc1, calc2);  // as above 29 \n  using ContElemT = typename C::value_type;     // type of  30 \n                                                // elements in  31 \n                                                // container 32 \n  if (std::all_of(                              // if all values 33 \n        container.begin(), container.end(),     // in container 34\n\n#### 第 253 页原文\n\nPage 253 \n \n        [&](const ContElemT& value)             // are multiples 1 \n        { return value % divisor == 0; })       // of divisor... 2 \n      ) { 3 \n    …                                           // they are... 4 \n  } else { 5 \n    …                                           // at least one 6 \n  }                                             // isn't... 7 \n} 8 \nIt’s true, this is safe, but its safety is somewhat precarious. If the lambda were 9 \nfound to be useful in other contexts  (e.g., as a function to be added to the filters 10 \ncontainer) and was copy-and-pasted into a context where its closure could outlive 11 \ndivisor, you’d be back in dangle-city, and there’d be nothing in the capture clause 12 \nto specifically remind you to perform lifetime analysis on divisor.  13 \nLong-term, it’s simply better software engineering to explicitly list the local vari a-14 \nbles and parameters that a lambda depends on.  15 \nBy the way , the ability to use auto in C++14 lambda parameter specifications  16 \nmeans that the code above can be simplified in C++14. The ContElemT typedef can 17 \nbe eliminated, and the if condition can be revised as follows: 18 \nif (std::all_of(container.begin(), container.end(), 19 \n                [&](const auto& value)                // C++14 20 \n                { return value % divisor == 0; }))    // only 21 \nOne way to solve our problem with divisor would be a default by-value capture 22 \nmode. That is, we could add the lambda to filters as follows: 23 \nfilters.emplace_back(                                 // now 24 \n  [=](int value) { return value % divisor == 0; }     // divisor 25 \n);                                                    // can't 26 \n                                                      // dangle 27 \nThis suffices for this example, but, in general, default by-value capture isn’t the an-28 \nti-dangling elixir you might imagine. The problem is that if you capture a pointer 29 \nby value, you copy the pointer into the closures arising from the lambda, but you 30 \ndon’t prevent code outside the lambda from  deleteing th e pointer and causi ng 31 \nyour copies to dangle.  32 \n“That could never happen!,” you protest. “Having read Chapter 4, I worship at the 33 \nhouse of smart pointers. Only loser C++98 programmers use raw pointers and de-34\n\n#### 第 254 页原文\n\nPage 254 \n \nlete.” That may be true, but it’s irrelevant, because you do, in fact, use raw poin t-1 \ners, and they can, in fact, be deleted out from under you. It’s just that in your 2 \nmodern C++ programming style, there’s often little sign of it in the source code.  3 \nSuppose one of the things Widgets can do is add entries to the container of filters: 4 \nclass Widget { 5 \npublic: 6 \n  …                                  // ctors, etc. 7 \n  void addFilter() const;            // add an entry to filters 8 \nprivate: 9 \n  int divisor;                       // used in Widget's filter 10 \n}; 11 \nWidget::addFilter could be defined like this: 12 \nvoid Widget::addFilter() const 13 \n{ 14 \n  filters.emplace_back( 15 \n    [=](int value) { return value % divisor == 0; } 16 \n  ); 17 \n} 18 \nTo the blissfully uninitiated, this looks like safe code. The lambda is dependent on 19 \ndivisor, but the default by -value capture mode ensures that divisor is copied 20 \ninto any closures arising from the lambda, right?  21 \nWrong. Completely wrong. Horribly wrong. Fatally wrong. 22 \nCaptures apply only to non-static local variables (including parameters) visible 23 \nin the scope where the lambda is created. In the body of Widget::addFilter, 24 \ndivisor is not a local variable, it’s a data member of the Widget class. It can’t be 25 \ncaptured. Yet if the default capture mode is eliminated, the code won’t compile: 26 \nvoid Widget::addFilter() const 27 \n{ 28 \n  filters.emplace_back(                             // error! 29 \n    [](int value) { return value % divisor == 0; }  // divisor 30 \n  );                                                // not  31 \n}                                                   // available 32\n\n#### 第 255 页原文\n\nPage 255 \n \nFurthermore, if an attempt is made to explicitly capture divisor (either by value 1 \nor by reference —it doesn’t matter), the capture won’t compile, because divisor 2 \nisn’t a local variable or a parameter: 3 \nvoid Widget::addFilter() const 4 \n{ 5 \n  filters.emplace_back(    6 \n    [divisor](int value)                // error! no local  7 \n    { return value % divisor == 0; }    // divisor to capture 8 \n  );    9 \n} 10 \nSo if the default by -value capture clause isn’t capturing divisor, yet without the 11 \ndefault by-value capture clause, the code won’t compile, what’s going on?  12 \nThe explanation hinges on your implicit use of a raw pointer: this. Every non -13 \nstatic member function has a this pointer, and you use that pointer every time 14 \nyou mention a data member of the class. Inside any Widget member function, for 15 \nexample, compilers internally replace uses of divisor with this->divisor. In 16 \nthe version of Widget::addFilter with a default by-value capture, 17 \nvoid Widget::addFilter() const 18 \n{ 19 \n  filters.emplace_back( 20 \n    [=](int value) { return value % divisor == 0; } 21 \n  ); 22 \n} 23 \nwhat’s being captured is the Widget’s this pointer, not divisor. Compilers treat 24 \nthe code as if it had been written as follows: 25 \nvoid Widget::addFilter() const 26 \n{ 27 \n  auto currentObjectPtr = this; 28 \n  filters.emplace_back( 29 \n    [currentObjectPtr](int value) 30 \n    { return value % currentObjectPtr->divisor == 0; } 31 \n  ); 32 \n} 33 \nUnderstanding this is tantamount to understanding that the viability of the cl o-34 \nsures arising  from this lambda are tied to the lifetime of the Widget whose this 35\n\n#### 第 256 页原文\n\nPage 256 \n \npointer they contain a copy of. In particular, consider this code, which, in acc ord 1 \nwith Chapter 4, uses pointers of only the smart variety: 2 \nusing FilterContainer =                     // as before  3 \n  std::vector<std::function<bool(int)>>;  4 \nFilterContainer filters;                    // as before 5 \nvoid doSomeWork() 6 \n{ 7 \n  auto pw =                       // create Widget; see 8 \n    std::make_unique<Widget>();   // Item 23 for  9 \n                                  // std::make_unique 10 \n  pw->addFilter();                // add filter that uses 11 \n                                  // Widget::divisor 12 \n  … 13 \n}                                 // destroy Widget; filter 14 \n                                  // now holds dangling pointer! 15 \nWhen a call is made to doSomeWork, a filter is created that depends on the Widget 16 \nobject produced by std::make_unique, i.e., a filter that contains a copy of a 17 \npointer to that Widget—the Widget’s this pointer. This filter is added to fil-18 \nters, but when doSomeWork finishes, the Widget is destroyed by the 19 \nstd::unique_ptr managing its lifetime  (see Item 20). From that point on, fil-20 \nters contains an entry with a dangling pointer. 21 \nThis particular problem can be solved by making a local copy of the data member 22 \nyou want to capture and then capturing the copy: 23 \nvoid Widget::addFilter() const 24 \n{ 25 \n  auto divisorCopy = divisor;                // copy data member 26 \n  filters.emplace_back( 27 \n    [divisorCopy](int value)                 // capture the copy 28 \n    { return value % divisorCopy == 0; }     // use the copy 29 \n  ); 30 \n} 31 \nTo be honest, if you take this approach, default by-value capture will work, too, 32 \nvoid Widget::addFilter() const 33 \n{ 34 \n  auto divisorCopy = divisor;                // copy data member 35\n\n#### 第 257 页原文\n\nPage 257 \n \n  filters.emplace_back( 1 \n    [=](int value)                           // capture the copy 2 \n    { return value % divisorCopy == 0; }     // use the copy 3 \n  ); 4 \n} 5 \nbut why tempt fate? A default capture mode is what made it possible to accidental-6 \nly capture this when you thought you were capturing divisor in the first place. 7 \nIn C++14, a better way to capture a data member is to use generalized lambda cap-8 \nture (see Item 34): 9 \nvoid Widget::addFilter() const 10 \n{ 11 \n  filters.emplace_back(               // C++14 only: 12 \n    [divisor = divisor](int value)    // copy divisor to closure 13 \n    { return value % divisor == 0; }  // use the copy 14 \n  ); 15 \n} 16 \nThere’s no such thing as a default capture mode for a generalized lambda capt ure, 17 \nhowever, so even in C++14, the advice of this Item —to avoid default capture 18 \nmodes—stands. 19 \nAn additional drawback to default by -value captures is that they can suggest that 20 \nthe corresponding closures are self -contained and insulated from changes to dat a 21 \noutside the closure s. In general, that’s not true, because lambdas may be depen d-22 \nent not just on local variables and parameters (which may be captured), but also 23 \non objects with static storage duration . Such objects are defined at global or 24 \nnamespace scope or are declared static inside classes, functions, or files. These 25 \nobjects can be used inside lambdas, but they can’t be captured. Yet specification of 26 \na default by-value capture mode can lend the impression that  they are. Consider 27 \nthis  revised version of the addDivisorFilter template we saw earlier: 28 \nvoid addDivisorFilter() 29 \n{ 30 \n  static auto calc1 = computeSomeValue1();      // now static 31 \n  static auto calc2 = computeSomeValue2();      // now static 32 \n  static auto divisor =                         // now static 33 \n    computeDivisor(calc1, calc2); 34\n\n#### 第 258 页原文\n\nPage 258 \n \n  filters.emplace_back( 1 \n    [=](int value)                     // captures nothing! 2 \n    { return value % divisor == 0; }   // refers to above static 3 \n  );  4 \n  ++divisor;                           // modify divisor 5 \n} 6 \nA casual reader of this code could be forgiven for seeing “[=]” and thinking, “Okay, 7 \nthe lambda makes a copy of all the objects it uses and is therefore self -contained.” 8 \nBut it’s not self -contained. This lambda doesn’t use any non -static local variables, 9 \nso nothing is captured. Rather, the code for the lambda refers to the static varia-10 \nble divisor. When, at the end of each invocation of addDivisorFilter, divisor 11 \nis incremented, any lambdas that have been added to filters via this function 12 \nwill exhibit new behavior (corresponding to the new value of divisor). Practical-13 \nly speaking, this lambda captures divisor by reference, a direct contradiction to 14 \nwhat the default by -value capture clause seems to imply. If you stay away from 15 \ndefault by-value capture clauses, you eliminate the risk of your code being misread 16 \nin this way. 17 \nThings to Remember 18 \n Default by-reference capture can lead to hidden dangling references. 19 \n Default by-value capture is susceptible to hidden dangling pointers  (especially 20 \nthis), and it misleadingly suggests that lambdas are self-contained. 21 \n Item 34: Use init capture to move objects into closures. 22 \nSometimes neither by-value capture nor by-reference capture is what you want. If 23 \nyou have a move -only object (e.g., a std::unique_ptr or a std::future) that 24 \nyou want to get into a closure, C++11 offers no way to do it. If you have a n object 25 \nthat’s expensive to copy but cheap to move (e.g., most containers in the Standard 26 \nLibrary), and you’d like to get that object into a closure, you’d much rather move it 27 \nthan copy it. Again, however, C++11 gives you no way to accomplish that. 28 \nBut that’s C++11. C++14 is a different story. It offers direct support for moving o b-29 \njects into closures. If your compilers are C++14 -compliant, rejoice and read on. If 30\n\n#### 第 259 页原文\n\nPage 259 \n \nyou’re still working with C ++11 compilers, you should rejoice and read on, too, 1 \nbecause there are ways to approximate move capture in C++11. 2 \nThe absence of move capture was recognized as a shortcoming even as C++11 was 3 \nadopted. The straightforward remedy would have been to add it in  C++14, but the 4 \nStandardization Committee chose a different path. They introduced a new capture 5 \nmechanism that’s so flexible, capture -by-move is only one of the tricks it can pe r-6 \nform. The new capability is called init capture. It can do virtually everything the 7 \nC++11 capture forms can do , plus more. The one thing you can’t express with an 8 \ninit capture is a  default capture mode, but Item 33 explains that you should stay 9 \naway from those , anyway.  (For situations covered by C++11 captures, init ca p-10 \nture’s syntax is a bit wordier, so in cases where a C++11 capture gets the job done, 11 \nit’s perfectly reasonable to use it.) 12 \nUsing an init capture makes it possible for you to specify 13 \n1. the name of a data member  in the closure class generated from the lambda 14 \nand 15 \n2. an expression initializing that data member. 16 \nHere’s how you can use init capture to move a std::unique_ptr into a closure: 17 \nclass Widget {                          // some useful type 18 \npublic: 19 \n  … 20 \n  bool isValidated() const; 21 \n  bool isProcessed() const; 22 \n  bool isArchived() const; 23 \nprivate: 24 \n  … 25 \n}; 26 \n 27 \nauto pw = std::make_unique<Widget>();   // create Widget; see 28 \n                                        // Item 23 for info on 29 \n                                        // std::make_unique 30 \n…                                       // configure *pw 31\n\n#### 第 260 页原文\n\nPage 260 \n \nauto func = [pw = std::move(pw)]               // init data mbr 1 \n            { return pw->isValidated()         // in closure w/ 2 \n                     && pw->isArchived(); };   // std::move(pw) 3 \nThe highlighted text comprises the init capture. To the left of the “=” is the name of 4 \nthe data member in the closure class you’re specifying, and to the right is the in i-5 \ntializing expression. Interestingly, the scope on the left of the “=” is different from 6 \nthe scope on the right. The scope on the left is that of the closure class. The scope 7 \non the right is the same as where the lambda is being defined. In the example 8 \nabove, the name pw on the left of the “ =” refers to a data member in the closure 9 \nclass, while the name pw on the right refers to the object declared above the lam b-10 \nda, i.e., the variable initialized by the call to std::make_unique. So “ pw = 11 \nstd::move(pw)” means “create a data member pw in the closure, and initialize 12 \nthat data member with the result of applying std::move to the local variable pw.” 13 \nAs usual, code in the body of the lambda is in the scope of the closure class, so uses 14 \nof pw there refer to the closure class data member. 15 \nThe comment “configure *pw” in this example indicates that after the Widget is 16 \ncreated by std::make_unique and before the std::unique_ptr to that Widget 17 \nis captured by the lambda, the Widget is modified in some way. If no such configu-18 \nration is necessary, i.e., if the Widget created by std::make_unique is in a state 19 \nsuitable to be captured by the lambda, the local variable pw is unnecessary, b e-20 \ncause the closure class’s data member can be directly initialized by 21 \nstd::make_unique: 22 \nauto func = [pw = std::make_unique<Widget>()]  // init data mbr 23 \n            { return pw->isValidated()         // in closure w/ 24 \n                     && pw->isArchived(); };   // result of call 25 \n                                               // to make_unique 26 \nThis should make clear that the C++14 notion of “capture” is considerably general-27 \nized from C++11, because in C++11, it’s not possible to capture the result of an ex-28 \npression. As a result, another name for init capture is generalized lambda capture. 29 \nBut what if one or more of the compilers you  use lacks support for C++14’s init 30 \ncapture? How can you accomplish move capture in a language lacking support for 31 \nmove capture? 32\n\n#### 第 261 页原文\n\nPage 261 \n \nRemember that a lambda expression is simply a way to cause a class to be gene r-1 \nated and an object of that type to be created. There is nothing you can do with a 2 \nlambda that you can’t do by hand. The example C++14 code we just saw, for exam-3 \nple, can be written in C++11 like this: 4 \nclass IsValAndArch {                         // \"is validated 5 \npublic:                                      // and archived\" 6 \n  using DataType = std::unique_ptr<Widget>; 7 \n  explicit IsValAndArch(DataType&& ptr)      // Item 27 explains 8 \n  : pw(std::move(ptr)) {}                    // use of std::move 9 \n  bool operator()() const 10 \n  { return pw->isValidated() && pw->isArchived(); } 11 \nprivate: 12 \n  DataType pw; 13 \n}; 14 \nauto func = IsValAndArch(std::make_unique<Widget>()); 15 \nThat’s more work than writing the lambda, but it doesn’t change the fact that if you 16 \nwant a class in C++11 that supports move -initialization of its data members, the 17 \nonly thing between you and your desire is a bit of time with your keyboard. 18 \nIf you want to stick with lambdas (and given their convenience, you probably do), 19 \nmove capture can be emulated in C++11 by  20 \n1. moving the object to be captured  into a function object produced by 21 \nstd::bind and  22 \n2. giving the lambda a reference to the “captured” object.  23 \nIf you’re familiar with std::bind, the code is pretty straightforward. If you’re not  24 \nfamiliar with std::bind, the code takes a little getting used to, but it’s worth the 25 \ntrouble. 26 \nSuppose you’d like to create a local std::vector, put an appropriate set of values 27 \ninto it, then move it into a closure. In C++14, this is easy: 28 \nstd::vector<double> data;                 // object to be moved 29 \n                                          // into closure 30 \n…                                         // populate data 31\n\n#### 第 262 页原文\n\nPage 262 \n \nauto func = [data = std::move(data)]      // C++14 init capture 1 \n            { /* uses of data */ }; 2 \nI’ve highlighted key parts of this code: the type of object you want to move 3 \n(std::vector<double>), the name of that object ( data), and the initializing e x-4 \npression for the init capture (std::move(data)). The C++11 equivalent is as fo l-5 \nlows, where I’ve highlighted the same key things: 6 \nstd::vector<double> data;                 // as above 7 \n…                                         // as above 8 \nauto func = 9 \n  std::bind(                              // C++11 emulation 10 \n    [](std::vector<double>& data)         // of init capture 11 \n    { /* uses of data */ }, 12 \n    std::move(data) 13 \n  ); 14 \nLike lambda expressions, std::bind produces function objects. I call function o b-15 \njects returned by std::bind bind objects. The first argument to std::bind is al-16 \nways something callable (e.g., a callable entity). Subsequent arguments represent 17 \nvalues to be passed to the first argument.  18 \nA bind object contains copies of all the arguments passed to std::bind. For each 19 \nlvalue argument, the corresponding object in the bind object is copy constructed. 20 \nFor each rvalue, it’s move constructed. In this example, the second argument is an 21 \nrvalue (the result of std::move—see Item 25), so data is move-constructed into 22 \nthe bind object. This move construction is the crux of move capture emulation, be-23 \ncause moving an rvalue into a bind object is how we work around the inability to 24 \nmove an rvalue into a C++11 closure. 25 \nWhen a bind object is “called” (i.e., its function call operator is invoked) the objects 26 \nit stores are passed to the callable entity originally passed to std::bind. In this 27 \nexample, that means th at when func (the bind object) is called, the move -28 \nconstructed copy of data inside func is passed as an argument to the lambda that 29 \nwas passed to std::bind. 30 \nThis lambda is the same as the lambda we’d use in C++14, except a parameter, da-31 \nta, has been added to correspond to  our pseudo-move-captured object. This p a-32\n\n#### 第 263 页原文\n\nPage 263 \n \nrameter is an lvalue reference  to the copy of data in the bind object. (It’s not an 1 \nrvalue reference, because although the expression used to initialize the copy of 2 \ndata (“std::move(data)”) is an rvalue, the copy of data itself is an lvalue.) Uses 3 \nof data inside the lambda will thus operate on the move-constructed copy of data 4 \ninside the bind object.  5 \nBecause a bind object stores copies of all the arguments passed to std::bind, the 6 \nbind object in our example contains a copy of the closure produced by the lambda 7 \nthat is its first argument. The lifetime of the closure is therefore the same as the 8 \nlifetime of the bind object. That’s important, because it means that as long as the 9 \nclosure exists, the bind object containing the pseudo -move-captured object exists, 10 \ntoo.  11 \nIf this is your first exposure to std::bind, you may need to consult your favorite 12 \nC++11 reference before all the details of the foregoing discussion fall into place. 13 \nEven if that’s the case, these fundamental points should be clear: 14 \n It’s not possible to move-construct an object into a C++11 closure, but it is pos-15 \nsible to move-construct an object into a C++11 bind object. 16 \n Emulating move-capture in C++11 consists of move-constructing an object into 17 \na bind object, then passing the move-constructed object to the lambda by ref-18 \nerence. 19 \n Because the lifetime of the bind object is the same as that of the closure, it’s 20 \npossible to treat objects in the bind object as if they were in the closure. 21 \nAs a second example  of using std::bind to emulate move capture , here’s the 22 \nC++14 code we saw earlier to create a std::unique_ptr in a closure: 23 \nauto func = [pw = std::make_unique<Widget>()]    // as before, 24 \n            { return pw->isValidated()           // create pw 25 \n                     && pw->isArchived(); };     // in closure 26 \nAnd here’s the C++11 emulation: 27 \nauto func = std::bind( 28 \n              [](std::unique_ptr<Widget>& pw) 29 \n              { return pw->isValidated() 30\n\n#### 第 264 页原文\n\nPage 264 \n \n                     && pw->isArchived(); }, 1 \n              std::make_unique<Widget>() 2 \n            ); 3 \nIt’s ironic that I’m showing how to use std::bind to work around limitations in 4 \nC++11 lambdas, because in Item 36, I advocate the use of lambdas over 5 \nstd::bind. However, that Item explains that there are some cases in C++11 6 \nwhere std::bind can be useful, and this is one of them. (In C++14, features such 7 \nas init capture and auto parameters eliminate those cases.) 8 \nThings to Remember 9 \n Use C++14’s init capture to move objects into closures. 10 \n In C++11, emulate init capture via hand-written classes or std::bind. 11 \n Item 35: Use decltype on auto&& parameters to 12 \nstd::forward them. 13 \nOne of the most exciting features of C++14 is generic lambdas —lambdas that use 14 \nauto in their parameter specifications. The implementation of this feature is 15 \nstraightforward: the operator() in the closure class arising from the lambda is a 16 \ntemplate. Given this lambda, for example,  17 \nauto f = [](auto x){ return func(normalize(x)); }; 18 \nthe closure class’s function call operator looks like this: 19 \nclass SomeCompilerGeneratedClassName { 20 \npublic: 21 \n  template<typename T>                   // see Item 3 for  22 \n  auto operator()(T x) const             // auto return type 23 \n  { return func(normalize(x)); }  24 \n  …                                      // other closure class 25 \n};                                       // functionality 26 \nIn this example, the only thing the lambda does with its parameter  x is forward it 27 \nto normalize. If normalize treats lvalues differently from rvalues, this lambda 28 \nisn’t really written properly, because it always passes an lva lue (the parameter x) 29 \nto normalize, even if the argument that was passed to the lambda was an rvalue. 30\n\n#### 第 265 页原文\n\nPage 265 \n \nThe correct way to write the lambda is to have it perfect-forward x to normalize. 1 \nDoing that requires two changes to the code. First, x has to become a universal ref-2 \nerence (see Item 26), and second, it has to be passed to normalize via 3 \nstd::forward (see Item 27). In concept, these are trivial modifications: 4 \nauto f = [](auto&& x) 5 \n         { return func(normalize(std::forward<???>(x))); }; 6 \nBetween concept and realization, however, is the question of what type to pass to 7 \nstd::forward, ie., to determine what should go where I’ve written ??? above.  8 \nNormally, when you employ perfect forwarding, you’re in a template function ta k-9 \ning some type parameter T, so you just write std::forward<T>. In the lambda, 10 \nthough, there’s no type parameter T available to you.  There is a  T in the te m-11 \nplatized operator() inside the closure class generated by the lambda, but it’s not 12 \npossible to refer to it from the lambda, so it does you no good. 13 \nItem 30 explains that if an lvalue argument is passed to a universal reference p a-14 \nrameter, the type of that parameter becomes an lvalue reference. If an rvalue is 15 \npassed, the parameter becomes an rvalue reference. This means that in our lam b-16 \nda, we can determine whether  the argument passed was an lvalue or an rvalue  by 17 \ninspecting the type of the parameter, x. decltype gives us a way to do that  (see 18 \nItem 3). If an lvalue was passed in, decltype(x) will produce a  type that’s an  19 \nlvalue reference. If an rvalue was passed, decltype(x) will produce an rval ue 20 \nreference type. 21 \nItem 30 also explains that when calling std::forward, the type argument should 22 \nbe an lvalue reference for lvalues, and it should be a non -reference for rvalues. In 23 \nour lambda, if x is bound to an lvalue, decltype(x) will yield an lvalue reference, 24 \nwhich is exactly what std::forward wants. However, if x is bound to an rvalue, 25 \ndecltype(x) will yield an rvalue reference —not the non -reference that 26 \nstd::forward expects. 27 \nBut look at the sample implementation for std::forward from Item 30: 28 \ntemplate<typename T>                       // from Item 30 29 \nT&& forward(T&& param) 30 \n{ 31\n\n#### 第 266 页原文\n\nPage 266 \n \n  return static_cast<T&&>(param); 1 \n} 2 \nIf client code wants  to perfect -forward an rvalue of type Widget, it’ll call 3 \nstd::forward with the type Widget (i.e, a non -reference type), and the 4 \nstd::forward template will be instantiated to yield this function: 5 \nWidget&& forward(Widget&& param)           // instantiation of 6 \n{                                          // std::forward when 7 \n  return static_cast<Widget&&>(param);     // T is Widget 8 \n} 9 \nBut consider what would happen if the client code wanted  to perfect-forward the 10 \nsame rvalue of type Widget, but instead of following the convention of specifying T 11 \nto be a non-reference type, it specified it to be an rvalue r eference. That is, consid-12 \ner what would happen if T were specified to be Widget&&. After instantiation  of 13 \nstd::forward but before reference collapsing (once again, see Item 30), 14 \nstd::forward would look like this: 15 \nWidget&& && forward(Widget&& && param)     // instantiation of 16 \n{                                          // std::forward when 17 \n  return static_cast<Widget&& &&>(param);  // T is Widget&&  18 \n}                                          // (before reference- 19 \n                                           // collapsing) 20 \nApplying the reference-collapsing rule that an rvalue reference to an rvalue refe r-21 \nence becomes a single rvalue reference, this instantiation emerges: 22 \nWidget&& forward(Widget&& param)           // instantiation of 23 \n{                                          // std::forward when 24 \n  return static_cast<Widget&&>(param);     // T is Widget&&  25 \n}                                          // (after reference- 26 \n                                           // collapsing) 27 \nIf you compare this instantiation with the one that results when std::forward is 28 \ncalled with T set to Widget, you’ll see that they’re identical. That means that cal l-29 \ning std::forward with a non -reference type yields the same result as calling it 30 \nwith an rvalue reference type.  31 \nThat’s wonderful news, because decltype(x) yields an rvalue reference type 32 \nwhen an rvalue is passed as an argument  to our lambda’s parameter x. We estab-33 \nlished above that when an lvalue is passed to our lambda, decltype(x) yields the 34\n\n#### 第 267 页原文\n\nPage 267 \n \nproper type to pass to std::forward, and now we realize that for rvalues, 1 \ndecltype(x) yields a type to pass to std::forward that’s not co nventional, but 2 \nthat nevertheless yields the same outcome as the conventional type. So for both 3 \nlvalues and rvalues, passing decltype(x) to std::forward gives us the result 4 \nwe want. Our perfect-forwarding lambda can therefore be written like this: 5 \nauto f = 6 \n  [](auto&& x) 7 \n  { return func(normalize(std::forward<decltype(x)>(x))); }; 8 \nFrom there, it’s just a hop, skip, and three dots to a perfect-forwarding lambda that 9 \naccepts not just a single parameter, but any number of parameters, because C++14 10 \nlambdas can also be variadic: 11 \nauto f = 12 \n  [](auto&&... x) 13 \n  { return func(normalize(std::forward<decltype(x)>(x)...)); }; 14 \nThings to Remember 15 \n Use decltype on auto&& parameters to std::forward them. 16 \n Item 36: Prefer lambdas to std::bind. 17 \nstd::bind is the C++11 successor to C++98’s std::bind1st and std::bind2nd, 18 \nbut, informally, it’s been part of the Standard Library since 2005. That’s when the 19 \nStandardization Committee  adopted a document known as TR1, which i ncluded 20 \nbind’s specification. ( In TR1 , bind was in a different namespace, so it was 21 \nstd::tr1::bind, not std::bind, and a few interface details were different.) This 22 \nhistory means that some programmers have a decade or more of experience using 23 \nstd::bind. If you’re one of them, you may be reluctant to abandon a tool tha t’s 24 \nserved you well. That’s understandable, but in this case, change is good, because in 25 \nC++11, lambdas are almost always a better choice than std::bind. As of C++14, 26 \nthe case for lambdas isn’t just stronger, it’s downright iron-clad.  27 \nThis Item assumes th at you’re familiar with std::bind. If you’re not, you ’ll want 28 \nto acquire a basic understanding before continuing.  Such an understanding  is 29 \nworthwhile in any case, because you never know when you might encounter uses 30 \nof std::bind in a code base you have to read or maintain. 31\n\n#### 第 268 页原文\n\nPage 268 \n \nThe most important reason to prefer lambdas over std::bind is that lambdas are 1 \nmore readable. Suppose, for example, we have a function to set up an audible 2 \nalarm: 3 \n// typedef for a point in time (see Item 9 for syntax) 4 \nusing Time = std::chrono::time_point<std::chrono::steady_clock>; 5 \n// see Item 10 for \"enum class\" 6 \nenum class Sound { Beep, Siren, Whistle }; 7 \n// typedef for a length of time 8 \nusing Duration = std::chrono::steady_clock::duration; 9 \n// at time t, make sound s for duration d 10 \nvoid setAlarm(Time t, Sound s, Duration d); 11 \nFurther suppose that at some point in the program, we ’ve determined we’ll want 12 \nan alarm that will go off an hour after it’s set and that will stay on for 30 seconds. 13 \nThe alarm sound, however, remains undecided. We can write a lambda that revises 14 \nsetAlarm’s interface so that only a sound needs to be specified: 15 \n// setSoundL (\"L\" for \"lambda\") is a function object allowing a 16 \n// sound to be specified for a 30-sec alarm to go off an hour  17 \n// after it's set 18 \nauto setSoundL =                              19 \n  [](Sound s)  20 \n  {  21 \n    // make std::chrono components available w/o qualification 22 \n    using namespace std::chrono; 23 \n    setAlarm(steady_clock::now() + hours(1),  // alarm to go off 24 \n             s,                               // in an hour for 25 \n             seconds(30));                    // 30 seconds 26 \n  }; 27 \nI’ve highlighted the call to setAlarm inside the lambda. This is a normal -looking 28 \nfunction call, and even a reader with little lambda experience can see that the p a-29 \nrameter s passed to the lambda is passed as an argument to setAlarm. 30 \nThe corresponding std::bind call looks like this: 31 \nusing namespace std::chrono;           // as above 32 \nusing namespace std::placeholders;     // needed for use of \"_1\"  33 \nauto setSoundB =                       // \"B\" for \"bind\" 34 \n  std::bind(setAlarm, 35\n\n#### 第 269 页原文\n\nPage 269 \n \n            steady_clock::now() + hours(1), 1 \n            _1, 2 \n            seconds(30)); 3 \nI’d like to highlight the call to setAlarm here as I did above, but there’s no call to 4 \nhighlight. Readers of this code simply have to k now that calling setSoundB in-5 \nvokes setAlarm with the time and duration specified in the call to std::bind. To 6 \nthe uninitiated, the placeholder “_1” is essentially magic, but even readers in the 7 \nknow have to mentally map from the number in that placeholder  to its position in 8 \nthe std::bind parameter list in order to understand that the first argument in a 9 \ncall to setSoundB is passed as the second argument to setAlarm. The type of this 10 \nargument is not identified in the call to std::bind, so readers have to consult the 11 \nsetAlarm declaration to determine what kind of argument to pass to setSoundB. 12 \nIf setAlarm is overloaded , the situation becomes more interesting . Suppose 13 \nthere’s an overload taking a fourth parameter specifying the alarm volume: 14 \nenum class Volume { Normal, Loud, LoudPlusPlus }; 15 \nvoid setAlarm(Time t, Sound s, Duration d, Volume v); 16 \nThe lambda continues to work as before, because overload resolution chooses the 17 \nthree-argument version of setAlarm: 18 \nauto setSoundL =                               // same as before 19 \n  [](Sound s)  20 \n  {  21 \n    using namespace std::chrono; 22 \n    setAlarm(steady_clock::now() + hours(1),   // fine, calls 23 \n             s,                                // 3-arg version 24 \n             seconds(30));                     // of setAlarm 25 \n  }; 26 \nThe std::bind call, on the other hand, now fails to compile: 27 \nauto setSoundB = 28 \n  std::bind(setAlarm,                          // error! which 29 \n            steady_clock::now() + hours(1),    // setAlarm? 30 \n            _1, 31 \n            seconds(30)); 32\n\n#### 第 270 页原文\n\nPage 270 \n \nThe problem is that compilers have no way to determine  which of the two  1 \nsetAlarm functions they should pass to std::bind. All they have is a function 2 \nname, and the name alone is ambiguous. 3 \nTo get the std::bind call to compile, setAlarm must be cast to the proper func-4 \ntion pointer type: 5 \nusing SetAlarm3ParamType = void(*)(Time t, Sound s, Duration d); 6 \nauto setSoundB =                                        // now 7 \n  std::bind(static_cast<SetAlarm3ParamType>(setAlarm),  // okay 8 \n            steady_clock::now() + hours(1),  9 \n            _1, 10 \n            seconds(30)); 11 \nBut this brings up another difference between lambdas and std::bind. Inside the 12 \nfunction call operator for setSoundL (i.e., the function call operator of the lam b-13 \nda’s closure class), the call to setAlarm is a normal function invocation that can be 14 \ninlined by compilers in the usual fashion. Furthermore, C++ specifies that the func-15 \ntion call operator on the closure class r esulting from the lambda is inline, so in a 16 \ncall to the lambda, it’s entirely possible that the body of setAlarm is inlined at the 17 \ncall site: 18 \nsetSoundL(Sound::Siren);      // body of setAlarm may  19 \n                              // well be inlined here 20 \nThe call to std::bind, however, passes a function pointer to setAlarm, and that 21 \nmeans that inside the function call operator for setSoundB (i.e., the function call 22 \noperator for the class of the object returned by std::bind), the call to setAlarm 23 \ntakes place thr ough a function pointer. Compilers are much less likely to inline 24 \nfunction calls through function pointer s, and that means that  calls to setAlarm 25 \nthrough setSoundB are less likely to be  fully inlined than those through 26 \nsetSoundL: 27 \nsetSoundB(Sound::Siren);      // body of setAlarm is less 28 \n                              // likely to be inlined here 29 \nIt’s thus probable that using lambdas generates faster code than using std::bind. 30 \nThe setAlarm example involves only a simple function call. If you want to do an y-31 \nthing more complicated, the scales tip even further in favor of lambdas. For exam-32\n\n#### 第 271 页原文\n\nPage 271 \n \nple, consider this C++14 lambda, which returns whether its argument is between a 1 \nminimum value ( lowVal) and a maximal value ( highVal), where lowVal and 2 \nhighVal are local variables: 3 \nauto betweenL =                     4 \n  [lowVal, highVal] 5 \n  (const auto& val)                          // C++14 only  6 \n  { return lowVal <= val && val <= highVal; }; 7 \nstd::bind can express the same thing, but the construct is an example of job s e-8 \ncurity through code obscurity: 9 \nusing namespace std::placeholders;           // as above 10 \nauto betweenB = 11 \n  std::bind(std::logical_and<>(),            // C++14 only 12 \n              std::bind(std::less_equal<>(), _1, lowVal), 13 \n              std::bind(std::less_equal<>(), _1, highVal)); 14 \nWithin this use of std::bind, I’m taking advantage of the C++14 feature of not 15 \nhaving to specify a template type argument for the standard operator templates 16 \n(e.g., std::logical_and, std::less_equal, etc.). In C++11, we’d have to specify 17 \nthe types we wanted to compare, and the std::bind call would look like this: 18 \nusing namespace std::placeholders; 19 \nauto betweenB =                              // C++11 version 20 \n  std::bind(std::logical_and<bool>(), 21 \n              std::bind(std::greater_equal<int>(), _1, lowVal), 22 \n              std::bind(std::less_equal<int>(), _1, highVal)); 23 \nOf course, in C++11, the lambda couldn’t take an auto parameter, so it’d have to 24 \ncommit to a type, too: 25 \nauto betweenL =                              // C++11 version 26 \n  [lowVal, highVal] 27 \n  (int x) 28 \n  { return x >= lowVal && x <= highVal; }; 29 \nEither way, I hope we can agree that the lambda version is not just shorter, but 30 \nalso more comprehensible and maintainable. 31 \nEarlier, I remarked that for those with little std::bind experience, its placehol d-32 \ners (e.g., _1, _2, etc.) are essentially  magic. But i t’s not just the behavior of the 33\n\n#### 第 272 页原文\n\nPage 272 \n \nplaceholders that’s opaque. Suppose we have a function to create compressed cop-1 \nies of  Widgets, 2 \nenum class CompLevel { Low, Normal, High };  // compression 3 \n                                             // level 4 \nWidget compress(const Widget& w,             // make compressed 5 \n                CompLevel lev);              // copy of w 6 \nand we want to create a function object that allows us to speci fy how much a par-7 \nticular Widget w should be compressed. This use of std::bind will create such an 8 \nobject: 9 \nWidget w; 10 \nusing namespace std::placeholders; 11 \nauto compressRateB = std::bind(compress, w, _1);   12 \nNow, when we pass w to std::bind, it has to be stored for the later call to com-13 \npress. It’s stored inside the object compressRate, but how is it stored —by value 14 \nor by reference? It makes a difference, because if w is modified between the call to 15 \nstd::bind and a call to compressRate, storing w by reference will reflect the 16 \nchanges, while storing it by value won’t. 17 \nThe answer is that it’s stored by value,  but the only way to know that is to mem o-18 \nrize how std::bind works; there’s no sign of it in the call to std::bind. † Con-19 \ntrast that with a lambda approach, where whether w is captured by value or by ref-20 \nerence is explicit: 21 \nauto compressRateL =                         // w is captured by 22 \n  [w](CompLevel lev)                         // value; lev is  23 \n  { return compress(w, lev); };              // passed by value 24 \n                                                             \n† std::bind always copies it s arguments, but callers can achieve the effect of having an \nargument stored by reference by applying std::ref to it. The result of \nauto compressRateB = std::bind(compress, std::ref(w), _1); \nis that compressRateB acts as if it holds a reference to w, rather than a copy. A lambda, of \ncourse, would simply use an explicit by-reference capture mode to achieve this effect.\n\n#### 第 273 页原文\n\nPage 273 \n \nEqually explicit is how parameters are passed to the lambda. Here, it’s  clear that 1 \nthe parameter lev is passed by value. Hence: 2 \ncompressRateL(CompLevel::High);              // arg is passed 3 \n                                             // by value 4 \nBut in the call to the object resulting from std::bind, how is the argument 5 \npassed? 6 \ncompressRateB(CompLevel::High);              // how is arg 7 \n                                             // passed? 8 \nAgain, the only way to know is to memorize how std::bind works. (The answer 9 \nis that all arguments passed to objects created by std::bind are passed by refer-10 \nence, because the function call operator for such objects uses perfect forwarding.) 11 \nCompared to lambdas, then, code using std::bind is less readable, less expre s-12 \nsive, and less efficient. In C++14, there are no reasonable use cases for std::bind. 13 \nIn C++11, however, std::bind can be justified in two constrained situations: 14 \n Move capture. C++11 lambdas don’t offer move capture, but it can be emula t-15 \ned through a  combination of a lambda and std::bind. For details, consult 16 \nItem 34, which also explains that in C++14, lambdas’ support for init capture 17 \neliminates the need for the emulation. 18 \n Polymorphic function objects. Because the function call operator on the o b-19 \nject ret urned from std::bind uses perfect forwarding, it can accept arg u-20 \nments of any type (modulo the restrictions on perfect forwarding described in 21 \nItem 32). This can be useful when you want to bind an object with a te m-22 \nplatized function call operator. For example, given this class, 23 \nclass PolyWidget { 24 \npublic: 25 \n    template<typename T> 26 \n    void operator()(const T& param); 27 \n    … 28 \n}; 29 \nstd::bind can bind a PolyWidget as follows: 30 \nPolyWidget pw; 31\n\n#### 第 274 页原文\n\nPage 274 \n \nauto boundPW = std::bind(pw, _1); 1 \nboundPW can then be called with different types of arguments: 2 \nboundPW(1930);              // pass int to 3 \n                            // PolyWidget::operator() 4 \nboundPW(nullptr);           // pass nullptr to  5 \n                            // PolyWidget::operator() 6 \nboundPW(\"Rosebud\");         // pass string literal to  7 \n                            // PolyWidget::operator() 8 \nThere is no way to do this with a C++11 lambda. In C++14, however, it's easily 9 \nachieved via a lambda with an auto parameter: 10 \nauto boundPW = [pw](const auto& param)    // C++14 only 11 \n               { pw(param); }; 12 \nThese are edge cases, of course, and they're transient edge cases at that, because 13 \ncompilers supporting C++14 lambdas are increasingly common.  14 \nWhen bind was unofficially added to C++ in 2005, it was a big improvement over 15 \nits 1998 predecessors. The addition of lambda support to C++11 rendered 16 \nstd::bind all but obsolete, however, and as of C++14, there are just no use c ases 17 \nfor it. 18 \nThings to Remember 19 \n Lambas are more readable, more expressive, and more efficient than using 20 \nstd::bind. 21 \n In C++11 only, std::bind may be useful for implementing move capture or 22 \nfor binding objects with templatized function call operators. 23\n\n#### 第 275 页原文\n\nPage 275 \n \n \n1 \nOne of C++11’s great triumphs is the incorporation of concurrency into the la n-2 \nguage and library. Programmers familiar with other threading APIs (e.g., pthreads 3 \nor Windows’ Threads) are sometimes surprised at the comparatively Spartan fea-4 \nture set that C++ offers, but that’s because a great deal of C++’s support for concur-5 \nrency is in the form of constraints on compiler -writers. The resulting language as-6 \nsurances mean that for the first time in C++’s history, programmers can write mul-7 \ntithreaded programs with standard behavior across all computing platforms. This 8 \nestablishes a solid foundation on which expressive libraries can be built, and the 9 \nconcurrency elements of the Standard Library (tasks, futures, threads, mutexes, 10 \ncondition variables, atomic objects, and more) are merely the beginning of what is 11 \nsure to become an increasingly rich set of tools for the development of concurrent 12 \nC++ software. 13 \nThe existing features are impressive in their own right, of course, and this chapter 14 \nfocuses on the questions that inform their effective application. What are the di f-15 \nferences between tasks and  threads, and which should be used when? What b e-16 \nhavioral guarantees does std::async offer, and how can they be controlled? 17 \nWhat are the implications of the varying behavior s of thread handle destructors? 18 \nWhat are the pros and cons of different inter-thread event communication strat e-19 \ngies? How do std::atomics differ from volatiles, and what is the proper appl i-20 \ncation of each? The coming pages address these issue, and more. 21 \nIn the Items that follow, bear in mind that the Standard Library has two templates 22 \nfor futures: std::future and std::shared_future. In many cases, the distin c-23 \ntion is not important, so I often simply talk about futures, by which I mean both 24 \nkinds. 25 \n Item 37: Prefer task-based programming to thread-based. 26 \nIf you want to run a function doAsyncWork asynchronously, you have two basic 27 \nchoices. You can create a std::thread and run doAsyncWork on it, thus employ-28 \ning a thread-based approach: 29", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.276–p.300", "pages": "p.276–p.300", "raw_text": "#### 第 276 页原文\n\nPage 276 \n \nint doAsyncWork(); 1 \nstd::thread t(doAsyncWork); 2 \nOr you can pass doAsyncWork to std::async, a strategy known as task-based:  3 \nauto fut = std::async(doAsyncWork);        // \"fut\" for \"future\" 4 \nIn such calls, the callable entity passed to std::async (e.g., doAsyncWork) is con-5 \nsidered a task. 6 \nThe task-based approach is typically superior to its thread-based counterpart, and 7 \nthe tiny amount of code we've seen already demonstrates some reasons why. Here, 8 \ndoAsyncWork produces a return value, which we can reasonabl y assume the code 9 \ninvoking doAsyncWork is interested in. With the thread -based invocation, there ’s 10 \nno straightforward way to get access to it . With the task-based approach, it’s easy, 11 \nbecause the future returned from std::async offers the get function. Th e get 12 \nfunction is even more important if doAsyncWork emits an exception, because get 13 \nprovides access to that, too. With the thread -based approach, if doAsyncWork 14 \nthrows, the program dies (via a call to std::terminate or, if there was one,  the 15 \nfunction most recently specified via std::set_terminate). 16 \nA more fundamental difference between thread -based and task -based progra m-17 \nming is the higher level of abstraction that task -based embodies. It frees you from 18 \nthe details of thread management, an observation that reminds me that I need to 19 \nsummarize the three meanings of “thread” in concurrent C++ software: 20 \n Hardware threads are the threads that actually perform computation. Contem-21 \nporary machine architectures offer one or more hardware threads per C PU 22 \ncore. 23 \n Software threads (also known as OS threads or system threads) are the threads 24 \nthat the operating system † manages across all processes and schedules for e x-25 \necution on hardware threads. Typically, it’s possible to create  many more 26 \nsoftware threads th an hardware threads, because when a software thread is 27 \n                                                             \n† Assuming you have one. Some embedded systems don’t.\n\n#### 第 277 页原文\n\nPage 277 \n \nblocked (e.g., on I/O or waiting for a mutex or condition variable), th roughput 1 \ncan be improved by executing other, unblocked, threads.  2 \n std::threads are objects in a C++ process that  act as handles to  underlying 3 \nsoftware threads.  Some std::thread objects represent “null” handles, i.e., 4 \ncorrespond to no software thread, because  they’re in a default -constructed 5 \nstate (hence have no function to execute), have been moved from (the moved -6 \nto std::thread then acts as the handle to the underlying software thread), 7 \nhave been joined (the function they were to run has finished), or detached 8 \n(the connection between them and their underlying software thread has been 9 \nsevered). 10 \nSoftware threads are a limited resou rce. If you try to c reate more than the system 11 \ncan offer, a std::system_error exception is thrown. This is true even if the func-12 \ntion you want to run can’t throw. For example, even if doAsyncWork is noexcept, 13 \nint doAsyncWork() noexcept;          // see Item 16 for noexcept 14 \nthis statement could result in an exception: 15 \nstd::thread t(doAsyncWork);          // throws if no more 16 \n                                     // threads are available 17 \nWell-written software must somehow deal with this possibility, but how? One a p-18 \nproach is to run doAsynchWork on the current thread, but that could lead to u n-19 \nbalanced loads and, if the current thread is a GUI thread, responsiveness issues. 20 \nAnother option is to wait for some existing software threads to complete and then 21 \ntry to create a new std::thread again, but it’s possible that the existing threads 22 \nare waiting for an action  that doAsyncWork is supposed to perform  (e.g., produce 23 \na result or notify a condition variable). 24 \nEven if you don’t run out of threads, you can have trouble with oversubscription. 25 \nThat’s when there are more ready -to-run (i.e., unblocked) software threads than 26 \nhardware threads. When that happens, the thread scheduler (typically part of the 27 \nOS) time -slices the software threads on the hardware. When one thread’s time -28 \nslice is finished and another’s begins, a context switch is performed. Such context 29 \nswitches increase the overall th read management overhead of the system, and 30 \nthey can be particularly costly when the hardware thread on which a software 31\n\n#### 第 278 页原文\n\nPage 278 \n \nthread is scheduled is on a different core than the software thread was for its last 1 \ntime-slice. In that case, (1) the CPU caches are typically cold for that software 2 \nthread and (2) the running of the “new” software thread on that core “pollutes” the 3 \nCPU caches for “old” threads that had been running on that core and are likely to 4 \nbe scheduled to run there again. 5 \nAvoiding oversubscription  is difficult, because the optimal ratio of software to 6 \nhardware threads depends on how often the software threads are runnable, and 7 \nthat can change dynamically, e.g., when a program goes from an I/O -heavy region 8 \nto a computation -heavy region. The best ratio of software to hardware threads is 9 \nalso dependent on the cost of context switches and how effectively the software 10 \nthreads use the CPU caches. Furthermore the number of hardware threads and the 11 \ndetails of the CPU caches (e.g., how large they are and the ir relative speeds) d e-12 \npend on the machine architecture, so even if you tune your application to avoid 13 \noversubscription (while still keeping the hardware busy) on one platform, there’s 14 \nno guarantee that your solution will work well on other kinds of machines. 15 \nYour life will be easier if you dump these problems on somebody else, and using 16 \nstd::async does exactly that. 17 \nauto fut = std::async(doAsyncWork);   // onus of thread mgmt is 18 \n                                      // on implementer of  19 \n                                      // the Standard Library 20 \nThis call  shifts all the thread management responsibility to the implementer s of 21 \nthe C++ Standard Library. For example, you don’t need to worry about an out -of-22 \nthreads exception arising, because this call will nev er yield one. “How can that 23 \nbe?”, you might wonder. “If I ask for more software threads than the system can 24 \nprovide, why does it matt er whether I do it by creating  std::threads or by call-25 \ning std::async?” It matters, because std::async, when called in this form (i.e., 26 \nwith the default launch policy—see Item 38), doesn’t guarantee that it will create a 27 \nnew software thread. Rather, it permits the scheduler  to arrange for the specified 28 \nfunction (in this example , doAsyncWork) to be run on the current thread, and the 29 \nscheduler will take advantage of that freedom if the system is oversubscribed or is 30 \nout of threads.  31\n\n#### 第 279 页原文\n\nPage 279 \n \nIf you pulled this “run it on the current thread” trick yourself, of course, I remarked 1 \nthat it could lead to load -balancing or responsiveness issues, and those issues 2 \ndon’t go away simply because it’s std::async and the runtime scheduler tha t 3 \nconfront them instead of you. When it comes to load -balancing, however, the 4 \nruntime scheduler is likely to have a more comprehensive picture of what’s ha p-5 \npening on the machine than you do, because it manages the threads from all pr o-6 \ncesses, not just the one your code is running in.  7 \nWith std::async, r esponsiveness on a GUI thread can still be problematic, b e-8 \ncause the scheduler has no way of knowing which of your threads has tight r e-9 \nsponsiveness requirements . I n that case, you’ll want to pass the 10 \nstd::launch::async launch policy  to std::async. That will  ensure tha t the 11 \nfunction you want to run really executes on a different thread (see Item 38). How-12 \never, you ’ll have to be prepared for the possibility of an exception, because 13 \nstd::async called with std::launch::async can run out of threads and emit a 14 \nstd::system_error exception, just like the std::thread constructor can. 15 \nState-of-the-art thread schedulers employ system -wide thread pools to avoid 16 \noversubscription, and they improve load balancing across hardware cores through 17 \nwork-stealing algorithms. The C++ Standard does not require the use of thread 18 \npools or work-stealing, but it’s deliberately written to permit them, and some ve n-19 \ndors already take advantage of this technology in their Standard Library impl e-20 \nmentations. More are expected to follow. If you take a task-based approach to your 21 \nconcurrent programming, you automatically reap the benefit s of such technology 22 \nas it becomes more widespread . If, on the other hand, you program directly with 23 \nstd::threads, you assume the burden of dealing with thread exhaustion, ove r-24 \nsubscription, and load balancing your self, not to mention how your solutions to 25 \nthese problems mesh with the solutions implemented in programs running in ot h-26 \ner processes on the same machine. 27 \nCompared to thread-based programming, a task-based design spares you the trav-28 \nails of manual thread management, and it provides a natural way to  examine the 29 \nresults of asynchronously executed functions (i.e., return values or exceptions). 30 \nNevertheless, there are some situations where using threads directly may be a p-31 \npropriate. They include: 32\n\n#### 第 280 页原文\n\nPage 280 \n \n You need access to the API of the underlying threading imple mentation. 1 \nThe C++ concurrency API is typically  implemented using a lower -level pla t-2 \nform-specific API, usually pthreads or Windows’ Threads. Those APIs are cu r-3 \nrently richer than what C++ offers . (For example,  C++ has no notion of thread 4 \npriorities or affin ities.) To provide access to the API of the underlying threa d-5 \ning implementation, std::thread objects offer the native_handle member 6 \nfunction. There is no counterpart to this functionality for std::futures (i.e., 7 \nwhat std::async returns). 8 \n You need to and are able to optimize thread usage for your application . 9 \nThis could be the case, for example, if you’re developing server software with a 10 \nknown execution profile that will be deployed as the only significant process 11 \non a machine with fixed hardware characteristics. 12 \n You need to implement threading technology beyond the C++ concurre n-13 \ncy API, e.g., thread pools on platforms where your C++ implementations don’t 14 \noffer them. 15 \nThese are uncommon cases , however. Most of the time, you should choose task-16 \nbased designs instead of programming with threads. 17 \nThings to Remember 18 \n The std::thread API offer no direct way to get return values from asynchr o-19 \nnously-run functions, and if those functions throw, the program is terminated. 20 \n Thread-based programming calls for manua l management of thread exhau s-21 \ntion, oversubscription, load balancing, and adaptation to new platforms. 22 \n Task-based programming via std::async with the default launch policy su f-23 \nfers from none of these drawbacks. 24 \n Item 38: Specify std::launch::async if asynchronicity is 25 \nessential. 26 \nWhen you call std::async to execute a function (or other callable entity ), you’re 27 \ngenerally intending to run the function asynchronously. But that’s not necessarily 28 \nwhat you’re asking std::async to do. You’re really requesting that the function  29\n\n#### 第 281 页原文\n\nPage 281 \n \nbe run in accord with a std::async launch policy. There are two standard pol i-1 \ncies, each represented by an enumerator in the std::launch scoped enum.  (See 2 \nItem 10 for information on scoped enums.) Assuming a function f is passed to 3 \nstd::async for execution,  4 \n The std::launch::async launch policy means that f must be run asynchr o-5 \nnously, i.e., on a different thread. 6 \n The std::launch::deferred launch policy means that f may run only when 7 \nget or wait is called on the future returned by std::async.† That is, f’s exe-8 \ncution is deferred until such a call is made. When get or wait is invoked, f will 9 \nexecute synchronously, i.e., on the thread that made the invocation. (The caller 10 \nwill block until f finishes running.) If neither get nor wait is ever called, f will 11 \nnever run. 12 \nPerhaps surprisingly, std::async’s default launch policy —the one it uses if you 13 \ndon’t expressly specify one —is neither of these. Rather, it’s these or-ed together. 14 \nThe following two calls have exactly the same meaning: 15 \nauto fut1 = std::async(f);                     // run f using  16 \n                                               // default launch 17 \n                                               // policy 18 \nauto fut2 = std::async(std::launch::async |    // run f either 19 \n                       std::launch::deferred,  // async or 20 \n                       f);                     // deferred 21 \nThe default policy thus permits f to be run either asynchronously or synchronous-22 \nly. As Item 37 points out, this flexibility permits std::async and the  thread-23 \n                                                             \n† This is not rigorously true. What matters isn’t the future on which get or wait is invoked, \nit’s the shared state to which the future refers. ( Item 40 discusses the relationship between \nfutures and shared states.) Because std::futures support moving and can also be used to \nconstruct std::shared_futures, and because std::shared_futures can be copied, the \nfuture object referring to the shared state arising from the call to std::async  to which f \nwas passed is likely to be different  from the one returned by std::async. That’s a mouth-\nful, however, so it’s common to fudge the truth and simply talk about invoking get or wait \non the future returned from std::async.\n\n#### 第 282 页原文\n\nPage 282 \n \nmanagement components of the Standard Library to assume full responsibility for 1 \nthread creation and destruction, avoidance of oversubscription, and load balan c-2 \ning. That’s among the things that make concurrent programming with 3 \nstd::async so convenient. 4 \nBut using std::async with the default launch policy has some noteworthy impli-5 \ncations. Given a thread t executing this statement, 6 \nauto fut = std::async(f);                 // run f using default 7 \n                                          // launch policy 8 \n It’s not possible to predict whether f will run concurrently with t, because f 9 \nmight be scheduled to run deferred. 10 \n It’s not possible to predict whether f runs on a thread different from t. 11 \n It may not be possible to predict whether f runs at all , because it may not be 12 \npossible to guarantee that get or wait will be called on the future correspond-13 \ning to f along every path through the program. 14 \nThe default launch policy’s scheduling flexibility often mixes poorly with the use of 15 \nthread_local variables, because it means that if f reads or writes such thread-16 \nlocal storage (TLS), it’s not possible to predict which thread’s variables will be ac-17 \ncessed: 18 \nauto fut = std::async(f);         // TLS for f possibly for  19 \n                                  // different thread, but  20 \n                                  // possibly for current thread 21 \nIt also complicates wait-based loops using timeouts, because calling wait_for or 22 \nwait_until on a task (see Item 37) that’s deferred  yields the value 23 \nstd::launch::deferred. This means that the following loop, which looks like it 24 \nshould eventually terminate,  may, in reality, run forever: 25 \nvoid f()                                      // f sleeps for 26 \n{                                             // 1 second, 27 \n  std::this_thread::sleep_for(                // then returns 28 \n    std::chrono::seconds(1) 29 \n  ); 30 \n} 31\n\n#### 第 283 页原文\n\nPage 283 \n \nauto fut = std::async(f);                     // run f  1 \n                                              // asynchronously 2 \n                                              // (conceptually) 3 \nwhile (fut.wait_for(                          // loop until f 4 \n         std::chrono::milliseconds(100)) !=   // has finished 5 \n       std::future_status::ready)             // running... 6 \n{                                             // which may  7 \n  …                                           // never happen! 8 \n} 9 \nIf f runs concurrently with the thread calling std::async (i.e., if the launch policy 10 \nchosen for f is std::launch::async), there’s no problem here, but if f is d e-11 \nferred, fut.wait_for will always return std::future_status::deferred. 12 \nThat will never be equal to std::future_status::ready, so the loop will never 13 \nterminate.  14 \nThis kind of bug is easy to overlook during development and unit testing, because 15 \nit’s likely to manifest itself only under heavy loads. Those are the conditions that 16 \npush the machine towards oversubscription or thread exhaustion, and that’s when 17 \na task is  most likely to be deferred. After all, if the hardware isn’t threatened by 18 \noversubscription or thread exhaustion, there’s no reason for the runtime system 19 \nnot to schedule the task for concurrent execution. 20 \nThe fix is simple : just check the future correspo nding to the std::async call to 21 \nsee whether the task is deferred, and, if so, avoid entering the timeout -based loop. 22 \nUnfortunately, there’s no direct way to ask a future whether its task is deferred. 23 \nInstead, you have to call a timeout -based function—a function such as wait_for. 24 \nOf course, you don’t really want to wait  for anything, you just want to see if the r e-25 \nturn value is std::future_status::deferred, so stifle your mild disbelief at 26 \nthe necessary circumlocution and call wait_for with a zero timeout: 27 \nauto fut = std::async(f);                       // as above 28 \nif (fut.wait_for(std::chrono::seconds(0)) !=    // if task 29 \n    std::future_status::deferred)               // isn't 30 \n{                                               // deferred... 31 \n  while (fut.wait_for(                          // infinite loop 32 \n           std::chrono::milliseconds(100)) !=   // no longer 33 \n           std::future_status::ready) {         // possible 34\n\n#### 第 284 页原文\n\nPage 284 \n \n    …                   // fut is neither deferred nor ready, so 1 \n                        // do concurrent work until it's ready 2 \n  } 3 \n  …                     // fut is ready 4 \n} else { 5 \n  …                     // task is deferred, so use wait or  6 \n                        // get to call it synchronously 7 \n} 8 \nC++14 offers nothing to improve testing a future to see if it corresponds to a d e-9 \nferred task , but it does make the specification of time durations more palatable, 10 \nbecause it takes advantage of C++11’s support for user-defined literals to offer suf-11 \nfixes for seconds ( s), millis econds (ms), hours (h), etc. These  suffixes are impl e-12 \nmented in the std::literals namespace, so the above code can be rewritten as 13 \nfollows: 14 \nusing namespace std::literals;          // for duration suffixes 15 \nif (fut.wait_for(0s) != std::future_status::deferred) { // C++14 16 \n  while (fut.wait_for(100ms) !=                         // only   17 \n         std::future_status::ready) { 18 \n    …                  19 \n  } 20 \n  …                                     // as before 21 \nThe upshot of these various considerations is that using std::async with the de-22 \nfault launch policy for a task is fine as long as the following conditions are fulfilled: 23 \n Execution of the task need not run concurrently with the calling thread. 24 \n It doesn’t matter which thread’s thread_local variables are read or written. 25 \n There’s either (1) a guarantee that get or wait will always be called on the 26 \nfuture returned by std::async or (2) it’s acceptable that the task may never 27 \nbe executed. 28 \n Code using wait_for or wait_until always checks for deferred status. 29\n\n#### 第 285 页原文\n\nPage 285 \n \nIf any of these conditions fails to hold , you probably want to guarantee that 1 \nstd::async will schedule the task for truly asynchronous execution . The way to 2 \ndo that is to pass std::launch::async as the first argument when you make the 3 \ncall: 4 \nauto fut = std::async(std::launch::async, f);  // launch f 5 \n                                               // asynchronously 6 \nIn fact, having a function that acts like std::async, but that automatically uses 7 \nstd::launch::async as the launch policy, is a convenient tool to have around, so 8 \nit’s nice that it’s easy to write: 9 \ntemplate<typename F, typename... Args> 10 \ninline 11 \nstd::future<typename std::result_of<F(Args...)>::type> 12 \nreallyAsync(F&& f, Args&&... args)         // return future 13 \n{                                          // for asynchronous 14 \n  return std::async(std::launch::async,    // call to f(args...) 15 \n                    std::forward<F>(f), 16 \n                    std::forward<Args>(args)...); 17 \n} 18 \nThis function takes an executable entity f and zero or more arguments args and 19 \nperfect-forwards them  (see Item 27) to std::async, passing 20 \nstd::launch::async as the launch policy. Like std::async, it returns a 21 \nstd::future for the result of invoking f on args. Determining the type of that 22 \nresult is easy, because the type trait  std::result_of gives it to you . (See Item 9 23 \nfor information on type traits.) 24 \nreallyAsync is used just like std::sync, except that, as Item 37 explains, it may 25 \nemit an exception indicating that it’s not possible to create a new thread: 26 \nauto fut = reallyAsync(f);            // run f asynchronously or 27 \n                                      // throw std::system_error 28 \nIn C++14, the ability to deduce reallyAsync’s return type streamlines the func-29 \ntion declaration: 30 \ntemplate<typename F, typename... Args> 31 \ninline 32 \nauto                                           // C++14 only 33 \nreallyAsync(F&& f, Args&&... args) 34 \n{ 35\n\n#### 第 286 页原文\n\nPage 286 \n \n  return std::async(std::launch::async,  1 \n                    std::forward<F>(f), 2 \n                    std::forward<Args>(args)...); 3 \n} 4 \nThis version makes it even clearer  that reallyAsync does nothing but invoke 5 \nstd::async with the std::launch::async launch policy. 6 \nThings to Remember 7 \n The default launch policy for std::async permits both a synchronous and 8 \nsynchronous task execution. 9 \n This flexibility leads to uncertainty when accessing thread_locals, implies  10 \nthat the task may never execute, and complicates program logic for timeout -11 \nbased wait calls. 12 \n Specify std::launch::async if asynchronous task execution is essential. 13 \n Item 39: Make std::threads unjoinable on all paths. 14 \nEvery std::thread object is in one of two states : joinable or unjoinable. A join a-15 \nble std::thread corresponds to an underlying asynchronous thread of execution 16 \nthat is or could be running. A std::thread corresponding  to a n underlying  17 \nthread that’s blocked or waiting to be scheduled is joinable, for example. 18 \nAn unjoinable std::thread is what you’d expect: a std::thread that’s not join-19 \nable. Unjoinable std::thread objects include: 20 \n Default-constructed std::threads. Such std::threads have no function to 21 \nexecute, hence don’t correspond to an underlying thread of execution. 22 \n std::thread objects that have been moved from . The result of a move is 23 \nthat the underlying thread of execution a std::thread used to correspond to 24 \n(if any) now corresponds to a different std::thread. 25 \n std::threads that have been joined. After a join, a std::thread’s under-26 \nlying thread of execution has finished running. 27\n\n#### 第 287 页原文\n\nPage 287 \n \n std::threads that have been detached. A detach severs the connection 1 \nbetween a std::thread object and the underlying thread of execution it co r-2 \nresponds to. 3 \nOne reason a std::thread’s joinability is important is that if the destructor for a 4 \njoinable thread is invoked, execution of the program is terminated.  5 \nFor example, suppose  we have a function doWork that takes a filtering function, 6 \nfilter, and a maximum value , maxVal, as parameters . doWork checks to make  7 \nsure that all conditions necessary for its computation are satisfied, then  performs 8 \nthe computation with all the values between 0 and maxVal that pass the filter . If 9 \nit’s time-consuming to do the filtering and it’s also time -consuming to determine 10 \nwhether doWork’s conditions are satisfied, it would be reasonable to do those two 11 \nthings concurrently. We might come up with code like this: 12 \nconstexpr int tenMillion = 10000000;          // see Item 14  13 \n                                              // for constexpr 14 \nbool doWork(std::function<bool(int)> filter,  // returns whether 15 \n            int maxVal = tenMillion)          // computation was 16 \n{                                             // performed 17 \n  std::vector<int> vals;                      // values that 18 \n                                              // satisfy filter  19 \n  std::thread t([&filter, maxVal, &vals]      // compute vals' 20 \n                {                             // content  21 \n                  for (auto i = 0; i <= maxVal; ++i) 22 \n                   { if (filter(i)) vals.push_back(i); } 23 \n                }); 24 \n  if (conditionsAreSatisfied()) { 25 \n    t.join();                                 // let t finish 26 \n    performComputation(vals);  27 \n    return true;                              // computation was 28 \n  }                                           // performed 29 \n  return false;                               // computation was 30 \n};                                            // not performed 31 \nBefore I explain why this code is problematic, I’ll remark that tenMillion’s ini-32 \ntializing value  can be made more readable in C++14 by taking advantage of 33 \nC++14’s ability to use an apostrophe as a digit separator: 34\n\n#### 第 288 页原文\n\nPage 288 \n \nconstexpr auto tenMillion = 10'000'000;       // C++ 14 only 1 \nBut back to doWork. If conditionsAreSatisfied() returns true, all is well, but 2 \nif it returns false or throws an exception, the std::thread object t will be join-3 \nable when its destructor is called at the end of doWork. That would cause program 4 \nexecution to be terminated. 5 \nYou might wonder why the std::thread destructor behaves this way . It’s b e-6 \ncause the two other obvious options are arguably worse. They are: 7 \n An implicit join. In this case, a std::thread’s destructor would wait for its 8 \nunderlying asynchronous thread of execution to complete. That sounds re a-9 \nsonable, but it could lead to performance anomalies that would be difficult to 10 \ntrack down. For example, it would be counte rintuitive that doWork would wait 11 \nfor its filter to be applied to all values if conditionsAreSatisfied() had al-12 \nready returned false.   13 \n An implicit detach. In this case, a std::thread’s destructor would sever the 14 \nconnection between the std::thread object and its underlying thread of exe-15 \ncution. The underlying thread would continue to run. This sounds no less rea-16 \nsonable than the join approach, but the debugging problems it can lead to are 17 \nactually worse. In doWork, for example, vals is a local stack variable  that is 18 \ncaptured by reference. It's also modified inside the lambda (via the call to 19 \npush_back). Suppose, then, that while the lambda is running asynchronously, 20 \nconditionsAreSatisfied() returns false. In that case, doWork would re-21 \nturn, and its local variabl es (including vals) would be destroyed. Its stack 22 \nframe would be popped, and execution of its thread would continue at 23 \ndoWork’s call site.  24 \nStatements following that call site would, at some point, make additional fun c-25 \ntion calls, and at least one such call would probably end up using some or all of 26 \nthe memory that had once been occupied by the doWork stack frame. Let’s call 27 \nsuch a function f. While f was running, the lambda that doWork initiated 28 \nwould still be running  asynchronously. That lambda could call push_back on 29 \nthe stack memory that used to be vals, but that is now somewhere inside f’s 30 \nstack frame. Such a call would modify the memory that used to be vals, and 31\n\n#### 第 289 页原文\n\nPage 289 \n \nthat means that from f’s perspective, the content of memory in its stack frame 1 \ncould spontaneously change! Imagine the fun you’d have debugging that.  2 \nThe Standardization Committee decided that the consequences of destroying a 3 \njoinable thread were sufficiently dire that they essentially banned it (by specifying 4 \nthat destruction of a joinable thread causes program termination). 5 \nThis puts the onus on you to ensure that if you use a std::thread object, it’s 6 \nmade unjoinable on every path out of the scope in which it’s defined. But covering 7 \nevery path can be compli cated. It includes flowing off the end of the scope as well 8 \nas jumping out via a return, continue, break, goto or exception. That can be a 9 \nlot of paths. 10 \nAny time you want to perform some action along every path out of a scope, the 11 \nnormal approach is to put  that action in the destructor of a local object. Such o b-12 \njects are known as RAII objects, and the classes they come from are known as RAII 13 \nclasses. (RAII itself stands for “resource acquisition is initializa tion,” although the 14 \ncrux of the technique is destruction, not initialization). RAII classes are common in 15 \nthe Standard Library. Examples include the STL containers (each container’s d e-16 \nstructor destroy the container’s contents and releases its memory), the standard 17 \nsmart pointers ( Items 20-22 explain that std::unique_ptr’s destructor invokes 18 \nits deleter on the object it points to, a nd the destructors in std::shared_ptr and 19 \nstd::weak_ptr decrement reference counts), std::fstream objects (their d e-20 \nstructors close the files they correspond to), and many more. And yet  there is no 21 \nstandard RAII class for std::thread objects—perhaps because the Standardiza-22 \ntion Committee, having rejected both join and detach as default options, simply 23 \ndidn’t know what such a class should do.  24 \nFortunately, it’s not difficult to write one yourself. For example , the following class 25 \nallows callers to specify a std::thread member function (e.g., join or detach) 26 \nthat should be called when a ThreadRAII object (an RAII object for a 27 \nstd::thread) is destroyed: 28 \nclass ThreadRAII { 29 \npublic: 30 \n  using RAIIAction =                // type of mbr func to 31 \n    void (std::thread::*)();        // invoke in ThreadRAII dtor 32\n\n#### 第 290 页原文\n\nPage 290 \n \n  ThreadRAII(std::thread&& t, RAIIAction a)  // in dtor, 1 \n  : action(a), t(std::move(t)) {}            // invoke a on t 2 \n  ~ThreadRAII()                              // see below for 3 \n  { if (t.joinable()) (t.*action)(); }       // joinability test 4 \n  std::thread& get() { return t; }           // see below 5 \nprivate: 6 \n  RAIIAction action; 7 \n  std::thread t; 8 \n}; 9 \nWith any luck, this code is largely self-explanatory, but the following points may be 10 \nhelpful: 11 \n The constructor accepts only std::thread rvalues, because we want to move 12 \nthe passed-in std::thread into the ThreadRAII object. 13 \n The parameter order in the constructor is designed to be intuitive  to callers 14 \n(specifying the std::thread first and the destructor  action second makes 15 \nmore sense than vice -versa), but the member initialization list is designed to 16 \nmatch the order of the data members’ declarations. That order puts the 17 \nstd::thread object last. In this class, the order makes no difference, but in 18 \ngeneral, it’s possible for the initialization of one data member to depend on 19 \nanother, and because std::thread objects may start running a function i m-20 \nmediately after they are initialized, it’s a good habit to declare them last in a 21 \nclass. That guarantees that at the time they are constructed, all the data me m-22 \nbers that precede them have already been initialized and can therefore be safe-23 \nly accessed  by the asynchronously running thread  that corresponds to the 24 \nstd::thread data member. 25 \n ThreadRAII offers a get function to provide access to the underlying 26 \nstd::thread object. This is analogous to the get functions offered by the 27 \nstandard smart pointer classes that give access to their underlying raw poin t-28 \ners. Providing get avoids the need for ThreadRAII to replicate the full 29 \nstd::thread interface, and it also means that ThreadRAII objects can be 30 \nused in contexts where std::thread objects are required, e.g., because an in-31 \nterface requires that a reference to a std::thread be passed.  32\n\n#### 第 291 页原文\n\nPage 291 \n \n Before the ThreadRAII destructor invokes the action a on the std::thread 1 \nobject t, it checks to make sure that t is joinable. This is necessary, because in-2 \nvoking join or detach on an unjoinable thread yields undefined behavior, 3 \nand it’s possible that a client constructed a std::thread, created a Thread-4 \nRAII object from it, used get to acquire access to t, and then did a move from 5 \nt or called join or detach on it. Each of those actions would render t unjoin-6 \nable. 7 \nIf you’re worried that in the statement, 8 \nif (t.joinable()) (t.*action)(); 9 \na race condition exists, because between execution of t.joinable() and 10 \n(t.*action)(), another thread could render t unjoinable, your intuition is 11 \ncommendable, but your fears are unfounded. A std::thread object can 12 \nchange state from joinable to unjoinable only through a member function call, 13 \ne.g., join, detach, or a move operation. At the time a ThreadRAII object’s de-14 \nstructor is invoked, no other thread should be making member function calls 15 \non that objec t. If there are simultaneous calls, there is certainly a race cond i-16 \ntion, but the race isn’t inside the destructor, it’s in the client code that is trying 17 \nto invoke two  member functions (the destructor and something else) on one 18 \nobject at the same time. In  general, simultaneous member function calls on a 19 \nsingle object are safe only if all are to const member functions (see Item 15). 20 \nEmploying ThreadRAII in our doWork example would look like this: 21 \nbool doWork(std::function<bool(int)> filter,  // as before 22 \n            int maxVal = tenMillion)  23 \n{  24 \n  std::vector<int> vals;                      // as before  25 \n  ThreadRAII t( 26 \n    std::thread([&filter, maxVal, &vals]      // use RAII object 27 \n                {                              28 \n                  for (auto i = 0; i <= maxVal; ++i) 29 \n                    { if (filter(i)) vals.push_back(i); } 30 \n                }), 31 \n                &std::thread::join           // RAII action 32 \n  ); 33\n\n#### 第 292 页原文\n\nPage 292 \n \n  if (conditionsAreSatisfied()) { 1 \n    t.get().join();                           // let t finish 2 \n    performComputation(vals);  3 \n    return true;  4 \n  }                                     5 \n  return false;          6 \n};                                            // join thread 7 \n                                              // running lambda 8 \nIn this case, we’ve chosen to do a join on the asynchronously running thread in 9 \nthe ThreadRAII destructor, because, as we saw earlier, doing a detach could lead 10 \nto some truly nightmarish debugging. We also saw earlier that doing a join could 11 \nlead to performance anomalies (that, to be frank, could also be unpleasant to d e-12 \nbug), but given a choice between undefined behavior (which detach would get 13 \nus), program termination (which use of a raw std::thread would yield), or pe r-14 \nformance anomalies, performance anomalies seems like the best of a bad lot.  15 \nAlas, Item 41 demonstrates that using ThreadRAII to perform a join on 16 \nstd::thread destruction can sometimes lead not just to a performance anomaly, 17 \nbut to a hung program. The “proper” solution to these kinds of problems would be 18 \nto communicate to the asynchronously running lambda that we no longer need its 19 \nwork and that it should return early, but there’s no support in C++11 or C++14 for 20 \ninterruptible threads. They can be implemented by hand, but that’s a t opic beyond 21 \nthe scope of this book.†  22 \nItem 19 explains that because ThreadRAII declares a destructor, there will be no 23 \ncompiler-generated move operat ions, but there is no reason ThreadRAII objects 24 \nshouldn’t be movable. If compilers were to generate these functions, the functions 25 \nwould do the right thing, so explicitly requesting their creation is appropriate: 26 \nclass ThreadRAII { 27 \npublic: 28 \n  using RAIIAction = void (std::thread::*)();       // as before 29 \n                                                             \n† A nice treatment of this topic is in Anthony Williams’ C++ Concurrency in Action (Manning \nPublications, 2012), section 9.2.\n\n#### 第 293 页原文\n\nPage 293 \n \n  ThreadRAII(std::thread&& thread, RAIIAction a)    // as before 1 \n  : action(a), t(std::move(thread)) {} 2 \n  ~ThreadRAII()                                     // as before 3 \n  { if (t.joinable()) (t.*action)(); }  4 \n  ThreadRAII(ThreadRAII&&) = default;               // support 5 \n  ThreadRAII& operator=(ThreadRAII&&) = default;    // moving 6 \n  std::thread& get() { return t; } 7 \nprivate:                                            // as before 8 \n  RAIIAction action; 9 \n  std::thread t; 10 \n}; 11 \nA class like ThreadRAII helps avoid early program termination due to destruction 12 \nof joinable std::threads, but the drawbacks associated with join-on-13 \ndestruction and detach-on-destruction may convince you that a better solution is 14 \nto follow the advice of Item 37 and stay away from std::threads in the first 15 \nplace. Doing that would allow you to deal with futures instead of std::threads, 16 \nand fu tures don’t invoke std::terminate in their destructors. What they do in 17 \ntheir destructors is sufficiently interesting , however, that it’s worth an Item of its 18 \nown. That Items is Item 40. It’s coming right up. 19 \nThings to Remember 20 \n Make std::threads unjoinable on all paths. 21 \n join-on-destruction can lead to difficult-to-debug performance anomalies. 22 \n detach-on-destruction can lead to difficult-to-debug undefined behavior. 23 \n Item 40: Be aware of varying thread handle destructor  be-24 \nhavior. 25 \nItem 39 explains that a joinable std::thread corresponds to an underlying  sys-26 \ntem thread of execution. A future for a non-deferred task (see Item 38) has a simi-27 \nlar relationship to a system thread. As such, both std::thread objects and future 28 \nobjects can be thought of as handles to system threads.  29 \nFrom this perspective , it’s interesting that std::threads and futures have such 30 \ndifferent behaviors in their destructors. As noted in Item 39, destruction of a joi n-31\n\n#### 第 294 页原文\n\nPage 294 \n \nable std::thread terminates your program, because the two obvious altern a-1 \ntives—an implicit join and an implicit detach—were considered worse  choices. 2 \nYet destruction of a future sometimes performs an implicit join, sometimes it per-3 \nforms an implicit detach, and sometimes it does neither. It never causes program 4 \ntermination.  5 \nThis thread handle behavioral bouillabaisse deserves closer examination. 6 \nWe’ll begin with the obs ervation that a future is one end of a communications 7 \nchannel through which a callee transmits a result to a caller.† The callee (running 8 \nasynchronously, except in the case of deferred tasks) writes the result of its co m-9 \nputation into the communications cha nnel (typically via a std::promise object), 10 \nand the caller reads that result using a future. You can think of it as follows, where 11 \nthe dashed arrow shows the flow of information from callee to caller: 12 \n 13 \nBut where is the callee’s result stored? The callee could finish before the caller in-14 \nvokes get on a corresponding future, so the result can’t be stored in the callee’s 15 \nstd::promise. That object, being local to the callee, would be destroyed when the 16 \ncallee finished. 17 \nThe result can’t be stored in the caller’s future, either, because  (among other re a-18 \nsons) a std::future may be used to create a std::shared_future (thus trans-19 \nferring ownership of the callee’s result from the std::future to the 20 \nstd::shared_future), which may then be copied many times after t he original 21 \nstd::future is destroyed. Given that not all result types can be copied (e.g., 22 \nstd::unique_ptr—see Item 20) and that the result must live at least as long as 23 \nthe last future referring to it, which of the potentially many futures corresponding 24 \nto the callee would be the one to contain its result? 25 \n                                                             \n† Item 41 explains that the kind of  communications channel associated with a future can be \nemployed for other purposes. For this Item, however, we’ll consider only its use as a mec h-\nanism for a callee to convey its result to a caller.\n\n#### 第 295 页原文\n\nPage 295 \n \nBecause neither objects associated with the callee  nor objects associated with the 1 \ncaller are suitable places to store the callee’s result, it’s stored in a location outside 2 \nboth. This location is known as the shared state. The shared state is typically rep-3 \nresented by a heap -based object, but its type, in terface, and implementation are 4 \nnot specified by the Standard. Standard Library authors are free to implement 5 \nshared states in any way they like.  6 \nWe can envision the relationship among the callee, the caller,  and the shared state 7 \nlike this, where dashed arrows once again represent the flow of information: 8 \n 9 \nThe existence of the shared state is important, because the behavior of a future’s 10 \ndestructor—the topic of this Item —is determined by the shared state associated 11 \nwith the future. In particular, 12 \n The destructor for the last future referring to a shared state for a non -13 \ndeferred task launched via std::async blocks until the task completes. In 14 \nessence, the destructor for such a future does an implicit join on the thread 15 \non which the asynchronously-executing task is running.  16 \n The destructor for all other futures simply destroys the future object . For 17 \nasynchronously running tasks, this amounts to an implicit detach on the u n-18 \nderlying thread. For deferred tasks for which this is the final future, it means 19 \nthat the deferred task will never run. 20 \nThese rules sound more complicated than they are. What we’re really dealing with 21 \nis a simple “normal” behavior and one lone exception to it. The “normal” behavior 22 \nis that a future’s destructor destroys the future obje ct. That’s it. It doesn’t join 23 \nwith anything, it doesn’t detach from anything, it doesn’t run anything. It just d e-24 \nstroys the future’s data members. (Well, okay, it does one more thing. It also de c-25 \nrements the reference count inside the shared state that’s manipulated by both the 26 \nfutures referring to it and the callee’s std::promise. This reference count makes 27\n\n#### 第 296 页原文\n\nPage 296 \n \nit possible for the library to know when the shared state can be destroyed. For i n-1 \nformation about reference counting, see Item 21.) 2 \nThe exception to this normal behavior arises only for a future for which all of the 3 \nfollowing apply: 4 \n It refers to a shared state that was created due to a call to std::async. 5 \n The task’s launch policy is std::launch::async (see Item 38), either b e-6 \ncause that was chosen by the runtime system or because it was specified in the 7 \ncall to std::async. 8 \n The future is the last future referring to the shared state . For 9 \nstd::futures, this will always be the case . For std::shared_futures, how-10 \never, if other std::shared_futures refer to the same shared state as the fu-11 \nture being destroyed, the future being destroyed follows the “normal” behavior 12 \n(i.e., it simply destroys its data members). 13 \nOnly when all of these conditions are fulfilled does a future’s destructor exhibit 14 \nspecial behavior, and that behavior is to block until the asynchronously running 15 \ntask completes. Practically speaking, this amounts to an implicit join on the 16 \nthread running the std::async-created task. 17 \nIt’s not uncommon to hear this exception to “normal” future destructor behavior 18 \nsummarized as “Futures from std::async block in their destructors.” To a first 19 \napproximation, that’s correct, but  sometimes you need more than a f irst approxi-20 \nmation. Now you know the truth in all its glory and wonder. 21 \nWhat you may wonder is why there’s a special rule for shared states for non -22 \ndeferred tasks that are launched by std::async. It’s a reasonable question. From 23 \nwhat I can tell, the Standardization Committee wanted to avoid the problems asso-24 \nciated with an implicit detach (see Item 39), but they didn’t want to adopt as radi-25 \ncal a policy a s mandatory program termination (as they did for joinable 26 \nstd::threads—again, see Item 39), so they compromised on an implicit join. 27 \nThe decision was not without controversy, and there was serious talk about aban-28\n\n#### 第 297 页原文\n\nPage 297 \n \ndoning this behavior for  C++14. In the end, however, no change was made, so the 1 \nbehavior of destructors for futures is consistent in C++11 and C++14.  2 \nThe API for futures offers no way to determine whether a future refers to a shared 3 \nstate arising from a call to std::async, so given a random future object , it’s not 4 \npossible to know whether it will block in its destructor waiting for an asynchr o-5 \nnously running task to finish. This has some interesting implications: 6 \n// this container might block in its dtor, because one or more 7 \n// contained futures could refer to shared state for a non- 8 \n// deferred task launched via std::async 9 \nstd::vector<std::future<void>> futs;   // see Item 41 for info 10 \n                                       // on std::future<void> 11 \nclass Widget {                         // Widget objects might 12 \npublic:                                // block in their dtors 13 \n  … 14 \nprivate:                               15 \n  std::shared_future<double> fut;  16 \n};                         17 \nvoid doWork(std::future<int> fut);     // fut might block  18 \n                                       // in its dtor  19 \n                                       // (see Item 17 for info 20 \n                                       // on by-value params) 21 \nOf course, if you have a way of knowing that a given future does not  satisfy the 22 \nconditions that trigger the special des tructor behavior (e.g., due to program logic), 23 \nyou’re assured that that future won’t block in its destructor. For example, only 24 \nshared states arising from calls to std::async qualify for the special behavior, but 25 \nthere are other ways that shared states get  created. One is the use of  26 \nstd::packaged_task.  A std::packaged_task object prepares a function  (or 27 \nother callable entity)  for asynchronous execution by wrapping it such that its r e-28 \nsult is put into a shared state . A future referring to that shared state can then be 29 \nobtained via std::packaged_task’s get_future function: 30 \nint calcValue();                      // func to run 31 \nstd::packaged_task<int()>             // wrap calcValue so it 32 \n  pt(calcValue);                      // can run asynchronously 33 \nauto ptFut = pt.get_future();         // get future for pt 34\n\n#### 第 298 页原文\n\nPage 298 \n \nOnce created, the std::packaged task pt can be run on a thread.  (It could be run 1 \nvia a call to std::async, too, but if you want to run a task using std::async, 2 \nthere’s little reason to create a std::packaged task, because std::async does 3 \neverything std::packaged_task does before it schedules the task for execution.)  4 \nstd::packaged_tasks aren’t copyable, so  when pt is passed to the 5 \nstd::thread constructor, it must be cast to an rvalue (via std::move—see 6 \nItem 25), thus ensuring that it will be moved into the data area associated with the 7 \nthread, not copied: 8 \nstd::thread t(std::move(pt));         // run pt on t 9 \nAt this point, we know that the future ptFut doesn’t refer to a shared state created 10 \nby a call to std::async, so its destructor will behave normally. We can therefore 11 \ncall the doWork function (declared above) without fear that when doWork’s by -12 \nvalue parameter fut is destroyed, it will block waiting for t to finish.  13 \nstd::packaged_task::get_future returns a std::future object, which is  a 14 \nmove-only type, so for the call to doWork to compile, we must apply std::move to 15 \nptFut , just as we did when passing pt to the std::thread constructor:  16 \ndoWork(std::move(ptFut));             // doWork won't block on t 17 \nThis example actually lends some insight into the normal behavior for future d e-18 \nstructors, but it’s easier to see if the statements are put together inside a scope: 19 \n{                                         // begin scope 20 \n  std::packaged_task<int()>               // as above 21 \n    pt(calcValue); 22 \n  auto ptFut = pt.get_future();           // as above 23 \n  std::thread t(std::move(pt));           // as above 24 \n  doWork(std::move(ptFut));               // as above 25 \n  …                                       // see below 26 \n}                                         // end scope 27\n\n#### 第 299 页原文\n\nPage 299 \n \nThe most interesting code here is the “ …” that follows the call to doWork and pre-1 \ncedes the e nd of the scope . W hat makes it interesting is what can happen to the 2 \nstd::thread object t inside the “…” region. There are three basic possibilities: 3 \n Nothing happens to t. In this case, t will be joinable at the end of the scope . 4 \nThat will cause the program to be terminated (see Item 39). 5 \n A join is done on t. In this case, there would be no need for ptFut (or the 6 \nparameter fut) to block in its destructor, because the join call is already pre-7 \nsent in the calling code. 8 \n A detach is done on t. In this case, there would be no need for ptFut (or 9 \nfut) to detach in its destructor, because the calling code already does that. 10 \nIn other words, when you have a future corresponding to a shared state that arose 11 \ndue to a std::packaged_task, there’s usually no need to adopt a special destruc-12 \ntion policy, because the decision among termination, joining, or detaching will be 13 \nmade in the code that ma nipulates the std::thread on which the 14 \nstd::packaged_task is typically run. 15 \nThings to Remember 16 \n Future destructors normally just destroy the future’s data members. 17 \n The final future referring to a shared state for a non-deferred task launched via 18 \nstd::async blocks until the task completes. 19 \n Item 41: Consider void futures for one-shot event commu-20 \nnication. 21 \nSometimes it’s useful for a task to tell a second (asynchronously-running) task that 22 \na particular event has occurred , because the second task can’t proceed until the 23 \nevent has taken place . Perhaps a data structure has been initialized, a stage of 24 \ncomputation has been completed, or a significant sensor value has been detected. 25 \nWhen that’s the case, what’s the best way for this kind of inter -thread communica-26 \ntion to take place? 27\n\n#### 第 300 页原文\n\nPage 300 \n \nAn obvious approach is to use a condition variable. If we call the task that detects 1 \nthe condition the detecting task and the task reacting to the condition the reacting 2 \ntask, the strategy is simple: the reacting task waits on a condition variable  3 \n(condvar), and the detecting t hread notifies that condvar when the event occurs. 4 \nGiven 5 \nstd::condition_variable cv;             // condvar for event 6 \nstd::mutex m;                           // mutex for use with cv 7 \nthe code in the detecting task is as simple as simple can be: 8 \n…                                       // detect event 9 \ncv.notify_one();                        // tell reacting task 10 \nIf there were multiple reacting tasks to be notified, it would be appropriate to r e-11 \nplace notify_one with notify_all, but for now, we’ll assume there’s only one 12 \nreacting task. 13 \nThe code for the  reacting task is a bit more complicated, because before calling 14 \nwait on the condvar, it must lock the mutex through a std::unique_lock object. 15 \n(Locking a mutex before waiting on a condition variable is typical for  threading 16 \nlibraries. The need to lock the mutex through a std::unique_lock object is 17 \nsimply part of  the C++11 API.) Here ’s the conceptual approach, although by this 18 \npoint in this book, you probably suspect that when I call code “conceptual,” it’s not 19 \nreally correct. Your suspicion is well-founded, but set that aside for a moment: 20 \n…                                      // prepare to react 21 \n{                                      // open critical section 22 \n  std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);  // lock mutex 23 \n  cv.wait(lk);                         // wait for notify; 24 \n                                       // this isn’t correct! 25 \n  …                                    // react to event  26 \n                                       // (mutex is locked) 27 \n}                                      // close crit. section; 28 \n                                       // unlock mutex via 29 \n                                       // lk’s dtor 30", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}, {"title": "原书逐页正文 p.301–p.318", "pages": "p.301–p.318", "raw_text": "#### 第 301 页原文\n\nPage 301 \n \n…                                      // continue reacting 1 \n                                       // (mutex now unlocked) 2 \nThe first issue with this code is what’s sometimes termed a code smell: even if the 3 \ncode works, something doesn’t seem quite right. In this case, the odor emanates  4 \nfrom the need to use a mutex. Mutexes are used to control access to shared data, 5 \nbut it’s entirely possible that the detecting and reacting tasks have no need for 6 \nsuch mediation. For example, the detecting task might be responsible for initiali z-7 \ning a glob al data structure, then turning it over to the reacting task for use. If the 8 \ndetecting task never accesses the data structure after initializing it, and if the re-9 \nacting task never accesses it before the detecting task indicates that it’s ready, the 10 \ntwo tasks will stay out of each other’s way through program logic. There will be no 11 \nneed for a mutex. The fact that the condvar approach requires one leaves behind 12 \nthe unsettling aroma of suspect design. 13 \nEven if you look past that, there are two other problems you should definitely pay 14 \nattention to: 15 \n If the detecting tas k notifies the condvar before the reacting task waits, 16 \nthe reacting task will hang. In order for notification of a condvar to wake an-17 \nother task, the other task must be waiting on that condvar. If the detecting task 18 \nhappens to execute the notification before the reacting task executes the wait, 19 \nthe reacting task will miss the notification, and it will wait forever.  20 \n The wait statement fails to account for spurious wake ups. A fact of life in 21 \nthreading APIs (in many languages —not just C++) is that code waiting on a 22 \ncondition variable may be awakened even if the condvar wasn’t  notified. Such 23 \nawakenings are known as spurious wakeups. Proper c ode deals with them by 24 \nconfirming that  the condition being waited for has actually occurred , and it 25 \ndoes this as its first action after waking. The C++ condvar API makes this e x-26 \nceptionally easy, because it permits a lambda (or other callable entity) that 27 \ntests for the waited-for condition to be passed to wait. That is, the wait call in 28 \nthe reacting task could be written like this: 29 \ncv.wait(lk, 30 \n        []{ return whether the event has occurred; }); 31\n\n#### 第 302 页原文\n\nPage 302 \n \nTaking advantage of this capability requires that the reacting task be able to 1 \ndetermine whether the condition it’s waiting for is true. But in the scenario 2 \nwe’ve been considering, the condition it’s waiting for is the occurrence of an 3 \nevent that the detecting thread is responsible for recognizing. The reacting 4 \nthread may have no way of determining whether the event it’s waiting for has 5 \ntaken place. That’s why it’s waiting on a condition variable! 6 \nThere are many situations where having tas ks communicate using a condvar is an 7 \ngood fit for the problem at hand, but this doesn’t seem to be one of them. 8 \nFor many developers, the next trick in their bag is a shared boolean flag. The flag is 9 \ninitially false. When the detecting thread recognizes the event of interest, it sets 10 \nthe flag: 11 \nstd::atomic<bool> flag(false);      // shared flag; see  12 \n                                    // Item 42 for std::atomic 13 \n 14 \n…                                   // detect event 15 \nflag = true;                        // tell reacting task 16 \nFor its part, the reacting thread simply polls the flag. When it sees that the flag is 17 \nset, it knows that the event it’s been waiting for has occurred: 18 \n…                                   // prepare to react 19 \nwhile (!flag);                      // wait for event 20 \n…                                   // react to event 21 \nThis approach suffers from none of the drawbacks of the condvar -based design. 22 \nThere’s no need for a mutex, no problem if the detecting task sets the flag before 23 \nthe reacting task starts polling, and nothing akin to a spurious wakeup. Good, good, 24 \ngood. 25 \nLess good is the cost of polling in the reacting task. During the time the task is 26 \nwaiting for the flag to be set, the task is essentially blocked, yet it’s still running. As 27 \nsuch, it occupies a hardware thread that another task might be able to make use of, 28 \nit incurs the cost of a context switch each time it starts or completes its timeslice, 29 \nand it could keep a core running that might otherwise be shut down to save power. 30\n\n#### 第 303 页原文\n\nPage 303 \n \nA truly blocked task would do none of these things. That’s an advantage of the 1 \ncondvar-based approach, because a task in a wait call is truly blocked. 2 \nBut there’s another way to block a task—one that doesn’t suffer from the problems 3 \nassociated with condition variables. It’s to have the reacting task wait on a future 4 \nthat’s set by the detectin g task. This may seem like an odd idea. After all, Item 40 5 \nexplains that a future represents the receiving end of a communications channel 6 \nfrom a callee to a (typically asynchronous) caller, and here there’s no callee -caller 7 \nrelationship between the detecting and reacting  tasks. However, Item 40 also 8 \nnotes that a communications channel whose transmitting end is a std::promise 9 \nand whose receiving end is a future  can be used for more than just callee -caller 10 \ncommunication. Such a  communications channel can be used in any situation 11 \nwhere you need to transmit information from one place in your program to anot h-12 \ner. In this case, we’ll use it to transmit information from the detecting task to the 13 \nreacting task, and the information we’ll convey will be that the event of interest 14 \nhas taken place. 15 \nThe design is simple. The detecting task has a std::promise object (i.e., the writ-16 \ning end of the communications channel), and the reacting task has a corresponding 17 \nfuture. When the detecting task sees that the event of interest has occurred, it sets 18 \nthe std::promise (i.e., write into the communications channel) . Meanwhile, the 19 \nreacting task waits on its future. That wait blocks the reacting task until the 20 \nstd::promise has been set. 21 \nNow, both std::promise and futures (i.e.,  std::future and 22 \nstd::shared_future) are templates that require a type parameter . That param-23 \neter indicates the type of data to be transmitted through the communications 24 \nchannel. In our case, however, there’s no data to be conveyed. The only thing of 25 \ninterest to the reacting task is that its future ha s been set (via the corresponding 26 \nstd::promise). What we need for the std::promise and future templates  is a 27 \ntype that indicates that no data is to be conveyed across the communications 28 \nchannel. That type is void. The detecting task will thus use a 29 \nstd::promise<void>, and the reacting task a std::future<void> or 30 \nstd::shared_future<void>. The detecting task will set its 31 \nstd::promise<void> when the event of interest occurs, and the reacting task 32\n\n#### 第 304 页原文\n\nPage 304 \n \nwill wait on its future. Even though the reacting task wo n’t receive any data from 1 \nthe detecting task, the communications channel will permit the reacting task to 2 \nknow when the detecting task has “written” its void data by calling set_value on 3 \nits std::promise. 4 \nSo given  5 \nstd::promise<void> p;               // promise for 6 \n                                    // communications channel 7 \nthe detecting task’s code is trivial, 8 \n…                                   // detect event 9 \np.set_value();                      // tell reacting task 10 \nand the reacting task’s code is equally simple: 11 \n…                                   // prepare to react 12 \np.get_future().wait();              // wait on future 13 \n                                    // corresponding to p 14 \n…                                   // react to event 15 \nLike the approach using a flag, this design requires no mutex, works regardless of 16 \nwhether the detecting task sets its std::promise before the reacting task waits, 17 \nand is immune to spurious wakeups. (Only condition variables are susceptible to 18 \nthat problem.) Like the condvar-based approach, the reacting task is truly blocked 19 \nafter making the wait call, so it consumes no system resources while waiting. Per-20 \nfect, right?  21 \nNot exactly. Sure, a future-based approach  skirts those shoals, but there are other 22 \nhazards to worry about. For example, Item 40 explains that between a 23 \nstd::promise and a future is a shared state, and shared states are typically d y-24 \nnamically allocated. You should therefore assume that this design incurs the cost 25 \nof heap-based memory allocation and deallocation.  26 \nPerhaps more importantly, a std::promise may be set only once. The commun i-27 \ncations channel between a std::promise and a future is a one-shot mechanism: it 28 \ncan’t be used repeatedly. This is a notable difference from the condvar - and flag-29\n\n#### 第 305 页原文\n\nPage 305 \n \nbased designs, both of which can be used to communicate multiple times. (A 1 \ncondvar can be repeatedly notified, and a flag can always be cleared and set again.) 2 \nThe one-shot restriction isn’t as limiting as you might think. Suppose  you’d like to 3 \ncreate a system thread in a suspended state. That is, you’d like to get all the ove r-4 \nhead associated with thread creation out of the way, so tha t when you’re ready to 5 \nexecute something on the thread, the normal thread -creation latency will be 6 \navoided. Or you might want to create a suspended thread so that you could confi g-7 \nure it before letting it run. Such configuration might include things like se tting its 8 \npriority or core affinity. The C++ concurrency API offers no way to do those things, 9 \nbut std::thread objects offer the native_handle member function, the resu lt 10 \nof which is intended to give  you access to the platform’s underlying threading API 11 \n(usually POSIX threads or Windows threads). The lower-level API often does make 12 \nit possible to configure thread characteristics such as priority and affinity. 13 \nAssuming you want to suspend a thread only once (after creation, but before it’s 14 \nrunning its thread function), a design using a void future is a reasonable choice . 15 \nHere’s the essence of the technique: 16 \nstd::promise<void> p; 17 \nvoid react();                        // func for reacting task 18 \nvoid detect()                        // func for detecting task         19 \n{ 20 \n  std::thread t([]                   // create thread 21 \n                {  22 \n                  p.get_future().wait();     // suspend t until 23 \n                  react();                   // future is set 24 \n                });  25 \n  …                                  // here, t is suspended 26 \n                                     // prior to call to react 27 \n  p.set_value();                     // unsuspend t (and thus 28 \n                                     // call reset) 29 \n  …                                  // do additional work 30 \n  t.join();                          // make t unjoinable 31 \n};                                   // (see Item 39) 32\n\n#### 第 306 页原文\n\nPage 306 \n \nBecause it’s important that t become unjoinable on all paths out of detect, use of 1 \nan RAII class like Item 39’s ThreadRAII seems like it would be advisable. Code 2 \nlike this comes to mind: 3 \nvoid detect() 4 \n{ 5 \n  ThreadRAII tr(                          // use RAII object 6 \n    std::thread([] 7 \n                {                        8 \n                  p.get_future().wait(); 9 \n                  react(); 10 \n                }),  11 \n    &std::thread::join                    // risky! (see below)  12 \n  ); 13 \n  …                                       // t's suspended here 14 \n  p.set_value();                          // unsuspend t 15 \n  … 16 \n}; 17 \nThis looks safer than it is. The problem is that if in the first \"…\" region (the one with 18 \nthe “t’s suspended here” comment), an exception is emitted, set_value will never 19 \nbe called on p. That means that the call to wait inside the lambda that’s running on 20 \nt will never return. That, in turn, means that t will never finish running, and that’s 21 \na problem, because the RAII object tr has been configured to perform a join on t 22 \nin tr’s destructor. In other words, if an exception is emitted from the first “ …” re-23 \ngion of code, this function will hang, because tr’s destructor will never complete. 24 \nThere are ways to address this problem, but I’ll leave them in the form of the ha l-25 \nlowed exercise for the reader.† Here, I’d like to show how the original code (i.e., not 26 \nusing ThreadRAII) can be extended to suspend and then unsuspend not  just one 27 \nreacting task, but many. It’s a simple generalization, because the key is to use 28 \nstd::shared_futures instead of a std::future in the react code. Once you 29 \n                                                             \n† A reasonable place to begin researching the matter is the 24 December 2013 blog post, \n“ThreadRAII + Thread Suspension = Trouble?”\n\n#### 第 307 页原文\n\nPage 307 \n \nknow that the std::future’s share member function transfers ownership of its 1 \nshared state to the std::shared_future object produced by share, the code 2 \nnearly writes itself. The only subtlety is that each reacting thread needs its own 3 \ncopy of the std::shared_future that refers to the shared state, so the 4 \nstd::shared_future obtained from share is captured by value by the lambdas 5 \nrunning on the reacting threads: 6 \nstd::promise<void> p;                // as before 7 \nvoid detect()                        // now for multiple  8 \n{                                    // reacting tasks 9 \n  auto sf = p.get_future().share();  // sf's type is 10 \n                                     // std::shared_future<void> 11 \n  std::vector<std::thread> vt;              // container for 12 \n                                            // reacting threads 13 \n  for (int i = 0; i < threadsToRun; ++i) { 14 \n    vt.emplace_back([sf]{ sf.wait();        // wait on local 15 \n                          react(); });      // copy of sf; see 16 \n  }                                         // Item 18 for info 17 \n                                            // on emplace_back 18 \n  …                                  // detect hangs if  19 \n                                     // this \"…\" code throws!                          20 \n  p.set_value();                     // unsuspend all threads 21 \n  … 22 \n  for (auto& t : vt) t.join();       // make all threads 23 \n};                                   // unjoinable; see Item 2 24 \n                                     // for info on \"auto&\" 25 \nThe fact that design  using futures can achieve this effect is noteworthy, and that’s 26 \nwhy you should consider it for one -shot event communication. Other approaches 27 \nalso work, however. Use of a boolean flag yields source co de that’s just as simple , 28 \nbut its tradeoffs are different: reacting threads don’t block  (bad), but there’s no 29 \nneed for use of the heap  (good). A condvar -based design could also be made to 30 \nwork here, but because there’s no shared data to protect by the mute x a condvar 31 \nrequires, that approach isn't really appropriate.  32\n\n#### 第 308 页原文\n\nPage 308 \n \nThings to Remember 1 \n For simple event communication, condvar-based designs require a superfluous 2 \nmutex, impose constraints on the relative progress of detecting and reacting 3 \ntasks, and require reacting tasks to verify that the event has taken place. 4 \n Designs employing a boolean flag avoid those problems, but are based on poll-5 \ning, not blocking. 6 \n Using std::promises and futures dodges these issue , but it uses  heap 7 \nmemory for shared states, and it’s limited to one-shot communication. 8 \n Item 42: Use std::atomic for concurrency, volatile for 9 \nspecial memory. 10 \nPoor volatile. So misunderstood. It shouldn’t even be in this chapter, because it 11 \nhas nothing to do with concurrent programming. But in other programming la n-12 \nguages (e.g., Java and C#), it is useful for such programming, and even in C++, some 13 \ncompilers have imbued volatile with semantics that render it helpful in concur-14 \nrent software (but only when compiled with those compilers). It’s thus worthwhile 15 \nto discuss volatile in a chapter on currency  if for no other reason than to dispel 16 \nthe confusion surrounding it. 17 \nThe C++ feature that programmers sometimes confuse volatile with—the fe a-18 \nture that definitely does belong in this chapter —is the std::atomic template. 19 \nInstantiations of this template (e.g., std::atomic<int>, std::atomic<bool>, 20 \nstd::atomic<Widget*>, etc.) offer operations that are guaranteed to be seen as 21 \natomic by other threads. Operations on std::atomic objects behave as if they 22 \nwere inside a mutex -protected critical section, but the operations are generally 23 \nimplemented using special machine instructions that are more efficient than they 24 \nwould be if a mutex were employed.  25 \nConsider this code using std::atomic: 26 \nstd::atomic<int> ai(0);    // atomically initialize ai to 0 27 \nai = 10;                   // atomically set ai to 10 28 \nstd::cout << ai;           // atomically read ai's value 29\n\n#### 第 309 页原文\n\nPage 309 \n \n++ai;                      // atomically increment ai to 11 1 \nai--;                      // atomically decrement ai to 10 2 \nDuring execution of these statements, other threads reading ai may see only va l-3 \nues of 0, 10, or 11. No  other values are  possible (assuming, of course, that this is 4 \nthe only thread modifying ai). 5 \nTwo aspects of this  example are worth noting. First, in the “ std::cout << ai;” 6 \nstatement, the fact that ai is a std::atomic guarantees only that the read of ai is 7 \natomic. There is no guarantee that the entire statement proceeds atomically. B e-8 \ntween the time ai’s value is read  and operator<< is invoked to write it to the 9 \nstandard output, another thread —possibly several threads —may have modified 10 \nai’s value. That has no effect on the behavior of the statement, because opera-11 \ntor<< for ints uses a by -value parameter for the int to o utput (the outputted 12 \nvalue will therefore be the one that was read from ai), but it’s important to under-13 \nstand that what’s atomic in that statement is nothing more than the read of ai. 14 \nThe second noteworthy aspect of the example is the behavior of the last two 15 \nstatements—the increment and decrement of ai. These are each read -modify-16 \nwrite (RMW) operations, yet, they execute atomically. This is one of the nicest 17 \ncharacteristics of the std::atomic types: all the member functions, including 18 \nthose comprising RMW operations, are guaranteed to be seen by other threads as 19 \natomic. 20 \nIn contrast, the corresponding code using volatile guarantees virtually nothing 21 \nin a multithreaded context: 22 \nvolatile int vi(0);        // initialize vi to 0 23 \nvi = 10;                   // set vi to 10 24 \nstd::cout << vi;           // read vi's value 25 \n++vi;                      // increment vi to 11 26 \nvi--;                      // decrement vi to 10 27 \nDuring execution of this code, if other threads are reading the value of vi, they 28 \nmay see anything (e.g, -12, 68, 4090727—anything!). Such code would have und e-29 \nfined behavior, because these statements modify vi, so if other threads are rea d-30\n\n#### 第 310 页原文\n\nPage 310 \n \ning vi at the same time, there are simultaneous readers and writers of memory 1 \nthat’s neither std::atomic nor protected by a mutex, and that’s the definition of a 2 \ndata race.  3 \nAs a concrete example of how the behavior of std::atomics and volatiles can 4 \ndiffer in a multithread ed program, consider a simple counter of each type that’s 5 \nincremented by multiple threads. We’ll initialize each to 0: 6 \nstd::atomic<int> ac(0);    // \"atomic counter\" 7 \nvolatile int vc(0);        // \"volatile counter\"  8 \nWe'll then increment each counter one time  in two simultaneously-running 9 \nthreads: 10 \n/*-----  Thread 1  ----- */     /*-------  Thread 2  ------- */ 11 \n         ac++;                             ac++; 12 \n         vc++;                             vc++; 13 \nWhen both threads have finished, ac’s value (i.e., the value of the std::atomic) 14 \nmust be 2, because each increment occurs as an indivisible operation. vc’s value, 15 \non the other hand, need not be 2, because its increments may not occur atomically. 16 \nEach increment consists of reading vc’s value, incrementing th e value that was 17 \nread, and writing the result back into vc. But these three operations are not gua r-18 \nanteed to proceed atomically  for volatile objects, so it’s possible that the co m-19 \nponent parts of the two increments of vc are interleaved as follows: 20 \n1. Thread 1 reads vc’s value, which is 0. 21 \n2. Thread 2 reads vc’s value, which is still 0. 22 \n3. Thread 1 increments the 0 it read to 1, then writes that value into vc. 23 \n4. Thread 2 increments the 0 it read to 1, then writes that value into vc. 24 \nvc’s final value is therefore 1, even though it was incremented twice.  25 \nThis is not the only possible outcome. vc’s final value is, in general, not predict a-26 \nble, because vc is involved in a data race, and the Standard’s decree that data races 27 \ncause undefined behavior means that compilers may generate code to do literally 28\n\n#### 第 311 页原文\n\nPage 311 \n \nanything. Compilers don’t use this leeway to b e malicious, of course . Rather, they 1 \nperform optimizations that would be valid in programs without data races, and 2 \nthese optimizations yield unexpected and unpredictable behavior in programs 3 \nwhere races are present. 4 \nThe use of RMW operations isn’t the only  situation where std::atomics co m-5 \nprise a concurrency success story and volatiles suffer ignominious failure.  Sup-6 \npose one task computes an important value needed by a second task. When the 7 \nfirst task has computed the value, it must communicate this to the second task. 8 \nItem 41 explains that one way for the first task to communicate the availability of 9 \nthe desired value to the second task is by using a std::atomic<bool>. Code in 10 \nthe task computing the value would look something like this: 11 \nstd::atomic<bool> valAvailable(false); 12 \nauto imptValue = computeImportantValue();  // compute value 13 \nvalAvailable = true;                       // tell other task 14 \n                                           // it's available 15 \nAs humans reading this code, we know it ’s crucial that the assignment to impt-16 \nValue take place before the assignment to valAvailable, but all compilers see is 17 \na pair of assignments to independent variables. As a general rule, compilers are 18 \npermitted to reorder such unrelated assignments. That is, given this sequence of 19 \nassignments (where a, b, x, and y correspond to independent variables), 20 \na = b; 21 \nx = y; 22 \ncompilers may generally reorder them as follows: 23 \nx = y; 24 \na = b; 25 \nEven if compilers don't reorder them, the underlying hardware might do it (or 26 \nmight make it seem to other cores as if it had), because that can sometimes make 27 \nthe code run faster. 28 \nHowever, the use of std::atomics imposes restrictions on how code can be reor-29 \ndered, and one such restriction is that no code that, in the source code, precedes a 30\n\n#### 第 312 页原文\n\nPage 312 \n \nwrite of a std::atomic variable may take place (or appear to other cores to take 1 \nplace) afterwards. That means that in our code, 2 \nauto imptValue = computeImportantValue();  // compute value 3 \nvalAvailable = true;                       // tell other task 4 \n                                           // it's available 5 \nnot only must compilers retain the order of the assignments to imptValue and 6 \nvalAvailable, they must generate code that ensures that the underlying har d-7 \nware does, too. As a result, declaring valAvailable as std::atomic ensures that 8 \nour critical ordering requirement —imptValue must be seen by all threads to 9 \nchange no later than valAvailable does—is maintained. 10 \nDeclaring valAvailable as volatile doesn't impose the same code reordering 11 \nrestrictions: 12 \nvolatile bool valAvailable(false); 13 \nauto imptValue = computeImportantValue(); 14 \nvalAvailable = true;  // other threads might see this assignment 15 \n                      // before the one to imptValue! 16 \nHere, compilers might flip the order of the assignments to imptValue and 17 \nvalAvailable, and even if they don ’t, they might fail to generate machine code 18 \nthat would prevent the underlying hardware from making it possible  for code on 19 \nother cores to see valAvailable change before imptValue. 20 \nThese two issues —no guarantee of operation atomicity and insufficient r e-21 \nstrictions on code reordering —explain why volatile’s not useful for concurrent 22 \nprogramming, but it doesn’t explain  what it is useful for. In a nutshell, it’s for tel l-23 \ning compilers that they’re dealing with memory that doesn’t behave normally.  24 \n“Normal” memory has the characteristic that if you write a value to a memory loca-25 \ntion, the value remains there until something overwrites it. So if I have a normal 26 \nint, 27 \nint x; 28 \nand a compiler sees the following sequence of operations on it, 29\n\n#### 第 313 页原文\n\nPage 313 \n \nauto y = x;           // read x 1 \ny = x;                // read x again 2 \nthe compiler can optimize the generated code by eliminating  the assignment to y, 3 \nbecause it’s redundant with y’s initialization.  4 \nNormal memory also has the characteristic that if you write a value to a memory 5 \nlocation, never read it, and then write to that memory location again, the first write 6 \ncan be eliminated, because it was never used. So given these two adjacent stat e-7 \nments, 8 \nx = 10;               // write x  9 \nx = 20;               // write x again 10 \ncompilers can eliminate the first one. That means that if we have this in the source 11 \ncode, 12 \nauto y = x;           // read x 13 \ny = x;                // read x again 14 \nx = 10;               // write x  15 \nx = 20;               // write x again 16 \ncompilers can treat it as if it had been written like this: 17 \nauto y = x;           // read x 18 \nx = 20;               // write x 19 \nLest you wond er who’d write code that performs these kinds of redundant reads 20 \nand superfluous writes (technically known as redundant loads  and dead stores ), 21 \nthe answer is that humans don’t write it directly —at least we hope they don’t . 22 \nHowever, after compilers take reasonable-looking source code and perform te m-23 \nplate instantiation, inlining, and various common kinds of reordering optimiz a-24 \ntions, it’s not uncommon for the result to have redundant loads and dead stores 25 \nthat compilers can get rid of. 26 \nSuch optimizations are valid only if memory behaves normally. “ Special” memory 27 \ndoesn’t. Probably the most common kind of special memory is memory used for 28 \nmemory-mapped I/O . Locations in such memory actually communicate with p e-29 \nripherals, e.g., external sensors or displays, pri nters, network ports, etc. rather 30\n\n#### 第 314 页原文\n\nPage 314 \n \nthan reading or writing normal memory (e.g., RAM). In such a context, consider 1 \nagain the code with seemingly redundant reads: 2 \nauto y = x;           // read x 3 \ny = x;                // read x again 4 \nIf x corresponds to, say, the temperature reported by a temperature sensor, the 5 \nsecond read of x is not redundant, because the temperature may have changed be-6 \ntween the first and second reads.  7 \nIt’s a similar situation for seemingly superfluous writes. In this code, for example, 8 \nx = 10;               // write x  9 \nx = 20;               // write x again 10 \nif x corresponds to the control port for a radio transmitter, it could be that the 11 \ncode is issuing commands to the radio, and the value 10 corresponds to a different 12 \ncommand from the value 20. Optimizing out the first assignment would change the 13 \nsequence of commands sent to the radio. 14 \nvolatile is the way we tell compilers that we’re dealing with special memory. Its 15 \nmeaning to compilers is  “Don’t perform any optimizations on operations on this 16 \nmemory.” So if x corresponds to special memory, it’d be declared volatile: 17 \nvolatile int x; 18 \nConsider the effect that has on our original code sequence: 19 \nauto y = x;           // read x  20 \ny = x;                // read x again (can't be optimized away) 21 \nx = 10;               // write x  22 \nx = 20;               // write x again (can't be optimized away) 23 \nThis is precisely what we want if x is memory-mapped (or has been mapped to a 24 \nmemory location shared across processes, etc.).  25\n\n#### 第 315 页原文\n\nPage 315 \n \nPop quiz! In that last piece of code, what is y’s type: int or volatile int?†  1 \nThe fact that seemingly redundant loads and dead stores must be preserved when 2 \ndealing with special memory explains, by the way, why std::atomics are unsuit-3 \nable for this kind of work. Compilers are permitted to eliminate such redundant 4 \noperations on std::atomics. The code isn’t written quite the same way it is for 5 \nvolatiles, but if we overlook that for a moment and focus on what compilers are 6 \npermitted to do, we can say that, conceptually, compilers may take this, 7 \nstd::atomic<int> x; 8 \nauto y = x;           // conceptually read x (see below) 9 \ny = x;                // conceptually read x again (see below) 10 \nx = 10;               // write x  11 \nx = 20;               // write x again 12 \nand optimize it to this: 13 \nauto y = x;           // conceptually read x (see below) 14 \nx = 20;               // write x  15 \nFor special memory, this is clearly unacceptable behavior. 16 \nNow, a s it happens, neither of these two statements will compile  when x is 17 \nstd::atomic: 18 \nauto y = x;           // error! 19 \ny = x;                // error! 20 \nThat’s because the copy operations for std::atomic are deleted (see Item 11). 21 \nAnd with good reason. Consider what would happen if the initialization of y with x 22 \ncompiled. Because x is std::atomic, y’s type would be de duced to be 23 \n                                                             \n† y’s type is auto-deduced, so it uses the rules described in Item 2. Those rules dictate that \nfor the declaration of non -reference non -pointer types (which is the case for y), const \nand volatile qualifiers are dropped. y’s type is therefore simply int. This means t hat \nredundant reads of and writes to y can be eliminated. In the example, compilers must pe r-\nform both the initialization of and the assignment to y, because x is volatile, so the sec-\nond read of x might yield a different value from the first one.\n\n#### 第 316 页原文\n\nPage 316 \n \nstd::atomic, too (see Item 2.) I remarked earlier that one of the best things 1 \nabout std::atomics is that all their operations are atomic, but in order for the 2 \ncopy construction of y from x to be atomic, compilers would have to generate code 3 \nto read x and write y in a single atomic operation. Hardware generally can’t do 4 \nthat, so copy construction isn’t supported for std::atomic types. Copy as sign-5 \nment is deleted f or the same reason , which is why the assignment from x to y 6 \nwon’t compile . (The move operations aren’t explicitly de clared in  std::atomic, 7 \nso, p er the rules for compiler -generated special functions described in Item 19, 8 \nstd::atomic offers neither move construction nor move assignment.) 9 \nIt’s possible, of course, to get the value of x into y, but it requires use of 10 \nstd::atomic’s memb er functions load and store. The load member function 11 \nreads a std::atomic’s value atomically, while the store member function writes 12 \nit atomically. To initialize y with x, then, followed by putting x’s value in y, the 13 \ncode must be written like this: 14 \nstd::atomic<int> y(x.load());     // read x 15 \ny.store(x.load());                // read x again 16 \nThis compiles, but the fact that reading x (via x.load()) is a separate function call 17 \nfrom initializing or storing to y makes clear that there is no reason to expect either 18 \nstatement as a whole to execute as a single atomic operation. 19 \nGiven that code, compilers could “optimize” it by storing x’s value in a register i n-20 \nstead of reading it twice: 21 \nregister = x.load();              // read x into register 22 \nstd::atomic<int> y(register);     // init y with register value 23 \ny.store(register);                // store register value into y 24 \nThe result, as you can see, reads from x only once, and that’s the kind of optimiz a-25 \ntion that must be avoided when dealing with special memory . (The optimization 26 \nisn’t permitted for volatile variables.) 27 \nThe situation should thus be clear: 28\n\n#### 第 317 页原文\n\nPage 317 \n \n std::atomic is useful for concurrent programming, but not for accessing spe-1 \ncial memory. 2 \n volatile is useful for accessing special memory, but not for concurrent pr o-3 \ngramming. 4 \nBecause std::atomic and volatile serve different purposes, they can even be 5 \nused together: 6 \nvolatile std::atomic<int> vai;    // operations on vai are 7 \n                                  // atomic and can't be  8 \n                                  // optimized away 9 \nThis could be useful if vai corresponded to a memory -mapped I/O location that 10 \nwas concurrently accessed by multiple threads. 11 \nAs a final note,  some developers prefer to u se std::atomic’s load and store 12 \nmember functions even when they’re not syntactically required, because it makes 13 \nexplicit in the source code that the variables involved aren’t “normal.” Emphasi z-14 \ning that fact isn’t unreasonable. Accessing a std::atomic is typically much slower 15 \nthan accessing a non -std::atomic, and we’ve already  seen that the use of 16 \nstd::atomics prevents compilers from performing certain kinds of statement 17 \nreorderings that would otherwise be permitted . C alling out loads and stores of 18 \nstd::atomics can therefore help identify potential scalability chokepoints. From 19 \na correctness perspective, not seeing a call to store on a variable meant to co m-20 \nmunicate information to other threads (e.g., a flag indicating the availability of d a-21 \nta) could mean that the variable wasn’t declared std::atomic when it should 22 \nhave been. 23 \nThis is more a stylistic issue, however, and as such is quite different from the 24 \nchoice between std::atomic and volatile. std::atomic is a tool for writing 25 \nconcurrent software. volatile is a tool for working with special memory. 26 \nThings to Remember 27 \n std::atomic is for data accessed from multiple threads without using mute x-28 \nes. 29\n\n#### 第 318 页原文\n\nPage 318 \n \n volatile is for memory where reads and writes should never be optimized 1 \naway. 2", "lecture": "", "exercises": ""}]};
